1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun04paul/page/16/mode/1up
2 CONT
2 CONT From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on Walther Ogilvy of Lintrathen:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p10823.htm#i108230
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Walter Ogilvy of Lintrathen1
2 CONT *M, #108230, 
2 CONT *d. 1440
2 CONT *Last Edited=22 Feb 2011
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=1.56%
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Walter Ogilvy of Lintrathen married, firstly, unknown Durward.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, secondly, Isabel Glen, daughter of Sir John Glen and Margaret Erskine of Inchmartin, after 1427.[2],[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1440.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Sir Walter Ogilvy of Auchterhouse and Isabel Ramsay.[1] 
2 CONT *He held the office of High Treasurer [Scotland] in 1425.[3] 
2 CONT *He lived at Lintrathen, Angus, Scotland.[2] 
2 CONT *He was a Commissioner to England negotiating for peace between 1431 and 1437.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Sir Walter Ogilvy of Lintrathen and unknown Durward
2 CONT *1. Sir John Ogilvy of Lintrathen+[1] b. b 1414, d. Jun 1489
2 CONT *2. James Ogilvy [1] b. b 1427
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Sir Walter Ogilvy of Lintrathen and Isabel Glen
2 CONT *1. Isabel Ogilvy+[4] d. 1484
2 CONT *2. Walter Ogilvy+[1]
2 CONT *3. Giles Ogilvy [5]
2 CONT *4. David Ogilvy [1] b. b 28 Jan 1426/27
2 CONT *5. Alexander Ogilvy [1] b. b 1440
2 CONT *6. Patrick Ogilvy [1] b. b 1440
2 CONT *7. George Ogilvy [1] b. b 1440
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT *1. [S8] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 106th edition, 2 volumes (Crans, Switzerland: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 1999), volume 1, page 45. Hereinafter cited as Burke's Peerage and Baronetage
2 CONC , 106th edition.
2 CONT *2. [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Exti
2 CONC nct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume VII, page 121. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *3. [S323] Sir James Balfour Paul, The Scots Peerage: founded on Wood's edition of Sir Robert Douglas's The Peerage of Scotland (Edinburgh, Scotland: David Douglas, 1904), volume I, page 111. Hereinafter cited as The Scots Peerage
2 CONC .
2 CONT *4. [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 228. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
2 CONT *5. [S323] Sir James Balfour Paul, The Scots Peerage, volume I, page 112.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on Lord High Treasurer of Scotland:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treasurer_of_Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT The Treasurer was a senior post in the pre-Union government of Scotland, the Privy Council of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The full title of the post was Lord High Treasurer, Comptroller, Collector-General and Treasurer of the New Augmentation, formed as it was from the amalgamation of four earlier offices. Of these, the Treasurer and Comptroller had 
2 CONC originated in 1425 when the Chamberlain's financial functions were transferred to them.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT *1. ^ Accounts of the Lord High Treasurer, vol. 1, (1877), xiv.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Lord High Treasurer
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Walter was from Auchterhouse, co. Angus and was Sheriff of co. Angus. He died at the battle of Glenbrierachan.
