1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hugh Fraser, Lord Fraser of Lovat (C. 1489 – 1544) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Fraser, 3rd Lord Fraser of Lovat and Jusdiciary of the North for Queen Mary, was a Scottish peer and Chief of Clan Fraser of Lovat from 1524 until 1544. When Hugh was born, his father, Sir Thomas Fraser, 2nd Lord Fraser of Lo
2 CONC vat, was 29 and his mother, Janet Gordon, was 19. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His first wife, taken in 1512, was Anne Grant, widow of John Haliburton of Pitcur.  She was the daughter of John Grant of Grant and Freuchie by Margaret, daughter of Sir James Ogilvie of Deskford.  They had a son, Hugh, who had he
2 CONC  outlived his father would have been his heir. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1539, according to Mackenzie, Hugh resigned the entirety of his inherited properties and castles to the King in exchange of a new charter uniting all those resigned properties into ‘one free and entire barony to be called in al
2 CONC l time coming, The Barony of Lovat.’ For the first time, all of the holdings of the Frasers of the Highlands were consolidated into a single vast empire situated on both sides of Loch Ness in Inverness.  What is more, the new char
2 CONC ter changed the terms in which heirs could inherit. In the case of failure to produce a male her, eligible daughters could inherit.  
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Anne (date unknown), Hugh married Janet Ross, daughter of Walter Ross of Balnagowan and together they produced three sons and two daughters:  Alexander Fraser, eventually 4th Lord Fraser of Lovat; William Fraser
2 CONC  of Struy; Hugh Fraser; Margaret Macrawald of Lovat and Catherine Fraser.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Fraser, Master of Lovat, the only son of the 3rd Lord and Anne Grant fought against the Macdonalds in the battle of Loch Lochy in 1544, the same battle that would claim his father’s life and in which the Master of Lovat would
2 CONC  be killed before he could succeed to the title of 4th Lord Fraser of Lovat. There is in the history reviewed a claim that Janet Ross, the 3rd Lord Fraser’s second spouse had taunted young Hugh, her stepson, to disobey his father'
2 CONC s orders and join him in the battle of Loch Lochy, thus allowing her own son, Alexander, to inherit, assuming young Hugh died in the fight.  Hugh did die in the same battle as did his father and Alexander thereby became heir and 4
2 CONC th Lord Fraser of Lovat. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Fraser died in the Battle of Loch Lochy on June 2, 1544, at the age of 55, and was buried in Beauly Priory.
