1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Margaret Maitland''', daughter of Sir William Maitland, Knight of Thirlestane & Lord Levington and Margaret Wardlaw, was born about 1440. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret married Sir James SCRIMGEOUR of Dudhope, Constable of Dundee, son of Sir John SCRIMGEOUR Constable of Dundee (Sir James SCRIMGEOUR of Dudhope, Constable of Dundee was born about 1428 and died by 31 December 1478 1.).
2 CONT
2 CONT from Scots Peerage (vol 3) 1
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret next married David HERING of Lethendy.1 (David HERING of Lethendy was born about 1425.) Marriage Notes:  "He (James Scrymgeour) married ... secondly, Margaret Maitland, who survived him, and married secondly, David Hering
2 CONC  of Lethendy."
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT SCRIMGEOUR, Matilda+
2 CONT SCRIMGEOUR, James of Dudhope, Constable of Dundee+
2 CONT SCRIMGEOUR, John of Glassary+
2 CONT SCRIMGEOUR, Jane+
2 CONT SCRIMGEOUR, Elizabeth+
2 CONT SCRIMGEOUR, Mariota+
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Maitland-7#_ref-1
2 CONT
2 CONT She married as second wife to James Scrymgeour, Constable of Dundee about 1465 and was mother to his younger children.
2 CONT
2 CONT She survived the Constable who died about 1478.
2 CONT
2 CONT The widow, Margaret married about 1480 as second wife to David Hering of Lethendy.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT At Lethendy on 8 October 1490, when David Hering granted his son and heir James Hering of Cluny the lands of Lethendy and others in Fife and Berwickshire, he reserved a terce for Margaret Maitland.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000227296017959&size=large}}
2 CONT > ''The Scots Peerage'' ed. Sir James Balfour Paul vol. 3 (1906).  Page 307.  < [https://books.google.ht/books?id=6UhmAAAAMAAJ&hl=fr&pg=PA307#v=onepage&q&f=true GoogleBooks] > 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT # https://www.patrickspeople.scot/ourfamily/6503.htm cites 
2 CONT ## Paul, Sir James Balfour (Ed.)  ''The Scots Peerage'' Vol. 3 (1906).  Page 307.  < [https://books.google.ht/books?id=6UhmAAAAMAAJ&hl=fr&pg=PA307#v=onepage&q&f=true GoogleBooks] > 
2 CONT # https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi02scot/page/418/mode/1up… Jacobo Hering filio Johannis Hering gerentibus, a dioto David de rege : — Eeservatis libero tenemento dicto David, ac rationabili tertia '''Margarete Maitland
2 CONC ''' spouse sue cum contigerit: — Test. Job. Hering de Wormet, Wil. Hering de Forenotli, Alex. Falconer, Ric. Law, Job. Hering : — Apud Lethinde, 8 Oct. 1490] :— Test. 1. 21. 4. 26. 19. 25. 14. 20. 3. 9. 8. 24. 12. xii. 245
2 CONT # http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p97.htm#i2913
2 CONT #  https://www.stravaiging.com/history/clan/heron/ John’s son, also John, took possession of the Tower of Lethendy either towards the end of the 14th century or the beginning of the 15th century, being designed John Herring of Let
2 CONC hendy and Glasclune. The family evidently expanded their Perthshire landholdings as the younger John’s grandson, James, received a charter for the lands of Cluny in 1479 and had a charter from his father, David Herring of Glasclun
2 CONC e, in Glasclune, Lethendy and Tullibole in 1490. James’s second son, Andrew, succeeded his father in Glasclune, Lethendy and Tullibole early in the 16th century. (This man:   https://www.geni.com/people/David-Hering-of-Glasclune/6
2 CONC 000000184406028830?through=6000000184406007837)
