1 NAME Patrick /Guthrie/
2 GIVN Patrick
2 SURN Guthrie
2 _MARNM Guthrie
2 NICK Patrick Guthrie of Colliston
1 NAME Patrick Guthrie (goldsmith)
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1550
2 ADDR
3 CITY Saint Andrews
3 STAE Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1614
2 ADDR
3 CITY Saint Andrews
3 STAE Scotland
1 OCCU Goldsmith; Bailee of St Andrews (1601-02)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Guthrie DNA Project: 
2 CONT Require Y-DNA matches traceable to earlier generations for verification of historical paper trail.
2 CONT ______________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT ===NOTES===
2 CONT Caution:  This man was not a Laird of Guthrie. His son, the Rt. Rev. John Guthrie, Bishop of Moray, purchased the lands and title of the Barony of Guthrie becoming the 11th chief in that line. He did not inherit it from his father
2 CONC .
2 CONT __________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in or about 1550, Patrick Guthrie was the son of Charles Guthrie and Agnes Brown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also known as "Patrick of Collision".
2 CONT
2 CONT Patrick Guthrie's marriage to Margaret Raitt took place sometime before 1575, but no Old Parish Records have been located for the couple.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burke's Gentry lists only the following children: John Guthrie (Bishop of Moray, 11th of Guthrie), Charles Guthrie, James Guthrie (Minister in Kinnell and Arbirlot), Helen Guthrie. There are no OPR records.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a goldsmith by trade and also served as a Bailee of St. Andrews from 1601-1602.
2 CONT
2 CONT Minting of Coins
2 CONT 1590 • St Andrews
2 CONT "In St Andrews, in 1590, "at command of the Session," the token irons (communion tokens) were made by Patrick Guthrie, a goldsmith in the town, who also at the same time made 2000 tokens." (Proceedings of the Society of Antiquarie
2 CONC s of Scotland Vol 41.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Patrick Guthrie died in or about 1614.
