1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Sir Robert Graham==
2 CONT * died April 1437. Executed for the assassination of  [http://www.geni.com/people/James-I-of-Scotland/6000000002277472620 '''King James I of Scotland'''] on 20 February 1437; "said to have delivered the fatal blow"
2 CONT * Father:	Sir Patrick Graham, Lord Dundaff, Knight-Banneret d. 1400
2 CONT * Mother: Euphemia (Egidia) Stewart1 d. a 1430
2 CONT * Married: Marion Oliphant
2 CONT
2 CONT >" ... ''Yet I do not doubt but that you shall see the day and time that you shall pray for my soul, for the great good that I have done to you, and to all in this realm of Scotland, that I have thus slain and delivered you of '''
2 CONC so cruel a tyrant'''..." Sir Robert Graham of Kinpont [4]''
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://griffinrampant.wordpress.com/2013/08/15/from-the-handbook-to-english-heraldry-by-charles/ From The Handbook to English Heraldry] by Charles Boutell, p243
2 CONT
2 CONT Seal of Robert Graham of Kinpoint, 1433
2 CONT * Blazon: (?) on a chief three escallops
2 CONT * Supporters: Two lions rampant
2 CONT * The crest appears to be a stag’s attire.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT Married:
2 CONT # unknown partner
2 CONT # by contract dated 1399 to Marion (Mariot) Oliphant, daughter of John Oliphant of Aberdalgie (TSP page 214, below)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children by unknown partner 
2 CONT # Thomas Graham
2 CONT
2 CONT Children by Mariot Oliphant 
2 CONT *  	1. Robert Graham, of Cessfurd
2 CONT *  	2. Walter Graham
2 CONT *  	3. Patrick Graham
2 CONT *  	4. Umfrid Graham
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I4300&tree=CC
2 CONT
2 CONT ===family notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert Graham He was married and left issue.1 He was born at of Kilpont, Scotland.1 He died in 1437 at Stirling, Stirlingshire, Scotland; Executed for assassinating King James I at Perth.1
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft#page/214/mode/2up The Scots Peerage]: Page 214:
2 CONT
2 CONT >" .. An indenture of the date 1399 between Sir Patrick Grahame of Kincardine and Sir John Oliphant of Abergaldie that '''Robin de Grahame''' son the said Sir Patrick, shall wed to wyfe, God willand, daughter of the said Sir John.
2 CONC  ..."
2 CONT
2 CONT ==brief biography==
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Graham was the third son of Patrick Graham of Kincardine. He attended the University of Paris in the 1390s, potentially in preparation for entering the priesthood. In 1399 he married Marion Oliphant, daughter of John Olipha
2 CONC nt of Aberdalgie.[2] Robert's brother Sir Patrick Graham (died 1413) acquired the Earldom of Strathearn through his 1406 marriage to Euphemia Stewart, Countess of Strathearn. Robert became tutor to his nephew, Malise Graham.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He is described as "a grete gentilman... a man of grete wit and eloquence, wounder suttilye willyd and expert in the lawe".[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT An unfortunate episode began in 1413 after his brother, Patrick , was murdered by the Drummonds. He had been created Earl Palatine of the royal Earldom of Strathearn after marrying the grand-daughter of Robert III, and had acquire
2 CONC d the vastly rich estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had left his infant son in the care of his younger brother, Sir Robert. In 1427 King James I seized the wealthy earldom and gave the boy only the poor Highland parish of Aberfoyle and the empty title of Earl of Menteith.
2 CONT He also sent the unfortunate child as a hostage to England where he was imprisoned for nearly twenty five years.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Grahams always resented injustice and Sir Robert Graham of Kilpont protested loudly. He tried to arrest the king in Parliament, and then publicly renounced his allegiance to a tyrant.
2 CONT
2 CONT . . . Comment: However, more recent historians have doubted that the deprivation of Malise Graham was such a strong motivation for Sir Robert's actions.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT On February 21, 1437, Sir Robert led a band of Highlanders to Perth where they trapped the King in the cellar of the Blackfriars Monastery and stabbed him to death. For this crime Sir Robert and his sons were tortured and executed
2 CONC  in a most horrible manner at Stirling.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==historical context of the King's assassination==
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.rossettiarchive.org/docs/5-1881.raw.html The King's Tragedy (James I of Scots - 20 February 1437) by Dante Gabriel Rossetti]
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in 1394, James I became heir to the throne of Scotland after the murder of his older brother at the hands of a rival political faction. Fearing that James might suffer the same fate, his father, Robert III, attempted to send 
2 CONC the prince to France in secret. But James was intercepted by the army of Henry Bolingbroke (Henry IV), which spirited him off to England where he remained in captivity (alternating between stints at court, in the Tower, and in mil
2 CONC itary service) until 1424. While he was thus immured, James wrote The Kingis Quair, which speaks of his love for Joan Beaufort, whom he married shortly before his return to Scotland. After James's return to Scotland, he attempted 
2 CONC to implement a number of sweeping reforms that served to strengthen his own central authority through the often violent suppression of an unruly and rebellious feudal nobility. The strong opposition to these efforts eventually led
2 CONC  to James's murder by '''rebel lords''' in late February 1437, six months after the failed campaign at Roxburgh.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Katy, bar the door!==
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_Douglas
2 CONT
2 CONT Legend has it that during the King's stay at a Dominican chapterhouse in Perth, a group of men led by Sir Robert Graham came to the door searching for the King in order to kill him. The King's Chamberlain, Robert Stewart, Master o
2 CONC f Atholl, had removed the bolt from the door of the room in which James and his queen were staying.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT James fled into a sewer tunnel as the queen and her ladies quickly replaced the floorboards to hide his location.[2] Catherine sprang to the door and placed her arm through the staples to bar the assassins' entrance.[3] However, t
2 CONC hey forced the door open anyway, breaking Catherine's arm, and discovered and killed the King. From that point on, according to the story, Catherine took the surname of "Barlass".
2 CONT
2 CONT The idiomatic phrase "Katy, bar the door!" (a warning of the approach of trouble) may have its origins in the story of Catherine Douglas.[4] Dante Gabriel Rossetti recounted the story of Catherine Douglas in verse in 1881, under t
2 CONC he title "The King's Tragedy". This poem contains the line "Catherine, keep the door!"
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.scotlandmag.com/magazine/issue41/12008888.html Scotland Magazine] Issue 41 - The murder of King James 1:
2 CONT
2 CONT They [the conspirators] threatened, some say wounded, the Queen and her attendants, but of the King there was no sign. They searched the chamber and the search spread to other rooms in the palace. Assuming that the silence above m
2 CONC eant that the danger had passed, James called out for assistance in escaping from his refuge. The killers heard and returned to the bedchamber where they found their victim. Two of them went down after him. In the confined space, 
2 CONC the King took them by their throats and tried to wrest the knives from their hands. Then Sir Robert Graham joined them and the King was finally dispatched, receiving 16 stab wounds to his body. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://allkindsofhistory.wordpress.com/2011/03/17/they-dont-like-it-up-em-revisiting-the-sordid-deaths-of-edmund-ironside-edward-ii-and-james-i-of-scotland/ “They don’t like it up ‘em…” Revisiting the sordid deaths of Edmund
2 CONC  Ironside, Edward II, Kenneth II and James I of Scotland]
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Finally, Sir Robert Graham jumped down into the void ‘with an horribill and mortall weapon in his hand’ – no light decision, given the ease with which it would have been possible to pick up a fatal illness in the pit. Refusing J
2 CONC ames’s plea for mercy, Grahame ran him through with his sword, and – the first two killers having by now freed themselves from the ordure – the king was finished off by all three men. ‘It was,’ Shirley concludes, ‘reported by true
2 CONC  persons that saw him dead, that he had sixteen deadly wounds in his breast, withouten many and other in diverse places of his body.’ [Matheson pp.42-3]"
2 CONT
2 CONT The conspirators then fled.
2 CONT
2 CONT More on the assassination:  http://thefreelancehistorywriter.com/2013/05/24/the-assassination-of-king-james-i-of-scotland/
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sir Robert's capture and death==
2 CONT
2 CONT But they [the conspirators] had miscalculated. They found they had no support and had a vengeful Queen – a chip off the block of Edward I, the Hammer of the Scots – after them. Within a month the leaders were caught. The Earl of A
2 CONC tholl had a red-hot coronet placed on his head before he and his son were beheaded.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Stewart was tortured to death. Sir Robert Graham was found cowering beneath a rock on the edge of Loch Bhac above Blair Atholl, still known as Graham’s Rock.
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.electricscotland.com/history/rannoch7.htm A History of Rannoch]
2 CONT
2 CONT James I was assassinated in Perth in 1437 and his murder Sir Robert Graham sought sanctuary in Atholl where he was captured by Robert Riabhach, the 4th Chief of Clan Donnachaidh.  Some accounts give the place of his capture as Gle
2 CONC nmore, the wild country south of Schichallion.  Much more likely is the shelter bed where he was supposed to be surprised at the burn that now has his name Allt Ghramaich (Graham’s Burn) which flows into Loch Bhac.  He was said to
2 CONC  have fought ferociously but he had determined opponents, for not only did have Robert to contend with but also Stewart of Garth joined in the chase.  John Graham would without a doubt have fought even harder if he had know what h
2 CONC is fate was to be.  If he expected mercy from James’ Queen; after all James used to call her his ‘milk-white dove’, he certainly did not get it.  He was nailed to a tree and dragged through the streets; his body was torn with pinc
2 CONC ers, his son was tortured and beheaded before him, and at length he was put to death.
2 CONT
