1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===THOMAS DUNBAR, EARL OF MORAY===
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Evidence from the Register of the Bishopric of Moray===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''21 September 1393: Renunciation by Thomas de Dunbar, Earl of Moray, acting as procurator for his brother Alexander of Dunbar, acknowledging that he had no right to Maison Dieu next to Elgin: "Domo Dei juxta Elgyn". [https://arc
2 CONC hive.org/details/registrumepiscop00bann/page/129/mode/1up Registrum Episcopatus Moraviensis etc: charter number 118 on pp. 129-30]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT == [http://thepeerage.com/p39240.htm#i392395 Biographical Summary by Darryl Lundy's Peerage] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas de Dunbar, 2nd Earl of Moray [1]
2 CONT *M, #392395, 
2 CONT *d. 1415
2 CONT *Last Edited=17 Mar 2011
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=0.62%
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas de Dunbar, 2nd Earl of Moray married Margaret (?) before February 1391/92.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1415, in or after spring.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of John de Dunbar, 1st Earl of Moray and Marjorie Stewart.[2] 
2 CONT *He held the office of Sheriff of Inverness before 1392.[1] 
2 CONT *He succeeded to the title of 2nd Erl of Moray [S., 1372] circa February 1391/92.[1] 
2 CONT *He fought in the Battle of Homildon Hill in 1402, where he was captured by the English.[1] 
2 CONT *He held the office of Sheriff of Banff in 1405.[1] 
2 CONT *In 1405 he was released by the English.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Thomas de Dunbar, 2nd Earl of Moray and Margaret (?)
2 CONT *1. James Dunbar, 4th Earl of Moray+[2] d. c 10 Aug 1429
2 CONT *2. Thomas Dunbar, 3rd Earl of Moray [2] d. 1427
2 CONT *3. Janet Dunbar+[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT *1. [S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 1, page 1207. Hereinafter cited as Burke's 
2 CONC Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT *2. [S37] Charles Mosley, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
