1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sir John Oliphant of Aberdalgie, Kt. was our 20th great grandfather.
2 CONT He is no longer. He is now............
2 CONT Sir John Oliphant of Aberdalgie, Kt. is our first cousin 18 times removed.
2 CONT Janet MIlburn 3/24/23
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE:  Change in Sir John Oliphant's Date of Death from 1/23/1444 to DOB 1/23/1445, as the Battle of Arbroath took place on either 1/24/1445 or 1/24/1446 (scholars are still debating this).  It is also mention in the below referen
2 CONC ces
2 CONT
2 CONT Also, the dates of marriage for both wives do not make sense as that marriage date for Sir John and Margaret Ogilvy states 1424, when he was 9 years of age and Margaret wasn't even born yet.  The conclusion is that John Oliphant w
2 CONC as never married to Margaret Ogilvy as she was married to Thomas Dunbar in 1391
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Oliphant, of Aberdalgy. This gentleman took part with the Ogilvys in their feud with the Lindseys, and was slain in the encounter between the contending parties at Arbroath in 1445, when the Earl of Crawfurd also fell. He
2 CONC  espoused Isabel, daughter of Walter Ogilvy, esq. of Auchterhouse, and had issue,
2 CONT
2 CONT # Laurence (Sir), his heir. 
2 CONT # James. 
2 CONT # Margaret, m. to William Wardlaw,esq. of Torrie, in Fife.  
2 CONT # Isabel, m. o Alexander Blair, of Balthyock. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://thepeerage.com/p21088.htm#i210871
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 23 January 1445/46 at Arbroath, Angus, Scotland, killed in a battle between the Lindsays and his wife's family.1  In 1442 he was retoured heir to grandfather.1
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clan_Oliphant
2 CONT
2 CONT '''15th century and clan conflicts'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Members of Clan Oliphant fought on the side of the Clan Ogilvy who were also supported by men from the Clan Seton, Clan Gordon and men from the Clan Forbes of Pitsligo at the Battle of Arbroath on 24 January 1445.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their enemy was the Master of Crawford and his Clan Lindsay who advanced with over one thousand men. His father, the Earl of Crawford, rode between the two armies in an attempt to call a truce. However, an ill-advised Ogilvie, thi
2 CONC nking that this was the start of the Lindsay's attack, threw his spear at the Earl, hitting him in the mouth and killing him instantly.
2 CONT
2 CONT So began the battle, which went in Clan Lindsay's favour. Here fell Ogilvie of Inverquharty, Forbes of Pitsligo, Brucklay of Gartley, Gordon of Borrowfield, and '''Sir John Oliphant of Aberdalgie''' (father of Laurence, first Lord
2 CONC  Oliphant), along with 500 or so Ogilvis. However, the Lindsays lost a disproportionate amount of men, most notably the Earl himself. [2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Riddare, Dog i strid vid battle of Arbroath,Angusshire,Scotland
2 CONT
