1 NAME Sir Laurence Oliphant of Aberdalgie, 1st Lord Oliphant
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE BET 1434 AND 1438
2 PLAC Aberdalgie
2 ADDR
3 CITY Gask
3 CTRY Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1500
2 ADDR
3 CITY Perth
3 CTRY Scotland
1 OCCU Sheriff of Perthshire, 1st Lord Oliphant
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sir Laurence Oliphant of Aberdalgie, first Lord Oliphant (d. 1500?) was the eldest son of Sir John Oliphant of Aberdalgie (d. 1446) by Isabel, daughter of Walter Ogilvy of Auchterhouse. and sister of Alexander Ogil
2 CONC vy, second Baron Ogilvy of Inverquharity.
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://sites.google.com/site/goodwhiskytastingsbelgium/home/history/official-scottish-clans-and-families/clan-oliphant
2 CONT
2 CONT By his wife, Lady Isabel Hay, youngest daughter of William, first Earl of Erroll, he had three sons: 
2 CONT # John, second Lord Oliphant. (d. 1516) 
2 CONT # William of Berriedale, Caithness (acquired through marriage with Christian, heiress of Alexander Sutherland of Duffus :) and 
2 CONT # George.
2 CONT
2 CONT In. his youth he went to France to study the art of war, and subsequently travelled in Italy and elsewhere. He was created a peer some time before 30 October. 1458. when his name so appears as witness to a charter: and under the t
2 CONC itle of Lord Oliphant he sat in the parliament of 14 October 1467. He had a charter of the Barony of Owres, Kincardineshire, from his maternal grandfather. Walter Ogilvy on 7 November 1468. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1470 he held the office of Sheriff of Perthshire. (2) On 24 July, 1474. the Marchmond herald was sent with letters to him and the Earl of Buchan to "staunch their gathering for the Court of Forfar". (3) and on 28 August to summ
2 CONC on them for their gathering. (4) The gathering seems to have resulted in bloodshed, for in September Oliphant was summoned to answer for the slaughter of Thom of Preston. (5)
2 CONT
2 CONT Oliphant was one of a Commission named on 30 August, 1404, to negotiate a marriage between James, Duke of Rothesay, heir-apparent of the Scottish throne, and Lady Anne de la Pole daughter of John, Duke of Sheffield, a niece of Ric
2 CONC hard III of England (6): and also to treat for a peace and alliance with England (7). Of the treaty concluded at Nottingham on 12 September (8) he was one of the conservators (9). He sat in the first parliament of James IV on 6 Oc
2 CONC tober 1488, when he was chosen a lord of articles for the barons. He was also sworn a privy councillor, and in 1490 constituted a justiciary within his own bounds and those of Strathbaird.
2 CONT
2 CONT He sided with the King during the rebellion of 1489, and, while the King was crushing the rising in the west, sent information to him of the movements of the rebel nobles in the north. (10)
2 CONT
2 CONT On 26 February 1490-1 he had a safe-conduct to England for six months (11); and on 14 June he received a safe-conduct and protection for a year from Henry VII as Ambassador to Charles, King of’ France, and the King and Queen of Ca
2 CONC stile, Aragon and Sicily. (12) In 1491 he was Bailie of Methven, (13) and in 1493 and subsequent years he was Keeper of Edinburgh Castle. (14) He was one of the lords chosen by the King to the Session of 14 October, 1495.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died about 1500. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dictionary of National Biography Volume 42 T F. Henderson. 1895.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
