2 CONT
2 CONT == Research Notes ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Ogilvy, 1st Laird of Dunlugas
2 CONT Birth - 1509, Dunlugas, Aberdeenshire, Scotland
2 CONT Death 29 Nov 1558 (aged 48–49), Dunlugas, Aberdeenshire, Scotland
2 CONT Burial
2 CONT Banff Old Parish Churchyard
2 CONT Banff, Aberdeenshire, Scotland
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/144014541/walter-ogilvy
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Walter Ogilvy of Dunlugas (c. 1509–1558)''' was a Scottish courtier, landowner, and Provost of Banff. A prominent figure in 16th-century Scottish politics, he served in the royal household and was involved in major political mo
2 CONC vements during the reigns of James V and Mary, Queen of Scots. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life and career'''
2 CONT Family and early life: Ogilvy was the son of Walter Ogilvy of Boyne and Margaret Edmondstone. His family was part of the influential Ogilvy clan, which had significant land holdings and political power in the northeast of Scotland
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal court: As early as 1524, he was a member of King James V's household, holding the position of master of the stables and steward.
2 CONT
2 CONT Provost of Banff: He was a major figure in the town of Banff, where he served as provost multiple times. During this period, the Ogilvy family held substantial power in the town, with the provostship occupied by Ogilvies for decad
2 CONC es.
2 CONT
2 CONT Residences: In 1538, King James V granted Ogilvy a license to build a "palace-wise" house in Banff, complete with fortifications. This residence, known as Inchdrewer Castle, was used to accommodate the king during his visits to th
2 CONC e north.
2 CONT
2 CONT Political actions: In 1543, he was a signatory of the "Secret Bond," a protest against the policies of Regent Arran. The bond's signers opposed Arran's pro-English stance and advocated for Mary, Queen of Scots, to be moved from Li
2 CONC nlithgow Palace to the more secure Stirling Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Death:''' He died on November 29, 1558, and was later commemorated by a monument erected by his son George in Banff. 
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT Walter Ogilvy married '''Alison Hume / Home''', who was a co-heiress of Cuthbert Home of Fast Castle. They had several children, including: 
2 CONT '''George Ogilvie of Dunlugas''' (d. 1621): George succeeded his father and married '''Beatrix Seton''', whose sister, Mary Seton, was one of Mary, Queen of Scots's famous "Four Maries".
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Magdalen Ogilvie''': She married '''Alexander Fraser of Philorth'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Walter Ogilvie''': He married '''Helen Stewar'''t, the daughter of Lord Innermeath.
