1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === Mr. John Robertson, M.A. (c. 1520 — c. 1580) ===
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Summary: High-status Scottish notary public, court procurator, and university-educated academic (Master of Arts). Son of James Robertson of Killiechangie and grandson of Andrew Robertson the Elder. He served as the primary legal
2 CONC  mind and trusted judicial agent for both the paramount Chiefs of Clan Donnachaidh (Struan) and the ruling merchant-guild elite of Perth.''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1. Lineage and Academic Distinction'''
2 CONT John Robertson was born around 1520 into the prominent regional branch of **Robertson of Killiechangie**, a highly influential cadet line of the Clan Donnachaidh. He was a son of James Robertson of Killiechangie (b. c. 1500) and a
2 CONC  direct paternal grandson of [https://www.geni.com/people/Andrew-Robertson/6000000007981009318 '''Andrew Robertson (the Elder)'''] (d. c. 1521). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In official contemporary notary and crown records, John is invariably distinguished by the highly prestigious title of '''«Mr.» (Master / Magister)'''. In 16th-century Scotland, this academic prefix was strictly restricted to indi
2 CONC viduals who had successfully completed higher university education and obtained a Master of Arts degree (likely from the University of St Andrews). This academic training elevated him to the supreme ranks of the Scottish legal and
2 CONC  ecclesiastical intelligentsia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2. Legal Mind of the Clan Chief (The Struan Connection)'''
2 CONT John's status as a highly trusted elite agent for the paramount core of his clan is permanently secured in the protocol books of the realm:
2 CONT * '''The 1546 Royal Charter (Record № 18)''': On 5 November 1546, Mr. John Robertson traveled to St Andrews to act as the primary professional witness and notary for '''Robert Robertson of Strowane (Chief of Clan Donnachaidh)''' d
2 CONC uring a massive territorial conveyance securing the Barony of Strowan, Rannoch, and the mills of Loch Tay for the Chief's son, William. His personal management of the Chief's private sovereign charters cements his position as the 
2 CONC "legal brain" of the wider clan.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources''':
2 CONT # '''Angus, William (ed.)'''. Protocol Book of Sir Robert Rollok, 1534–1552. Edinburgh: Scottish Record Society, 1931, Records [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecordso50scotuoft/scottishrecordso50scotuoft/page/6/mode/1up?q=18
2 CONC  № 18] (pp. 5-6), № 19 (p. 6), № 28 (p. 9), № 30 (p. 10), № 44-45 (pp. 14-15), № 150 (p. 45).
