1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =[https://www.clackmannanshire.scot/index.php/history/barony-of-clackmannan The Barony of Clackmannan]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1358 – 1403 [https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-Bruce-2nd-Baron-Clackmannan-Rate-proprietor-of-lands-of-Kennet/303632884430002646?through=6000000001806551174 Sir Robert Bruce - 2nd Baron of Clackmannan]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert Bruce inherited the Barony after his father's death. As a minor when his father died, he was put under the care of "appropriate men" who were Sir Robert Erskine & Sir John de Menteith. He received his charter for the la
2 CONC nds of Clackmannan from King David II in December 1359. He received a further charter for the lands of Rait, in Perthshire from King David II in January 1368. He received a third charter for the Lands of Kennet, Grassmainston, Pit
2 CONC foulden, Carse & Gragory from King Robert II in October 1375. Sir Robert granted a charter for the lands of Kennet, Pitfoulden and Cruicket to his youngest son who became Thomas Bruce 1st of Wester Kennet, Pitfoulden and Cruicket.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert Bruce was captured and killed on 23rd July 1403 at the Battle of Shrewsbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barony passed from father to son
2 CONT
2 CONT =='''1403 – 1405 [https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-Bruce-3rd-Baron-of-Clackmannan/6000000001806551174?through=6000000168873893042 Sir Robert Bruce - 3rd Baron of Clackmannan]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir Robert Bruce inherited the Barony of Clackmannan and its lands at the death of his father. He received the charter of the Lands of Rait from King Robert III in 1393. Died around 1405.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Barony passed from father to son
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1405 – 1428 [https://www.geni.com/people/David-Bruce-4th-Baron-of-Clackmannan/6000000168874433890?through=303632326640007080 Sir David Bruce - 4th Baron of Clackmannan]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir David Bruce renounced the rights to the mills of Clackmannan from the Canons of Cambuskenneth on October 6th, 1406. Died around 1428.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT a1 https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-Bruce-2nd-Baron-Clackmannan-Rate-proprietor-of-lands-of-Kennet/303632884430002646?through=6000000001806551174 & https://www.geni.com/people/Isobell-Stewart/6000000003615927768?through=60000000
2 CONC 01806551174
2 CONT >'''a1b1 https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-Bruce-3rd-Baron-of-Clackmannan/6000000001806551174?through=6000000168873893042 <1389 - d<1406'''
2 CONT >x NN mistress
2 CONT >>a1b1c1 https://www.geni.com/people/Thomas-Bruce-of-Wester-Kennet/6000000168873893042?through=6000000001806551174
2 CONT >xx https://www.geni.com/people/Annabelle-Scrymgeour/303632326640007080?through=6000000001806551174
2 CONT >>a1b1c2 https://www.geni.com/people/David-Bruce-Baron-of-Clackmannan/6000000168874433890?through=303632326640007080, who succeeded.
2 CONT >>a1b1c3 Alexander of Stanehous and Airth
2 CONT
2 CONT = [https://archive.org/stream/collectionstowar1868clac/collectionstowar1868clac_djvu.txt From the Charters of Clackmannen]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''9th December, 1359'''
2 CONT is the first charter yet known containing any grant of the Castle and Barony of Clackmannan. It was made by King David Bruce to and in favour of his beloved and faithful cousin, ("dilecto et fedeli consanguineo nostro,") Robert de
2 CONC  Bruys of the Castle and Barony of Clackmannan, the lands of Grasmenston, Gartlove, Wester Kennet, Hillend, Carsehill, Greys, Park Meadow, Dry- field, Tullygarth, Pitfoulden, and others, within the Sheriffdom of Clackmannan (.It I
2 CONC s probable that he may have been the son of Sir Robert de Bruce mentioned in Nos. 1.[above] and 2,who was living between 1326 and 1330. Robert de Bruce, it will be observed, who obtains the lands of Clackmannan, is not called "Dom
2 CONC inus," or Sir, in the first charter ; he therefore was probably very young at the time of his father's death, (which Chalmers, in his MS. notes to Douglas' Peerage of Scotland, fixes in 1332), and this may account for a period of 
2 CONC 27 years before we again find among the present existing charters any notice of Clackmannan or the Bruces. On the authority of Johnston's MS., before quoted, this second Robert is stated to have married Isabel, daughter of Sir Rob
2 CONC ert Stewart of Rosyth Castle, a second cousin of King Robert II.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1363''' — From the Acts of Parliament of this date, vol. 1, p. 168, " Robert de Bruce "is then stated to be proprietor of the lands of Kennet.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''20 Oct. 1364.''' — A Crown Charter by King David Bruce to and in favour of " Robert de Brays," who is again styled " consanguineo nostri" of the lands of Grassmynston, Gartlove Carse, "La Park meadow," Crage Roy,Dryfield,and ot
2 CONC hers, with the pertinents all lying within the Sheriffdom of Clackmannan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''17th Jan. 1367.''' — A Crown Charter by King David II  to and in favour of Robert de Bruys who is again designed " consanguineo nostro," granting the lands and barony of Rate in the county of Perth to be holden of the King and 
2 CONC his successors in fee and heritage, and in free barony for service of 3 suits at the King's Courts at Perth — granted at Restenneth on the above date.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2 May 1389''' Thomas obtains Kennet from his father .— Charter of Robert de Bruys of Clackmannan and Rate to and in favour of Thomas de Bruys, his son, where- by " for good services" he grants the said Thomas the lands of Weste
2 CONC r Kennet and others for payment to his said father of a silver penny yearly. In the history of Noble British Families, part 3, p. 15, it is stated that Robert de Bruce died about this period, and the next Charters of resignation w
2 CONC ere probably made by the possessor of the estate on succeeding thereto. [WHY, THO?]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''4th Oct., 1393''' — Crown Charter of Resignation by King Robert III., proceeding on the Rocuratory of Resignation of Robert de Bruys, whereby the King new grants the lands and Barony of Clackmannan, and all the pertinents, to R
2 CONC obert de Bruys, whom failing, to David Bruys, his eldest son, and the heirs male, procreated or to be procreated, of his body, whom failing, to Thomas Bruys, also his son, and the heirs male of his body, to be held of the King and
2 CONC  his successors in fee and heritage.
2 CONT
2 CONT By this Charter, Robert deBruys had gone through the ceremony of resigning or giving them back to the King during his life, and the King then made a new grant of them to him, and his two sons, and their heirs male.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''10th June, 1393''' — Charter by Robert de Bruys, Lord of Rate and Clackmannan, to his " beloved son, Thomas de Bruys," and his heirs, and assigns of an annualent of ten merks, payable furth off his lands of Tullygarth, lying in
2 CONC  the county of Clackmannan, and that in con- sideration of a certain sum of money paid to him, the said Robert de Bruys, in his urgent necessity. To be holden of the said Robert de Bruys and his heirs redeemable on payment of the 
2 CONC sum of 25 merks.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''18th February, 1399''' — Crown Charter of Resignation and Confirmation by King Robert III., ratifying and confirming the Char- ter No. 9, granted by Robert de Bruys to his son, Thomas, of the lands of Wester Kennet, Pitfoulden,
2 CONC  and the Cruikitland, all lying in the county of Clackmannan. From this time the estate of Kennet con- tinued separate from that of Clackmannan, and although the families continued on the most intimate terms, and the heiress of Ke
2 CONC nnet, in 1568, married one of the cadets of Clackmannan, the estates have never been united. https://archive.org/stream/collectionstowar1868clac/collectionstowar1868clac_djvu.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert Bruce, 3rd Baron of Clackmannan & Rate (died c. 1405)[1] was the son of Sir Robert Bruce, 2nd Baron of Clackmannan & Rate and Isabel Stewart of Fife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Bruce was born into the Scottish aristocracy in Clackmannan, Scotland. He married the daughter of John Scrimgeour of Dudhope, by whom he had at least three children:[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT * David Bruce, who succeeded his father as 4th Baron of Clackmannan;
2 CONT * Edward Bruce, who married Agnes, the daughter and co-heiress of Sir William Airth of Airth;
2 CONT * James Bruce, later Bishop of Dunkeld.[1] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Bruce,_2nd_Baron_of_Clackmannan#:~:text=Robert%20was%20captured%20and%20killed,succeeded%20him%20as%20third%20baron.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT =='''BRUCE,  EARLS  OF  ELGIN  AND AILESBURY''' [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun03paul/page/466/mode/1up?view=theater Balfour Paul, James, ed. (1906). The Scots Peerage, Vol. III. Edinburgh: David Douglas. pp. 467–468
2 CONC . ISBN 0-9711966-2-1.]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-Bruce-3rd-Baron-of-Clackmannan/6000000001806551174 Sir  Robert  de  Brus],  the  elder  son,  succeeded  his  father ante  1389''',  as  in  that  year  he  gave  a  charter   to  his  natura
2 CONC l son  Thomas  of  the  lands  of  Wester  Kennet,  which  charter was  confirmed  by  Robert  in  1399.  Sir  Robert  had  upon his  own  resignation  a  charter  "  from  Robert  III of  the  lands of  Rait  in  Perthshire  to  
2 CONC himself,  in  liferent,  and  to  David, his  eldest  lawful  son,  and  the  heirs-male  of  his  body  in  fee, whom  failing,  to  his  own  nearest  heirs  whatsoever,  date  Linlithgow  12  August  1393.  He  had  also  a  ch
2 CONC arter ' from  the  same  King  of  the  lands  of  Clackmannan,  etc.,  to himself,  in  liferent,  and  to  David  his  lawful  son,  and  the heirs-male  of  his  body,  in  fee,  whom  failing,  to  his  son Thomas,  and  his  
2 CONC heirs-male,  whom  failing,  to  return  to the  King,  dated  24  October  1394.  In  both  these  charters he  is  styled  '  consanguineus,'  and  his  son  David  is  so termed  in  the  last. ''' Sir  Robert  died  before  14
2 CONC 06'''.  '''His wife  is  said  to  have  been  a  [https://www.geni.com/people/Annabelle-Scrymgeour/303632326640007080?through=6000000001806551174 daughter  of  Scrimgeour]'''  of Dudhope,  Constable  of  Dundee,  but  there  is  
2 CONC no  proof  of  this. He  had  issue : —
2 CONT 1 .  David,  who  succeeded.
2 CONT 2.  Alexander  of  Stanehous  and  Airth,  who  in  an  original birthbrief  from  Charles  I.,  dated  23  July  1633,  to Adam  Bruce,  ancestor  of  the  Comtes  de  Bruce  in France,  is  styled  ' legitimate  son  of  Sir  Ro
2 CONC bert  Bruce of  Clackmannan,  Knight.'  It  is  evident  from  the charter  of  1394  to  his  father,  Sir  Robert  Bruce  of Clackmannan,  that  at  that  date  Alexander  was  not born,  as  the  remainder  was,  after  David  
2 CONC his  lawful son,  to  Thomas  his  [natural]  son,  there  being  no mention  of  Alexander.  Alexander  was  the  founder of  the  baronial  house  of  Bruce  of  Airth,  and was  ancestor  of  the  following  cadet  branches,  v
2 CONC iz. Earlshall,  Kinnaird,  Auchenbowie,  Powfoullis,  Leth- bertshiells,  Waltoun,  Comtes  de  Bruce  in  France, Kincavel,  Bangour,  Stenhouse,  Newtoun,  Benburb, and  Downhill.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Had three sons;
2 CONT
2 CONT DAVID (Sir), his heir; James, Bishop of Dunkeld;  ALEXANDER, of Stenhouse and Airth, of whom we treat.
2 CONT
2 CONT The above ALEXANDER BRUCE was first of the house of AIRTH.
