1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sir John Graham, 9th Earl of Menteith''' became Earl of Menteith by courtesy of his wife. He accompanied David II in his invasion of England in 1346. He was present at the battle of Neville's Cross and, when the
2 CONC  archers were almost within bowshot, earnestly urged the King to send a body of cavalry to charge them in flank. His advice was unhappily disregarded and when the archers were about to direct their deadly volleys on the serried ra
2 CONC nks of the Scottish spearmen, the Earl exclaimed, 'Give me but a hundred horse and I engage to disperse them all; so shall we be able to fight more securely.' His appeal was, however, unheeded and hastily leaping upon his horse, a
2 CONC nd followed only by his own retainers, he rushed upon the advancing bowmen but his gallant attack was not supported. His horse was killed under him and after bravely, but vainly, striving to arrest the advance of the enemy, he was
2 CONC  compelled to retire to the main body of the Scottish army.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a stout battle, which lasted for three hours, the Earl was taken prisoner, along with his sovereign, and was imprisoned in the Tower of London. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By the direct orders of Edward I, he was tried and condemned as a traitor, on the plea that he had at one time sworn fealty to the English King, and was drawn, hanged, beheaded and quartered. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Mary Stewart, only daughter of Earl Alan, who appears to have been under age at the time of her father’s death, now became Countess of Menteith. She married Sir John Graham, who is supposed to have been the younger son of Sir
2 CONC  Patrick Graham of Kincardine, ancestor of the Montrose family, and became Earl of Menteith apparently by courtesy through his wife. He accompanied David II. in his invasion of England in 1346. He was present at the battle of Durh
2 CONC am, and, when the archers were almost within bowshot, earnestly urged the King to send a body of cavalry to charge them in flank. His advice was unhappily disregarded, and when the archers were about to direct their deadly volleys
2 CONC  on the serried ranks of the Scottish spearmen, the Earl exclaimed, ‘Give me but a hundred horse and I engage to disperse them all; so shall we be able to fight more securely.’ His appeal was, however, unheeded, and hastily leapin
2 CONC g upon his horse, and followed only by his own retainers, he rushed upon the advancing bowmen. But his gallant attack was not supported. His horse was killed under him, and after bravely, but vainly, striving to arrest the advance
2 CONC  of the enemy, he was compelled to retire to the main body of the Scottish army. After a stout battle, which lasted for three hours, the Earl was taken prisoner, along with his sovereign, and was imprisoned in the Tower of London.
2 CONC  By the direct orders of King Edward, he was tried and condemned as a traitor, on the plea that he had at one time sworn fealty to the English King, and was drawn, hanged, beheaded, and quartered.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.electricscotland.com/WEBCLANS/families/menteith.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Burkes Peerage 1934 merely identifies this son as Sir John, living in 1317. The Scots Peerage (identifying him as a younger son of Sir Patrick by Annabella of Strathearn) reports a suggestion that he was the Sir John who became Ea
2 CONC rl of Menteith through his marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Wyntoun, whose "Metrical Chronicle" was written in 1418, says:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Schyre Jhon of Menteith in tha days
2 CONT
2 CONT Tuk in Glasgow William Walays;
2 CONT
2 CONT And sent hym until Ingland sune,
2 CONT
2 CONT There was he quartayrd and undone."
2 CONT
2 CONT The English chronicler Piers Langtoft states that Menteith discovered the retreat of Wallace through the treacherous information of Jack Short, his servant, and that he came under cover of night and seized him in bed. A passage in
2 CONC  the Scala Chronica, quoted by John Leland, notes, "William Walleys was taken of the Counte of Menteith, about Glasgow, and sent to King Edward, and after was hanged, drawn, and quartered at London."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: John; living 1317. [Burke's Peerage] 1 1
2 CONT
