1 OCCU 7th Earl of Douglas, 1st Earl of Avondale
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Douglas,_7th_Earl_of_Douglas
2 CONT
2 CONT James Douglas, 7th Earl of Douglas, 1st Earl of Avondale (1371 – 24 March 1443), latterly known as James the Gross, and prior to his ennoblement as James of Balvenie, was a late mediaeval Scottish magnate.[1] He was the second son
2 CONC  of Archibald Douglas, 3rd Earl of Douglas and Joan Moray of Bothwell and Drumsargard (now Cambuslang), d. after 1408.
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.douglashistory.co.uk/history/james7thearldouglas.htm#.UjX-uqN5mSM The Douglas Archives]
2 CONT
2 CONT His exceptional rise to dominance in his family and in the kingdom began with the disastrous defeat of his elder brother, Archibald Douglas, fourth earl of Douglas, at Hamildon Hill in 1402. After the capture of the earl and his l
2 CONC eading followers James was left to maintain Black Douglas influence in southern Scotland. He deputized for the earl as warden of the Scottish marches and keeper of Edinburgh Castle, but when he tried to maintain his family's posit
2 CONC ion found himself increasingly challenged by a rival faction in the south led by Robert III's councillors, Sir David Fleming and Henry Sinclair, second earl of Orkney. Most worryingly for James, Fleming's and Orkney's support of t
2 CONC he rebel Henry Percy, first earl of Northumberland, created tensions with England which led to attacks on Douglas lands and jeopardized negotiations for the earl's release. In early 1406 these tensions resulted in open conflict. J
2 CONC ames Douglas led a force from Edinburgh which caught Fleming, Orkney, and the young heir to the throne, the future James I, in Haddingtonshire. Orkney and Prince James escaped by sea, but Fleming was killed by Douglas's men in a r
2 CONC unning fight.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Douglas's success preserved Black Douglas dominance in the south. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT By inclination and experience, Douglas was not a border magnate like his predecessors. His main residences at Abercorn and Lanark confirm him as a magnate whose lands and personal connections centred on Clydesdale and Lothian. The
2 CONC  marriages of his daughters to landowners in these two sheriffdoms support this impression. It was thus appropriate that it was at Abercorn Castle that Douglas died on 10 March 1443. His grossly fat body, which earned him his nick
2 CONC name and which at his death reportedly contained 4 stone of tallow, was buried in a magnificent tomb in St Bride's Church in Douglas.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==family==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Father: Archibald the Grim (3rd Earl of Douglas) Douglas b: ABT. 1346
2 CONT * Mother: Joan\Johana (of Strathearn) Moray b: ABT. 1350
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT #  1 Beatrix Sinclair, dau of Earl of Orkney Married: BEF. 7 MAR 1424/25 in Orkney, ORKN, Scot.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT # William 8th Earl of Douglas b: ABT. 1425
2 CONT #  James 9th (Black Douglas) Earl of Douglas b: ABT. 1440
2 CONT #  Archibald (Earl of Moray) Douglas
2 CONT #  Hugh (Earl of Ormond) Douglas,  Executed in 1455
2 CONT #  John (Lord of Balveny) Douglas
2 CONT #  Margaret Douglas = Henry Douglas, son of Sir James Douglas,1st Lord Dalkeith 
2 CONT #  Henry Douglas
2 CONT #  George Douglas
2 CONT #  Beatrice Douglas = William (1st Earl of Erroll) 2nd Lord Hay
2 CONT #  Elizabeth Douglas = Adam (of Craigie) Wallace (or William?)
2 CONT #  Janet Douglas b: 1398 in Brechin, Lanarkshire, Scotland = Robert (Sir) 1st Lord Fleming
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Douglas,_7th_Earl_of_Douglas Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1437 he was created Earl of Avondale. He succeeded to the earldom of Douglas on the summary execution of his great-nephew William Douglas, 6th Earl of Douglas, and his brother David, in 1440. James Douglas was himself implicate
2 CONC d in this attempt to neutralise the power of the 'Black' Douglas line.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT He married first Beatrice Stewart, daughter of Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany and Margaret Graham, Countess of Menteith. They had one daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice Douglas 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married second Beatrice Sinclair, daughter of Henry Sinclair, 2nd Earl of Orkney, around 1425. They had four children.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Douglas (1425–1452), who succeeded his father 
2 CONT
2 CONT James Douglas (1426–1491), who succeeded his brother as 9th earl 
2 CONT
2 CONT Janet Douglas 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Douglas (d.1473) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT # [http://books.google.com/books?id=k7IKAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA155&lpg=PA155&dq=Archibald+Douglas+1410&source=bl&ots=da-29Xcepk&sig=jRJHS8VxbPQuDtf1AHyG10kG1pw&hl=en&ei=XUs3TIqUEISmsQOm1qBS&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=5&ved=0CCIQ6
2 CONC AEwBDgK#v=onepage&q=Archibald%20Douglas%201410&f=false Complete peerage of England,] Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and ..., Volume 3 By George Edward Cokayne Pg.155
2 CONT
