2 CONT == [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella,_Countess_of_Lennox Biography by Wikipedia] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Isabella of Lennox (died 1458) was the ruler of Lennox, at least from 1437 to 1458, and last in the line of Mormaers or native Scottish rulers. As the wife of Murdoch Stewart, 2nd Duke of Albany (died 1425), she was also Duches
2 CONC s of Albany (1420–1425), but in 1425 her family would be almost completely destroyed when her husband, father and two sons were executed by King James I of Scotland because of their rebellion. Only one son, James the Fat, would es
2 CONC cape their family's punishment, and he would die in exile in Ireland. Isabella succeeded in escaping the fate of her family, and would eventually regain her title and estates, retiring to her castle in Loch Lomond where she raised
2 CONC  her grandchildren. She would eventually live to see the assassination of her family's opponent, King James. Though none of her four sons survived her, her grandson Andrew Stewart, 1st Lord Avandale, became Lord Chancellor of Scot
2 CONC land.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Isabella was the daughter of Donnchadh, Earl of Lennox and Helena, the daughter of Sir Archibald Campbell. Her father Duncan sought to create powerful links with the great Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany, who was the second 
2 CONC son of King Robert II by his first wife Elizabeth Mure of Rowallan, and who was to a certain extent the de facto ruler of Scotland, at points during the reigns of his father and elder brother. In 1392, Duncan agreed to marry Isabe
2 CONC lla to Robert's son, Murdoch Stewart. Murdoch and Isabella had at least five children:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT # 
2 CONT # 
2 CONT # 
2 CONT # 
2 CONT # 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''After her father and sons Walter and Alasdair were executed in 1425, Isabella was forced to spend eight years as a royal hostage at Tantallon Castle. In 1437 she recovered her lands and title. In the next few years, although fo
2 CONC rced to govern her province from Loch Lomond, she issued a large numbers of charters, was popular in the province, and was tolerated by James II. She died in 1458.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
