1 NAME John /Swinton of that Ilk/
2 GIVN John
2 SURN Swinton of that Ilk
2 _MARNM Swinton of that Ilk
1 NAME Sir John Swinton, 14th of that Ilk
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 PLAC of
2 ADDR
3 CITY Swinton
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 14 SEP 1402
2 PLAC Battle of Homildon Hill
2 ADDR
3 STAE England
1 OCCU the hero of Noyon in Picardy, of Otterburn, and Homildon
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.scotweb.co.uk/info/swinton/
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Wars of Scottish Independence'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry de Swinton appears on the Ragman Rolls as one of the nobility swearing fealty to King Edward I of England in 1296. He was joined in this by his brother, William, priest of the church of Swinton. However later the Swintons wo
2 CONC uld support Robert the Bruce during the Wars of Scottish Independence. Sir John Swinton, great-grandson of Henry, was a distinguished soldier and statesman in the reigns of Robert II of Scotland and Robert III of Scotland. He was 
2 CONC a commander at the Battle of Otterburn in July 1388 when the Scots won the day and defeated the English, although their leader, the Earl of Douglas, was slain. Sir John Swinton was later killed leading the clan at the Battle of Hu
2 CONC mbleton Hill also known as Homildon Hill in 1402.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''15th Century & Hundred Years’ War'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Swinton’s second wife was the Countess of Douglas and Mar, but they had no issue. His third wife was Princess Margaret, who bore Swinton a son, later Sir John Swinton of Swinton, reckoned to be the fifteenth Lord of the name. Duri
2 CONC ng the Hundred Years’ War he was a doughty warrior who fought and led the Clan Swinton at the Battle of Baugé against the English in France in 1421, where the French-Scottish forces were victorious. Although the credit for this is
2 CONC  claimed by others, he is said to have been the knight who slew the Duke of Clarence, brother of King Henry V of England. The incident appears in Sir Walter Scott’s poem, ‘The Lay of the Last Minstrel’. However Sir John Swinton wa
2 CONC s killed when the Clan Swinton fought at the Battle of Verneuil in France in 1424.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_John_Swinton,_14th_of_that_Ilk
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Swinton, great-grandson of Henry de Swinton who appears on the Ragman Roll, was a distinguished soldier and statesman in the reigns of Robert II of Scotland and Robert III of Scotland. (See Clan Swinton) http://en.wikiped
2 CONC ia.org/wiki/Clan_Swinton
2 CONT
2 CONT France, Hundred Years War
2 CONT
2 CONT He was one of the greatest fighters of his time. In youth, the Borders being too quiet for him, he had signed on with John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster. He made an interesting agreement with John of Gaunt which included the fol
2 CONC lowing among other terms:
2 CONT
2 CONT  a) Swinton was not to be required to fight against his own country.
2 CONT
2 CONT  b) He was to be given double pay, and free transport for himself, his horses and his men.
2 CONT
2 CONT  c) The Duke was to replace any of his horses that were lost or taken. In return, he was to have one-third share in the ransom of Swinton’s future prisoners and in his other "profits of war".
2 CONT
2 CONT This unusual "contract" shows that Sir John must already have acquired a solid reputation as a fighter, perhaps in Prussia or Spain or even both, some time before 1371, when it was made.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John fully justified the trust placed in him, through his conduct in a series of campaigns and particularly at Noyon (between Amiens and Paris) when he fought his way single-handed into the town. Legend says he was the hero wh
2 CONC o, according to Jean Froissart, leaped the barrier gates at Noyon and for love of the fray fought the chivalry of France for more than an hour "alone against them all" - "giving many grand strokes with his lance." When the army be
2 CONC gan to move and he had to rejoin it, he cleared the way with a thrust or two, sprang back, and mounting, with his page in front, cried : "Adieu, adieu, Seigneurs, grands mercis!" and spurred away.
2 CONT
2 CONT About this time, he married a young wife, Joan, who died without children and whose jewels were stolen by Alice Perrers, Edward III’s mistress (who also stole the King’s rings from his fingers as he lay dying). He appealed to the 
2 CONC King for their return, but they could not be traced, and it is not altogether surprising that he returned to Scotland soon after.
2 CONT
2 CONT Battle of Otterburn
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a commander at the Battle of Otterburn in July 1388 when the Scots won the day and defeated the English, although their leader, the Earl of Douglas, was slain. The Scotichronicon, talking of the battle, mentions "a very exp
2 CONC erienced, strong, and brave Scot", John Swinton, who carved a path through the English: "Because of this the Scots were able to penetrate the English line with their spears, so that the English were forced to give ground to this s
2 CONC trong force".
2 CONT
2 CONT It is related of Sir John, that in the wars with the English, he visited the enemy's camp, and gave a general challenge to fight any of their army.
2 CONT
2 CONT Appointments
2 CONT
2 CONT He was appointed one of the ambassadors extraordinary by King Robert III to negotiate a treaty with the court of England, for which they got a safe conduct from King Richard II for themselves and sixty knights in their retinue, 4t
2 CONC h July, 1392. He was afterwards employed upon another negotiation, and obtained a safe conduct from King Henry IV to go to England, with twenty horsemen in his retinue, 7th July, 1400.
2 CONT
2 CONT Battle of Homildon Hill
2 CONT
2 CONT The gallant bearing and heroic death of the Lord of Swinton, at the fatal battle of Homildon, have afforded a subject for the poetic genius of Scott, and are the materials on which he founded the drama of "Haledon Hill". Pinkerton
2 CONC  thus records Swinton's fall:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The English advanced to the assault, and Henry Percy was about to lead them up the hill, when March caught his bridle, and advised him to advance no farther, but to pour the dreadful shower of English arrows into the enemy. This 
2 CONC advice was followed with the usual fortune; for in all ages the bow was the English weapon of victory, and though the Scots, and perhaps the French, were superior in the use of the spear, yet this weapon was useless after the dist
2 CONC ant bow had decided the combat. Robert the Great, sensible of this at the battle of Bannockburn, ordered a prepared detachment of cavalry to rush among the English archers at the commencement, totally to disperse them, and stop th
2 CONC e deadly effusion. But Douglas now used no such precaution; and the consequence was, that his people, drawn up on the face of the hill, presented one general mark to the enemy, none of whose arrows descended in vain. The Scots fel
2 CONC l without fight and unrevenged, till a spirited knight, Swinton, exclaimed aloud, "O my brave countrymen! what fascination has seized you to-day, that you stand like deer to be shot, instead of indulging your ancient courage, and 
2 CONC meeting your enemies hand to hand? Let those who will, descend with me, that we may gain victory, and life, or fall like men." This being heard by Adam Gordon, between whom and Swinton there existed a deadly feud, attended with th
2 CONC e mutual slaughter of many followers, he instantly fell on his knees before Swinton, begged his pardon, and desired to be dubbed a knight by him whom he must now regard as the wisest and boldest of that order in Britain. The cerem
2 CONC ony performed, Swinton and Gordon descended the hill, accompanied by only one hundred men, and a desperate valour led the whole body to death. Had a similar spirit been shewn by the Scottish army, it is probable that the event of 
2 CONC that day would have been different."
2 CONT
2 CONT Family life
2 CONT
2 CONT Swinton’s second wife was the Countess of Douglas and Mar, but they had no offspring. His third wife was Princess Margaret, daughter of Robert Stewart, Duke of Albany who served as Regent from 1406 to 1419. The Princess bore Swint
2 CONC on a son, later Sir John Swinton of Swinton, reckoned to be the fifteenth Lord of the name.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Swinton of Swinton married Margaret, Countess of Mar, daughter of Donald, 8th Earl of Mar and Isabella Stewart, between 1385 and July 1388.1 He died on 14 September 1402 at Homildon Hill, Scotland, killed in action.1
2 CONT
2 CONT  As a result of his marriage, Sir John Swinton of Swinton was styled as Lord of Mar in 1388.1 He fought in the Battle of Homildon Hill on 14 September 1402.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT # [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinc
2 CONC t or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume IV, page 431. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT # [S37] BP2003 volume 2, page 2603. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT # [http://books.google.com/books?id=6UhmAAAAMAAJ&vq=Annabella%20drummond&pg=PA154#v=snippet&q=Annabella%20drummond&f=false The Scots Peerage:] Founded on Wood's Edition of Sir Robert Douglas's Peerage of Scotland; Containing an Hi
2 CONC storical and Genealogical Account of the Nobility of that Kingdom, Volume 3 (Google eBook) James Balfour Paul. D. Douglas, 1906 - Nobility.  Page 154 Links
2 CONT * http://www.thepeerage.com/p10950.htm#i109492
