1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Confusion between him and his son Walter ll Haliburton.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From The Scots Peerage <[https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun04pauluoft/page/333/mode/1up?view=theater Archive.Org]>
2 CONT
2 CONT WALTER HALIBURTON of Dirleton. His name appears in several charters in the reign of Robert lll and the regency of Robert, Duke of Albany.  He married, probably as his second wife, between 1402 and 1406,1 Isobel, one of the daughte
2 CONC rs of Robert, Duke of Albany,2 and widow of Alexander Leslie, Earl of Ross, who died 1402. Many persons of the name of Haliburton appear in the records at this period, and some of them may have been the issue of this Sir Walter. H
2 CONC e had at least one son, SIR WALTER, who married Marjorie Douglas.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT A dispensation was granted to Walter and Isabel on 21 December 1415, long after their marriage ( Vatican Reg., An. 22 Benedict xin.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I663&tree=CC
2 CONT
2 CONT The Scots Peerage shows 2 generations of Walters. Burkes Extinct Peerages 1883 shows just one Sir Walter, High Treasurer and 1st Lord, who married Isabel Stewart. The Scots Peerage reports that, whilst it "is generally stated that
2 CONC " the second Walter was created a Peer, "this is very doubtful, and it is more probable that it was his son [Walter l’s grandson] who was the first Peer".
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________
