1 OCCU 2nd Lord of Lorn, Lord of Lorn
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He had two wives:
2 CONT
2 CONT daughter MacDougall and daughter MacLaren. All other wives linked to him do not have valid sources and will be disconnected. 
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The name of John's first wife is not known. With his first wife Janet, Isabel and Marion were born. With a mistress (daughter of MacLaren) Dugald of Appin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT John Stewart, 2nd Lord Lorn was born between 1397 and 1434.3 He was the son of Sir Robert Stewart, 1st Lord Lorn and Joan Stewart.  He gained the title of 2nd Lord Lorn [S., 1439] circa 1448.1 and  also went by the nick-name of 'M
2 CONC uireach' (or in English, 'the Leper').4
2 CONT
2 CONT John, Lord Lorn, called "the Leper," was surprised and mortally wounded by some of the Clan Dougall led by the 10th Chief's wild son, Black Alan MacDougall. (Alan M'Coule (3).  He is said to have married on his death-bed (but this
2 CONC  was not recognised by the authorities). John died of his wounds in his castle of Dunstaffnage on 20 Dec 1463.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1449 and 1455 he sat in the Scottish Parliament as Lord Lorn.3 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT # [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinc
2 CONC t or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume VIII, page 138. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT # [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 218. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
2 CONT # [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume VIII, page 139.
2 CONT # [S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 2, page 2766. Hereinafter cited as Burke's Pe
2 CONC erage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://www.thepeerage.com/p10798.htm#i107974
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sources 
2 CONT # [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun01pauluoft#page/356/mode/2up Scots Peerage] page 332
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John Stewart, 2nd Lord Lorn was born between 1397 and 1434.3 He was the son of Sir Robert Stewart, 1st Lord Lorn and Joan Stewart. He gained the title of 2nd Lord Lorn [S., 1439] circa 1448.1 and also went by the nick-name of 'Mui
2 CONC reach' (or in English, 'the Leper').4
2 CONT
2 CONT John, Lord Lorn, called "the Leper," was surprised and mortally wounded by some of the Clan Dougall led by the 10th Chief's wild son, Black Alan MacDougall. (Alan M'Coule (3). He is said to have married on his death-bed (but this 
2 CONC was not recognised by the authorities). John died of his wounds in his castle of Dunstaffnage on 20 Dec 1463.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1449 and 1455 he sat in the Scottish Parliament as Lord Lorn.3 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I929&tree=CC
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart, John 2nd Lord of Lorn (Sir ) [929] 4 5 6
2 CONT
2 CONT     Born: 1400 1
2 CONT     Marriage (1): MacDougall, dau. [15692] circa 1429 in Argyllshire, Scotland 1
2 CONT     Marriage (2): MacLaren, dau. [3929] in 1463 2 3
2 CONT     Died: 20 December 1463, Dunstaffnage, Argyllshire, Scotland at age 63 1 7
2 CONT
2 CONT    Cause of his death was killed by a renegade MacDougall in the pay of the English.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Another name for John was Stewart, John "Mourach" of Lorn.
2 CONT
2 CONT   General Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 - On the murder of John, the second Stewart Lord of Lorne, in 1463 by a renegade MacDougall in the pay of the English, the Lordship and castle passed to his brother Sir Walter. There was a dispute, since the murdered man was on 
2 CONC his way to be married to his mistress so as to legitimate his natural son. The last Stewart Lord of Lorne is said to have died on the threshold while reciting his marriage vows. Local sympathy seemingly favored the boy and for six
2 CONC  years there was conflict in Lorne. Sir Walter, perhaps finding the lands more trouble than they were worth, exchanged the Lordship with Colin Earl of Argyll for richer and more peaceful lands in eastern Scotland. The exchange was
2 CONC  ratified by royal charter in 1470.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 - Tradition tell us that in 1445, while returning to his seat at Dunstaffnage castle from the great cattle tryst at Crieff, Sir John met and fell in love with the daughter of MacLaren of Ardvech. Although married, he began an af
2 CONC faire with his new love which one year later produced a son. He was christened Dugald and was to be the first Chief of the Stewarts of Appin. After the death of his first wife, Sir John waited, for reasons we are unaware of today,
2 CONC  for 5 years until setting up the marriage between himself and Dugald's mother, but it may have had something to do with the politics of the day. In 1463, Sir John set a wedding date and sent for Dugald and his mother to come to D
2 CONC unstaffnage. Unknown to Sir John, there was a plot to kill the Lord of Lorn. It is not fully known, but it is thought to have been set up by the Lord of the Isles who was in a power struggle with the King of Scots, and who saw it 
2 CONC as being in his best interest to neutralize this powerful and loyal representative of the King in the west highlands. The other plotters, which some feel included Colin Campbell, Lord Argyll, Sir John's son-in-law, were primarily 
2 CONC represented by Alan MacCoul, the illegitimate grandson of an earlier MacDougall Chief. As the lightly armed wedding party made it's way from Dunstaffnage to the small chapel located approximately 180 yards from the castle walls, t
2 CONC hey were attacked by a superior force lead by Alan MacCoul. Although better armed, MacCoul's force was defeated, but not before mortally wounding Lord of Lorn. Sir John was rushed into the chapel and MacCoul and his henchmen ran i
2 CONC nto and occupied the deserted Dunstaffnage. With his last breath Sir John married Dugald's mother, legitimizing him and making him the de jure Lord of Lorn. After receiving the last rites, Sir John expired and a new chapter in wes
2 CONC t highland history was opened. 8
2 CONT
2 CONT John married dau. MacDougall [15692] [MRIN: 1529] circa 1429 in Argyllshire, Scotland.1 (dau. MacDougall [15692] was born circa 1412 in Argyllshire, Scotland.)
2 CONT
2 CONT John possibly married dau. MacLaren [3929] [MRIN: 2451], daughter of ?? MacLaren of Ardveich [22529], in 1463.2 3
2 CONT
