2 CONC gister House, Edinburgh, 1882), 1153 pp. including indices and errata]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Genealogy ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun07pauluoft/page/408/mode/1up The Scots Peerage VII: 408-9]'''
2 CONT # '''[https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/aa/abernethy1.php Stirnet: Abernethy 1]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [http://www.cracroftspeerage.co.uk/online/content/index340.htm Biographical Summary by Cracroft's Peerage] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT William [Abernethy], 2nd Lord Saltoun of Abernethy, PC
2 CONT *born
2 CONT *mar. (1)
2 CONT *children by first wife
2 CONT **1. ..... Abernethy, mar. Walter Ogston of that Ilk, and had issue
2 CONT **2. Christian Abernethy, mar. 1468 Sir John Wemyss of that Ilk (d. c. May 1508), 1st son and heir of John Wemyss of Wemyss by his first wife Margaret Livingstone, dau. of Sir Robert Livingstone of Drumry, and had issue
2 CONT *mar. (2) in or bef. 1464 Isabella Borthwick
2 CONT *died s.p.m. Jun 1488
2 CONT *suc. by brother
2 CONT *note: Sheriff of Banffshire 1458/9; Privy Councillor bef. 1482/3; possibly died at the Battle of Sauchieburn between King James III and the rebels led by his son and successor; on his death the Lordship of Saltoun of Abernethy pa
2 CONC ssed to the heir male, his brother, rather than to the heir of line, his eldest daughter
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Sauchieburn Battle of Saucieburn] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Sauchieburn was fought on June 11, 1488, at the side of Sauchie Burn, a stream about two miles south of Stirling, Scotland. The battle was fought between as many as 30,000 troops of King James III of Scotland and som
2 CONC e 18,000 troops raised by a group of dissident Scottish nobles including Alexander Home, 1st Lord Home, nominally led by the king's 15-year-old son, Prince James, Duke of Rothesay (assertion noted as dubious).
2 CONT
2 CONT James III had faced open rebellion for a few months, but chose to face the rebels close to where his ancestor Robert I of Scotland had defeated the English at the Battle of Bannockburn.
2 CONT
2 CONT The battle went badly for the Royalists. Persistent legends, based on the highly coloured and unreliable accounts of sixteenth century chroniclers such as Adam Abell, Robert Lindsay of Pitscottie, John Lesley, and George Buchanan,
2 CONC  claim that James III was assassinated at Milltown,[1] near Bannockburn, soon after the battle. There is no contemporary evidence to support this account, nor the allegation that he fled the battle, nor the tale that his assassin 
2 CONC impersonated a priest in order to approach James.
2 CONT
2 CONT A story is told that, on the eve of the battle, Sir David Lindsay, son of Sir John, Lord Lindsay of the Byres, presented James III with a "great grey horse" that would carry him faster than any other horse into or away from the ba
2 CONC ttle. Unfortunately, the horse threw the King during the battle, and James III was either killed in the fall, or was finished off by enemy soldiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince James ascended to the throne, and reigned as James IV for 25 years. Throughout his reign he wore a heavy iron chain around his waist, next to the skin, as a constant reminder of his role in the death of his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnote:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. ^ Recent archaeological survey of Milton, alleged site of assassination
2 CONT * http://www.scotlandsplaces.gov.uk/search_item/index.php?service=RCAHMS&id=293913
