1 NAME Mary /MacLeod/ 10th Chief and 1st female Chief of Clan MacLeod
2 GIVN Mary
2 SURN MacLeod
2 NSFX 10th Chief and 1st female Chief of Clan MacLeod
2 _MARNM MacLeod
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1545
2 ADDR
3 CITY Harries
3 STAE West Isle Of Dunvegan
3 CTRY Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1602
2 ADDR
3 CITY Achinbreck
3 STAE Kilmichael
3 CTRY Scotland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  
2 CONT
2 CONT The tomb of Mary’s grandfather at St Clement's Church, Rodel
2 CONT Mary MacLeod was the daughter and heir of William MacLeod of Harris, and a granddaughter of Alasdair Crotach MacLeod. Her name was sometimes spelled "McCloyde" or "McCloid", or "Marie Clawde". She is regarded as a Chief of the Cla
2 CONC n McLeod.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her father died, in 1562 Queen Mary sent orders to Kenyeouth MacKenzie of Kintail that he should bring Marie MacLeod to her in Edinburgh. Another man, James McConeill, claimed her custody, but MacKenzie brought her to the qu
2 CONC een. Marie joined the queen's household as a lady of her chamber. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On Christmas Eve 1562, Mary bought clothes for Marie MacLeod, including black velvet for a riding hood and veil, and silk chamlet for a petticoat, bodice, and sleeves. In February 1563 she was a given a length of plaiding and a fa
2 CONC rthingale.
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1565 Mary ordered an outfit for Marie made of scarlet stemming for a cloak and a skirt front, known as a "devanter". Marie and another young woman, Margaret Fame, were bought clothes made of black stemming as maidens in t
2 CONC he queen's chamber and were provided with sheets and a mattress to sleep near the queen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not known where Mary was born, but it was probably at Dunvegan, and probably she spent a part of her early childhood there. Her father, William, the 9th Chief, died in 1551, and his death created a difficult situation. He ha
2 CONC d held his lands in Harris, Glenelg and Skye (including a part of Trotternish, which was in the possession of the MacDonalds of Sleat) by a charter of 1542 with remainder to heirs general, while the Island of North Uist, Sleat and
2 CONC  the rest of Trotternish (also MacDonald lands) had been granted to his father and himself with remainder to heir's male. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This meant that the legal owner of the ancient heritage of the Clan MacLeod was Mary, while her father's next brother, Donald, was in name only the possessor of lands occupied by another clan.
2 CONT
2 CONT At a meeting of the Clan MacLeod, which was always held after the funeral of a chief, they put aside Mary's claims, and as her uncles were away, and it was not known whether they were alive or not, the temporary leadership of the 
2 CONC Clan was given to Iain a'Chuil Bhain (John of the Fair Cue), who was her great-grandfather. It was felt that a young girl would be of no use in governing the clan and leading it in war. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Since the chiefship of a clan, however, did not and does not depend on the clan's consent, being hereditary and not elective, Mary was Chief de Jure from the day of her father's death until the return of her uncle Donald, who was 
2 CONC the heir male. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The clansmen's decision at Rodel in Harris that Iain a'Chuil Bhain should take upon himself the leadership of the clan meant that Mary and her mother had to leave the Castle of Dunvegan, which now became his home, 
2 CONT
2 CONT We have no knowledge where they went after leaving Skye. Agnes Fraser, Mary's mother, probably did not have much say in the upbringing of her daughter, who, being the legal owner of the MacLeod lands, was a ward of the Crown. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her wardship was a prize not beneath the notice of the greatest nobles in Scotland. In 1553 the Earl of Huntly got the coveted wardship, but in 1555 he fell into disgrace with the Queen Regen, who forced him to relinquish his clai
2 CONC ms to the heiress, whose wardship he was on the point of selling to the Earl of Argyll, and hand Mary over to herself. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Argyll, however, did not give up all hope of getting the prize for himself. In 1558, the year after her uncle Donald's murder, the Earl sent a party of Campbells to Skye to ascertain what chance there was of any husband he might c
2 CONC hoose for her being accepted as Chief of the Clan. The Campbells were received at Dunvegan by Iain Dubh (son of Iain a'Chuil Bhain), who had them all treacherously murdered, and Argyll was convinced that Mary and a Campbell husban
2 CONC d would never be acceptable to the MacLeods. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Argyll died in this year, but his successor, the 5th Earl, in 1560, entered into negotiations with Mary's uncle, Norman, for the Campbells were determined to get the heiress into their hands to serve their own interests, as we sha
2 CONC ll see later on. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1562, the same year as her mother's Contract of Marriage with Alexander Bayne of Tulloch, Mary's person was in the possession of Kenneth MacKenzie of Kintail, illegally it would appear, for Mary, Queen of Scots, ordered him to 
2 CONC present her to herself. The MacKenzie Chief appeared on 21st May of that year, and maintained he could not give up the heiress because James MacDonald of Dunnyveg was bringing an action against him before the Lords of Session for 
2 CONC her possession. Her Majesty assured MacKenzie that he would not be held guilty by MacDonald of Dunnyveg or anybody else and insisted on his handing her over to her. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus, Mary MacLeod went to Court. Whether she was one of the Queen's Mary's or not, we do not know, but that she was at the Court of Mary, Queen of Scots, from 1562 to 1565, is proved by entries in the Accounts of the Lord High Tr
2 CONC easurer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earl of Argyll finally obtained the wardship of Mary in 1566. In that year the Earl made an agreement with her uncle, Norman, in which he promised, among other things, to find Mary a husband. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He implemented his promise by giving her in marriage to his kinsman, Dugald Campbell of Castle Sween, a son of Sir Duncan Campbell of Auchinbreck. The marriage is said in Burke's PEERAGE [1938, p. 480] to have taken place in 1573,
2 CONC  but this date must be incorrect as there is a discharge, dated 1571, from Mary in existence, in which she states she is the 'spouse of an honourable man Dugald Campbell'. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [The discharge is among papers relating to Mary MacLeod in the Muniment Room in Dunvegan Castle. On these papers is based the above account of Mary's life-history, supplemented by an article, entitled: An Heiress Of The Isles, by 
2 CONC Mrs. Osbaldeston-Mitford (Brenda MacLeod) in The Clan MacLeod Magazine.] By Dugald Campbell of Auchinbreck, she had issue.... 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is said that Mary of Dunvegan married Roderick MacNeil of Barra after the death of Duncan [sic? Dugald] Campbell. We do not know the exact date of Mary's death, but she was living in 1602. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rev. A. MacLean Sinclair, in THE MACNEILS OF BARRA (CELTIC REVIEW, III, p. 217), gives the marriage of Mary to Roderick MacNeil as an established fact without supplying any documentary evidence.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ---- 
2 CONT
