1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''James Hamilton, 2nd Marquess of Hamilton and 4th Earl of Arran KG PC''' (1589 – 2 March 1625), styled Lord Aven from 1599 to 1604, was a Scottish politician. He was the son of John Hamilton, 1st Marquess of Hami
2 CONC lton.
2 CONT
2 CONT He inherited his father's titles and estates in 1604. In 1608 he was created Lord Aberbrothwick, and the following year he inherited the earldom of Arran from his insane and childless uncle James Hamilton. He moved to England with
2 CONC  King James VI, and invested into the Somers Isles Company, an offshoot of the Virginia company, buying the shares of Lucy Harrington, Countess of Bedford. The Parish of Hamilton in the Somers Isles (alias Bermuda) is named for hi
2 CONC m. He was created Earl of Cambridge and Baron of Inner Dale in the peerage of England on 16 June 1619. In 1621 he served as Lord High Commissioner to the Parliament of Scotland, the King's representative in the Parliament of Scotl
2 CONC and.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1603, he married Lady Ann Cunningham, a daughter of James Cunningham, 7th Earl of Glencairn and they had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Lady Anne Hamilton, married Hugh Montgomerie, 7th Earl of Eglinton and had issue
2 CONT # Lady Margaret Hamilton, married John Lindsay, 17th Earl of Crawford and had issue
2 CONT # Lady Mary Hamilton (died 1633), married James Douglas, 2nd Earl of Queensberry, no issue
2 CONT # James Hamilton, 1st Duke of Hamilton (1606–1649)
2 CONT # William Hamilton, 2nd Duke of Hamilton (1616–1651)
2 CONT
2 CONT He also had an illegitimate daughter, Margaret (who married John Hamilton, 1st Lord Belhaven and Stenton and had issue) by Anne Stewart, a daughter of Walter Stewart, 1st Lord Blantyre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton died on 2 March 1625 at Whitehall, London, from a fever and was buried in the family mausoleum at Hamilton, on 2 September of that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT * James Hamiton, National Portrait Gallery, London. Retrieved July 2009
2 CONT * Parliament of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He inherited his father's titles and estates in 1604. King James granted him the property and lands of Arbroath Abbey, or "Aberbrothwick", and on 5 May 1608 created him, Lord Aberbrothwick.[2] In the following year, Aberbrothwick 
2 CONC inherited the earldom of Arran from his insane and childless uncle James Hamilton. He moved to England with King James VI, and invested into the Somers Isles Company, an offshoot of the Virginia company, buying the shares of Lucy 
2 CONC Harrington, Countess of Bedford. The Parish of Hamilton in the Somers Isles (alias Bermuda) is named for him. He was created Earl of Cambridge and Baron of Inner Dale in the peerage of England on 16 June 1619. In 1621 he served as
2 CONC  Lord High Commissioner to the Parliament of Scotland, the King's representative in the Parliament of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1603, he married Lady Ann Cunningham, a daughter of James Cunningham, 7th Earl of Glencairn and they had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Anne Hamilton, married Hugh Montgomerie, 7th Earl of Eglinton and had issue Lady Margaret Hamilton, married John Lindsay, 17th Earl of Crawford and had issue Lady Mary Hamilton (died 1633), married James Douglas, 2nd Earl of 
2 CONC Queensberry, no issue James Hamilton, 1st Duke of Hamilton (1606–1649) William Hamilton, 2nd Duke of Hamilton (1616–1651) He also had an illegitimate daughter, Margaret (who married John Hamilton, 1st Lord Belhaven and Stenton and
2 CONC  had issue) by Anne Stewart, a daughter of Walter Stewart, 1st Lord Blantyre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton died on 2 March 1625 at Whitehall, London, from a fever and was buried in the family mausoleum at Hamilton, on 2 September of that year.
2 CONT
