1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == PATRICK, LORD GRAY ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun04pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun04pauluoft/page/285/mode/1up The Scots Peerage IV: 285-86]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Patrick Gray, 6th Lord Gray''' (died 1612), known most of his life as '''Patrick, Master of Gray''', was a Scottish nobleman and politician during the reigns of Mary, Queen of Scots and James VI of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patrick_Gray,_6th_Lord_Gray Biographical Summary by Wikipedia] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Patrick Gray, the son of Patrick, 5th Lord Gray, and of his wife Barbara (a daughter of William Ruthven, 2nd Lord Ruthven) grew up as a Protestant and attended the University of Glasgow. In 1575 he married to Elizabeth Lyon, daugh
2 CONC ter of John Lyon, 8th Lord Glamis, a marriage that failed shortly afterwards. Patrick traveled to France, converted to Roman Catholicism and became a supporter of Mary, Queen of Scots (died 1587).
2 CONT
2 CONT Political life
2 CONT
2 CONT On his return to Scotland in 1583 Patrick gained notability as a political schemer and diplomat, endearing himself to the young King James whilst he plotted with James Stewart, Earl of Arran to keep Mary in prison. In October 1584
2 CONC  he was appointed a Gentleman of the Privy Chamber and made Master of the King's wardrobe and menagerie, in charge of the king's jewels, clothing and tapestry, and the employment of tailors and shoemakers. He was sent by James VI 
2 CONC as Scottish Ambassador to England in 1584 to broker with Elizabeth I over the fate of Mary. On his return to Scotland, after the execution of Mary, Patrick was declared a traitor, jailed and later banished from Scotland. James VI 
2 CONC forgave Patrick and allowed him to return to Scotland in 1589, restoring his offices to him, although the Master of Gray continued his scheming career implicating himself in a number of intrigues and plots.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Master of Gray married again in 1585 to Lady Mary Stewart, daughter of Robert Stewart, 1st Earl of Orkney and cousin to King James. In November 1598, he was travelling in Picardy then visited the Earl of Gowrie at Orléans. The
2 CONC n he spoke to Esmé Stewart's widow, Catherine de Balsac, at Aubigny-sur-Nère, who feared her son, Ludovic Stewart, 2nd Duke of Lennox was plotting with Henry Kier, (a Catholic agent). Gray was trying to secure Lennox's inheritance
2 CONC . Then he had an audience with the King of France who was waiting for James VI to send him Scottish hunting hounds.
2 CONT
2 CONT In May 1601, the Earl of Mar and Edward Bruce, Commendator of Kinloss returned from an embassy to London. Although they had reached an understanding on the succession of James VI to the throne of England, it was kept secret. The a
2 CONC pparent lack of achievement was seen as an opportunity for Mar's political opponents to supplant him, and the Master of Grey attempted, without success, to gain the confidence of Robert Cecil in England. Cecil however did not even
2 CONC  tell Gray of his secret correspondence with the Scottish King. In the domestic arena, Gray also was involved in a coalition of Anne of Denmark and the Duke of Lennox against Mar. Patrick became 6th Lord Gray, on his father's deat
2 CONC h in 1609, three years before his own death in 1612. He was succeeded by his son Andrew Gray, 7th Lord Gray.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Gray married secondly, on 20 July 1585, Mary Stewart, a daughter of Robert Stewart, 1st Earl of Orkney. Their children included:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Elizabeth Gray, (b. 1586).[25]
2 CONT # Andrew Gray, 7th Lord Gray
2 CONT # Jean Gray (d. 1639), married John Wemyss, 1st Earl of Wemyss
2 CONT # Agnes Gray, Countess of Menteith (d. 1667), married William Graham, 7th Earl of Menteith
2 CONT In September 1597 Anne of Denmark was planning her formal entry to Dundee and she wrote to Mary Stewart, Mistress of Gray to meet her in Perth and accompany her to Dundee.[26]
2 CONT
2 CONT Literature
2 CONT
2 CONT The author Nigel Tranter wrote the historical novels The Master of Gray trilogy Lord and Master, The Courtesan and Past Master about Patrick, 6th Lord Gray.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
