1 NAME Thomas /Stewart/ Bishop of St Andrews, Dunkeld and Dunblane
2 GIVN Thomas
2 SURN Stewart
2 NSFX Bishop of St Andrews, Dunkeld and Dunblane
2 _MARNM Stewart
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1332
2 PLAC Dunkeld, Perthshire, , Scotland
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1411
2 ADDR
3 STAE Scotland
1 OCCU Archdeacon of St Andrews.  Dean of Dunkeld.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Thomas Stewart, son of Robert ll by an unknown mistress.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Stewart_(bishop)_
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Stewart was an illegitimate son of King Robert II of Scotland. In 1380, Avignon Pope Clement VII provided Thomas with the Archdeaconry of the Bishopric of St. Andrews, as well as the canonry (and prebend) of Stobo in the Bi
2 CONC shopric of Glasgow. In 1389, the king petitioned and obtained for Thomas from the Pope the right to hold the deanery of the Bishopric of Dunkeld along with his other offices, and in 1393, the Pope provided a canonry in the Bishopr
2 CONC ic of Brechin. In this period, Archdeacon Thomas obtained a Bachelor of Canon Law at the University of Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 1 July 1401, following the deeath of Walter Trail, Bishop of St. Andrews, Thomas was elected to fill the see's vacancy. However, because of the problems experienced by Avignon Pope Benedict XIII, who was being besieged by the K
2 CONC ing of France, Thomas had problems obtaining Papal confirmation. In this context, Thomas' election fell victim to the political struggles of the time. Thomas was supported by his nephew, David Stewart, Duke of Rothesay, who active
2 CONC ly campaigned in the region of St. Andrews during the year 1401. However, this aligned him against his half-brother, Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany. In order to obtain control of Dumbarton Castle, the Duke of Albany offered it
2 CONC s keeper, Walter de Danyelston, the semi-vacant see of St. Andrews. After a meeting between Albany and the Bishop elect at Abernethy in the summer of 1402, Thomas renounced his rights as Bishop and allowed a new "election" to take
2 CONC  place.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT *Boardman, Stephen, The Early Stewart Kings: Robert II and Robert III, 1371-1406, (East Linton, 1996)
2 CONT * Dowden, John, The Bishops of Scotland, ed. J. Maitland Thomson, (Glasgow, 1912)
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Stewart_(bishop)_
2 CONT * https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archbishop_of_St_Andrews_
2 CONT **el. 1401–1402	Never consecrated. He was the bastard son of King Robert II of Scotland, and renounced his rights soon after his election.
2 CONT *[https://www.crockford.org.uk/historical-successions/st-andrews-dunkeld-and-dunblane St Andrews, Dunkeld and Dunblane] in Crockford’s Clerical Directory. “1401 Thomas Stewart (elect)”
