1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====Comments
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.geni.com/people/Walter-Stewart/6000000001464679138?through=6000000019149104126 and https://www.geni.com/people/Isabella-Margaret-Countess-of-Fife-and-Angus-Heiress-of-Hokettle/6000000001374384141?through=600000001914
2 CONC 9104126 had no surviving children. Disconnected from them as parents, April 2016.
2 CONT * Robert Stewart 1st Duke of Albany had '''no''' son named Walter. — Disconnected 12/19/2015
2 CONT
2 CONT >====[https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1885-1900/Stewart,_Walter_(d.1437) Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900], Vol. 54, by Henderson, T. F.:–
2 CONT >'''Stewart, Walter, Earl of Atholl''' (d. 1437), was the second son of King Robert II [q. v.], by his second wife, Euphemia Ross. Robert III [q. v.], Robert Stewart, first duke of Albany [q. v.], and Alexander Stewart, earl of Bu
2 CONC chan [q. v.], were his half-brothers. Another son of King Robert II, named Walter, by his first wife, was still alive in July 1362, so that possibly the second Walter was born subsequently to this; but in any case as early as 19 O
2 CONC ct. 1378 he married Margaret, only daughter and heiress of Sir David de Barclay, lord of Brechin, with whom he obtained the estate and also the title of lord of Brechin (''Reg. Mag. Sig. Sco''t. pp. 146, 147, quoted in Burnet's Pr
2 CONC eface to the ''Exchequer Rolls,'' vol. iv. p. clx). 
2 CONT >'''On 15 Nov. 1391''', he had a safe-conduct to go to England with thirty attendants (Cal. Documents relating to Scotland, 1357–1509, No. 431), and he had similar safe-conducts on 5 Dec. 1391 (ib. No. 433), 10 Jan. 1402–3 (''ib''
2 CONC . No. 627), and 8 June 1404, in the last instance that he might make a pilgrimage to the shrine of Thomas of Canterbury (''ib''. No. 656). In 1398 and 1400 he received a hundred marks as keeper of Edinburgh Castle (''Exchequer Rol
2 CONC ls'', iii. 437, 487). 
2 CONT >'''He had a charter''' of the earldom of Caithness, on the resignation of his niece Euphemia, countess palatine of Strathearn, and is so designated in July 1402 (ib. iii. 545). 
2 CONT >'''In charters''' of 20 Oct. 1416 and 22 Aug. 1421 he is also mentioned as tutor of Malise, earl of Strathearn [q. v.] (Hist. MSS. 7th Rep. p. 706). 
2 CONT >'''In the safe-conduct of 8 June 1404''', he is designated Earl of Atholl and Caithness, the earldom of Atholl having been previously vested in his father, Robert II. On 22 Sept. 1409 he received from the regent Albany a grant of
2 CONC  the barony of Cortachy in Forfarshire. He took a leading part in the movement for the return of James I to Scotland in 1424; was a conservator for Scotland of the truce with England, signed 28 March (Cal. Documents relating to Sc
2 CONC otland, 1357–1509, No. 949); and at the same time gave surety in twelve hundred marks that his son David would remain a hostage in England for King James's ransom (''ib''. No. 950). 
2 CONT >'''He was also one of the jury''' which after the king's return condemned Murdac Stewart, second duke of Albany [q. v.] On 22 July 1427 he had a grant of the earldom of Strathearn for life (''Reg. Mag. Sig. Scot''. i. No. 93), an
2 CONC d on 15 May 1430 he resigned the earldom of Caithness in favour of his son Alexander. 
2 CONT >'''In 1437''', he engaged in the plot for the assassination of James I, in order that his grandson, Sir Robert Stewart, chamberlain to the king, might succeed to the crown. It was successful so far as the king's assassination was
2 CONC  concerned; but the cruel deed in the Blackfriars monastery, on 20 Feb., was approved of by few except those immediately concerned in it. Atholl was captured by the Earl of Angus, and, along with the other conspirators, was put to
2 CONC  death in April 1437 after enduring unspeakable tortures. He affirmed that although he had been made aware of the conspiracy, he had used every endeavour to persuade his grandson against it, and believed that he had succeeded. Bef
2 CONC ore execution he was placed on a pillory, and, in bitter mockery of his supposed purpose, his head was encircled with a red-hot iron crown, on which was inscribed ‘The king of traitors.’ 
2 CONT >'''By his wife''', Margaret, daughter of Sir David de Barclay, he had two sons: David, who seems to have died before him in England, leaving a son, Sir Robert, conspirator with his grandfather; and Alan, in whose favour his fathe
2 CONC r resigned the earldom of Caithness in 1430, and who was killed by Donald Balloch in 1431, leaving no issue.
2 CONT >'''Sources:''' ''Exchequer Rolls of Scotland'', vols. iii–iv.; Cal. Documents relating to Scotland, 1357–1509; ''Reg. Mag. Sig. Scot''. vol. i.; Douglas's ''Scottish Peerage'' (Wood):
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Burke, "Vicissitudes of Families", pp 103-4:–
2 CONT >"'''Walter Stewart,''' Earl of Atholl, Strathearn and Caithness (died 26 March 1437) was a Scottish nobleman, the son of Robert II of Scotland and Lady Euphemia Ross.
2 CONT >"'''Hatred against King James I''' rankled deeply in the hearts of some of the principal nobility, who resented his severity, and especially the relentless rigour with which he had destroyed the illustrious princes of the House o
2 CONC f Albany. The King's uncles, sons of the second marriage of his grandfather, had escaped at that time, and the Earl of Athol had been distinguished by his nephew's favour. He had even been benefited at the expense of his grandneph
2 CONC ew, Malise, Earl of Strathern, son of the daughter of his elder brother, David: for under pretence that Strathern was a male fief, the King deprived Malise of that earldom in 1427, and conferred it on Walter, Earl of Athol, for li
2 CONC fe.
2 CONT >"'''Notwithstanding the high favour''' in which Athol and his grandson and heir, Sir Robert Stewart, were held by the King, they were deeply concerned in the conspiracy which terminated in his murder, in the monastery of the Domi
2 CONC nicans at Perth, on the night of the 20 Feb 1436. Sir Robert Stewart, who was chamberlain, availed himself of the privileges of his office, in preparing for the admission of the conspirators; and he and his grandfather were in the
2 CONC  King's company up to the very moment when the murder took place. Sir Robert Steward was taken, and, after cruel tortures, was beheaded. The aged Earl was also taken, tried, and condemned; and although he protested his own innocen
2 CONC ce, he admitted that he had knowledge of his grandson's complicity in the conspiracy, from which he had vainly attempted to dissuade him. The cross on which his grandson had been tortured previous to his death, was taken down, and
2 CONC  a pillar was set up in its stead, to which the Earl was bound, with a paper crown fastened on his head, inscribed with the title "Traitor!". His head was then struck off, and having been adorned with an iron crown, was stuck on t
2 CONC he point of a spear. His extensive estates were forfeited, and among them the spoils of his elder brother. The earldom of Strathern reverted to the crown, and it was annexed thereto by Act of Parliament in 1455."
2 CONT
2 CONT ====[https://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p216.htm Our Royal, Title, Noble and Commoner Ancestors and Cousins]:
2 CONT >'''Sir Walter Stewart, Earl Caithness, Atholl, & Strathearn, Lord Brechin'''{1,2,3}
2 CONT >Last Edited: 27 May 2013
2 CONT >M, #6483, d. 26 March 1437
2 CONT >'''Father:''' Sir Robert II Stewart, King of Scotland, Earl of Atholl, Strathearn, 7th Steward of Scotland4,5 b. 2 Mar 1316, d. 19 Apr 1390 
2 CONT >'''Mother:''' Euphemia Ross4,5 b. c 1329, d. 20 Feb 1388
2 CONT >'''Sir Walter Stewart''', Earl Caithness, Atholl, & Strathearn, Lord Brechin married '''Margaret Barclay''', daughter of Sir David Barclay, Lord of Brechin and Janet Keith, before 19 October 1378; They had '''2 sons''' ('''David;
2 CONC  & Alan, Earl of Caithness'''). By an '''unknown mistress''' he also had a son (A'''lan, Canon of Aberdeen, Dunblane, & Dunkeld''').{2,3} 
2 CONT >'''Sir Walter Stewart,''' Earl Caithness, Atholl, & Strathearn, Lord Brechin and''' Elizabeth Graham''' obtained a marriage license on 1 August 1404; Date of dispensation for being related in the 3rd degree of affinity. However, 
2 CONC the''' marriage never took place'''.{3}
2 CONT >'''Sir Walter Stewart''', Earl Caithness, Atholl, & Strathearn, Lord Brechin died on 26 March 1437 at Stirling, Stirlingshire, Scotland; Beheaded for being an accessory to the assassination of King James I of Scotland.{2,3}
2 CONT >'''Spouse 1:''' Margaret Barclay d. b 1 Aug 1404
2 CONT >'''Children'''
2 CONT >'''1.''' David Stewart2,3 d. a Feb 1434
2 CONT >'''2.''' Alan Stewart, Earl of Caithness2,3 d. 1431
2 CONT >>'''Spouse 2:''' Elizabeth Graham
2 CONT >'''Citations'''
2 CONT >1. Unknown author, ''Burke's Guide to the Royal Family'', p. 318.
2 CONT >2. Douglas Richardson, ''Magna Carta Ancestry'', 2nd Edition, Vol. III, p. 560.
2 CONT >3. Douglas Richardson, ''Royal Ancestry'', Vol. I, p. 637.
2 CONT >4. Douglas Richardson, ''Magna Carta Ancestry'', 2nd Edition, Vol. III, p. 538.
2 CONT >5. Douglas Richardson, ''Royal Ancestry,'' Vol. I, p. 614.
2 CONT >—————————————————
