2 CONT ====from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Stewart,_Earl_of_Atholl Wikipedia]:
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Stewart, Earl of Atholl, Strathearn and Caithness (c. 1360 – 26 March 1437) was a Scottish nobleman, the son of Robert II of Scotland. Stewart advocated for the ransom and return to Scotland of the future king in exile, Jam
2 CONC es I, in 1424. In 1425 he served as a member of the jury of 21 which tried and executed his nephew Murdoch Stewart, 2nd Duke of Albany. Eventually, however, Atholl turned against the King and conspired in his assassination in 1437
2 CONC . He was tried for murder and was executed after three days of torture.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart was a son of Robert II of Scotland by his second wife Euphemia de Ross, daughter of Aodh, Earl of Ross. He was also a younger half-brother of Robert III of Scotland and an uncle of the above-mentioned James I of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart married first, sometime before 1378, Margaret Barclay, Lady of Brechin,[1] by whom he had two sons:
2 CONT Alan Stewart, 4th Earl of Caithness (d. 1431)
2 CONT David Stewart, Master of Atholl (d. bef. 1437)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1390, Stewart's niece Euphemia resigned to him the Earldom of Caithness. In 1404, he was created Earl of Atholl.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ransom and return of James I of Scotland'''
2 CONT Stewart was energetic in retrieving his nephew James I from the Kingdom of England, which was accomplished in 1424, and was a member of the jury which tried his half nephew, Murdoch Stewart, 2nd Duke of Albany, and culminated in t
2 CONC he execution of Albany and two of his sons.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart was made Great Justiciar of Scotland and Earl of Strathearn, with such title being taken from Malise Graham, who subsequently became the Earl of Menteith in 1427.[3] He resigned Caithness to his son Alan in 1428 but regain
2 CONC ed it on Alan's death without issue in 1431.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT The depth of Stewart's loyalties to James is unclear. The chronicler Buchanan (1582) saw in his efforts to return James to Scotland and support him against Albany and his children a deep-laid plan for those two branches of the Hou
2 CONC se of Stewart to destroy each other – and clear his own way to the throne, reviving the old charge of illegitimacy against his half-brother Robert III. Others aver that it was the imprisonment and subsequent death of his son David
2 CONC  that turned him against the king.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Assassination of James I'''
2 CONT Whatever the cause of Stewart's rage against the King, he joined with his grandson Robert Stewart, Master of Atholl, and Sir Robert Graham in a conspiracy against James I, which assassinated the king on 20 February 1437. Robert St
2 CONC ewart unbarred the doors to the royal apartments, permitting assassins to enter the King's lodging at the Dominican Blackfriars in Perth. The King hid under the floorboards, only to be discovered by Sir Robert Graham, who personal
2 CONC ly murdered the monarch.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Execution of Walter Stewart'''
2 CONT Stewart had little popular support for his cause, and the conspirators were swiftly apprehended. They were attainted and put to death in Edinburgh by a series of tortures remarkable and hideous even for that era. He was tortured o
2 CONC ver a period of three days.
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''On the first day:''' - he was put in a cart with a crane, hoist up, dropped, and jerked violently to stretch his joints. He was then placed in a pillory and "crowned with a diadem of burning iron"[5] bearing the inscription "K
2 CONC ing of all Traitors".
2 CONT >'''On the second day:''' - he was bound to a hurdle and dragged along the high street of Edinburgh (some claim he was also blinded and tortured with red-hot iron pincers on this day, but Buchanan speaks only of the hurdle).
2 CONT >'''On the third and final day:''' - he was disembowelled while alive, his entrails burnt before his face, and his heart was torn out and burnt. Finally, his corpse was beheaded and quartered, and the quarters displayed around the
2 CONC  realm.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Notes
2 CONT # McAndrew, Bruce A., (2006), [https://books.google.com/books?id=QFkI3G31HTMC&pg=PA180 ''Scotland's Historic Heraldry''], p. 180.
2 CONT # George Crawfurd, p. 159, [https://books.google.com/books?id=bpnmAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA159#v=onepage&q&f=false ''A General Description of the Shire of Renfrew'' (1818)].
2 CONT # Nicholas, Harris (1842). [https://archive.org/details/historyofearldom00nicouoft/page/n19/mode/2up?view=theater ''History of the Earldoms of Strathern, Monteith, and Airth'']. William Pickering: Stevens and Norton: Clark, Edinbu
2 CONC rgh. pp. 19–20.
2 CONT # Campbell, Alastair, (2000) [https://books.google.com/books?id=4ek2lrDPjF0C&pg=PA121 ''A History of Clan Campbell: From Origins to Flodden''], p.121.
2 CONT # McAndrew, Bruce A., (2006), [https://books.google.com/books?id=QFkI3G31HTMC&pg=PA180 ''Scotland's Historic Heraldry''], p.181.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Other References
2 CONT * Anonymous, [https://books.google.com/books?id=V3mujbY7BxkC&pg=PA277 ''Notes and Queries'', London (1867)], p. 277.
2 CONT * Sutton, D. F., ed. (2009) ''Rerum Scoticarum Historia'' (1582). Translated by G. Buchanan. Irvine, California, USA: The University of California. Available at: [https://philological.cal.bham.ac.uk/scothist/ The Philological Muse
2 CONC um], online.
2 CONT * Riddell, John, [https://books.google.com/books?id=ST0sAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA201 ''Tracts, Legal and Historical: with other Antiquarian Matters'' (1835)], p. 201.
