1 NOTE REFN: P2960
2 CONT EUPHEMIA ROSS, the 2nd wife and only queen of Robert II, was the d aughter
2 CONT of Hugh de Ross, 4th Earl of Ross. She was probably born between abou t
2 CONT 1325 and 1330, but this is very uncertain. Her mother was the earl's 2nd
2 CONT wife, Margaret, daughter of Sir David Graham of Montrose. Euphemia's
2 CONT parents were granted a dispensation to marry, and a legitimation of past
2 CONT and future c hildren born to them, 29 November 1329.
2 CONT She had married 1stly, John Randolph, 3rd Earl of Moray, who was killed
2 CONT at the disastrous battle of Neville's Cross , 17 October 1346, leaving her
2 CONT a childless and vulnerable widow of, at the mo st, 21 years of age, and
2 CONT probably rather younger.
2 CONT She married 2ndly (dispens ation granted by Pope Innocent VI at Avignon, 2
2 CONT May 1355), as his 2nd wife, R obert Stewart, Earl of Strathearn, who
2 CONT succeeded to the throne as Robert II, 22 February 1371. She gave him 2
2 CONT sons and 2 daughters. See: Genealogical Note s page. She was crowned at
2 CONT Scone by Alexander de Kyninmund II, bishop of Aber deen, in 1372.
2 CONT She died in 1387, predeceasing her husband by about 3 years, bu t the
2 CONT exact date and the place of her death and burial seem to have gone
2 CONT un recorded. She must have been a very good-natured and long-suffering
2 CONT lady, put ting up with the numerous and flagrant infidelities of her royal
2 CONT husband. Her personal charm and beauty may be inferred from the fact that
2 CONT both her daught ers possessed those qualities in a marked degree.
1 NOTE She requested and received Papal dispensation to marry.
1 NOTE Euphemia Ross, the 2nd wife and only queen of Robert II, was the daughter of Hugh de Ross, 4th Earl of Ross. She was probably born between about 1325 and 1330, but this is very uncertain. Her mother was the earl's 2nd wife, Margar
2 CONC et, daughter of Sir David Graham of Montrose. Euphemia's parents were granted a dispensation to marry, and a legitimation of past and future children born to them, 29 November 1329.
2 CONT She had married 1stly, John Randolph, 3rd Earl of Moray, who was killed at the disastrous battle of Neville's Cross, 17 October 1346, leaving her a childless and vulnerable widow of, at the most, 21 years of age, and probably rath
2 CONC er younger.
2 CONT She married 2ndly (dispensation granted by Pope Innocent VI at Avignon, 2 May 1355), as his 2nd wife, Robert Stewart, Earl of Strathearn, who succeeded to the throne as Robert II, 22 February 1371. She gave him 2 sons and 2 daught
2 CONC ers. See: Genealogical Notes page. She was crowned at Scone by Alexander de Kyninmund II, bishop of Aberdeen, in 1372.
2 CONT She died in 1387, predeceasing her husband by about 3 years, but the exact date and the place of her death and burial seem to have gone unrecorded. She must have been a very good-natured and long-suffering lady, putting up with th
2 CONC e numerous and flagrant infidelities of her royal husband. Her personal charm and beauty may be inferred from the fact that both her daughters possessed those qualities in a marked degree.
