1 NAME Robert /Logan/ of Grugar and Restalrig, Kt.
2 GIVN Robert
2 SURN Logan
2 NSFX of Grugar and Restalrig, Kt.
2 _MARNM Logan
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1371
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1439
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Scotland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Notes from http://www.electricscotland.com/history/leith/5.htm Sir Robert Logan was one of the leading men of the land, and in his time had held high office in the State. He had taken a leading share in the stormy 
2 CONC life of the time. He was now advanced in years, and his old age was not without sorrow, for his eldest son had died before him. This would seem to have given his thoughts a graver turn, and to have brought him more under the influ
2 CONC ence of the Church. And in order that he and his might be for ever remembered in the prayers of grateful souls, he founded in Leith in 1430 the Hospital of St. Anthony, where the Logans were ...to be prayit for ilk Sunday till the
2 CONC  day of doom... by the beneficiaries of his charity. He died in the year 1439, when his many possessions were divided among his four grandsons, two only of whom concern our story. Sir John, the eldest, succeeded him in the barony 
2 CONC of Restalrig, and, like Thomas de Lestalric, his ancestor of the twelfth century, held the high office of Sheriff of Edinburgh.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes from http://hometown.aol.com/oleoghain/loganhistory.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT .....Another of the families beholding to the Stewarts was the Logans. The Logans were at the height of their influence at this time. The Workman's manuscript of 1565 records that Sir Robert Logan of Grugar married a heiress of Lo
2 CONC thian family of Lestalrig or Restalrig and succeeded to that estate by 1397. This was a strategically important estate because it controlled the port of Leith outside Edinburgh. It is unlikely that the marriage would have been gra
2 CONC nted without the blessing of the king. Sir Robert was appointed Lord High Admiral of Scotland by Robert III in 1400. In that same year, Logan soundly defeated an English fleet in the Firth of Fourth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Logan of Grugar's son, also named Robert Logan, married Princess Katherine Stewart. General Logan-Home lists several sources documenting the marriage. (In one contemporary document, for example, King Robert III identifies R
2 CONC obert Logan for legal purposes as delecto fratri suo or my beloved brother-in-law.) This was not the first family tie between the Logans and Stewarts; John Logan of Antrim's daughter had married the son of Alan Stewart.
2 CONT
2 CONT Katherine is thought to have been a daughter of Robert II by his third wife Euphemia Ross, daughter of Walter Mac an t-Sargait and widow of John Randolph, Earl of Moray. As such, Katherine's dowry may have included estates in Ross
2 CONC . Since the King's marriage to Euphemia was circa 1355 and guessing that Katherine Stewart was at least 15 years old at the time of the marriage to Logan, it is likely to have occurred no earlier than 1370 and no later than 1394. 
2 CONC But, some sources list a daughter, Katherine, by Robert's second wife/ mistress, Elizabeth Mure; in which case and if she were the same Katherine, the marriage may have been earlier.
2 CONT
