2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Lindsay of Glenesk From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Sir Alexander Lindsay of Glenesk (died 1381) was a Scottish knight banneret. Active in jousting and as a crusader he was in favour with the Scottish kings David II
2 CONC  and Robert II.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents   1	Biography 2	Family 3	Notes 4	References 5	Further reading Biography Lindsay was the second surviving son to Sir David de Lindsay of Crawford and the Byres,[2][1] and Mary Abernethy, widow of Andre de Leschelyn (Leslie
2 CONC ), and a daughter and co-heiress of Alexander de Abernethy.[3] Lindsay's father had been Constable of Edinburgh Castle and Berwick and active during the Wars of Independence.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lindsay was esquire to his cousin Thomas Stewart, 2nd Earl of Angus.[1][4] Lindsay inherited his mother's lands in Angus, and also aquted some of the baronies alloted to his aunt Marget Aberhethy, Countess of Angus.[5] Upon his ma
2 CONC rriage to Catherine Stirling around 1358, he consolidated his already large maternal inheritance, with that of his wife, which would add to the extensive landholdings of the Earldom of Crawford in north-east Scotland and elsewhere
2 CONC .[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lindsay was knighted before 1368,[citation needed] He was party to a truce with England as "Chevalier et Baron" in 1369. He sealed with his nephew the settlements of the Crown (1371–1373) and was Justiciary in the North possibly a
2 CONC s early of 1371 but definitely by 1373.[1][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT He had many safe-conducts from the English kings Edward III and Richard II, and on December 1381, he obtained a passport to travel through England on a pilgrimage to the Holy Land. He died on Crete some time before March 1382.[1][
2 CONC 5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Lindsay married Catherine Stirling (died by 1378), widow of Andrew Leslie of that Ilk and daughter to John Stirling of Glenesk.[1][a] They had children:
2 CONT
2 CONT David Lindsay, 1st Earl of Crawford.[5] Sir Alexander Lindsay of Baltrody. He fought in a tournament with Ralph de Nevill 1391, a warrant to this effect being granted by King Richard II 20 June 1391. He had a pension from Crail, t
2 CONC he entries in the Exchequer Rolls proving that he died between 1397 and 1398. He probably died childless.[5] unnamed daughter, who married David Stewart, Earl of Strathearn[6] Lindsay married secondly to Marjorie,[1] daughter to S
2 CONC ir John Stewart of Ralston, and niece to Robert II of Scotland.[6] They had children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Lindsay of Rossie, known as one of those responsible for the death of David, Duke of Rothesay. He was tutor to David, younger son of the Earl of Crawford in 1407, executor to the Earl, had charter of lands in Ballenbre
2 CONC ich from Earl Alexander in 1423. He married Matilda Stewart (died 1485). He died between 1435 and 1437.[6] Sir Walter Lindsay of Kinneff who was killed at the battle of Verneuil (1424). Katherine his widow marred Walter Dempster. 
2 CONC Sir Walter had a son also Walter [7] Euphemia Lindsay who was engaged to David Duke of Rothesay, but the marriage did not take place.[8] Lindsay also had an illegitimate son Jon who was living in 1378. He also had a son James, Rec
2 CONC tor of St. Brioc, Canon and Treasurer of Aberdeen. He had dispensation for illegitimacy on taking Holy Orders, but as Sir Alexander Lindsay and his second wife must have been cousins James may have been their son.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes Jump up ^ or John Stirling of Edzell (Paul 1906, p. 13) ^ Jump up to: a b c d e f g Cameron 2004. Jump up ^ Paul 1906, p. 11. ^ Jump up to: a b Paul 1906, p. 10. Jump up ^ Paul 1906, pp. 12–13. ^ Jump up to: a b c d e f Paul
2 CONC  1906, p. 13. ^ Jump up to: a b c Paul 1906, p. 14. Jump up ^ Paul 1906, p. 14–15. ^ Jump up to: a b Paul 1906, p. 15. References[edit] Cameron, Sonja (2004), "Lindsay family of Barnweill, Crawford, and Glenesk (per. c.1250–c.1400
2 CONC )", Oxford Dictionary of National Biography (online ed.), Oxford University Press, doi:10.1093/ref:odnb/54260 (Subscription or UK public library membership required.) Attribution  This article incorporates text from a publication 
2 CONC now in the public domain: Paul, Sir James Balfour (1906), Scots Peerage, volume III (IX volumes ed.), Edinburgh: David Douglas, pp. 11–12
2 CONT
2 CONT Further reading Wikisource has the text of the 1885–1900 Dictionary of National Biography's article about Lindsay, James. Carwford, Alexander Lindsay (1858), Lives of the Lindsays; or, A memoir of the houses of Crawford and Balcar
2 CONC res 1 (IV volumes ed.), London, p. 53
2 CONT
