1 NOTE His mother, in her share of the Abernathy lands, was esquire to the Earl of Angus in 1368. He was named a Baron in 1369 and Justiciary in 1378.
1 NOTE He succeeded to the lands in Northumberland granted to his father by Margaret, Countess of Pembroke, in 1244, as well as to Brenevil and the Byres, and, afterwards, to Crawford. He was a ward of John Comyn of Tynedale, whose wife 
2 CONC Alicia was probably a sister of the Lady Margaret, above. Both were Lindsays. He was a conspicuous supporter of Sir William Wallace and King Robert Bruce and sat in Parliament in 1308.
1 NOTE He was named in the records in 1280. He was given the lands of Crawford, confiscated from the Pinkeneys, who apparently  sided with the English in the preceding war.
