2 CONT '''David Lindsay of Glenesk, 1st Earl of Crawford''' (c. 1360 – 1407) was a Scottish peer who was created Earl in 1398.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Life===
2 CONT
2 CONT Crawford was the son of '''Sir Alexander Lindsay of Glenesk and Katherine Stirling'''. Succeeding his father in 1381, he was known until his elevation to the peerage as Sir David Lindsay of Glenesk.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many historians believe that Lindsay was also the organiser for the Battle of the Clans at Perth in 1396. Additionally, Lindsay was a noted jousting champion who fought the English champion Lord Welles in a remarkable duel on St. 
2 CONC George's Day 1390. In the duel, Lindsay unhorsed Welles so easily that the crowd began yelling that he had nailed himself to his saddle. To prove he had not, Lindsay jumped off his horse and then back on, while still wearing his f
2 CONC ull suit of armour. After he realized Welles was wounded he rushed to his aid and helped him to a nearby hospital. He visited Welles every day while he was recovering and they became good friends.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Crawford died at Finavon Castle in 1407 and was buried at the church of the Greyfriars at Dundee.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriage and issue===
2 CONT
2 CONT He married '''Elizabeth Stewart, daughter of King Robert II and Euphemia de Ross'''. They had seven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Alexander Lindsay, 2nd Earl of Crawford (c. 1387–1438)
2 CONT * Gerard Lindsay (d. 1421)
2 CONT * Ingelram Lindsay, Bishop of Aberdeen (d. 1458)
2 CONT * Marjorie Lindsay, married Sir William Douglas of Lochleven. They were parents of Catherine Douglas.
2 CONT * David Lindsay, Lord of Newdosk (1407–?)
2 CONT * Elizabeth Lindsay (1407–?), married Robert Erskine, 1st Lord Erskine and had issue.
2 CONT * Isabella Lindsay (1407–?), married Sir John Maxwell.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT 1^ James Balfour Paul, Scots Peerage, vol iii, p. 17. Edinburgh 1904
2 CONT
