3 STAE Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 9 DEC 1529
2 ADDR
3 CITY Foulis
3 STAE Scotland
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} She [Margaret Douglas] had no children by the Earls of Douglas, but had three daughters by the Earl of Atholl: Janet, married Alexander Gordon; Catherine, married John, 6th Lord Forbes; '''Elizabeth''' Stewart, mar
2 CONC ried before 1483, to Andrew, 2nd Lord Gray, (he was widower of Janet, only daughter of Robert, Lord Keith, by whom he had issue.  he d. in February of 1514), and had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Elizabeth Stewart1
2 CONT *'''F, #2915, b. after 1460
2 CONT *Father	Sir John Stewart, 1st Earl Atholl, Ambassador to England b. c 1440, d. 15 Sep 1512
2 CONT *Mother	Margaret 'Fair Maid of Galloway' Douglas2,3 d. bt 22 Jan 1473 - 19 Apr 1475
2 CONT *'''     Elizabeth Stewart was born after 1460. She married Andrew Gray, 2nd Lord Gray, Sheriff of Forfar, Justice of Scotland''', son of Patrick Gray, Master of Gray and Annabella Forbes, '''before 1483.
2 CONT *'''Family     Andrew Gray, 2nd Lord Gray, Sheriff of Forfar, Justice of Scotland''' d. Feb 1514
2 CONT *'''Children	
2 CONT **Isobel 'the Younger' Gray+
2 CONT **Margaret Gray+
2 CONT **Janet Gray4 d. Oct 1539
2 CONT **Andrew Gray+5 d. b 13 Apr 1541
2 CONT **Edward Gray5 d. a 5 Oct 1521
2 CONT **Marjory Gray5
2 CONT **Elizabeth Gray4
2 CONT **Gilbert Gray+ d. 1541
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Comments 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Wives and Daughters of John Stewart, 1st Earl of Atholl - Part 1" (Brad Verity et al, May 15, 2014).  < [https://groups.google.com/g/soc.genealogy.medieval/c/eq3_G4VOLiI/m/NhYosxelf7oJ soc.genealogy.medieval] >
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughters of the 1st Earl of Atholl by his first wife Margaret Douglas:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) [Elizabeth?] Stewart, married as his 2nd of 4 wives, Andrew, 2nd Lord Gray
2 CONT She is called the first daughter of the 2nd wife by DP; the 2nd daughter of the 1st wife by BEP, listed as the 6th daughter in SP Atholl (1904), called the third daughter by the 1st wife in SP Gray (1907), and the 6th daughter of 
2 CONC the 1st wife by CP 6 (1926), sub Gray.
2 CONT
2 CONT I've not yet located a single primary document to verify this daughter's first name and marriage to Lord Gray. Considering that she was one of only four of the 1st Earl's daughters to marry peers, this is rather odd. Andrew, 2nd L
2 CONC ord Gray, had a complicated marital history. SP Gray says his parents were married "before 1445", and that he married Elizabeth Stewart "before 1483", but doesn't cite a source for either date:
2 CONT https://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun04pauluoft#page/276/mode/2up
2 CONT
2 CONT The ODNB bio of the 2nd Lord Gray by Michael A. Penman simply follows SP when it comes to his wives and children, and doesn't estimate a birthdate for him, except to say he was probably a minor when he succeeded his grandfather in
2 CONC  January 1470, which would make him born after 1450. He could not have been born too much later, as a marriage for him with a daughter of the Earl of Erroll was contracted in May 1457. SP Gray says Andrew the 2nd Lord had three ch
2 CONC ildren with his first wife Janet Keith, but provides no hint as to when that marriage occurred.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other than each being involved in the rebellion against James III in 1482, there is nothing in the ODNB bios of the 1st Earl of Atholl and the 2nd Lord Gray to link them together politically, and Gray's ODNB bio has no particulars
2 CONC  at all as to his activities in the 1470s. A document of October 1474 does however connect Atholl and Gray. The three named witnesses to the marriage contract Atholl made with the Earl of Huntly (see link to it below) are "Jamys E
2 CONC rle of Buchan & lorde Ouchterhous owr deirast brother Androw lorde Gray Silvester of Retray".
2 CONT
2 CONT As Andrew Gray could have been born at any point between 1445 and 1457, it's possible he had married Atholl's daughter by October 1474 when he witnessed the marriage contract. Sadly neither of Gray's first two wives appear in the 
2 CONC Registers of the Great Seal or Privy Seal of Scotland. Primary documents are needed to verify the first names and identification of Gray's first two spouses - it's odd that no marriage contract or settlement for either spouse exis
2 CONC ts.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''At this point, Gray's marriage to a daughter of the 1st Earl of Atholl needs to be considered unsupported outside of secondary pedigrees.''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT *'''Lady Elizabeth Stewart1
2 CONT *F, #28224
2 CONT *Last Edited=6 Oct 2012
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=0.09%
2 CONT *'''     Lady Elizabeth Stewart was the daughter''' of John Stewart, 1st Earl of Atholl and Lady Eleanor Sinclair.1 '''She married Andrew Gray, 2nd Lord Gray''', son of Patrick Gray, Master of Gray and Annabella Forbes, '''before 
2 CONC 1483.1
2 CONT *'''      From before 1483, her married name became Gray.1
2 CONT *'''Child of Lady Elizabeth Stewart and Andrew Gray, 2nd Lord Gray
2 CONT **Gilbert Gray+ d. b 1541
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S37] BP2003 volume 2, page 1642. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT *From: http://thepeerage.com/p2823.htm#i28224 ____________
2 CONT
2 CONT This person and their pedigree are currently documented from "The Royal Lineage of Our Noble and Gentle Families together with Their Paternal Ancestry" Compiled by Joseph Foster, 1885
2 CONT
2 CONT [Source: http://www.archive.org/details/royallineageofou02fost ]
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________
2 CONT
2 CONT According to tradition , she was captured in childhood by Sir John Campbell and a party of the Campbells, while out with her nurse near Calder castle. Her uncles pursued and overtook the division of the Campbells to whose care she
2 CONC  had been intrusted, and would have rescued her but for the presence of mind of Campbell of Inverliver, who, seeing their approach, inverted a large camp kettle as if to conceal her, and commanding his seven sons to defend it to t
2 CONC he death, hurried on with his prize. The young men were all slain, and when the Calders lifted up the kettle, no Muriel was there. Meanwhile so much time had been gained that further pursuit was useless. The nurse, just before the
2 CONC  child was seized, bit off a joint of her li ttle finger, in order to mark her identity - a precaution which seems to have b een necessary, from Campbell of Auchinbreck's reply to one who, in the midst of their congratulations on 
2 CONC arriving safely in Argyll with their charge, asked wh at was to be done should the child die before she was marriageable. "She can ne ver die", said he, "as long as a red-haired lassie can be found on either side of Lochawe!". It 
2 CONC would appear that the heiress of the Calders had red hair.
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________
2 CONT
2 CONT This name was taken from p. 113 of the book: A Royal Lineage... 
2 CONT
