1 NOTE bruce_statue
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=6dded248-b72c-4c1b-94ab-97d3d1162c7d&tid=2539545&pid=-1836038643
1 NOTE      "On Oct 27, 1292, Robert's father resigned the earldom of Carrick to him and he swore fealty to Edward I on Aug 24, 1296.  In 1297, he fought with the English against William Wallace, but after changing sides more than once h
2 CONC e joined the fighters for Scottish freedom and was chosen one of th guardians of the kingdom at Peebles on Aug 19, 1299.
2 CONT      "Bruce succeeded his father as Lord of Annandale in the spring of 1304, and for a time he resumed fealty to Edward I until learning that the king was planning his execution...He fled to Scotland and ...assembled his followers
2 CONC  at Lochmaben and proceeded to Scone, where he was proclaimed king and the crown was placed on his head by Isabella, Countess of Buchanm 'in the presence, and with the consent of four birhops, five earls, and the people of the lan
2 CONC d' on Mary 27, 1306...
2 CONT      "In May 1307 he landed on the Carrick coast and defeated the Earl of Pembroke at Loudon Hill.  In the course of the next two years he won back most of the country.  The Scottish clergy recognized him as king in 1301, and in 1
2 CONC 312 the king of Norway ceded the Hebrides to him.  Raids into the north of England took place in 1312 and 1313 and on June 24, 1314 the decisvie battle of Bannockburn, near Stirling, totally defeated the forced of Edward II and es
2 CONC tablished Scottish independence.  According the legend, the Monymusk Religqary, containing the relics of St. Columba, was carried into battle."  Williamson
2 CONT
2 CONT      With the execution of William Wallace in 1305,   "it was time for Robert Bruce to free himself from his fealty to Edward I and to lead the fight for Scotland.  At a meeting in Greyfriar's Kirk at Dumfrieds between the two sur
2 CONC viving claimants for the Scotish throne, the perfidtious but crafty Robert Bruce murdered John Comyn, thus earning the enmity of the many powerful supporters of the Comyn family .  He was also excommunicated from the Church.  His 
2 CONC answer was to strike out boldly, raising the Royal Standard at Scone and, on Mary 27, 1306, he declared himself King of Scots.  Edward's reply was predictable; he sent a large army north, defeated Bruce at the battle of Methven, e
2 CONC xcuted many of his supporters and forced the Scottish king to become a hunted outlaw.
2 CONT      "Again, the indefatigable Scottish leader bided his time.  After a year of demoralization and widespread English terror, during which two of his brothrs were killed, Bruce came out of hiding.  Aided mightly by his chief liete
2 CONC nant, Sir James Douglas, "The Black Douglas," he won a first victory on Plam Sunday, 1307.  From all over Scotland, the clans answered the call and Bruce's forces gathered in strength to fight the English invaders, winning many en
2 CONC courters against cavalry with his spearman.
2 CONT      The outstanding military success of Robert Bruce was followed by his Scottish kingdom's diplomatic overtures.  After an appeal from the Scottish nobility, the new Pop of Rome lifed Bruce's (excommunication?).  May 1328 brough
2 CONC t about a peace treaty signed at Northampton by the weary, helpless English king that recognized Scotand as an independent kingdom and Robert Bruce as king.  The Declaration of Independence signed at ARbroath was the culmination o
2 CONC f Bruce's career.  All his dreams fulfilled, he died one year later.  One who for years had been an Anglo-Norman vassal of the King of England had made himself into a truly national Scotish hero."  www.britannia.com/celtic/scotlan
2 CONC d/scot5.html
2 CONT
2 CONT     "On 25 March 1306, Robert the Bruce was chosen to be King of Scots and to lead the fight for Scottish independence against Edward I of England. Born in 1274 in Ayr, the son of Robert Bruce, Earl of Carrick, he was the grandson
2 CONC  of the Robert Bruce who had been one of the competitors for the throne after the death of the Maid of Norway. Robert I had been on the English side when Edward moved against Balliol, but he had subsequently joined Wallace's revol
2 CONC t. When Wallace gave up the Guardianship of Scotland in 1298, Robert became joint Guardian with Sir John Comyn of Badenoch (Balliol's nephew). A few weeks before his coronation, Robert killed his greatest rival for the crown - his
2 CONC  joint Guardian - in a Dumfries church, during the last of many arguments between them. For this murder, Robert was outlawed by Edward I and excommunicated by Pope Clement V. His reign did not begin well. He was defeated by the En
2 CONC glish at Methven in Perthshire; his wife, daughter and sisters were imprisoned; and three of his brothers were executed by the English. Robert fled westward to the Antrim coast. (The story of Robert drawing inspiration from a pers
2 CONC istent spider mending its web in a cave dates from the sixteenth century.) However, he possessed real military genius and he was helped by the fact that in 1307 Edward I, the self-styled 'Hammer of the Scots', died and was succeed
2 CONC ed by his less effective son Edward II. From 1307 onwards, with energy and determination, Robert waged highly successful guerrilla warfare against the English occupiers, establishing control north of the Forth, and gradually won b
2 CONC ack his kingdom; by 1314, Stirling was the only castle in English hands. His campaign culminated in resounding victory over Edward II (whose larger army of 20,000 outnumbered Robert's forces by three to one) at the Battle of Banno
2 CONC ckburn, near Stirling on 24 June 1314. Bannockburn confirmed the re-establishment of an independent Scottish monarchy. Two years later, his brother and heir presumptive, Edward Bruce, was inaugurated as High King of Ireland (which
2 CONC  increased pressure on the English), but was killed in battle in 1318. Even after Bannockburn, and the Scottish capture of Berwick in 1318, Edward II refused to give up his claim to the overlordship of Scotland, and so in 1320 the
2 CONC  Scottish Earls, Barons and the 'community of the realm' sent a letter to Pope John XXII declaring that Robert I was their rightful monarch. This 'Declaration of Arbroath' has become perhaps the most famous document in Scottish hi
2 CONC story. The Declaration asserted the antiquity of the Scottish people and their monarchy: '...we gather from the deeds and books of the ancients, that among other distinguished nations our own nation, namely of Scots, has been mark
2 CONC ed by many distinctions. It journeyed from Greater Scythia by the Tyrrenhian Sea and the Pillars of Hercules, and dwelt for a long span of time in Spain among the most savage peoples, but nowhere could it be subjugated by any peop
2 CONC le, however barbarous. From there it came, twelve hundred years after the people of Israel crossed the Red Sea and, having first driven out the Britons and altogether destroyed the Picts, it acquired, with many victories and untol
2 CONC d efforts, the places which it now holds ... As the histories of old time bear witness, it has held them free of all servitude ever since. In their kingdom one hundred and thirteen kings of their own royal stock have reigned, the 
2 CONC line unbroken by a single foreigner.' The Declaration also had a stark warning for Robert: 'were he to desist from what he has undertaken and be willing to subject us or our kingdom to the king of the English or the English, we wo
2 CONC uld strive to expel him forthwith as our enemy and as a subverter of right, his own and ours, and make someone else our king who is equal to the task of defending us.' In 1324, the Pope recognised Robert as king of an independent 
2 CONC Scotland. Two years later, the Franco-Scottish alliance was renewed in the Treaty of Corbeil, by which the Scots were obliged to make war on England should hostilities break out between England and France. In 1327, the English dep
2 CONC osed Edward II in favour of his son Edward III and peace was then made between Scotland and England with the treaty of Edinburgh-Northampton, which began with England's total renunciation of all claims to superiority over Scotland
2 CONC . Robert had achieved all he had fought for: ejecting the English, re-establishing peace and gaining recognition as the true king. By that time, King Robert was seriously ill, probably with leprosy, and he died at Cardross, Dunbar
2 CONC tonshire on 7 June 1329, aged 54. A few days later, in response to an earlier request by him, the Pope granted permission for kings of Scots to be anointed at their coronation (Scottish kings had previously been enthroned in a mai
2 CONC nly secular ceremony at Scone). This was a clear acknowledgement that the Pope recognised Scotland's independence."  Ancestry World Tree - Charlie-  genealogy@ntelos,net, May 2003
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
