1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sir Robert Erskine of that Ilk (<1343- 1385) High Chamberlain of Scotland'''
2 CONT s/o Sir William de Erskine
2 CONT x Beatrice Lindsay, d/o Alexander Lindsay of Crawford, and wid/o Sir Archibald Douglas (-<1352)
2 CONT *Sir Thomas (<1354-<1413), keeper of Edinburgh Castle, Sheriff of Edinburgh x Mary Douglas,xx Janet Keith or Barclay
2 CONT * Nicholas of Kinuoull (-<Dec 1406) x Jean Cameron xx Elizabeth Keith
2 CONT * Marion x (<1362) Maurice Drummond,
2 CONT xx Christian Menteith or Keith, wid/o Sir Edward Keith of Sinton
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT == Sir Robert Erskine of that Ilk (<1343 -1385) ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir Robert Erskine of that Ilk''' (d. between Whitsunday and Martinmas 1385) was a prominent Scottish nobleman, knight, statesman, and diplomat. He expanded the Erskine estates considerably and held important offices in 14th-ce
2 CONC ntury Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Career ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert first appears on record around 1343 as a knight and charter witness. Following the Battle of Neville’s Cross (1346), he acted as High Chamberlain of Scotland, officially holding the post from 1350 to 1357, and again fro
2 CONC m 1363 to 1364.
2 CONT
2 CONT He played a central role in negotiations for the release of King David II from English captivity. His son served twice as a royal hostage. Sir Robert helped ratify the King’s ransom treaty in 1357 and applied to the Papacy in 1359
2 CONC  for release from a crusading vow due to his national obligations. There is no evidence he ever left Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He undertook several diplomatic missions to England, France, and the Papal Court. On one occasion in 1363, he was given a gold cup in England as a diplomatic gift.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Landholdings and Royal Grants ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert acquired and exchanged several important estates, including:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Erskine (confirmed by charter in 1351/52)
2 CONT * Garioch (chartered in 1358 by the Earl of Mar)
2 CONT * Strathgartney (temporarily held before being reassigned by the King)
2 CONT * Alloa, Clackmannan, and surrounding areas (restored by charter in 1368)
2 CONT * New and Old Parks near Stirling, Brewhaugh, Ferrytoun, and others
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1367, Parliament revoked crown lands to fund the King’s ransom, affecting Sir Robert’s holdings. However, these were regranted to him in recognition of his services, and many of these lands remained in Erskine family possession
2 CONC  for generations.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Political Role and Royal Succession ===
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of King David II in 1371, Sir Robert supported the High Steward’s successful claim to the throne (Robert II). He is credited with restraining his stepson, William, Earl of Douglas, from making a rival claim—though 
2 CONC the basis of Douglas’s claim remains unclear.
2 CONT
2 CONT In return for his loyalty:
2 CONT * He received a formal bond of alliance from Robert, Earl of Fife, and his brother John (future Robert III), sealed in 1373
2 CONT * He exchanged the lands of Ednam for an annual rent of £100 drawn from the customs of Aberdeen
2 CONT * He was paid 500 merks in compensation for surrendering the keepership of Stirling Castle
2 CONT He also held the barony of Kinnoull, and other estates in Perthshire, some of which he granted to Cambuskenneth Abbey in the early 1360s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert remained active in Parliament and Council until 1384 and died in 1385.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Marriages and Issue ===
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-Erskine-of-that-Ilk/6000000220051411840 Sir Robert] married twice:
2 CONT
2 CONT x (1) '''Beatrice Lindsay''', daughter of Alexander Lindsay of Crawford and widow of Sir Archibald Douglas, Regent of Scotland, who died in 1333 at Halidon Hill. Children:
2 CONT *'''Thomas Erskine''' – his heir.
2 CONT *'''Nicholas of Kinnoull''' – uterine brother of Thomas. Received Kinnoull from his father in 1367. Present at King Robert II’s coronation in 1371. Died before December 1406. x (1) Jean Cameron, daughter and co-heiress of Sir John
2 CONC  Cameron of Balegarno. Son: William, succeeded at Kinnoull. Grandson: Sir John, died in 1445, leaving a daughter and heiress, who  was successively: contracted to James, son of James, 1st Lord Livingstone (unmarried); married to J
2 CONC ohn Crichton (details unknown), and later married Robert, 1st Lord Crichton of Sanquhar xx (2) Elizabeth Keith, widow of Sir Adam Gordon of that Ilk
2 CONT *'''Marion Erskine''' – married before 1362 to Maurice Drummond, 2nd of Concraig, and had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT xx (2) '''Christian Menteith (or Keith)''', widow of Sir Edward Keith of Sinton. This marriage was part of a reconciliation after Sir Robert had wounded her cousins during a feud. A dispensation was needed due to their kinship (Be
2 CONC atrice and Christian were related in the fourth degree). Due to clerical error, they married before the dispensation arrived. The marriage was later legitimised by the Pope. Although they had children, none appear to have survived
2 CONC . Christian survived Sir Robert and died around 1387.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References
2 CONT *[https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun05paul/page/592/mode/2up James Balfour Paul, ed., ''The Scots Peerage'', vol. 5 (Edinburgh: D. Douglas, 1904), p592-96]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sir Robert Erskine of that Ilk===
2 CONT * Birth: 1320 at Erskine; Renfrewshire; Scotland
2 CONT * Death: 1385 at Erskine; Renfrewshire; Scotland
2 CONT * son of Sir William Erskine of that Ilk.2 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: 
2 CONT # ? at ? to Beatrice Lindsay, 1291 - 1352 [17]
2 CONT # 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married,
2 CONT *  firstly, Beatrice de Lindsay, daughter of Sir Alexander de Lindsay.1 (1291-1352)
2 CONT *  secondly, Christian Menteith, daughter of Sir John Menteith, Lord of Arran and Helen of Mar, in 1352.2   She was the widow of Sir William Keith, the Grand Marischal of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT # 	Mary Erskine		Abt 1338 - ? 	
2 CONT # 	Thomas Erskine		Abt 1340 - 1405  	
2 CONT # 	Nicholas Erskine		Abt 1345 - ? 	
2 CONT #          Jean Erskine		Abt 1350 - ?
2 CONT
2 CONT ===notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT ERSKINE, anciently spelled Areskin, and sometimes Irskyn, a surname of great antiquity, and one which has been much distinguished in all periods of Scottish history, was originally derived from the lands and barony of Erskine in R
2 CONC enfrewshire, situated on the south side of the Clyde, the most ancient possession of the noble family who afterwards became Lords Erskine and earls of Mar.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.electricscotland.com/history/nation/erskine.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert de Erskine, knight, his eldest son, made an illustrious figure in his time, and for his patriotic services, was, by David the Second, appointed constable, keeper, and captain of Stirling castle. He was one of the ambass
2 CONC adors to England, to treat for the ransom of that monarch, after his capture in the battle of Durham in 1346. IN 1350 he was appointed by David, while still a prisoner, great chamberlain of Scotland, and in 1357 he was one of thos
2 CONC e who accomplished his sovereign’s deliverance, on which occasion his eldest son, Thomas, was one of the hostages for the payment of the king’s ransom. On his restoration, David, in addition to his former high office of chamberlai
2 CONC n, appointed Sir Robert Justiciary north of the Forth, and constable and keeper of the castles of Edinburgh and Dumbarton. In 1358 he was ambassador to France, and between 1360 and 1366 he was five times ambassador to England. In 
2 CONC 1367 he was warden of the marches, and heritable sheriff of Stirlingshire. In 1371 he was one of the great barons who ratified the succession to the crown of Robert the Second, grandson, by his daughter Marjory, of Robert the Bruc
2 CONC e, and the first of the Stuart family. To his other property he added that of Alloa, which the king bestowed on him, in exchange for the hunting district of Strathgartney, in the Highlands. He died in 1385.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
