1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The MacIains of Ardnamurchan were a sept of Clan MacDonald, Lord of the Isles.  Ardnamurchan was a promitory on the west side of the Isle of Mull.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clan MacIain - Clann MhicIain
2 CONT Close to Kilchoan are the ruins of Mingary (Mioghairidh) Castle built in the 1200s on an outcrop of rock on the edge of the sea. The castle was built for the MacIains of Ardnamurchan and had an eventful history. The MacIains are s
2 CONC aid to owe their origin to an appeal to Clan Donald, by the people of Ardnamurchan, asking protection. The Clan Donald chief sent his brother, Iain Sprangach (John the Bold) to take possession of the region, and this was confirmed
2 CONC  by Robert the Bruce in return for aid rendered during Scotland's wars for independence in the early 14th century. Iain Sprangach and his descendants, the MacIains, ruled the Ardnamurchan peninsula as chieftains in the Clan Donald
2 CONC  federation and were committed to retaining their independence from the growing influence of the Scottish monarchy. By the end of the 15th century, the power of the King, James IV, broke the MacDonald hegemony and garrisoned Minga
2 CONC ry Castle in 1493 and 1495 during this campaign. The MacIain chief at this time, John MacIain, was a staunch royalist, and actively supported the King's plans at the expense of Clan Donald, thereby becoming the most powerful chief
2 CONC tain of the Clan Donald.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of James IV at the Battle of Flodden Field, however, chaos erupted in the west. In 1517, MacIain power waned and in the middle of the 16th century, Campbell of Argyll was made the legal possessor of the Ardnamurcha
2 CONC n lands, although the MacIains remained in actual possession of them for some time. In 1624, the MacIains took to a life of piracy owing to the unfair treatment of the Campbells and the government. The clan became the scourge of t
2 CONC he western seas, and action was taken to suppress them. The clan was driven from the Ardnamurchan region, and many found refuge in the neighbouring Moidart region, which belonged to their kinsmen and allies, the Clanranald MacDona
2 CONC lds. Others emigrated to Ireland and emigrated again some years later to America.
