1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Sir James Douglas of Dalkeith1
2 CONT * M, #107929, d. 1420
2 CONT * Last Edited=2 Apr 2013
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir James Douglas of Dalkeith was the son of Sir John Douglas of Lothian and Agnes (?).2 He died in 1420. He lived at Dalkeith, Scotland.4
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Married 
2 CONT # Lady Agnes Dunbar, daughter of Patrick de Dunbar, 8th Earl of Dunbar and Ermengarde (?).1 
2 CONT # by contract to Egidia Stewart, daughter of Walter Stewart, 6th High Steward of Scotland and Isabella Graham, in October 1378.3 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Sir James Douglas of Dalkeith and Lady Agnes Dunbar
2 CONT # Janet Douglas+
2 CONT # Sir James Douglas, 1st Lord of Dalkeith+2 d. bt Feb 1440 - 22 May 1441
2 CONT # Margaret Douglas+4
2 CONT # William Douglas2
2 CONT # Agnes Douglas+2 d. c 1422
2 CONT
2 CONT He has also been seen to have these children by an unknown partner 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 	
2 CONT # Margaret Douglas
2 CONT # John Douglas, of Aberdour
2 CONT # James Douglas, of Aberdour, b. Abt 1390
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 - In 1373, James de Douglas was granted lands in Peebles by King Robert II (1370-1390) "for his support of a chaplain in the chapel Dalkeith" and the grant was subsequently confirmed by Robert II at Irvine on 25 October 1377.
2 CONT In 1384, a charter was granted to James de Douglas by King Robert III(1390-1406) "to endow a chaplaincy in the Chapel of St Nicholas, in the town of Dalkeith" and in 1406, having obtained the consent of Bishop Wardlaw of St Andrew
2 CONC s, within whose diocese Dalkeith lay, the Chapel was raised to the status of a Collegiate Church, with five prebendaries or priests, presided over by a provost or Head. 2 - On 21st November 1372 (SP, III, 261;SP, VI, 346), charter
2 CONC  of confirmation under the Great Seal by Robert II dated 6th December 1372 (RMS, I, 521), George Dunbar, 10th Earl of Dunbar (d. after 8th September 1422), granted terras nostras de Mordyntona (the lands of Mordington to be held o
2 CONC f the Earl, who retained the barony) to Sir James Douglas of Dalkeith (d. 1420), on the occasion of Sir James's marriage to Agnes, the Earl's sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that Sir James Douglas of Dalkeith was granted the barony of Dalkeith on 5th January 1368-9.
2 CONT
2 CONT
