2 CONT '''Lady Elizabeth Douglas'''1 
2 CONT F, #107996, '''b. between 1385 and 1401, d. circa 1451'''
2 CONT Last Edited=16 Jan 2016
2 CONT Consanguinity Index=0.42%
2 CONT '''     Lady Elizabeth Douglas was born between 1385 and 1401. She was the daughter of''' Archibald Douglas, 4th Earl of Douglas and Margaret Stewart, Lady of Galloway.1 '''She married, thirdly, William Sinclair, 1st Earl of Caith
2 CONC ness''', son of Henry Sinclair, 2nd Earl of Orkney and Egidia Douglas.2 '''She married, firstly, John Stewart, 3rd Earl of Buchan''', son of Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany and Muriel Keith, '''in November 1413.1 She married, s
2 CONC econdly, Sir Thomas Stewart, Master of Mar''', son of Alexander Stewart, 12th Earl of Mar, '''circa 1425.1,3 She died circa 1451.'''4
2 CONT '''     Her married name became Sinclair.2 From November 1413, her married name became Stewart. After her marriage, Lady Elizabeth Douglas was styled as Dowager Countess of Buchan on 17 August 1424.'''5
2 CONT '''Children of Lady Elizabeth Douglas and William Sinclair, 1st Earl of Caithness'''
2 CONT 1. Lady Elizabeth Sinclair+6 d. 1508
2 CONT 2. William Sinclair, 2nd Lord Sinclair+3 d. c 14 Jul 1487
2 CONT 3. Katherine Sinclair7
2 CONT 4. Susan Sinclair7
2 CONT 5. Mariot Sinclair7
2 CONT '''Child of Lady Elizabeth Douglas and John Stewart, 3rd Earl of Buchan'''
2 CONT 1. Lady Margaret Stewart+8 d. b 8 Jan 1461
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1.[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Families: The Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 219. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Families.
2 CONT 2.[S37] BP2003 volume 1, page 642. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT 3.[S37] BP2003. [S37]
2 CONT 4.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinc
2 CONC t or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume II, page 478. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT 5.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume I, page 79.
2 CONT 6.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume XI, page 188.
2 CONT 7.[S323] Sir James Balfour Paul, The Scots Peerage: founded on Wood's edition of Sir Robert Douglas's The Peerage of Scotland (Edinburgh, Scotland: David Douglas, 1904), volume II, page 334. Hereinafter cited as The Scots Peerage.
2 CONT 8.[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Families, page 220.
2 CONT From: https://www.thepeerage.com/p10800.htm#i107996
2 CONT _____
2 CONT '''William Sinclair''' (1410–1480), 1st Earl of Caithness (1455–1476), last Earl (Jarl) of Orkney (1434–1470 de facto, –1472 de jure), 2nd Lord Sinclair and 11th Baron of Roslin was a Norwegian and Scottish nobleman and the builde
2 CONC r of Rosslyn Chapel, in Midlothian.[1]
2 CONT In The Scots Peerage by James Balfour Paul he is designated as the 1st Lord Sinclair,[2] but historian Roland Saint-Clair designates him the 2nd Lord Sinclair in reference to his father, Henry II Sinclair, Earl of Orkney, being th
2 CONC e first person recorded as Lord Sinclair by public records.[3]
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT He was the son of Henry II Sinclair, Earl of Orkney and Egidia Douglas, daughter of Sir William Douglas of Nithsdale and maternal granddaughter of Robert II of Scotland. He was also the grandson of Henry I Sinclair, Earl of Orkney
2 CONC .[4]
2 CONT His father Henry, who had been a de facto Jarl of Orkney, died in 1420; William travelled to Copenhagen in 1422 to establish his claim to the Jarldom, but David Menzies was appointed instead, to rule as William's guardian until he
2 CONC  came of age. In 1424, William succeeded in wresting de facto control of the earldom from his guardian, but it was not until 1434 that he was acknowledged as Jarl of Orkney by King Eric VII of Denmark, Norway and Sweden, sovereign
2 CONC  of Orkney.[5][6]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT On 15 November 1456, the earl's father-in-law, Alexander Sutherland of Dunbeath, made his will in the earl's presence at Roslin Castle. The earl had married Sutherland's daughter Marjory, by Sutherland's wife Mariota, daughter of 
2 CONC Donald of Islay, Lord of the Isles.[1]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT Earl of Caithness
2 CONT Exchanging his inherited lordship of Nithsdale for lands in Caithness, '''William was granted the hereditary title Earl of Caithness in 1455.'''[11][10] He resigned the Earldom in favour of his second son from his second marriage,
2 CONC  William, in 1476.[1]
2 CONT '''William, Earl of Orkney''' died before 3 July 1480.[1]
2 CONT Family
2 CONT '''William Sinclair was married three times: firstly to Lady Elizabeth Douglas''', daughter of Archibald Douglas, 4th Earl of Douglas; secondly to Marjory Sutherland (married 1456), daughter of Alexander Sutherland of Dunbeath; an
2 CONC d thirdly to Janet Yeman.[12]
2 CONT '''By Lady Elizabeth Douglas he had the following children:'''
2 CONT 1. William Sinclair, 3rd Lord Sinclair who was reportedly disinherited by his father, only receiving Ravenscraig Castle in Fife.
2 CONT 2. Lady Catherine Sinclair, who married Alexander Stewart, Duke of Albany.
2 CONT 3. Elizabeth Sinclair, who married Andrew, Master of Rothes
2 CONT By Marjory Sutherland he had the following children:
2 CONT 1. Oliver St Clair, 12th Baron of Roslin, who received the Barony of Roslin.[13]
2 CONT 2. William Sinclair, 2nd Earl of Caithness (b. 1460 - d. 1513)[13]
2 CONT 3. Alexander Sinclair (c. 1454)
2 CONT 4. George Sinclair (c. 1453)
2 CONT 5. Robert Sinclair (1447)
2 CONT 6. Arthur Sinclair (c. 1452)
2 CONT 7. Lady Eleanor Sinclair (b. 1457- d. 1518), who married John Stewart, 1st Earl of Atholl.
2 CONT 8. Lady Elizabeth Sinclair (b. c. 1455 - d. 1498), who married the Laird of Houston.
2 CONT 9. Lady Margaret Sinclair (c. 1450), who married David Boswell of Balmuto.
2 CONT 10. Lady Katherine Sinclair (b. 1440 - d. 1479)
2 CONT 11. Lady Susan Sinclair (c. 1451)
2 CONT 12. Lady Marjory Sinclair (1455–80), married Andrew Leslie, Master of Rothes. With Andrew she had issue including William Leslie, 3rd Earl of Rothes
2 CONT 13. Lady Mariota Sinclair (c. 1455)
2 CONT 14. Lady Euphemia Sinclair (c. 1470), who married John Kincaid Laird of Warriston.
2 CONT Illegitimate:
2 CONT 1. Sir David Sinclair of Sumburgh, died 1507.[14]
2 CONT The earl's second son of his second marriage, William Sinclair, became the designated heir of the Earldom of Caithness, and continued that title. The Barony of Roslin went to his first son by that marriage, Sir Oliver Sinclair.[1]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Sinclair,_1st_Earl_of_Caithness
2 CONT _______
2 CONT '''Sinclair, William''' (1404?-1480) by John Anderson
2 CONT '''SINCLAIR, Sir WILLIAM, third Earl of Orkney and first Earl of Caithness''' (1404?–1480), chancellor of Scotland, born about 1404, was the only son of Henry Sinclair, second earl of Orkney [q. v.], by his wife Egidia or Giles Do
2 CONC uglas, daughter of Sir William Douglas of Nithsdale, and of the Princess Egidia, a daughter of Robert II.
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT The earl died apparently in the early part of 1480, when his pension ceased to be paid (Exchequer Rolls, ix. 78). '''He was twice married''': first, before 1437, to Margaret, eldest daughter of Archibald, fourth earl of Douglas, a
2 CONC nd widow of John Stewart, earl of Buchan [q. v.], and also of Thomas, master of Mar. By her he had a daughter Catharine, who married Alexander Stewart, duke of Albany [q. v.], and one son, William Sinclair of Newburgh, styled ‘Wil
2 CONC liam the Waster’ from his spendthrift habits, and who on that account was passed over by the earl in disposing of his estates, though he and his half-brother, Sir Oliver Sinclair, afterwards entered into a compromise as to their l
2 CONC ands. The title of Baron Sinclair was first conferred, 26 Jan. 1488–9, on Henry, son of William Sinclair of Newburgh. This Lord Sinclair, at whose request Gavin Douglas translated the Æneid into Scots, was slain at Flodden on 9 Se
2 CONC pt. 1513. His grandson Henry, third lord Sinclair, was a strenuous supporter of Mary Queen of Scots, signed the bond for her against Moray on 12 Sept. 1565, and joined the association in her support at Hamilton after her escape fr
2 CONC om Lochleven in 1568 [see for descendants Sinclair, John, seventh Lord Sinclair].
2 CONT '''The second wife of Earl William was Marjorie Sutherland, daughter of''' Alexander Sutherland of Dunbeath, '''and by her he had, with four daughters, four sons'''—William, Oliver, David, and John.
2 CONT '''The eldest son''', William, second earl of Caithness, was killed at Flodden in 1513, leaving two sons, of whom the eldest, John, succeeded as third Earl of Caithness; along with Lord Sinclair, the third earl in 1529 invaded Ork
2 CONC ney to endeavour to make good his professed claims to the earldom of Orkney, but was defeated and slain by the Orcadians under James Sinclair, governor of Kirkwall Castle, at Bigswell in Stenness on 18 May. His son George, fourth 
2 CONC earl, is noticed separately.
2 CONT '''The second son''', Sir Oliver Sinclair of Roslin, was father of Oliver Sinclair [q. v.], of Henry Sinclair [q. v.], bishop of Ross, and of John Sinclair [q. v.], bishop of Brechin.
2 CONT '''The third son''', Sir David Sinclair of Svenburgh or Sumburgh, was sometime captain of the castle of Bergen and governor of Shetland; and '''the fourth son''', John, was bishop of Caithness.
2 CONT [Barry's Hist. of the Orkney Islands, 1805; Orkneyinga Saga, ed. 1873, Introduction, pp. lxviii–lxxi; Exchequer Rolls of Scotland, vols. v–viii.; Antiquities of Aberdeen and Banff (Spalding Club), vols. iii. and iv.; Father Hay's 
2 CONC Genealogie of the Sainte Claires of Rosslyn, ed. 1835.]
2 CONT J. A-n.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1885-1900/Sinclair,_William_(1404%3F-1480)
2 CONT _______
2 CONT Archibald Douglas, 4th Earl of Douglas, Duke of Touraine (c. 1369 – 17 August 1424),[2] was a Scottish nobleman and warlord. He is sometimes given the epithet "Tyneman" (Old Scots: Loser), but this may be a reference to his great-
2 CONC uncle Sir Archibald Douglas.
2 CONT Master of Douglas
2 CONT The eldest legitimate son of Archibald Douglas, 3rd Earl of Douglas and Joanna de Moravia of Bothwell, he was born probably born at Bothwell Castle c. 1369 and was known as the Master of Douglas until his accession. By 1390 he had
2 CONC  married the Princess Margaret of Carrick, a daughter of King Robert III of Scotland. Around this time, his father bestowed upon him the regalities of the Ettrick Forest, Lauderdale and Romannobridge, Peeblesshire.[3]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT In 1390 he married Lady Margaret (d. 1451), eldest daughter of John Stewart, Earl of Carrick, who later became King Robert III. Of their children:
2 CONT *Archibald Douglas, 5th Earl of Douglas, and Wigton, &c., (1390–1439), who succeeded to the earldom.
2 CONT *'''Elizabeth (d. c. 1451), who married first John Stewart, Earl of Buchan, secondly Thomas, son of Alexander Stewart, Earl of Mar, and thirdly William Sinclair, 1st Earl of Caithness (d. 1480).'''
2 CONT *William Douglas (b. before 1401)
2 CONT *Sir James Douglas (d. 17 August 1424)
2 CONT *Mary Douglas, who married Sir Simon Glendinning of Parton. Their daughter Janet married Gilbert Grierson 1st Lord of Lag.
2 CONT Douglas in Literature
2 CONT The 4th Earl of Douglas is represented in William Shakespeare's Henry IV, Part 1 from the defeat at Homildon to his release following the Battle of Shrewsbury. Douglas also appears as a character in Edith Pargeter's 1972 novel A B
2 CONC loody Field by Shrewsbury from his defeat and capture at Homildon through his participation at Shrewsbury on the rebels' side.
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archibald_Douglas,_4th_Earl_of_Douglas
2 CONT ______
