1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Lord Robert Erskine, 1st Lord Erskine (<1400 -1452)'''
2 CONT x dau (likely Elizabeth) of David, Earl of Crawford
2 CONT *Sir Thomas Erskine, succeeded as 2nd Lord Erskine.
2 CONT *Janet Erskine x Walter of Lennox
2 CONT *Christian Erskine x Patrick, Lord Graham
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT = Lord Robert Erskine, 1st Lord Erskine (<1400 -1452) =
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert Erskine first appears in an indenture dated 20 December 1400 between David, Earl of Crawford, and Sir Thomas Erskine. He was already a knight and was captured with his father at the Battle of Homildon in 1402. He succee
2 CONC ded to the Erskine estates shortly after Martinmas 1403, and by 1405 he was back in Scotland. Between 1405 and 1415 he traveled multiple times to England, and from 1424 to 1427, he served as a hostage for King James I, later being
2 CONC  exchanged for the Earl of Menteith. In 1430, he acted as an ambassador to the English Court, and over the years received a hereditary £100 royal pension, held military commands such as keeper of Dumbarton Castle, and was generall
2 CONC y styled Lord of that Ilk or Sir Robert of Erskine until after 1438, when he began using the title Lord Erskine.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1435, he pursued a claim to the Earldom of Mar, following the death of Alexander Stewart, Earl of Mar. This claim stemmed from his mother’s rights, referenced as early as 1391. He formed a strategic alliance with Sir Alexande
2 CONC r Forbes and moved to secure legal possession, obtaining service as heir to Isabella, Countess of Mar, in two stages in April and October 1438. Subsequently, he assumed the style Earl of Mar and Garioch and Lord Erskine, although 
2 CONC the Crown recognized him only as Lord Erskine. However, official sources like the Exchequer Rolls referred to his son as Master of Mar, and in Aberdeen’s burgh records (1439), he is called Earl of Mar.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1440, an agreement at Stirling between Lord Erskine and King James II’s Council required that Kildrummy Castle be placed under his custody for the King, with the understanding that his claim to the earldom would be adjudicated.
2 CONC  Erskine fulfilled his part, but the Crown defaulted, leading to a breakdown in the agreement. He lodged formal complaints in 1442 and again in 1449, when his son Sir Thomas Erskine protested in Parliament that the King’s officers
2 CONC  had withheld the earldom’s revenues. A final protest was made on 26 January 1450, after which no resolution was reached.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Erskine was still alive on 7 September 1451, when he confirmed a charter as Earl of Mar and Garioch. He likely died before 26 August 1452, when Garioch was granted to Queen Mary of Gueldres, and was certainly deceased by 6 No
2 CONC vember 1452, when his son was styled Thomas, Lord Erskine.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Marriage and Issue ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Robert Erskine is generally said to have married a daughter of Robert Stewart of Lorn, but evidence suggests he married a daughter (likely Elizabeth) of David, Earl of Crawford, as per the 1400 indenture. Their children were:
2 CONT *Sir Thomas Erskine, succeeded as 2nd Lord Erskine.
2 CONT *Janet Erskine, named in a 1421 papal dispensation for marriage to Walter of Lennox, son of Murdach, Duke of Albany. Though questions remain about the canonical nature of the marriage, Janet is considered the mother of Walter Stew
2 CONC art of Morphie, ancestral to the Earls of Castle Stewart in Ireland.
2 CONT *Christian Erskine, married Patrick, Lord Graham, and was the mother of William, Lord Graham.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Reference
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun05paul/page/592/mode/2up James Balfour Paul, ed., ''The Scots Peerage'', vol. 5 (Edinburgh: D. Douglas, 1904), p601-5]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ROBERT ERSKINE; s. and h. of Sir Thomas E. of that ilk [i.e. Erskine, on the Clyde] (d. between Martinmas [11 Nov.] 1403 and Whitsunday  [18 May] 1404), by Janet,1 widow of Sir David Barclay, of Brechin, and probably dau. and h. o
2 CONC f Sir Edward Keith, of Syntoun; knighted before 20 Dec.1400; was taken prisoner at the battle of Homildon, 14 Sep. 1402; was one of the hostages for the ransom of King James in 1424, when his revenue was estimated at 1,ooo marks, 
2 CONC being set at liberty 19 June 1425.    He was made a Lord of Parl.,2 as LORD ERSKINE in or shortly before 1433.3 Soon after the death (1435) of Alexander, husband of Isabel,Countess of Mar (to which Alexander and his heirs by her t
2 CONC hat Earldom 4 had been resigned), Lord Erskine was served h. to the said Countess Isabel, and having had seizin 21 Nov. 1438, assumed the title of EARL OF MAR. He m., soon after 20 Dec. 1400,5 Elizabeth, dau. of David (LINDSAY), 1
2 CONC st EARL OF CRAWFORD, by Elizabeth, dau. of ROBERT II. He d. between 7 Sept. 1451 and 6 Nov. 1452.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.erskinclan.com/gene4.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of Thomas Erskine, 2nd Lord Erskine (d. 1494)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Erskine,_2nd_Lord_Erskine
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sir Robert Erskine, knight, was one of the hostages for the ransom of James the First in 1424. On the death of Alexander, earl of Mar, in 1435, he claimed that title in right of his mother, and assumed the title of earl of Mar, bu
2 CONC t the king unjustly kept him out of possession. He died in 1453.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Kildrummy Castle was seized briefly in 1442 by its rightful owner, Sir Robert Erskine. [Kildrummy Castle, p. 10]
