1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/n/i/c/Garrett-C-Nicholson/WEBSITE-0001/UHP-0504.html '''familytreemaker.genealogy.com....''']; 
2 CONT * '''Alexander Blair''' ,  was born 1584 in Blair, Ayrshire, Scotland, and died date unknown in Paisley, Renfrewshire, Scotland. He married Elizabeth Cochrane on 24 Jul 1593 in ,,Renfrewshire, Scotland, daughter of William Cochran
2 CONC e and Margaret Montgomerie.
2 CONT * son of '''John Blair''' and '''Grizel Semple''',
2 CONT * More About Alexander Blair and Elizabeth Cochrane:
2 CONT * Marriage: 24 Jul 1593, ,,Renfrewshire, Scotland.
2 CONT ===Children of Alexander Blair and Elizabeth Cochrane are:===
2 CONT >* +'''William Cochrane''', b. 1605, Cowdon, Ayrshire, Scotland, d. 1685, Dundonald, Ayrshire, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT rane
2 CONT In my Geni tree
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Cochrane
2 CONT Parents: 	John Blair of that Ilk and Grizel Hamilton (born Semple) Sisters: 	Mrs Countess Dirleton and Margaret Partner: 	Elizabeth Cochrane Sons: 	William, Gavin and Hugh View this profile on Geni
2 CONT
2 CONT Sort by Value Smart Matches™ (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Cochrane MyHeritage Family Trees hultarna Web Site, managed by Göran Hult (Contact) Death:	1641 Parents:	John Blair Of That Ilk, Grizel Blair Of That Ilk (född Sempill) Siblings:	Grizel Cunningham Of Robertland (född Ham
2 CONC ilton), Anna Brisbane (född Blair), Jean Blair, Bryce Blair Of That Ilk, John Blair Of That Ilk Son:	William Cochrane, 1st Earl Of Dundonald
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Alexander Cochrane was originally Alexander Blair and upon his marraige to his cousin Elizabeth Cochrane, in 1593, he assumed the last name Cochrane in accordance with a deed written by Elizabeth's father William.  The deed stated
2 CONC  that any person succeeding him must carry the name Cochrane as he had only daughters.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT
2 CONT @R850838881@  Ancestry Family Trees  Online publication - Provo, UT, USA: Ancestry.com.  Original data:  Family Tree files submitted by Ancestry members. 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT Ancestry Family Tree  http://trees.ancestry.com/pt/AMTCitationRedir.aspx?tid=115337401&pid=1181 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The title of Earl of Dondonald The first Earl was succeeded by his grandson John Cochrane, the second Earl (died 1690). He was the son of William Cochrane, Lord Cochrane (died 1679), eldest son of the 1st Earl. The 2nd Earl was a 
2 CONC member of the Scottish Privy Council. On his death the titles passed to his eldest son William Cochrane, the third Earl (died 1705). He died unmarried at an early age and was succeeded by his younger brother John Cochrane, the fou
2 CONC rth Earl (died 1720). He sat in the House of Lords as a Scottish Representative Peer from 1713 to 1715. [Wikipedia] This is from a primary source: Cochrane, Sir William, of Cowdon, first Earl of Dundonald d. 1686, was the second s
2 CONC on of Alexander Blair, of the ancient family of Blair of Blair, who, on his marriage to Elizabeth Cochrane, of the ancient family of Cochrane of Cochrane, assumed the name of Cochrane. By prudent management he came to be one of th
2 CONC e largest proprietors in the counties of Ayr and Renfrew, and was returned member of the Scottish parliament for Ayrshire in 1644 (Members of Parliament for Scotland in Foster's Collectanea Genealogica, i. 7). For his services in 
2 CONC behalf of the king he was created a peer by the title of Lord Cochrane of Dundonald, by patent dated Scarborough, 27 Dec. 1647, with limitation to heirs male of his body. When it was resolved to raise an army in behalf of Charles 
2 CONC I, in 1648, he was sent over to Ireland to bring home the Scotch troops (Guthry, Memoirs, 268). In 1653 he acquired the lordship of Paisley, where he fixed his residence, and lived in great splendour. The following year he was fin
2 CONC ed by Cromwell for his loyalty 5,000l., which was reduced to 2,000l. (State Papers, Dom. Ser. 1655, p. 71), and afterwards to 1,666l. 13s. 4d. (ib. 116). At the Restoration he was appointed a privy councillor and one of the commis
2 CONC sioners of the treasury, and for these services was created a peer by the title Earl of Dundonald, Lord Cochrane of Paisley and Ochiltree, 12 May 1669. His tremulous signature appears attached to Claverhouse's marriage contract in
2 CONC  1684. The same year an accusation was preferred against him on the ground that his son, Lord Cochrane, when he was dying in 1679, kept a chaplain who prayed God to bless the rebels in the west with success (Fountainhall, Decision
2 CONC s, i. 299). He died in 1686, and was buried at Dundonald. By his marriage to Eupheme, daughter of Sir William Scot of Ardross, Fifeshire, he had two sons, William, lord Cochrane, who died in his father's lifetime, in 1679, and Sir
2 CONC  John Cochrane of Ochiltree, and one daughter, Grizel, married to George, tenth lord Ross. He was succeeded in the earldom by his grandson John, the son of William, lord Cochrane. (Dictionary of National Biography, Contributor: Th
2 CONC omas Finlayson Henderson Published: 1887)
