1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/19/72/d1/2b/534448633b569286/an_example_of_the_flags_flown_by_the_covenanters_original.jpg?hash=5ba2a2fe610e583310fd1ca9b538b0161c25924e95f07d261e11e7d445c42a3b.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL An example of the flags flown by the Covenanters
2 NOTE Anne Cunningham, 2nd Marchioness of Hamilton (Cunyngham) (widow of James Hamilton, 2nd Marquess of Hamilton) led a cavalry troop during the "Battle" of Berwick (5 June 1639) and supported the Covenanters against her son James who 
3 CONC was later beheaded by the English Parliament. This James was 3rd Marquis 1st Duke of Hamilton, KG, PC (adviser to King Charles I of England vs English Parliament), who fought Covenanters, and lost the Battle of Preston, England 16
3 CONC 48. Beheaded per Oliver Cromwell (as was King Charles I of England). Had No surviving sons. The dukedom eventually went to the Douglas family by way of one of his daughters, Anne.
3 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady_Ann_Cunningham
2 NOTE Major-General John, Comte de France, Hamilton (Jacobite) and Anthony (Antoine) Hamilton (Jacobite) were sons of Sir George Hamilton of Donalong, Baronet. There is a discussion as to whether Alexander Hamilton Captain (Covenanter) 
3 CONC was also a son of Sir George Hamilton of Donalong, Baronet but disowned. 
3 CONT
3 CONT Covenanters (1638) were for the rights of the Church and people over the King. 
3 CONT
3 CONT Jacobites (1689) were for James II and VII Stuart (converted to Catholicism in approximately 1668, but not revealed until his refusal of the English Parliament's Test Act in 1673 ) to remain on the throne after the death of his br
3 CONC other Charles II. The English and Scottish parliaments disagreed on his proposals and the "divine right of kings". James II and VII was replaced by his Anglican daughter Mary II and her Protestant husband William of Orange.
2 NOTE Covenanters (Scottish Gaelic: Cùmhnantaich) were members of a 17th-century Scottish religious and political movement, who supported a Presbyterian Church of Scotland and the primacy of its leaders in religious affairs. The name is
3 CONC  derived from covenant, a biblical term for a bond or agreement with God.
3 CONT
3 CONT The origins of the movement lay in disputes with James VI and his son Charles I over church structure and doctrine. In 1638, thousands of Scots signed the National Covenant, pledging to resist changes imposed by Charles on the kir
3 CONC k; following victory in the 1639 and 1640 Bishops' Wars, the Covenanters took control of Scotland, and the 1643 Solemn League and Covenant brought them into the First English Civil War on the side of parliament. After his defeat i
3 CONC n May 1646, Charles I surrendered to the Scots Covenanters, rather than parliament. By doing so, he hoped to exploit divisions between Presbyterians and English Independents.
3 CONT
3 CONT As a result, the Scots supported Charles in the 1648 Second English Civil War. After the king's execution in 1649, the Covenanter government, in order to protect the Presbyterian polity and Calvinist doctrine of the Church of Scot
3 CONC land, signed the Treaty of Breda (1650) restoring Charles' son to the Scottish throne and supporting him against the English parliamentary forces as Charles II. Charles II was crowned King of Scots in Scone in January 1651, but by
3 CONC  then the terms agreed at Breda were already a dead letter. The army associated with the kirk party under David Leslie, 1st Lord Newark was destroyed by Oliver Cromwell at the Battle of Dunbar in September 1650, while the English 
3 CONC parliamentarian New Model Army had taken Edinburgh and much of Lowland Scotland. The resulting annexation of Scotland by the Commonwealth of England abolished Scotland's legislative institutions and disestablished Presbyterianism.
3 CONC  There was freedom of religion under the Commonwealth, except for Roman Catholics, but the edicts of the kirk's assemblies were no longer enforced by law.
3 CONT
3 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Covenanters
