1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===JAMES HAMILTON===
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Duke of Hamilton; Marquess of Clydesdale, Earl of Arran and Cambridge, Lord Avon and Innerdale===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''James Hamilton, third Marquess of Hamilton, is the son of James Hamilton, second Marquess of Hamilton, and his wife Lady Anna Cunningham. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun04pauluoft/page/375/mode/1up The Scots Peera
2 CONC ge IV: pp. 376-8] He was born on Saturday 19 July 1606, at Hamilton Palace in Hamilton, Lanarkshire, Scotland. [Captain Luckless. James, First Duke of Hamilton, 1606-1649. By Hilary L. Rubinstein (Scottish Academic Press, Edinburg
2 CONC h and London, 1975), p. 6]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''16 May 1638: Archibald Campbell [of Glencarradale], Culros, to the laird of Glenurchy. Sends a letter, the outside written and signed by the Treasurer, and the inside by Lord Lorne; expects them both in Edinburgh either that ni
2 CONC ght or the next day; the Treasurer has cleared himself of accusations against him; the bishop of St Andrews is gone to Bath and "the rest of that crew are cuming downe I hoipe in god with small contentment," the Marquess of Hamilt
2 CONC on was expected in Edinburgh but he may be delayed by the death of his lady [Margaret Fielding, died 10 May 1638]; James Prymrois has a daughter about to be married - he asks for some venison and wildfowl for the marriage; burial 
2 CONC of the laird of Alwas, whose death is much regretted by all honest men. [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD112%2f39%2f66%2f10&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=margaret+fielding&ko=p&r=&ro=s&d
2 CONC f=&dt=&di=y National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Campbell Family, Earls of Breadalbane (Breadalbane Muniments), reference GD112/39/66/10]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Genealogy ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun04pauluoft/page/375/mode/1up The Scots Peerage IV: pp. 376-8]
2 CONT # '''[https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/hh4aa/hamilton09.php Stirnet: Hamilton 09]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Hamilton,_1st_Duke_of_Hamilton Biographical Summary by Wikipedia] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT General Sir James Hamilton, 1st Duke of Hamilton (19 June 1606 – 9 March 1649) was a Scottish nobleman and influential Civil war military leader.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of James Hamilton, 2nd Marquess of Hamilton, and of the Lady Ann Cunningham, daughter of James Cunningham, 7th Earl of Glencairn, was born on 19 June 1606 at Hamilton Palace, Lanarkshire. Following the death of his insane 
2 CONC great-uncle James Hamilton, 3rd Earl of Arran in 1609, the infant was styled Earl of Arran Heir to the Throne of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT The young James' near ancestor was the Princess Mary, daughter to James II of Scotland and Mary of Gueldres. Failing the House of Stewart, as looked increasingly possible after the death of Henry Frederick, Prince of Wales in 1612
2 CONC , the young Earl of Arran would become Heir presumptive to the throne of Scotland, should anything have happened to the young Charles, Duke of Rothesay. Education
2 CONT
2 CONT James VI's first visit to Scotland since the Union of the Crowns occurred in early 1617, whilst in Scotland, he was apparently charmed by the Marquess, and invited him to court in London. The Marquess duly arrived in London in Aug
2 CONC ust of that year, with his eleven year old son. Although like most Noblemen's sons of the time he had a private tutor, James Bale, Arran's time spent at court in the ensuing years did not consist of much formal education. To remed
2 CONC y this he was sent in 1621, at the age of fifteen to Exeter College, Oxford. Arran was not a natural scholar and was back at court within six months of entering the college.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT The Marquess meanwhile had been intriguing with George Villiers, 1st Duke of Buckingham. Like all ambitious upstarts at court, Buckingham was keen to consolidate his new found fortunes by allying himself and his family with establ
2 CONC ished and wealthy families. Buckingham proposed to wed Arran to his niece Mary, daughter to William, Viscount Feilding, an undistinguished Warwickshire Squire. Hamilton, despite his misgivings regarding Buckingham's lowly origins 
2 CONC was impressed enough by his influence with the King, to accept his suggestion. On 16 June 1622 the fifteen year old Arran married 9 year old Mary Feilding in the presence of the King. Arran was not consulted and later came to bitt
2 CONC erly resent it.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1623 Arran was made Gentleman of the Bedchamber to the Prince of Wales , and the following year was made Lord High Steward of the Royal Household.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marquess of Hamilton
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1625 the 2nd Marquess died at Whitehall of a seizure. His death was blamed on fever, although the speed of his death and his age, thirty-six, made many suspect poison. King James died three weeks later. The new Marquess receive
2 CONC d all his father's titles, and also the same annuity his father had received from the court of £2500 sterling. At the coronation of King Charles I, Hamilton bore the Sword of State at Westminster Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1631 Hamilton took over a force of 6,000 men to assist Gustavus Adolphus in the Thirty Years' War in Germany. He guarded the fortresses on the Oder while Gustavus fought Tilly at the Battle of Breitenfeld, and afterwards occupi
2 CONC ed Magdeburg, but his army was destroyed by disease and starvation, and after the complete failure of the expedition Hamilton returned to England in September 1634.
2 CONT
2 CONT He now became Charles I's chief adviser in Scottish affairs. In May 1638, after the outbreak of the revolt against the new Prayer-Book, he was appointed commissioner for Scotland to appease the discontents. He described the Scots 
2 CONC as being "possessed by the devil", and instead of doing his utmost to support the king's interests was easily intimidated by the covenanting leaders and persuaded of the impossibility of resisting their demands, finally returning 
2 CONC to Charles to urge him to give way. It is said that he so far forgot his trust as to encourage the Scottish leaders in their resistance in order to gain their favour.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 27 July 1638 Charles sent Hamilton back to Scotland with new proposals for the election of an assembly and a parliament, episcopacy being safeguarded but bishops being made responsible to future assemblies. After a wrangle conc
2 CONC erning the mode of election he again returned to Charles. Having been sent back to Edinburgh on 17 September 1638, he brought with him a revocation of the prayer-book and canons and another covenant to be substituted for the natio
2 CONC nal covenant. On 21 November 1638 Hamilton presided over the first meeting of the assembly in Glasgow cathedral, but dissolved it on 28 November 1638 on its declaring the bishops responsible to its authority. The assembly, however
2 CONC , continued to sit notwithstanding, and Hamilton returned to England to give an account of his failure, leaving the enemy triumphant and in possession.
2 CONT
2 CONT War was now decided upon, and Hamilton was chosen to command an expedition to the Forth to menace the rear of the Covenanters. On arrival on 1 May 1639 he found the plan impossible, despaired of success, and was recalled in June. 
2 CONC On 8 July 1639, after a hostile reception at Edinburgh, he resigned his commissionership. He supported Thomas Wentworth, Earl of Strafford's proposal to call the Short Parliament, but otherwise opposed him as strongly as he could,
2 CONC  as the, chief adversary of the Scots; and he aided Henry Vane the Elder, it was believed, in accomplishing Strafford's destruction by sending for him to the Long Parliament.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton now supported the parliamentary party, desired an alliance with his nation, and pers­ed Charles in February 1641 to admit some of their leaders into the council. On the death of Strafford (12 May 1641) Hamilton was confro
2 CONC nted by a new antagonist in James Graham, 5th Earl of Montrose, who detested both his character and policy and repudiated his supremacy in Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 10 August 1641 Hamilton accompanied Charles on his last visit to Scotland. His aim now was to effect an alliance between the king and Archibald Campbell, 8th Earl of Argyll, the former accepting Presbyterianism and receiving th
2 CONC e help of the Scots against the English parliament, and when this failed he abandoned Charles and adhered to Argyll. In consequence he received a challenge from Lord Ker, of which he gave the king information, and obtained from Ke
2 CONC r an apology. Montrose wrote to Charles declaring he could prove Hamilton to be a traitor. The king himself spoke of him as being "very active in his own preservation".
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly afterwards the plot—known as the "Incident"—to seize Argyll, Hamilton and the latter's brother, William Hamilton, Earl of Lanark, was discovered, and on 12 October 1641 they fled from Edinburgh. Hamilton returned not long 
2 CONC afterwards, and notwithstanding all that had occurred still retained Charles's favour and confidence. He returned with him to London and accompanied him on 5 January 1642 when he went to the city after the failure to secure the fi
2 CONC ve members.
2 CONT
2 CONT In July 1642 Hamilton went to Scotland on a hopeless mission to prevent the intervention of the Scots in the impending English Civil War, and a breach then took place between him and Argyll.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Duke of Hamilton
2 CONT
2 CONT On 12 April 1643, Hamilton was further ennobled. At Oxford King Charles conferred upon the Marquess, the titles of Duke of Hamilton, Marquess of Clydesdale, Earl of Cambridge, the Baronies of Aven and Innerdale, in addition he als
2 CONC o regranted the Earldom of Arran.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Earlier, in February 1643 proposals of mediation between Charles and the parliament came from Scotland, Hamilton instigated the "cross petition" which demanded from Charles the surrender of the annuities of tithes in order to emba
2 CONC rrass John Campbell, 1st Earl of Loudon, the chief promoter of the project, to whom they had already been granted. This failing, he promoted a scheme for overwhelming the influence and votes of Argyll and his party by sending to S
2 CONC cotland all the Scottish peers then with the king, thereby preventing any assistance to the parliament coming from that quarter, while Charles was to guarantee the establishment of Presbyterianism in Scotland only. This foolish in
2 CONC trigue was strongly opposed by Montrose, who was eager to strike a sudden blow and anticipate and annihilate the plans of the Covenanters. Hamilton, however, gained over the queen Henrietta Maria for his project, while Montrose wa
2 CONC s condemned to inaction. Hamilton's scheme, however, completely failed. He had no control over the parliament. He was unable to hinder the meeting of the convention of the estates which assembled without the king's authority, and 
2 CONC his supporters found themselves in a minority.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, on refusing to take the Covenant, Hamilton and Lanark were obliged to leave Scotland. They arrived at Oxford on 16 December 1643. Hamilton's conduct had at last incurred Charles's resentment and he was sent, in January 16
2 CONC 44, a prisoner to Pendennis Castle, in 1645 being removed to St Michael's Mount, where he was liberated by Lord Fairfax's troops on 23 April 1646.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1646 Charles conferred on the Duke the heritable office of Keeper of Holyroodhouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Subsequently he showed great activity in the futile negotiations between the Scots and Charles at Newcastle. In 1648, in consequence of the seizure of Charles by the army in 1647, Hamilton obtained a temporary influence and author
2 CONC ity in the Scottish parliament over Argyll, and led a large force into England in support of the king on 8 July 1648. He showed complete incapacity in military command; was kept in check for some time by Lambert; and though outnum
2 CONC bering the enemy by 24,000 to about 9000 men, allowed his troops to disperse over the country and to be defeated in detail by Cromwell during the three days 17–19 August 1648 at the so-called Battle of Preston, being himself taken
2 CONC  prisoner on 25 August.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was tried on 6 February 1649, condemned to death on 6 March and executed by decapitation on 9 March.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton, during his unfortunate career, had often been suspected of betraying the king's cause, and, as an heir to the Scottish throne, of intentionally laying into the hands of the Covenanters with a view of procuring the crown 
2 CONC for himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT The charge was brought against him as early as 1631 when he was levying men in Scotland for the German expedition, but Charles gave no credence to it and showed his trust in Hamilton by causing him to share his own room. The charg
2 CONC e, however, always clung to him, and his intriguing character and hopeless management of the king's affairs in Scotland gave colour to the accusation. There seems, however, to be no real foundation for it. His career is sufficient
2 CONC ly explained by his thoroughly weak and egotistical character. He took no interest whatever in the great questions at issue, was neither loyal nor patriotic, and only desired peace and compromise to avoid personal losses. "He was 
2 CONC devoid of intellectual or moral strength, and was therefore easily brought to fancy all future tasks easy and all present obstacles insuperable". A worse choice than Hamilton could not possibly have been made in such a crisis, and
2 CONC  his want of principle, of firmness and resolution, brought irretrievable ruin upon the royal cause.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue and Succession
2 CONT
2 CONT By his wife Mary Feilding, Hamilton had six children, of whom four died in childhood.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Henrietta Mary (1631–1632)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Anne (1632–1716), later suo jure Duchess of Hamilton
2 CONT
2 CONT * Susannah (1633–1694), married John Kennedy, 7th Earl of Cassilis in 1688
2 CONT
2 CONT * Charles, Earl of Arran (1634–1640)
2 CONT
2 CONT * James (1635–1639)
2 CONT
2 CONT * William (1636–1638)
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the death of his three sons, the dukedom passed by special remainder to his brother William Hamilton, Earl of Lanark. On the latter's death at Worcester in 1651 the Scottish titles reverted to the 1st duke's eldest daugh
2 CONC ter, Anne. She married William Douglas, Earl of Selkirk, who was created Duke of Hamilton for life.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. ^ Scots' Peerage Vol IV pp. 375–6
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. ^ Scot's Peerage Vol IV p.377
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT * Balfour Paul, Sir James, The Scots Peerage IX Vols. Edinburgh 1907
2 CONT
2 CONT * S. R. Gardiner, Dictionary of National Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT * S. R. Gardiner, History of England and of the Civil War
2 CONT
2 CONT * H.L. Rubenstein, Captain Luckless-James, First Duke of Hamilton, 1606–1649. Scottish Academic Press, Edinburgh 1975
2 CONT
2 CONT * Gilbert Burnet, Memoirs of the Dukes of Hamilton
2 CONT
2 CONT * Lauderdale Papers, Camden Society, 1884–1885)
2 CONT
2 CONT * The Hamilton Papers, ed. by S. R. Gardiner (Camden Society, 1880) and addenda (Camden Miscellany, vol. ix., 1895)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Thomason Tracts: British Museum, 550 (6), 1948 (30) (account of his supposed treachery), and 546 (21) (speech on the scaffold).
2 CONT
2 CONT *  This article incorporates text from a publication now in the public domain: Chisholm, Hugh, ed (1911). Encyclopædia Britannica (Eleventh ed.). Cambridge University Press.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hamilton, 1st Duke of Hamilton KG PC, was a Scottish nobleman and influential political and military leader during the Thirty Years' War and the Wars of the Three Kingdoms.
2 CONT
2 CONT James was born in 1606 at Hamilton Palace in Lanarkshire, the son of James, Marquis of Hamilton, and the Lady Ann Cunningham, daughter of James, Earl of Glencairn. Following the death of his insane great-uncle James, Earl of Arran
2 CONC  in 1609, the infant was styled Earl of Arran.
2 CONT
2 CONT First Duke of Hamilton Scotland UK  3rd Marquess of Hamilton, Scottish General premier peer of the realm
2 CONT
2 CONT Susan Villiers
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.wikiwand.com/en/James_Hamilton,_1st_Duke_of_Hamilton military leader during the Thirty Years' War and the Wars of the Three Kingdoms.
2 CONT
2 CONT Young Arran James was born in 1606 at Hamilton Palace in Lanarkshire, the son of James, Marquis of Hamilton, and the Lady Ann Cunningham, daughter of James, Earl of Glencairn. Following the death of his insane great-uncle James, E
2 CONC arl of Arran in 1609, the infant was styled Earl of Arran.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Heir to the Throne of Scotland The young Earl of Arran's close ancestor was the Princess Mary, daughter to James II of Scotland and Mary of Gueldres. After the death in 1612 of Henry Frederick, Prince of Wales, James became third 
2 CONC in line to the throne of Scotland, after Charles, Duke of Rothesay, and his sister Elizabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Education James VI's first visit to Scotland since the Union of the Crowns occurred in early 1617, whilst in Scotland, he was apparently charmed by the Marquis, and invited him to court in London. The Marquis duly arrived in Londo
2 CONC n in August of that year, with his eleven-year-old son. Although like most Noblemen's sons of the time he had a private tutor, James Bale, Arran's time spent at court in the ensuing years did not consist of much formal education.[
2 CONC citation needed] To remedy this, Arran was sent to Exeter College, Oxford; he matriculated on 14 December 1621.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Art collection The Duke was interested in art from a young age and collected Venetian paintings through his agent Viscount Basil Feilding.[3] An inventory of his collection was made sometime after Charles I's retreat from London i
2 CONC n November 1642 and before 12 April 1643, which included 600 entries, of which half were Venetian paintings from the collections of Bartolomeo della Nave and others.[3] A good portion of this collection later came into the hands o
2 CONC f the Archduke Leopold Wilhelm in Brussels and forms the core of the Kunsthistorisches Museum today.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage The Marquis meanwhile had been intriguing with George, Duke of Buckingham. Like all ambitious upstarts at court, Buckingham was keen to consolidate his new-found fortunes by allying himself and his family with established
2 CONC  and wealthy families. Buckingham proposed to wed Arran to his niece Mary, daughter to William, Viscount Feilding, an undistinguished Warwickshire Squire.[citation needed] Hamilton, despite his misgivings regarding Buckingham's lo
2 CONC wly origins was impressed enough by his influence with the King, to accept his suggestion.[citation needed] On 16 June 1622 the fifteen-year-old Arran married 9-year old Mary Feilding in the presence of the King.[citation needed] 
2 CONC Arran was not consulted and later came to bitterly resent it.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marquis of Hamilton Portrait by Daniël Mijtens of James Hamilton, 1st Duke of Hamilton in 1623 (age 17) Portrait by Daniël Mijtens of James Hamilton, 1st Duke of Hamilton in 1623 (age 17) In 1625 the 2nd Marquis died[2] at Whiteha
2 CONC ll of a seizure. His death was blamed on fever, although the speed of his death and his age, thirty-six, made many suspect poison.[citation needed] King James died three weeks later. The new Marquis received all his father's title
2 CONC s, and also the same annuity his father had received from the court of £2500 sterling. At the coronation of King Charles I, Hamilton bore the Sword of State at Westminster Abbey.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1628, Hamilton, was made a Gentleman of the Bedchamber, Knight of the Garter, a privy councillor in both England and Scotland, and in the same year was made Master of the Horse, a post he stayed in until 1644.:[5] He represente
2 CONC d the King of Bohemia at the baptism of the infant Prince Charles.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton's army in Germany In 1631 Hamilton took over an army to assist Gustavus Adolphus in the Thirty Years' War in Germany.[7] He raised these based on warrants to levy 6,000 men in England and a further 6,000 in Scotland. Ther
2 CONC e has been much historical debate as to how many men landed initially, how many served in total and how effective these were. What is now known is that the initial contingent of 8,000 landed in Germany and other regiments, such as
2 CONC  those of Sir Frederick Hamilton and Alexander Lord Forbes which were raised on warrants designed for the Marquis, actually arrived in Germany but served in the army group of Swedish commander Åke Tott.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Having no military training, Hamilton was assigned Major General Alexander Leslie as his mentor and other Scottish officers after Leslie was wounded. The command structure of the Hamilton Army was largely Scottish and was drawn fr
2 CONC om a mix of existing Scottish commanders in Swedish service. Major General Alexander Leslie and 'Dear Sandy' Alexander Hamilton (General of Artillery) were to be supported by Lieutenant General Archibald Douglas, also of the artil
2 CONC lery. Although collectively these men believed they served in the "Scots Army", Marquis Hamilton actually took the title of "General of British" from the existing commander with that title, General James Spens who confusingly was 
2 CONC compensated with the title "General of Scots" thereafter.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite being under-resourced, Hamilton's forces did greater service than they are usually given credit for. Under-clothed, and lightly armed, they lost many men to disease early on, but those who survived did very well. They guar
2 CONC ded the river Oder while Gustavus Adolphus fought Tilly at the Battle of Breitenfeld. Thereafter Hamilton's army linked up with that of the Swedish field marshal, Johan Banér, and the combined force attacked Magdeburg. To Hamilton
2 CONC 's chagrin, Banér wished to retire from the siege due to the cold and his response shows that even though not an experienced soldier, Hamilton was not without courage and made his feelings on the subject abundantly clear to Banér:
2 CONT
2 CONT your propositioun of the retiring of my trups in respeckt of the could is so preiuditial to me, both in regard of his Majesties strik commands (which was to dou my best for the attackting of Madeburg) and my oune reputatioun as I 
2 CONC had rader lous my lyf as faill in the performans of the woon, or in the leaist degri hazard the lousing of the other, and beliue me, so long as there is wone man before this toun (unless the King Majestie countermanding) I ame ful
2 CONC ly resolued not to budge from itt, being both myself, offisars and remnant soiours most willing and I hoope as abill to indoure whatsoeuer extremati of could or whatt eals may befall, for the aduansment of his Majesties saruis[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Thereafter the two armies split. Never being allowed to unite all the regiments he had raised into one single force, many of his regiments continued to fight in other theatres of the Thirty Years' War. Moreover, having been caught
2 CONC  up in a serious dispute with the Swedish king, the marquis and many of his officers were discharged from service on 22 October 1632. He returned to Britain taking with him Sir James Hamilton of Priestfield, Colonel James Ramsay '
2 CONC the fair' and Colonel John Hamilton along with a cohort of Scots.[11] Numerous others of the Hamilton army remained in Swedish service, not least generals Alexander Hamilton and Alexander Leslie, but also numerous of the lesser of
2 CONC ficers and the surviving common soldiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT RBP
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton and Scotland On his return to Britain, Hamilton became Charles I's chief adviser in Scottish affairs. In May 1638, after the outbreak of the revolt against the new Prayer-Book, he was appointed commissioner for Scotland t
2 CONC o appease the discontents. He described the Covenanters as being "possessed by the devil",[2] and instead of doing his utmost to support the king's interests is said to have been easily intimidated by the covenanting leaders. Neve
2 CONC rtheless, on 27 July 1638 Charles sent Hamilton back to Scotland with new proposals for the election of an assembly and a parliament, episcopacy being safeguarded but bishops being made responsible to future assemblies. After a wr
2 CONC angle concerning the mode of election he again returned to Charles. Having been sent back to Edinburgh on 17 September 1638, he brought with him a revocation of the prayer-book and canons and another covenant to be substituted for
2 CONC  the national covenant. On 21 November 1638 Hamilton presided over the first meeting of the assembly in Glasgow cathedral, but dissolved it on 28 November 1638 on its declaring the bishops responsible to its authority. The assembl
2 CONC y, however, continued to sit notwithstanding, and Hamilton returned to England to give an account of his failure, leaving the enemy triumphant and in possession.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT War was now decided upon, and Hamilton was chosen to command an expedition to the Forth to menace the rear of the Covenanters. However, he now faced his former military mentor Alexander Leslie, and the artillery specialist, Alexan
2 CONC der Hamilton. Realising the strength of hs opposition when he arrived in the Forth on 1 May 1639 he found Royalists had seriously underestimated their opposition. This included his own mother, Marchioness Anne Hamilton who served 
2 CONC as a colonel in the Army of the Covenant and is alleged to have threatened to shoot her son dead if he landed his forces in Scotland.[12] Whatever prompted his hesitance, the proposed plan to land proved impossible and Hamilton wa
2 CONC s recalled in June. On 8 July 1639, after a hostile reception at Edinburgh, he resigned his commissionership. He supported Thomas Wentworth, Earl of Strafford's proposal to call the Short Parliament, but otherwise opposed him as s
2 CONC trongly as he could, as the chief adversary of the Scots; and he aided Henry Vane the Elder, it was believed, in accomplishing Strafford's destruction by sending for him to the Long Parliament.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton now supported the parliamentary party, desired an alliance with his nation, and persuaded Charles in February 1641 to admit some of their leaders into the council. On the death of Strafford (12 May 1641) Hamilton was conf
2 CONC ronted by a new antagonist in James Graham, 5th Earl of Montrose, who detested both his character and policy and repudiated his supremacy in Scotland.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 10 August 1641 Hamilton accompanied Charles on his last visit to Scotland. His aim now was to effect an alliance between the king and Archibald Campbell, 8th Earl of Argyll, the former accepting Presbyterianism and receiving th
2 CONC e help of the Scots against the English parliament, and when this failed he abandoned Charles and adhered to Argyll. In consequence he received a challenge from Lord Ker, of which he gave the king information, and obtained from Ke
2 CONC r an apology. Montrose wrote to Charles declaring he could prove Hamilton to be a traitor. The king himself spoke of him as being "very active in his own preservation".[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly afterwards the plot—known as "The Incident" — to seize Argyll, Hamilton and the latter's brother, William Hamilton, Earl of Lanark, was discovered, and on 12 October 1641 they fled from Edinburgh. Hamilton returned not lon
2 CONC g afterwards, and notwithstanding all that had occurred still retained Charles's favour and confidence. He returned with him to London and accompanied him on 5 January 1642 when he went to the city after the failure to secure the 
2 CONC five members.[2] In July that year Hamilton went to Scotland on a hopeless mission to prevent the intervention of the Scots in the impending English Civil War, and a breach then took place between him and Argyll.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke of Hamilton Arms of James, Duke of Hamilton Arms of James, Duke of Hamilton On 12 April 1643, Hamilton was further ennobled. At Oxford King Charles conferred upon the Marquis the titles of Duke of Hamilton, Marquis of Clydesd
2 CONC ale, Earl of Cambridge, the Baronies of Aven and Innerdale, in addition he also regranted the Earldom of Arran.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT Earlier, in February 1643 proposals of mediation between Charles and the parliament came from Scotland, Hamilton instigated the "cross petition" which demanded from Charles the surrender of the annuities of tithes in order to emba
2 CONC rrass John Campbell, 1st Earl of Loudoun, the chief promoter of the project, to whom they had already been granted. This failing, he promoted a scheme for overwhelming the influence and votes of Argyll and his party by sending to 
2 CONC Scotland all the Scottish peers then with the king, thereby preventing any assistance to the parliament coming from that quarter, while Charles was to guarantee the establishment of Presbyterianism in Scotland only. This foolish i
2 CONC ntrigue was strongly opposed by Montrose, who was eager to strike a sudden blow and anticipate and annihilate the plans of the Covenanters. Hamilton, however, gained over the queen Henrietta Maria for his project, while Montrose w
2 CONC as condemned to inaction. Hamilton's scheme, however, completely failed. He had no control over the parliament. He was unable to hinder the meeting of the convention of the estates which assembled without the king's authority, and
2 CONC  his supporters found themselves in a minority.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, on refusing to take the Covenant, Hamilton and Lanark were obliged to leave Scotland. They arrived at Oxford on 16 December 1643. Hamilton's conduct had at last incurred Charles's resentment and he was imprisoned in the h
2 CONC ouse of a baker called Daniells, with only two servants and not allowed to leave the house. He was sent, in January 1644, a prisoner to Pendennis Castle, in 1645 being removed to St Michael's Mount, where he was liberated by Lord 
2 CONC Fairfax's troops on 23 April 1646.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1646 Charles conferred on the Duke the heritable office of Keeper of Holyroodhouse.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT Subsequently he showed great activity in the futile negotiations between the Scots and Charles at Newcastle. In 1648, in consequence of the seizure of Charles by the army in 1647, Hamilton obtained a temporary influence and author
2 CONC ity in the Scottish parliament over Argyll, and led a large force into England in support of the king on 8 July 1648. He showed complete incapacity in military command; was kept in check for some time by John Lambert; and though o
2 CONC utnumbering the enemy by 24,000 to about 9000 men, allowed his troops to disperse over the country and to be defeated in detail by Oliver Cromwell during the three days 17–19 August 1648 at the Battle of Preston, being himself tak
2 CONC en prisoner on 25 August. He was tried on 6 February 1649, condemned to death on 6 March and executed by decapitation on 9 March.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Character assessment This section possibly contains original research. Please improve it by verifying the claims made and adding inline citations. Statements consisting only of original research should be removed. (July 2015) (Lea
2 CONC rn how and when to remove this template message) Hamilton, during his unfortunate career, had often been suspected of betraying the king's cause, and, as an heir to the Scottish throne, of intentionally laying into the hands of th
2 CONC e Covenanters with a view of procuring the crown for himself.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The charge was brought against him as early as 1631 when he was levying men in Scotland for the German expedition, but Charles gave no credence to it and showed his trust in Hamilton by causing him to share his own room. The charg
2 CONC e, however, always clung to him, and his intriguing character and hopeless management of the king's affairs in Scotland gave colour to the accusation. There seems, however, to be no real foundation for it. His career is sufficient
2 CONC ly explained by his thoroughly egotistical character. Usually Hamilton is thought to have taken no interest whatever in the great questions at issue, and was neither loyal nor patriotic, and only desired peace and compromise to av
2 CONC oid personal losses.[15] It has been said that "He was devoid of intellectual or moral strength, and was therefore easily brought to fancy all future tasks easy and all present obstacles insuperable".[16] His resolve and dispositi
2 CONC on in Germany to serve Charles I and to face up to the egotist Gustavus Adolphus gives us a different insight. Although far from the most experienced commander, Hamilton was not without courage and resolve. His actions in Germany 
2 CONC and at Preston demonstrate that.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00029421&tree=LEO Biographical Summary by Genealogics]
2 CONT
2 CONT  supported both Royalist and Presbyterian causes
2 CONT
2 CONT By his wife Mary Feilding, Hamilton had six children, of whom four died in childhood.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Henrietta Mary (1631–1632)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Anne (1632–1716), later suo jure Duchess of Hamilton
2 CONT
2 CONT * Susannah (1633–1694), married John Kennedy, 7th      Earl of Cassilis in 1688
2 CONT
2 CONT * Charles, Earl of Arran (1634–1640)
2 CONT
2 CONT * James (1635–1639)
2 CONT
2 CONT * William (1636–1638)
2 CONT
