1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == WILLIAM HAMILTON second Duke of Hamilton ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Death and Burial ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William Hamilton, second Duke of Hamilton, died at the commandery in Worcester on 12 September 1651. He died from wounds received nine days earlier at the Battle of Worcester in England. [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Ha
2 CONC milton,_2nd_Duke_of_Hamilton Wikipedia] He was buried in Worcester Cathedral. [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=6897&ref=wvr Find A Grave Memorial] According to the Scots Peerage: "At Worcester, he charged wit
2 CONC h his regiment and fought bravely, but was severely wounded, his leg being crushed and broken by a shot, and he gradually sank and died on the 12 September 1651, nine days after the battle. It is said his life might have been save
2 CONC d had his leg been amputated at once, but the operation was delayed till it was too late. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun04pauluoft/page/379/mode/1up The Scots Peerage IV: 379]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Hamilton,_2nd_Duke_of_Hamilton Biographical Summary by Wikipedia] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT William Hamilton, 2nd Duke of Hamilton KG (14 December 1616 – 12 September 1651) was a Scottish nobleman who supported both Royalist and Presbyterian causes during the Wars of the Three Kingdoms.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton was born at Hamilton Palace in December 1616, the younger son of James Hamilton, 2nd Marquess of Hamilton and Lady Ann Cunningham. Hamilton was educated at the University of Glasgow, and from there travelled to The Contin
2 CONC ent, where he spent time at the court of Louis XIII of France, on his return aged 21 he established himself as a favourite at the court of Charles I in London.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created Earl of Lanark, Lord Machanshyre and Polmont in the Peerage of Scotland in 1639, and in April 1640 was elected Member of Parliament for Portsmouth in the House of Commons of England for the Short Parliament. He beca
2 CONC me Secretary of State for Scotland. In 1643, he was arrested at Oxford on the orders of King Charles I for "concurrence" with his brother the Duke of Hamilton. He escaped and was temporarily reconciled with the Presbyterian party.
2 CONT
2 CONT After taking part in the Battle of Kilsyth on the covenanter side, he was sent by the Scottish Estates of the Realm to treat with Charles I at Newcastle in 1646, when he sought in vain to persuade the king to consent to the establ
2 CONC ishment of Presbyterianism in England. On 26 September 1647 he signed, on behalf of the Scots, the treaty with Charles known as the "Engagement", at Carisbrooke Castle, and helped to organise the Second English Civil War.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1648 he fled to Holland to the court in exile of the Prince of Wales at The Hague. The following year he succeeded to the Dukedom of Hamilton, the Marquisate of Hamilton, the Earldoms of Arran and Cambridge and Lordhips of Aven
2 CONC  and Innerdale following his brother's execution, making him the most senior figure among the Scots Royalist exiles. In 1650, Hamilton was conferred with the insignia of the Order of the Garter. He returned to Scotland with King C
2 CONC harles II in 1650, but, finding a reconciliation with the Marquess of Argyll impossible, he refused to prejudice Charles’s cause by pushing his claims.
2 CONT
2 CONT He retired to his estates on the Isle of Arran until the Scottish invasion of England during the Third English Civil War, when he acted as colonel of a regiment drawn mainly from his tenantry.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died from the effects of wounds received at the Battle of Worcester, at The Commandery, Charles II's headquarters in that city. A neighbouring street, Hamilton Road, is named in his honour.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton married Lady Elizabeth Maxwell, daughter to James Maxwell, 1st Earl of Dirletoun on 26 May 1638, and had issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT  James Hamilton, Lord Polmont (died in infancy, buried at Westminster Abbey)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Lady Anne Hamilton, married Robert Carnegie, 3rd Earl of Southesk
2 CONT
2 CONT  Lady Elizabeth Hamilton, married 1st Lord Kilmaurs, 2nd Sir David Cunningham of Robertland
2 CONT
2 CONT  Lady Mary Hamilton, married 1st Alexander Livingston, 2nd Earl of Callendar, 2nd Sir James Livingstone of Westquarter, 3rd James Ogilvy, 3rd Earl of Findlater
2 CONT
2 CONT  Lady Margaret Hamilton, married William Blair of that ilk.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Lady Diana Hamilton, (died in infancy)
2 CONT
2 CONT Leaving four daughters but no male heirs, according to the remainder, the dukedom of Hamilton devolved on his eldest surviving niece, Anne, who became Duchess of Hamilton in her own right.
1 FAMC @F6000000010120670641@
1 FAMS @F6000000016410885639@
