1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From [Records of the Sheriff Court of Aberdeenshire,] Issue 31 (Google eBook) Aberdeenshire (Scotland). Sheriff Court, Jean E. Kennedy New Spalding Club, 1906 - Aberdeenshire (Scotland).  Page 28
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notable Persons==
2 CONT
2 CONT It was in the writer's mind to embrace in this Introduction some references to the names of the numerous persons of note, who flit across the pages of the Court Records; but for various reasons he has resolved to leave over everyt
2 CONC hing of that kind, with one exception, to the third or Index Volume. The exception is that of Dr. Arthur Johnston the poet, whose name occurs, and whose affairs, movements, and family are dealt with, in a group of entries reproduc
2 CONC ed on pp. 299, 300 and 301 infra. It may be remembered that according to Sir William Geddes' Memoir,1 Dr. Johnston had returned to Aberdeen from the Continent previous to August, 1622, because on the 5th of that month he had been 
2 CONC enrolled as a burgess. Farther proof has been found in the entries just referred to, from the first of which, under date 12 June, 1622, it appears that, before that date, he (1 Musa Latina Aberdonensis, Vol. II., p. xxx.) had boug
2 CONC ht a house—no doubt a residence—on the East side of the Gallowgate of Aberdeen. This house had belonged to one John Burnet, a burgess, then deceased, whose widow, Issobell Burnet, had apparently the liferent of it. With her consen
2 CONC t, however, it had been sold, and so on the date mentioned she appeared in Court to testify to that effect Her renunciation— and no doubt the clause was identical with the Disposition which had been granted—was "in favores of Mr A
2 CONC rthur Jhonstoune Doctor of Physick, and '''Marie Cagnoncle his spous''' now heritable proprietars of the samen." 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is probable that the information, which was obtained from the Registry of Births at Sedan, in which the lady's maiden name was given as '''de Cagniole''', was correct and final. At the same time it cannot be readily assumed tha
2 CONC t a scholarly man like Dr. Johnston would furnish a garbled spelling of his wife's name, to be used in such a serious matter as a conveyance of heritage to him and her, nor does it seem to the writer that it is to be taken for gra
2 CONC nted too easily, that the Clerk of Court made a mistake in copying from the conveyance. Be this as it may, the record in this volume fixes the poet as having taken up his residence in Aberdeen not later probably than Whitsunday, 1
2 CONC 622. This, as has been said, is quite in accordance with the conclusion arrived at by Sir William Geddes. There is a statement, however, by Abbe Bouillot,1 to the effect that Johnston remained on the Continent until 1623, and the 
2 CONC entry which is now to be referred to may quite well be interpreted as bearing out the accuracy of the conclusions of both the late learned Principal and oft the Abbe Bouillot. So soon after his return as 28 August, 1622, Dr. Johns
2 CONC ton appeared in Court, "being passing out of this realme and uncertaine of his returning," and nominated curators ad lites et negotia to his children, who are named. It is not likely that a mere visit to England would have induced
2 CONC  (1 Musa Latino, II., p. xz.) Johnston to take this step. His action rather points to this, that after settling his wife and family in Aberdeen, he returned to the Continent to wind up his concerns there, and did not finally quit 
2 CONC Sedan until 1623, as the Abbe has it.  
2 CONT
2 CONT What was the cause of his leaving Scotland does not appear from the Sheriff Court records; but the necessity laid on him for appointing curators to his children does. The house he had bought had belonged to the deceased John Burne
2 CONC t, and when it was sold, the price, after making provision for the Widow's right of liferent, would doubtless go to relatives of the deceased owner. Two of these at least had creditors, who were not very sure of being paid, so the
2 CONC se arrested in Dr. Johnston's hands the price payable to their debtors, Burnet's heirs. At a somewhat later date there is evidence that one of the arrestments was withdrawn, but it is unnecessary to allude to this matter farther t
2 CONC han to indicate, that these complications rendered it desirable for Dr. Johnston to take the somewhat unusual course which he did, of appointing curators to his children in his lifetime.
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
