1 NAME Alexander /Fraser/
2 GIVN Alexander
2 SURN Fraser
2 _MARNM Fraser
1 NAME Alexander Fraser, 10th Philorth,11th Lord Saltoun of Abernethy
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 4 MAR 1604
2 PLAC Water of Philorth
2 ADDR
3 CITY Fraserburgh
3 STAE Scotland
3 POST AB43
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 DEAT
2 DATE 11 AUG 1693
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>Alexander Fraser, tenth of Philorth, and first Lord Saltoun of the Fraser line</strong>, was born in the year 1604, and matriculated at the King's College, Aberdeen, in 1619. The family properties, which had, during his g
2 CONC randfather's lifetime, become seriously embarrassed, had been put under trust by various deeds of date from 1608 to 1616, by Sir Alexander Fraser, his grandfather, the trustees being Simon, Lord Lovat, George Ogilvy of Carnousie, 
2 CONC and William Forbes of Tolquhoun, for the purpose of selling such parts of the property as might be necessary for the payment of his debts and for infefting his eldest grandson in the remainder. Further, in 1620 Sir Alexander had r
2 CONC esigned the lands and barony of Philorth into the hands of the Lords Commissioners for new infeftment, to be granted to the eldest son of his heir apparent, Alexander Fraser, by Margaret Abernethy; so that Alexander, tenth of Phil
2 CONC orth, succeeded to Philorth during his father's lifetime. Lord Lovat and his co-trustees resigned their trust into the hands of the Royal Commissioners, who gave the estates over to Alexander Fraser, who had a royal charter of the
2 CONC m 15 March 1628. Alexander Fraser took an active part in the troubles which arose in the country during his lifetime ; he subscribed the Solemn League and Covenant at Aberdeen in 1638 ; was a member of the General Assembly at Glas
2 CONC gow in 1639, and in the same year served in the Army commanded by the Earl of Montrose, and led a contingent of two hundred men against the castles of Kellie and Gight. In 1643 he was chosen, together with the Laird of Drum, as Co
2 CONC mmissioner for the county of Aberdeen, to attend the Convention of Estates at Edinburgh ; in 1648 he had command of a regiment in the expedition into England to attempt the rescue of King Charles I. He advanced large sums of money
2 CONC  to King Charles II., joining the royal standard himself, and serving at the battle of Worcester. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1668 Alexander Abernethy, ninth Lord Saltoun, died without issue, and on the decease soon afterwards of his only sister, unmarried, his cousin, the Laird of Philorth, inherited the dignity of Lord Saltoun. He took the oaths and
2 CONC  his seat in the Scots Parliament on 9 August 1670. Lord Mordington protested, but without effect. On 22 August of the same year Parliament passed an Act, embodying a previous ratification made by the King on 11 July, which confir
2 CONC med to Philorth and the heirs of his body the dignity of Lord Abernethy of Saltoun. From this time onwards Lord Saltoun 's life was one long series of litigations and troubles, mainly brought about by the ravages made on the Abern
2 CONC ethy estates by the ninth Lord Saltoun.
2 CONT
2 CONT Saltoun was purchased by Sir Andrew Fletcher, Rothiemay and other lands by the Gordons, and Balvenie, after twenty ruinous years of lawsuits, was adjudged to Arthur Forbes of Echt. Pittullie and Pittendrum were also engulfed in th
2 CONC e whirlpool of debt, and finally, some years before his death, Lord Saltoun made over all his remaining possessions to his grandson William, afterwards eleventh Lord Saltoun. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1693 Lord Saltoun, who had reserved to himself rooms in the House of Philorth and the Castle on Kinnaird head, as well as a lodging in Fraserburgh, where he was maintained in great comfort by his grandson, went to his lodging o
2 CONC n 10 July and died on 11 August. His death is recorded thus : 'Alexander, Lord Saltoun, came to the lodging on the 10th day of July in the year of God 1693, and departed out of this life the 11th day of August 1693, and was buried
2 CONC  in his own Isle in Fraserburgh the 18th day of the present month. He was of age going in his eightyninth year. He was a man that was given to reading of good books, and very much in the exercise of prayer, both in his closet and 
2 CONC when he had occasion to meet with a minister or churchman of his own profession ; he would alwise desire them to pray before they parted with him. He was very civil and kind to all whom he had the freedom to converse with. He was 
2 CONC also very charitable to the poor, at all occasions, whenever he and they did meet. He was carried to the seatown on the 12th day of August at night. August began that year on Tuesday.'
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Saltoun married, first, a daughter of William Forbes of Tolquhoun, by whom he had one daughter, 
2 CONT * Janet, married to Alexander Fraser of Techmuiry.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, secondly (contract 27 June 1634 3 ) Elizabeth, daughter of Alexander Seton of Meldrum, widow of John Urquhart of Craigfintrie ; by her he had one son, 
2 CONT * Alexander, Master of Saltoun, born about 1630, and educated at King's College, Aberdeen; he matriculated there in 1647. He was a man of weak, but good-natured disposition, who placed too much confidence in the integrity of his f
2 CONC riends, who led him into debt and plundered him without mercy. He died in 1682, during the lifetime of his father. He married, first (contract 11 January 1652), Ann Kerr, daughter of William, third Earl of Lothian, who died 30 Aug
2 CONC ust 1658, leaving two sons."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun07pauluoft#page/440/mode/2up The Scots peerage], Vol. VII, page 441
