1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Aberdeen Register of Burgesses includes guild, trade, and honorary burgesses. On 3 Dec 1635, entries include: Mr William Clog, minister at Inverness; Mr Patrick Guthrie and Andrew Guthrie, sons of John, Bishop 
2 CONC of Moray, and John Murray, servant to John, Bishop of Ross. G. (Guild)
2 CONT
2 CONT The [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wars_of_the_Three_Kingdoms Wars of the Three Kingdoms ]was an intertwined series of conflicts occurring between 1639 and 1653 in the Kingdoms of England, Scotland, and Ireland - separate kingdoms
2 CONC  with the same king, Charles I. During this period, Andrew Guthrie joined forces with royalist, Montrose. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Aberdeen was an engagement in the War of the Three Kingdoms taking place outside the city of Aberdeen on 13 September 1644. Royalist forces, presumably including Andrew Guthrie, were led by James Graham, Lord Montros
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Alford took place on 2 July 1645. The two sets of cavalry were involved in fierce fighting when Montrose released his force of Gordon infantry; with this the Covenanter infantry broke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another battle at Kilsyth, Sterlingshire was fought on 15 Aug 1645.
2 CONT
2 CONT A month later, on 13 September 1645, the Royalist army of the Marquis of Montrose was destroyed by the Covenanter army of Sir David Leslie restoring the power of the Committee of Estates. This was the Battle of Philiphaugh, near S
2 CONC elkirk.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew Guthrie, son of the Bishop of Moray, was one of the prisoners brought to trial. He was beheaded at St Andrews on 20 January 1646. The instrument of death was the "Maiden" which had been brought over from Dundee. (St. Andrew
2 CONC s in 1645-46, D.R.Kerr)
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Robert Blair's biographer, Andrew Guthrie, "Died stupidly and impenitently." 
