1 OCCU Master Mason, Master Gunner
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} MYLNE, ROBERT (1633-1710), mason, eldest, son of Alexander Mylne (1513-1643), [see under Mylne, John (1611-1607)], and of his wife, Anna Yegilman, was born in Edinburgh in 1633. He was apprenticed to his uncle, Joh
2 CONC n Mylne, and succeeded him as principal master-mason to Charles II in 1668. In 1605 he erected Wood's Hospital at Largo (rebuilt in 1830), and in 1608 entered into an agreement with the magistrates of Perth to build a market cross
2 CONC , the old one having been destroyed by Cromwell's army in 1052 (cf. Penny, Traditions ofPerth, p. 15). Mylne's cross, which stood in the High Street, between the Kirkgate and the Skinner Gate, was completed in May 1069. It was tak
2 CONC en down and sold in 1705, when increased traffic rendered it inconvenient. In 1009 Mylne was occupied in reclaiming the foreshore at Leith, where he constructed a sea wall, and on the land thus acquired he in 1685 erected stone dw
2 CONC ellings, which are still in existence; in 1670 he was assisting Sir William Bruce [q. v.] in the designs for Holyrood Palace, the foundation-stone of which was laid 15 July 1671 by Mylne, who directed the erection of the building 
2 CONC till its completion in 1679. Mylne's name and tbe date 1671 are cut on a pillar in the plazza of the quadrangle. Six of his original drawings prepared for the king remained in his family, and are reproduced in Mylne's' Master Maso
2 CONC ns,' p. 168. Leslie House, Fifeshire, which had been commenced by his uncle, was erected under his direction about 1670. It was partially destroyed by fire in 1703. As master-mason or surveyor to the city of Edinburgh Mylne constr
2 CONC ucted cisterns in various parts of the town in connection with the new water supplyfrom Comiston, be tween 1674 and 1681. lie effected one of the first improvements in the old town by the construction of Mylne Square in 1689 (view
2 CONC  in Cassell's Old and New Edinburgh, i. 237), and in the same year assisted in the repair of Edinburgh Castle, one of the bastions being called after him, Mylne's Mount. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At that time he was not only king's master mason, but also hereditary master-gunner of the fortress. On 30 March 1682 he contracted for building a bridge of one arch over the Clyde at Romellweill Crags, now known as Rani's Horn Po
2 CONC ol, Lanarkshire. After the revolution he seems to have been superseded as master-mason by Sir A. Murray of Blackbarony, but was employed on Holyrood Palace in June and July 1089. In November 1708 he was petitioning for twenty year
2 CONC s' ar rears due to him as master-mason. In 1690 he erected Mylne's Court, and about that time completed many buildings in Edinburgh under the new regulation for the erection of stone buildings in lieu of timber in the principal st
2 CONC reets. In March 1693 he entered into a contract to complete the steeple of Heriot's Hospital, which had been begun in 1676. Mylne had been instructed on 3 May 1675 1 to think on a drawing thereof against the next council meeting;'
2 CONC  it is not known whether the work carried out was entirely his own design. He executed the statue of Heriot over the archway within the court, from an original painting. After the great fire in Edinburgh in 1700 Mylne bought many 
2 CONC sites in the town, and on them erected buildings, in which his style may still be traced. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mylne was active in his connection with the masonic lodge of Edinburgh. He was I entered prentice ' to his uncle on 27 Dec. 1053, made fellow craft on 23 Sept. 1660, chosen warden in 1653, re-elected in 1664, and filled the deacon
2 CONC 's chair during 1681 1683 and 1687-8. Till 1707 he took a leading part in the business of the lodge. He was made burgess of Edinburgh on 23 May 1000, and guild brother on 12 April 1005. As magistrate of Edinburgh his signature is 
2 CONC attached to letters to the Duke of Lauderdale and to Charles II, dated 1074 and 1075 {Addit. MSS. 23130 f. 200,23137 f. 72). 
2 CONT
2 CONT He acquired the estate of Balfarge in Fifeshire, and died at his house at Inveresk on 10 Dec. 1710, aged 77. He married, on II April 1661, Elizabeth Meikle, by whom he had a large family. He is commemorated on the monument to his 
2 CONC uncle at Greyfriars. A portrait of him from a picture by Roderick Chalmers is reproduced in Mylne's 1 Master Masons ' (p. 217). 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Mylne (1662-1728), mastermason, son of the above, was born in 1602. He was entered in the lodge of Edinburgh on 27 Dec. 1681, fellow craft on 9 Nov. 1685, and freeman mason on 16 July 1687. He was warden of the lodge in 16
2 CONC 95-7. He settledin Leith,and died 9 March 1728. By his wife Elizabeth Thomson he had several children [see under M.YLNE, Robert, 17341811]. lie also is commemorated on the family monument. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [Diet, of Architecture: Mylne's Master Ma sons, pp. 171-249; Lyon's Hist, of the Lodge of Edinburgh, pp. 93-4 ; Groome's Ordnance Gazetteer of Scotland ; Cant's notes to Adamson's Muses Threnodie, 1774, pp. 1'29, 134135; Builder, 
2 CONC 1866, p. 187 ; Hist, of Holyrood House, pp. 89-94 ; Mankind's Edinburgh, p. 205 ; Steven's Hist, of Heriot's Hospital, pp. 87, 236; Ritchie's Report as to -who was the architect of Heriot's Hospital, pp. 23-4 ; Brown's Inscription
2 CONC s at Greyfriars, p. 249.] B. P.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dictionary of National Biography 1894 VOL. XI. Myllar-Nichols
