1 NAME William /Douglas/ Sr., of Nithsdale
2 GIVN William
2 NSFX Sr., of Nithsdale
2 _MARNM Douglas
2 NICK Lord Galloway
1 NAME William /Pittendale/
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1364
2 ADDR
3 CITY Dumfries
3 CTRY Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1392
2 ADDR
3 CITY Gdańsk
3 STAE Pomeranian Voivodeship
3 CTRY Poland
1 OCCU Scotish Knight, Northern Crusader, nobleman.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_William_Douglas_of_Nithsdale
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Douglas of Nithsdale (c. 1370 – c. 1392) was a Scottish Knight and Northern Crusader.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT William Douglas was an illegitimate son of Archibald the Grim, 3rd Earl of Douglas and an unknown mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT A man of apparently dashing bearing, Douglas was with the Franco-Scots army when it unsuccessfully besieged Carlisle Castle in 1385, the defending Governor being Lord Clifford. He is recorded as there performing feats of valour an
2 CONC d killing many Englishmen. According to Andrew of Wyntoun:
2 CONT
2 CONT "A yhowng joly bachelere 
2 CONT
2 CONT Prysyd gretly wes off were, 
2 CONT
2 CONT For he wes evyr traveland 
2 CONT
2 CONT Qwhille be se and qwhille be land 
2 CONT
2 CONT To skathe his fays rycht besy 
2 CONT
2 CONT Swa that thai dred him grettumly" (Orygynale Cronykil of Scotland ix, c.21) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Douglas certainly had gained his spurs by 1387 when he married the Egidia (or Gelis) Stewart, princess of Scotland, a daughter of King Robert II. According to the Liber Pluscarden, Egidia Stewart's beauty was well renowned. Charle
2 CONC s V of France had "sent a certain most subtle painter to do her portrait and portray her charms, intending to take her to wife." But the King of France and all other of Egidia's admirers had lost out to the chivalric charms of Dou
2 CONC glas. As part of her marriage portion went the lands of Nithsdale in south-western Scotland, Herbertshire in the county of Stirling and an annuity of £300.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ireland
2 CONT
2 CONT Within his first year of marriage the young Nithsdale led a punitive raid against Irish raiders who had been troubling the tenantry of his father's Fiefdom of Galloway. In early summer 1388, with a party of 500 well prepared veter
2 CONC an men-at-arms he sailed into Carlingford Lough, landed outside the town and summoned their leaders. The chief of the townsfolk offered a sum for a temporary truce, to which Nithsdale agreed. Secretly the townsfolk sent off to Dun
2 CONC dalk for reinforcements, with which they were obliged. 800 spearmen from Dundalk surprised the Scots camp by night, and were supported by a sortie from Carlingford town. The Scots, veterans of years of brutal Border warfare, beat 
2 CONC the Irishmen off, captured the town and burnt it, seized the Castle and captured 15 ships in the harbour. Nithsdale and his expeditionary force sailed back into Loch Ryan with enough time to participate in the raiding of Northern 
2 CONC England that was to culminate in the Battle of Otterburn on the 19th of August, in which he fought with distinction.
2 CONT
2 CONT Feuding, Crusading and Death
2 CONT
2 CONT The year after Otterburn a truce was called between Scotland and England. Nithsdale on a knightly quest for glory decided, about 1389, to join the Teutonic Knights, who were fighting the Ottomans and Lithuanians in eastern Europe.
2 CONC  Nithsdale had previously quarrelled with Lord Clifford, a former adversary at Carlisle and whose forebear had claimed Douglasdale under Edward I of England's oppression. While both were abroad, it is alleged that Clifford challen
2 CONC ged Nithsdale to single combat, and that Douglas even went to France to obtain special armour for the fight. Clifford, however, died on August 18, 1391,[1] but Nithsdale is said to have kept their 'tryst', and whilst walking upon 
2 CONC on the bridge leading to the main gate at Danzig was "killed by the English".[2] The burgers of Danzig decided that "upon account of a signal service which the Douglas family did to this city in relieving it in its utmost extremit
2 CONC ies against the Poles, the Scotch were allowed to be free burghers of the town". Subsequently the stone facia of the Hohe Thor (High Gate) was adorned with the coat of arms of this nobleman and for centuries it was commonly referr
2 CONC ed to as the Douglas Port or Douglas Gate, described as such as late as 1734.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT As Nithsdale had drawn most of his rentals from the burgh of Dumfries in 1392 his death is assumed to have occurred that year or shortly afterwards.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT By Princess Egidia, Nithsdale had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Egidia Douglas, known as the "Fair Maid of Nithsdale" married: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Henry Sinclair, 2nd Earl of Orkney (d. 1422) 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Sir Alasdair Stewart (executed 1425) son of Murdoch Stewart, 2nd Duke of Albany 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Douglas, Knt., Lord of Nithsdale (d.c.1419), knighted when very young as he is described as chevalier in a safe-conduct dated January 30, 1406, when he could not have been more than nineteen.[4] 
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Richardson, Douglas, Plantagenet Ancestry, Baltimore, 2004, p216: ISBN 0-8063-1750-7 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ a b Maxwell, Sir Herbert, Bt., A History of the House of Douglas, London, 1902, vol.1, p.127-8 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Fischer Th. A., The Scots in Eastern and Western Prussia, Edinburgh, 1903, p123-3 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Maxwell, Sir Herbert, Bt., A History of the House of Douglas, London, 1902, vol.1, p128 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Maxwell, Sir Herbert. A History of the House of Douglas, 2 vols, London, 1902. Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Douglas_of_Nithsdale" 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Known as very handsome and dashing.
