2 CONT [http://www.thepeerage.com/p10800.htm#i107997[]]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Archibald Douglas, 4th Earl of Douglas''' was born circa 1370.3 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of '''Archibald Douglas, 3rd Earl of Douglas''' and '''Joan Moray'''.2 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married '''Margaret Stewart, Lady of Galloway''', daughter of Robert III Stewart, King of Scotland and Annabel Drummond, before 1390.4 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 17 August 1424 at Verneuil, France, killed in action.5  He was buried on 24 August 1424 at Tours Cathedral, Tours, France.5
2 CONT
2 CONT      Archibald Douglas, 4th Earl of Douglas also went by the nick-name of Archibald 'Tyneman'. He held the office of Lord Warden of the Marches in 1400.2 He succeeded to the title of 4th Earl of Douglas [S., 1358] circa 24 Decembe
2 CONC r 1400.2 He fought in the invasion of England by the Scots in 1401, which he commanded.2 He fought in the Battle of Homildon Hill on 14 September 1402, where he was wounded in 5 places, lost an eye, and was taken prisoner by Henry
2 CONC  Hotspur, whom he afterwards joined in a rebellion against King Henry IV.2 He gained the title of Lord of Annandale in 1409.5 He was created 1st Duc de Touraine [France] on 19 April 1424.5 He fought in the Battle of Verneuil on 17
2 CONC  August 1424, fighting against the Duke of Bedford.5
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Archibald Douglas, 4th Earl of Douglas
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Lady Mary Douglas+6
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Archibald Douglas, 4th Earl of Douglas and Margaret Stewart, Lady of Galloway
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Lady Elizabeth Douglas+1 b. bt 1385 - 1401, d. c 1451 2.William Douglas7 b. bt 1385 - 1401 3.James Douglas7 b. bt 1385 - 1401, d. 1424 4.Archibald Douglas, 5th Earl of Douglas+7 b. 1390, d. 26 Jun 1439
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations 1.[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 219. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family. 2.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geof
2 CONC frey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint 
2 CONC in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume IV, page 433. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage. 3.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume IV, page 453. 4.[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's R
2 CONC oyal Family, page 227. 5.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume IV, page 434. 6.[S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's
2 CONC  Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 2, page 1681. Hereinafter cited as Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition. 7.[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family, page 228.
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald Douglas, 4th Earl of Douglas also went by the nick-name of Archibald 'Tyneman'. He held the office of Lord Warden of the Marches in 1400.2 He succeeded to the title of 4th Earl of Douglas [S., 1358] circa 24 December 140
2 CONC 0.2 He fought in the invasion of England by the Scots in 1401, which he commanded.2 He fought in the Battle of Homildon Hill on 14 September 1402, where he was wounded in 5 places, lost an eye, and was taken prisoner by Henry Hots
2 CONC pur, whom he afterwards joined in a rebellion against King Henry IV.2 He gained the title of Lord of Annandale in 1409.5 He was created 1st Duc de Touraine [France] on 19 April 1424.5 He fought in the Battle of Verneuil on 17 Augu
2 CONC st 1424, fighting against the Duke of Bedford.5
2 CONT
