1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Douglas,_2nd_Earl_of_Angus
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://www.thepeerage.com/p10822.htm#i108218
2 CONT
2 CONT William Douglas, 2nd Earl of Angus (c. 1398 – 1437), was a Scottish nobleman and soldier. The son of George Douglas, 1st Earl of Angus and Princess Mary of Scotland, he was a grandson of King Robert III.
2 CONT
2 CONT The story of Angus' life is interwoven with that of his uncle and King, James I of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Angus was born about 1398 at Tantallon Castle in East Lothian. He inherited the Earldom of Angus in 1402, following his father's death of the plague whilst in English captivity, following the Battle of Homildon Hill.
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.familysearch.org/en/tree/person/details/9HCQ-CZM
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT William Douglas, 2nd Earl of Angus (c. 1398 – 1437), was a Scottish nobleman and soldier. The son of George Douglas, 1st Earl of Angus and Princess Mary of Scotland, he was a grandson of King Robert III.
2 CONT
2 CONT The story of Angus' life is interwoven with that of his uncle and King, James I of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Angus was born about 1398 at Tantallon Castle in East Lothian. He inherited the Earldom of Angus in 1402, following his father's death of the plague whilst in English captivity, following the Battle of Homildon Hill.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William Douglas, 2nd Earl of Angus (c. 1398 – 1437) was a Scottish nobleman and soldier. The son of George Douglas, 1st Earl of Angus and Princess Mary of Scotland, he was a grandson of King Robert III.
2 CONT
2 CONT The story of Angus' life is interwoven with that of his uncle and King, James I of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Angus was born about 1398 at Tantallon Castle in East Lothian. He inherited the Earldom of Angus in 1402, following his father's death of the plague whilst in English captivity, following the Battle of Homildon Hill.
2 CONT
2 CONT Return of King James
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1420, Angus was nominated as one of twenty-one noblemen to be delivered as hostages to the English court as security for the ransom of King James I. James had been captured by the English in 1406, and was held by first Henry IV
2 CONC  of England, and latterly by his son Henry V of England. During the king's captivity, Scotland was ruled by his uncle Robert Stewart, Duke of Albany, who had been in no hurry to pay his nephew's ransom. Following the death of Alba
2 CONC ny,in 1420 the Scots finally paid the ransom monies owed. Whatever the machinations that followed, Angus was not included on the final list of hostages, but was one of the party of Scots nobles who met their King at Durham, in 142
2 CONC 4. The King was escorted triumphally back to Scotland, and Angus received a Knighthood from the King at his coronation at Scone Abbey on the 2 June of that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal Gaoler
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1425, a purge took place of the Albany Stewarts and their adherents. The trial which followed at Stirling Castle, included Angus amongst a large faction of Douglas nobles within the jury. Facing execution were, Murdoch Stewart,
2 CONC  2nd Duke of Albany, his two sons Alaisdair and Walter, and the Earl of Lennox. The widowed Duchess of Albany, was held a close prisoner at Tantallon under the supervision of Angus for eight years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1429, King James went north to deal with the ever troublesome Lord of the Isles. Alexander of Islay, Earl of Ross. This in response to Alexander and his islemen's burning of Inverness. Angus was a captain in the Royal army and 
2 CONC when Islay finally submitted to the King at Holyrood he was entrusted to the keeping of Angus at Tantallon for two years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Warden of the Marches
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1430 Angus was sent on embassy to England as one of the commissioners to negotiate an extension of the truce with the newly crowned Henry VI of England which was prolonged for five years. Later in that year was constituted as t
2 CONC he Warden of the Middle March. In 1435 Angus led a troop of men to invest Dunbar Castle. The castellan, George II, Earl of March, had previously been made a ward of the King, and the garrison surrendered the castle bloodlessly.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dunbar castle was then held of the King by Angus and Sir Adam Hepburn of Hailes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dunbar fled to England calling for help in regaining Dunbar castle by force of arms. This help materialised in the spring of 1435 when Sir Robert Ogle, the Governor of Berwick upon Tweed, with Henry Percy and 4000 men marched nort
2 CONC h to retake the Castle. Angus , with Hepburn and Alexander Ramsay of Dalhousie, decided not to undergo a siege and engaged with the English forces at the Battle of Piperdean, near to Coldstream. This encounter resulted in defeat f
2 CONC or the English but with little loss of life. Fifteen hundred prisoners were taken and ransomed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later life
2 CONT
2 CONT Angus continued to consolidate his estates, often at the expense of his cousins the Black Douglases, taking positions and fortresses previously held by the Earls of Douglas, such as Lintalee and finally Hermitage Castle for a time
2 CONC . Following the assassination of his uncle, King James in February 1437, Angus was instrumental in the pursuit and capture of the conspirators. These included, Walter Stewart, Earl of Atholl, a great uncle of Angus himself. Angus 
2 CONC died in the October of the same year, aged thirty-nine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Angus' mother remarried in 1409 to Sir James Kennedy younger of Dunure. It is thought that around this time that Angus was betrothed through negotiations by his grandmother, Margaret Stewart, 4th Countess of Angus, to Margaret Hay
2 CONC , daughter of Sir William Hay of Yester. They married in 1425 and they had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT James Douglas, 3rd Earl of Angus
2 CONT
2 CONT George Douglas, 4th Earl of Angus
2 CONT
2 CONT William Douglas of Cluny
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Douglas, Rector of St. Andrews
2 CONT
2 CONT Helen Douglas, m. 1st. Patrick Graham, 2nd Lord Graham 2nd. James Ogilvy, 1st Lord Ogilvy of Airlie
2 CONT
2 CONT Latterly, Angus' sister, Lady Elizabeth Douglas, would marry his brother in law, Sir David Hay of Yester. Through their son John Hay, 1st Lord Hay of Yester they are the ancestors of the Marquesses of Tweeddale
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd Earl of Angus; d. Oct. 1437; Ambassador to England, 1430; commanded the Scots in their victory over the English at the battle of Piperdean, 10 Sep 1436; m. (dispensation, 1425) Margaret Hay of Yester; father of George Douglas,
2 CONC  4th Earl of Angus. [Magna Charta Sureties, p. 46]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT   1420, Angus was nominated as one of twenty-one noblemen to be delivered as hostages to the English court as security for the ransom of King James I. James had been captured by the English in 1406, and was held by first Henry IV 
2 CONC of England, and latterly by his son Henry V of England. During the king's captivity, Scotland was ruled by his uncle Robert Stewart, Duke of Albany, who had been in no hurry to pay his nephew's ransom. Following the death of Alban
2 CONC y,in 1420 the Scots finally paid the ransom monies owed. Whatever the machinations that followed, Angus was not included on the final list of hostages, but was one of the party of Scots nobles who met their King at Durham, in 1424
2 CONC . The King was escorted triumphally back to Scotland, and Angus received a Knighthood from the King at his coronation at Scone Abbey on the 2 June of that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Royal Gaoler
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1425, a purge took place of the Albany Stewarts and their adherents. The trial which followed at Stirling Castle, included Angus amongst a large faction of Douglas nobles within the jury. Facing execution were, Murdoch Stewart,
2 CONC  2nd Duke of Albany, his two sons Alaisdair and Walter, and the Earl of Lennox. The widowed Duchess of Albany, was held a close prisoner at Tantallon under the supervision of Angus for eight years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1429, King James went north to deal with the ever troublesome Lord of the Isles. Alexander of Islay, Earl of Ross. This in response to Alexander and his islemen's burning of Inverness. Angus was a captain in the Royal army and 
2 CONC when Islay finally submitted to the King at Holyrood he was entrusted to the keeping of Angus at Tantallon for two years.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Warden of the Marches
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1430 Angus was sent on embassy to England as one of the commissioners to negotiate an extension of the truce with the newly crowned Henry VI of England which was prolonged for five years. Later in that year was constituted as t
2 CONC he Warden of the Middle March. In 1435 Angus led a troop of men to invest Dunbar Castle. The castellan, George II, Earl of March, had previously been made a ward of the King, and the garrison surrendered the castle bloodlessly.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dunbar castle was then held of the King by Angus and Sir Adam Hepburn of Hailes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dunbar fled to England calling for help in regaining Dunbar castle by force of arms. This help materialised in the spring of 1435 when Sir Robert Ogle, the Governor of Berwick upon Tweed, with Henry Percy and 4000 men marched nort
2 CONC h to retake the Castle. Angus , with Hepburn and Alexander Ramsay of Dalhousie, decided not to undergo a siege and engaged with the English forces at the Battle of Piperdean, near to Coldstream. This encounter resulted in defeat f
2 CONC or the English but with little loss of life. Fifteen hundred prisoners were taken and ransomed.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Later life
2 CONT
2 CONT Angus continued to consolidate his estates, often at the expense of his cousins the Black Douglases, taking positions and fortresses previously held by the Earls of Douglas, such as Lintalee and finally Hermitage Castle for a time
2 CONC . Following the assassination of his Uncle, King James in February 1437, Angus was instrumental in the pursuit and capture of the conspirators. These included, Walter Stewart, Earl of Atholl, a great uncle of Angus himself. Angus 
2 CONC died in the October of the same year, aged thirty-nine.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
