1 NAME William /Douglas/
2 GIVN William
2 SURN Douglas
2 _MARNM Douglas
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE BEF SEP 1390
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1425
1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  	
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT died before his father without issue (dvpsp).  
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY%20LATER.htm#JamesDouglasdied1440
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         JAMES Douglas ([1373/74?]-before 22 May 1441). … He may have been created Lord Dalkeith in [1430][705].  m firstly (contract, Apr 1378, before 10 Nov 1387) Lady ELIZABETH Stewart, daughter of ROBERT III King of Scotland
2 CONC  & his wife Annabel Drummond (-before 1411).  
2 CONT
2 CONT James & his first wife had three children: 
2 CONT i)          WILLIAM Douglas of Whittinghame in East Lothian (before 30 Sep 1390-[1425]).  The testament of "Jacobus de Douglas dominus de Dalkeith", dated 30 Sep 1390, appointed "Jacobum filium suum primogenitum ex Agnete uxore me
2 CONC a" as his heir, and in default "Willielmum filium suum progenitum ex Elizabetha sponsa filia Regis…domini Henrici de Douglas avunculi sui"[339].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (1420, Papal dispensation 9 Dec 1421) as her second husband, MARGARET Borthwick, widow of WILLIAM Abernethy, daughter of ---.  The Papal dispensation for the marriage of "Wilhelmi de Douglas…filii…Jacobi de Douglas militis et do
2 CONC mini loci de Dalbrech primogeniti" and "Margarithe de Borchwilr relicte qm Wilhelmi de Abirnechi", issued by Pope Martin V, is dated 9 Dec 1421[340]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul, Sir James Balfour, ''The Scots Peerage 1904-1914'', nine volumes .  6:350-1.  < [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/page/352/mode/2up Archive.Org] >
2 CONT
2 CONT SIR JAMES DOUGLAS, succeeded his father as Lord of Dalkeith. … The second Laird of Dalkeith married, first, Elizabeth, a younger daughter of John, Earl of Carrick, afterwards King Robert III.  …  It is not certain when she died, b
2 CONC ut Sir James married, secondly, before 1439, Janet, eldest daughter of Sir William Borthwick. The marriage could not have taken place till after 2 November 1411, when a papal dispensation was issued for Janet Borthwick's marriage 
2 CONC with Adam Hepburn of Hailes.1 She survived Sir James, and married, secondly, Sir George Crichton, afterwards Earl of Caithness,2 whom she also survived. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT By his first wife Sir James had issue : —
2 CONT *1. William, born, as indicated, before September 1390, when he is named in his grandfather's will. He was in 1405 one of the hostages who went to England for Archibald, fourth Earl of Douglas, and he seems to have remained a pris
2 CONC oner in England until May 1413, when his grandfather paid 700 merks for his liberation. He appears also to have been one of the hostages for King James I., and was still in England in 1425,3 when he probably died, as he then disap
2 CONC pears from record, and his next brother succeeded to their father. On 9 December 1420 a dispensation was issued for his marriage with Margaret Borthwick, widow, not of Sir William Abernethy, as stated by some, but of his son Willi
2 CONC am, who had been slain at Harlaw in 141 1.4 By her he had no issue.
2 CONT 2. JAMES, who succeeded.
2 CONT 3. Henry, who had a grant from his father of the lands of Borg in Galloway. …
2 CONT
2 CONT By his second wife Sir James had issue a son : — 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. William, to whom the lands of Whittinghame and Morton were secured, although in 1474 he resigned his rights over Morton in favour of his nephew the first Earl of Morton.3 He was the ancestor of the Douglases of Whittinghame, wh
2 CONC ose principal male line ended in an heiress Elizabeth, who married Alexander, Viscount of Kingston, in 1661. She was served heir to her brother Archibald Douglas on 15 May 1662. The only one of her children who left issue married 
2 CONC William Hay of Drummelzier, and their descendants are the lineal representatives of the Douglases of Whittinghame. (See title Kingston.) 
2 CONT The male line of Whittinghame is represented by the Counts Douglas in Sweden, created Counts Skenninge and Barons of Skalby.4
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
