1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''James Douglas, 2nd Lord Dalkeith1,2,3
2 CONT *'''M, #3402, d. after 8 September 1456
2 CONT *Father	Sir James Douglas, Lord Dalkeith d. bt Feb 1440 - 22 May 1441
2 CONT *Mother	Elizabeth Stewart d. b 1439
2 CONT *'''     James Douglas, 2nd Lord Dalkeith married Elizabeth Giffard''', daughter of James Giffard, '''circa 1424.2,3 James Douglas, 2nd Lord Dalkeith died after 8 September 1456.
2 CONT *'''Family     Elizabeth Giffard''' b. c 1404, d. a 8 Sep 1456
2 CONT *'''Child	
2 CONT **James Douglas, 1st Earl Morton, 3rd Lord Dalkeith, Ambassador to France & Castile+2,3 b. c 1425, d. bt 22 Jun 1493 - 22 Oct 1493
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S385] Unknown author, The Complete Peerage, by Cokayne, Vol. IV, p. 39; Burke's Peerage, 1938, p. 1788; Wallop Family, p. 280.
2 CONT *[S16] Douglas Richardson, Magna Carta Ancestry, 2nd Edition, Vol. III, p. 579-580.
2 CONT *[S4] Douglas Richardson, Royal Ancestry, Vol. I, p. 658.
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p113.htm#i3402 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY%20LATER.htm#JamesDouglasdied1440
2 CONT
2 CONT * b)         JAMES Douglas ([1373/74?]-before 22 May 1441). … m firstly (contract, Apr 1378, before 10 Nov 1387) Lady ELIZABETH Stewart, daughter of ROBERT III King of Scotland & his wife Annabel Drummond (-before 1411).  
2 CONT **ii)         JAMES Douglas (-[8 Sep 1456/14 Mar 1458]).  m ELIZABETH Gifford, daughter of JAMES Gifford of Sheriffhall & his wife --- (-after 8 Sep 1456).  James & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT ***(a)       JAMES Douglas (-[22 Jun/22 Oct] 1493).  He was created Earl of Morton.  m (before 15 May 1459) Lady JOAN Stewart, daughter of JAMES I King of Scotland & his wife Lady Joan Beaufort ([1428]-after 16 Oct 1486).  She was
2 CONC  deaf and dumb, known as "the dumb lady of Dalkeith".  She lived in France from Aug 1445 to spring 1458[709].  They were ancestors of the later Earls of Morton, extinct in the male line in 1548. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul, Sir James Balfour, ''The Scots Peerage 1904-1914'', nine volumes .  6:350-1.  < [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/page/352/mode/2up Archive.Org] >
2 CONT
2 CONT JAMES DOUGLAS, the second, but eldest surviving, son, succeeded his father some time between February 1439-40 and May 1441. He appears, however, to have been considered mentally deficient, or perhaps a spendthrift, as on 22 May 14
2 CONC 41 the Government of King James n., then a minor, declared him to be incapable of managing his own affairs, and appointed James Giffard of Sheriffhall (his brother-in-law) to be his curator, to act as governor and administrator of
2 CONC  all his lordships, castles, manors, and of all his goods, and the receiver of his rents, for the space of nineteen years, constituting Giffard also constable of the castles of Dalkeith, Aberdour, and others, with full powers of j
2 CONC urisdiction over the lands. Failing James Giffard from any cause, it was provided that his brothers John and William successively should take office as curator till the nineteen years should expire.1 It was no doubt the great esta
2 CONC tes in question which led to this appointment, but another view is presented by a writ issued in the King's name in September of the following year, stating that, by advice of his council, he had taken the castle and lordship of D
2 CONC alkeith into his own hands because of the great and perilous strife between James of Douglas and Henry his brother, and the bloodshed, ' spuilzie, reif,' and disorders following thereon, and to prevent further evils.2 This documen
2 CONC t suggests that the Laird of Dalkeith was not really insane, but facile, and that his brother had designs on the estates, which he strenuously resisted. He seems to have been made a knight before June 1441, and he is styled Sir Ja
2 CONC mes Douglas by his son in 1479.3 The castle of Dalkeith appears to have been kept in repair and ammunition by the Crown until 1445, when the payments cease ; but if it be true, as was alleged, that the Douglas party burned the cas
2 CONC tle in 1452, it may then have been still in the hands of the Crown, though nothing is known as to the fact. James Douglas, third of Dalkeith, died between 8 September 1456, when he resigned his lands in favour of his son, and 14 M
2 CONC arch 1457-58, when his son was made Earl.4 James Douglas married Elizabeth, daughter of James Gifford of Sheriffhall, who was alive on 8 September 1456, and perhaps survived him, and had issue, so far as known, one son : 5 —
2 CONT
2 CONT * JAMES, who succeeded, and became first Earl of Morton.
2 CONT
2 CONT _____________
2 CONT *'''James Douglas, 2nd Lord of Dalkeith1
2 CONT *M, #108243, d. between 1456 and 1458
2 CONT *Last Edited=3 Jan 2015
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=0.08%
2 CONT *'''     James Douglas, 2nd Lord of Dalkeith was the son''' of Sir James Douglas, 1st Lord of Dalkeith and Lady Elizabeth Stewart.1 '''He married Elizabeth Gifford''', daughter of James Gifford.1 '''He died between 1456 and 1458.1
2 CONT *'''     He succeeded to the title of 2nd Lord of Dalkeith.1 On 22 May 1441 he was declared incapable of managing his own affair, by Act of Parliament [Scottish].2 On 8 September 1456 he resigend his lands in favour of his son.3
2 CONT *'''Child of James Douglas, 2nd Lord of Dalkeith and Elizabeth Gifford
2 CONT **James Douglas, 1st Earl of Morton+4 d. bt 22 Jun 1492 - 22 Oct 1492
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Families: The Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 229. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Families.
2 CONT *[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct
2 CONC  or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume IV, page 39. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *[S37] BP2003 volume 2, page 2786. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT *[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Families, page 231.
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p10825.htm#i108243 ____________
2 CONT *James Douglas, 1st Lord Dalkeith (born after 1372, died before 22 May 1441) was a Scottish nobleman born in Dalkeith, Midlothian, Scotland to Sir James Douglas and Agnes Dunbar. He married Elizabeth (Princess) Stewart, daughter o
2 CONC f King Robert III, about the year 1387. They had four children before she died: William, '''James''', Henry, and Margaret. He later remarried Janet Borthwick.
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Douglas,_1st_Lord_Dalkeith ________________
2 CONT *James Douglas, (1426 - 22 October 1493), the 4th Lord of Dalkeith, was created the 1st Earl of Morton in 1458.
2 CONT *He was the '''son of James Douglas, 2nd Lord of Dalkeith and Elizabeth Gifford''', daughter of James Gifford of Sheriffhall.[2] '''His father resigned all his estates''' to James in 1456 when James became the 4th Lord of Dalkeith
2 CONC .[3] James was created Earl of Morton in 1458[a][4] upon his marriage to Joan Stewart[b] (1428-1493), the daughter of James I, King of Scots.[5] She was a deaf-mute.[5]
2 CONT *The Earl entered into a marriage contract with Patrick Graham, Bishop of St. Andrews between the Bishop's niece and John Douglas, the Earl's eldest son and heir.[3] In turn the Grahams, the Bishop, his brother and nephew, allied 
2 CONC themselves to the Earl and pledged to assist him in recovering the diverted lands of Whittingehame and Morton.[3] It appears, however, that this pledge was intended to draw the Earl of Morton into a conspiracy that included the Bi
2 CONC shop, Lord Boyd and his party.[3] Robert Boyd, 1st Lord Boyd who, as one of the Regents during the minority of Robert III of Scotland, took possession of the young king and married his son to the king's elder sister, for which cri
2 CONC mes he was later attainted for high treason.[6] The Earl of Morton apparently did not participate since he sat on the jury which convicted the Boyds.[3] Bishop Graham was later excommunicated and deposed.[7]
2 CONT *The lands of Whittinghame and all rights over the barony of Morton, Dumfriesshire were resigned into the Earl's hands in 1473-4 and in that same year he recovered the lordship of Dalkeith increasing the Earls already vast estates
2 CONC .[8] He re-endowed the collegiate church at Dalkeith his 3rd great-grandfather founded and he also founded St. Martha's Hospital in Aberdour in 1474.[5] The Earl died on 22 October 1493 when his son John succeeded him as the 2nd E
2 CONC arl of Morton.[8] His wife Joan predeceased him by 4 months dying on 22 June 1493.[9]
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Douglas,_1st_Earl_of_Morton ___________________
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.douglashistory.co.uk/history/james2ndldofdalkeith.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT >Second son of Sir James, 1st Lord of Dalkeith, James succeeded his father circa 1441 due to the death of his elder brother, William, in 1425. James was married to Elizabeth Gifford, daughter of James Gifford of Sherifhall. James 
2 CONC reportedly became insane and was committed on May 22, 1441. He resigned his lands on September 8, 1456 in favour of his son, and died circa 1457.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Father: James (Sir) (1st Lord Dalkeith) Douglas b: ABT. 1356
2 CONT * Mother: Elizabeth (Princess) Stewart
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Elizabeth Gifford
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT #  James (1st Earl of Morton) Douglas
2 CONT # Janet, born about 1427.
2 CONT
