1 OCCU King of Scotland, Fiery Face, EARL OF BUCHAN STEWART
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''James II, King of Scots'''  was born on 16 October 1430 in  Holyrood Palace, Edinburgh and  Died on    3 August 1460 at the siege of  Roxburgh (Gaelic:  Rosbrog) Castle when one of the cannons exploded.  He was 
2 CONC also known as Fiery Face,  Seumas II Stiùbhairt , Jacobus 2, Rex Scotor                    
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruled:  21 February 1437 - 3 August 1460
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by:  James I     4 April 1406 - 21 February 1437
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded by: James III   3 August 1460  - 11 June 1488
2 CONT
2 CONT Coronation:   1437
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Basics==
2 CONT He was the son of James I, King of Scots and of Joan Beaufort  
2 CONT
2 CONT Married :
2 CONT # Mary of Guelders on 3 July 1449 at Holyrood Abbey, Edinburgh
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT # An unnamed son. (Both born and died on 19 May 1450)
2 CONT # James III of Scotland (1451/1452 - 1488)
2 CONT # Alexander Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany (c. 1454 - 1485)
2 CONT # David Stewart, Earl of Moray (c. 1456 - 1457)
2 CONT # John Stewart, 1st Earl of Mar and Garioch (c. 1459 - 1479)
2 CONT # Princess Margaret Stewart of Scotland
2 CONT # Princess Mary Stewart of Scotland (d. 1488)
2 CONT # John Stewart of Sticks/Ballechin (Illegitimate d. c. 1460)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography== 
2 CONT
2 CONT James II (Holyrood Palace, Edinburgh, 16 October 1430 – 3 August 1460) reigned as King of Scots from 1437 to 1460.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of James I, King of Scots and of Joan Beaufort (daughter of John Beaufort, 1st Earl of Somerset and of Margaret Holland). He had an elder twin, Alexander Stewart, Duke of Rothesay, who lived long enough to receive a
2 CONC  knighthood, but died in infancy. James became the father of James III.
2 CONT
2 CONT James, whose nickname "Fiery face" because of a conspicuous vermilion birthmark on his face, had six sisters, all of whom married into various European royal dynasties.
2 CONT
2 CONT Inheriting the throne at under seven years old, James saw the government in the hands of others for most of his reign. The assassination of his father James I had formed part of an attempt to usurp power by Walter Stewart, 1st Ear
2 CONC l of Atholl, but it failed miserably, and James's guardians had Atholl and his allies captured and executed in the months after the assassination.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1437 to 1439, Archibald Douglas, 5th Earl of Douglas, as lieutenant-general of the realm, headed the government. After his death, and with a general lack of high-status earls in Scotland because of deaths, forfeiture or youth
2 CONC , power became shared uneasily between William, 1st Lord Crichton, Lord Chancellor of Scotland (sometimes in co-operation with the Earl of Avondale) and Sir Alexander Livingston of Callendar, who had possession of the young king a
2 CONC s the warden of the stronghold of Stirling Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1440 Edinburgh Castle became the location for the 'Black Dinner', which saw the summary execution of the young William Douglas, 6th Earl of Douglas and his brother. Commentators tend to place the blame for the Black Dinner at t
2 CONC he feet of Crichton, Livingston and particularly Avondale, as Douglas's death brought him the earldom of Douglas (as 7th earl), and the position of the most powerful magnate in Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The precise details of who ran the government year by year between 1439 and 1445 appear complex and far from certain, but in 1445 the Livingstons co-operated with William Douglas, 8th Earl of Douglas, son of the recently-deceased 
2 CONC 7th Earl, to drive Crichton from power. Douglas now took the lead in governing Scotland until 1449, placing his brothers and other family members in positions of power. Yet the wily Crichton soon returned to influence, now co-oper
2 CONC ating with the Douglases.
2 CONT
2 CONT  In 1449 James II emerged into adulthood, yet in many ways his 'active kingship' differed little from his minority. The Douglases used his coming of age as a way to throw the Livingstons out of the shared government, as the young 
2 CONC king took revenge for the brief arrest of his mother (in turn as a means to remove her from political influence) that had taken place in 1439. Douglas and Crichton continued to dominate political power, and the king's ability to r
2 CONC ule without them remained arguably limited.
2 CONT
2 CONT But James did not acquiesce with this situation without argument, and between 1451 and 1455 he struggled to free himself from the power of the Douglases. Attempts to curb the Douglases' power took place in 1451, during the absence
2 CONC  of the Earl of Douglas from Scotland, and culminated with the murder of the 8th Earl of Douglas at Stirling Castle on 22 February 1452.
2 CONT
2 CONT The main account of Douglas's murder comes from the Auchinleck Chronicle, a near contemporary but fragmentary source. According to its account, the king accused the Earl (probably with justification) of forging links with John Mac
2 CONC donald, 11th Earl of Ross (a.k.a. the Lord of the Isles) and with Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford. This bond, if it existed, created a dangerous axis of power of independently-minded men, forming a major rival to royal aut
2 CONC hority. When Douglas refused to break the bond with Ross, James broke into a fit of temper and stabbed Douglas to his death. His court officials (many of whom would rise to great influence in later years, often in former Douglas l
2 CONC ands) then joined in the bloodbath, one allegedly striking out the Earl's brain with an axe.
2 CONT
2 CONT This murder did not end the power of the Douglases, but rather created a state of intermittent civil war between 1452 and 1455. James attempted to seize Douglas lands, but his opponents repeatedly forced him into humiliating climb
2 CONC downs, whereby he returned the lands to James Douglas, 9th Earl of Douglas and a brief and uneasy peace ensued.
2 CONT
2 CONT Military campaigns ended indecisively, and some have argued that James stood in serious danger of being overthrown, or of having to flee the country. But James's patronage of lands, titles and office to allies of the Douglases saw
2 CONC  their erstwhile allies begin to change sides, most importantly the Earl of Crawford, and in May, 1455 James struck a decisive blow against the Douglases, and they were finally defeated at the Battle of Arkinholm.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the months that followed the Parliament of Scotland declared the extensive Douglas lands forfeit and permanently annexed them to the crown, along with many other lands, finances and castles. The Earl fled into a long English ex
2 CONC ile. James finally had the freedom to govern as he wished, and one can argue that his successors as kings of Scots never faced such a powerful challenge to their authority again. Along with the forfeiture of the Albany Stewarts in
2 CONC  reign of James I, the destruction of the Black Douglases saw royal power in Scotland take a major step forward.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1455 and 1460 James II proved to be an active and interventionist king. Ambitious plans to take Orkney, Shetland and the Isle of Man did not succeed. The king travelled the country, and has been argued to have originated t
2 CONC he practice of raising money by giving remissions for serious crimes, and that some of the unpopular policies of James III originated in the late 1450s.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1458 an Act of Parliament commanded the king to modify his behaviour, but one cannot say how his reign would have developed had he lived longer.
2 CONT
2 CONT James II enthusiastically promoted modern artillery, which he used with some success against the Black Douglases. His ambitions to increase Scotland's standing saw him besiege Roxburgh Castle in 1460, one of the last Scottish cast
2 CONC les still held by the English after the Wars of Independence. On 3 August, one of his cannons exploded, killing the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Lindsay of Pitscottie wrote in his Historie concerning the accident that befell King James II, that as he stood near a piece of artillery "his thigh-bone was dug into two with a piece of misframed gun that broke in shooting
2 CONC , by which he was stricken to the ground and died hastily". It should be noted, however, that Pitscottie was writing a century after the events he was describing.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Scots carried on with the siege of Roxburgh Castle, led by George Douglas, 4th Earl of Angus, and it fell a few days later. James's son became king as James III, and his widow Mary of Gueldres acted as regent until her own dea
2 CONC th three years later.
2 CONT
