1 NOTE James II Stewart, King of Scotland was the son of James I Stewart, King of Scotland and Lady Joan Beaufort. He was born on 16 October 1430 at Holyrood Palace, Edinburgh, Edinburgh, Scotland.
2 CONT He was also reported to have been born on 16 October 1431 at Holyrood Monastery, Edinburgh, Edinburgh, Scotland. He married Marie von Geldern, daughter of Arnold Herzog von Geldern and Katherine von Kleve, on 3 July 1449 at Holyro
2 CONC od Abbey, Edinburgnburgh, Scotland.
2 CONT He died on 3 August 1460 at age 29 at Roxburgh Castle, Scotland, killed by a bursting piece of ordnance.
2 CONT He was buried at Holyrood Abbey, Edinburgh, Edinburgh.
2 CONT He succeeded to the title of Duke of Rothesay on 22 April 1431.2 James II Stewart, King of Scotland also went by the nick-name of 'Fiery Face'. He succeeded to the title of King James II of Scotland on 21 February 1437.2 He was cr
2 CONC owned King of Scotn 25 March 1437 at Holyrood Abbey, Edinburgh, Edinburgh.
2 CONT He fought in the siege of Roxburgh Castle in August 1460 at Roxburgh Castle.
2 CONT Succeeded at the age of 6 following the murder of his father, James I. A large birthmark bequeathed the nickname 'Fiery Face'. His coronation at Kelso Abbey was the first not at Scone. By giving authority to men of his own choice,
2 CONC  and travelling encally, James II strengthened a monarchy weakened by powerful nobles. In particular, he murdered the 8th, and killed the 9th. Earl of Douglas (1452-5). He married Mary of Guelders (died 1463), daughter of Arnold, 
2 CONC Duke of Guelders (a Rhine principali the 3rd. July 1449. They had four sons and two daughters. James died in a gun accident - the gun exploded whilst King standing nearby near Roxborough Castle (held by the British) during seige. 
2 CONC Succeeded by his son as James III.
