1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on Alexander Gordon of Strathavon:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p32944.htm#i329433
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Gordon of Strathavon [1]
2 CONT *M, #329433
2 CONT *Last Edited=25 Apr 2011
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=0.78%
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Gordon of Strathavon is the son of Alexander Gordon, 3rd Earl of Huntly and Lady Jean Stewart.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was ancestor of the Gordons of Cluny.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT *1. [S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 2, page 2012. Hereinafter cited as Burke's 
2 CONC Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on the Gordon Baronets:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gordon_Baronets
2 CONT
2 CONT Gordon of Cluny, Aberdeen (1625)
2 CONT *Sir Alexander Gordon, 1st Baronet (d. c. 1648)
2 CONT *Sir John Gordon, 2nd Baronet (d. c. 1668)
2 CONT *baronetcy dormant c 1668
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.thegordondnaproject.com/35045.html Gordon DNA Project]
2 CONT
2 CONT m. The youngest daughter of John Grant of Grant, by whom he had four daughters and two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Alexander d. young, leaving one daughter 2. John - succeeded to Cluny, and was the ancestor of the families of Cluny, Birsmoir, Pittendreich, and others
2 CONT
2 CONT The family of Cluny was one of the first to receive the new dignity of a Baronetcy, but it was only held for two generations and became extinct about 1650. Cluny has been subsequently owned by two other families of Gordons, one a 
2 CONC branch of the Sutherland Gordons, and another of unknown lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT The house of Birsmoir was one of those which lost their lands in consequence of the wars of the covenant. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The representative of the family was Governor of Pennsylvania in America in the 18th c and his descendants were in existence about 1840. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to this family, Alexander had also two natural sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. William Gordon, of Delmore, and ancestor of the Croughly Family 2. George Gordon of Tombae, ancestor of the families of Tombae and Auchdregnie
2 CONT
2 CONT These two sons were granted the rights and privileges of legitimate birth by a charter issued under the great seal of Scotland, at Perth, on the 24th June, 1553. Their mother's name has not been preserved but it appears most proba
2 CONC ble that they were the issue of a union peculiar to Scotland, known as "Handfasting." This custom had long prevailed among the Highlanders, and was merely a formal betrothal, which might at any subsequent time be made a marriage, 
2 CONC valid from the date of the preliminary ceremony; or the couple might separate when they pleased. The existing law in Scotland [at the time of publication in 1895], by which a marriage legitimises any previous offspring between the
2 CONC  couple, is probably a survival of this practice. Handfasting soon became extinct following a proclamation issued in December 1562 by the Kirk Session of Aberdeen outlawing the practice.
1 FAMC @F6000000006768642608@
1 FAMS @F6000000006290919966@
1 FAMS @F6000000024213346949@
1 RFN geni:6000000006290586217
1 SUBM @S6000000004973327351@
1 NOTE Alexander of Strathoun, later of Cluny.
2 CONT On 31st August 1539 Alexander Gordon exchanged the lands of Strathaven for the land of Cluny in Mar. Reserved for the heritable right of Blairfindy. In 1549, Alexander Gordon  had a "lack" for 19 years of the lands of Balfady, Bal
2 CONC four, Deirahouse and others in Birse, from his brother, Bishop William Gordon
