2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lord_Gray ___________
2 CONT *Andrew Gray, 1st Lord Gray (c. 1390–1469) was a Scottish nobleman, politician and diplomat. He was succeeded to the title by his grandson.[1]
2 CONT *He was the only son of Sir Andrew Gray (d. 1445) of Fowlis, Perthshire, by his first wife Janet, daughter of Sir Roger de Mortimer, whom he married in 1377.[2]
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *He died in 1469, probably towards the end of the year; he is mentioned as deceased in a document of 20 January 1469-70.
2 CONT *He married, on 31 August 1418, Elizabeth Wemyss, the eldest daughter of Sir John Wemyss of Wemyss and Reres, with whom it was stipulated he should receive as dowry a £30 land in Strathardle, Perthshire. This condition was not obs
2 CONC erved, and gave rise to litigation at a later date. His wife survived him.
2 CONT *They had two sons and two daughters. The elder son, Sir Patrick Gray of Kineff, predeceased his father. He had married Anabella Forbes, daughter of Alexander Forbes, 1st Lord Forbes, and had one son, who became '''Andrew Gray, 2n
2 CONC d Lord Gray'''. The other three children were the younger son Andrew, and daughters Margaret (married Robert, Lord Lyle) and Christian (married James Chrighton of Strathurd).
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Gray,_1st_Lord_Gray ______________
2 CONT *'''The Genealogy of the Existing British Peerage and Baronetage: Containing the Family Histories of ...     by Edmund Lodge
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/genealogyexisti02lodggoog
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/genealogyexisti02lodggoog#page/n264/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.246
2 CONT **	GRAY, BARON. (GRAY.)
2 CONT **	Peer of Scotland.
2 CONT *HIS Lordship is of the same family with Earl Grey, though the connexion cannot be distinctly traced. A younger son of the Greys of Northumberland settled in Scotland, as early as the 12th century, and was ancestor of Sir Andrew, 
2 CONC who joined Robert Bruce in 1306. Fourth in descent from him was Sir ANDREW GRAY, of Broxmouth, created LORD GRAY in 1446. ANDREW, his son and successor, 2nd LORD, d. in 1469 ; he had two sons, viz.,
2 CONT *   1 Patrick, Master of Gray, d. before his father, '''leaving a son, ANDREW, who succeeded his grandfather as 3rd LORD. 
2 CONT *   2 Andrew, ancestor of Sir William Gray of Plttendrum, and through him of the 9th and succeeding Lords Gray. 
2 CONT *'''   ANDREW, 3rd LORD, d. in 1514 ; his two elder sons were, 
2 CONT *   1 PATRICK, 4th LORD,who d. without issue male. 
2 CONT *   2 Gilbert, father of PATRICK, who succeeded his uncle as 5th LORD, He was taken prisoner at the rout of Solway, by Dacre and Musgrave, and committed to the custody of the Archbishop of York, but soon admitted to ransom. He was
2 CONC  one of the first Scotch noblemen who countenanced the Reformation, and joined the association in support of JAMES VI. in 1567. He d. in 1582, and was succeeded by his eldest son PATRICK, 6th LORD, to whom, in 1609, succeeded his 
2 CONC son PATRICK, 7th LORD, who d. in 1612. His son and successor, ANDREW, 8th LORD, d. in 1663, without surviving issue male; and his eldest daughter, Anne, having m. William, eldest son of Sir William Gray, of Pittendrum, his Lordshi
2 CONC p made, in 1639, a resignation of his honours into the hands of King CHARLES I., from whom he obtained a renewed charter of the Peerage to himself, with remainder to the said William Gray, husband of Anne, his eldest daughter, and
2 CONC  the heirs male of their marriage, failing which, to the nearest heirs male whatever of Sir William Gray of Pittendrum. 
2 CONT * .... etc. _________
