2 CONT _______________________
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Andrew Gray, 2nd Lord Gray1 M, #19658, d. February 1513/14 Last Edited=18 May 2014
2 CONT      Andrew Gray, 2nd Lord Gray was the son of Patrick Gray, Master of Gray and Annabella Forbes.2 He married, firstly, Janet Keith, daughter of Sir Robert de Keith and Janet Seton, circa March 1463/64.2 He married, secondly, Lady
2 CONC  Elizabeth Stewart, daughter of John Stewart, 1st Earl of Atholl and Lady Eleanor Sinclair, before 1483.2 He married, thirdly, Margaret Houston after 1497.2 He married, fourthly, Lady Elizabeth Hay, daughter of William Hay, 1st Ea
2 CONC rl of Erroll and Lady Beatrix Douglas.2 He died in February 1513/14.2
2 CONT      He succeeded to the title of 2nd Lord Gray [S., 1445] circa 1469.2 He held the office of Hereditary Sheriff of Forfar in 1488.2 He took part in the rebellion by King James IV against his father, King James III.2 He held the o
2 CONC ffice of Justiciary General North of Forth in 1489.2 He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) [Scotland].2 He held the office of Justiciary General South of Forth in 1506.2 Children of Andrew Gray, 2nd Lord Gray Isobel Gray+3 
2 CONC Marjorie Gray+4 Child of Andrew Gray, 2nd Lord Gray and Lady Elizabeth Stewart Gilbert Gray+5 d. b 1541 Children of Andrew Gray, 2nd Lord Gray and Janet Keith Elizabeth Gray+2 d. c 1526 Janet Gray+1 d. Oct 1539 Patrick Gray, 3rd L
2 CONC ord Gray2 d. Apr 1541 Citations [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the 
2 CONC United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume VIII, page 479. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage. [S37] BP200
2 CONC 3 volume 2, page 1642. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37] [S37] BP2003. [S37] [S1322] David Arathoon, "re: Acheson Family and Campbell Family," e-mail message to Darryl Lundy, 8 April 2005 - 14 
2 CONC July 2006. Hereinafter cited as "re: Acheson Family and Campbell Family." [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume VI, page 100.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Andrew, second Lord Gray, was served heir to his father on 5 November 1464 in the loch, mount, castle, and wood of Cluny in Perthshire, and had a precept of sasine from David, Earl of Crawford, for infefting him in the lands of Le
2 CONC itfle, in the Barony of Ballendoch, as son of Sir Patrick Gray of Kinneff, and nearest and lawful heir to Andrew, Lord Gray, his grandfather, 20 January 1469-70.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was one of the Privy council of King James IV, and 
2 CONT
2 CONT on the resignation of David, Earl of Crawford, had charters of the office of Sheriff of the county of Forfar 14 December 1488; 
2 CONT
2 CONT of the lands of Lundy in Forfarshire, forfeited by Robert, Lord Lyle, 29 June 1489; 
2 CONT
2 CONT of the rock and castle of Bruchty on the resignation of Archibald, Earl of Angus, 26 June 1490, 
2 CONT
2 CONT of the lands of Balgillo forfeited by John Wishart of Pitarrow 29 June 1499, 
2 CONT
2 CONT of part of Newton and Flisk 28 July 1503 
2 CONT
2 CONT of Longforgund, on his own resignation 7 January 1508-9, and 
2 CONT
2 CONT of Gothrastown 8 Jun 1512.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was appointed to the office of Justice-General north of the Forth on the forfeiture of Lord Lyle in 1489, and Justice-General south of the forth in 1506, as appears from a decreet in a court held at Ayr 20 October 1511.
2 CONT
2 CONT He took a leading part in the conspiracy against James III, which ended in the murder of that King after the battle of Sauchieburn in 1488, and had even been accused, but without authority, of having been one of the actual murdere
2 CONC rs.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in February 1513-14.
2 CONT
2 CONT By contract of marriage dated at Perth 31 May 1457, between William, Earl of Errol, on the one part, and Andrew, Lord Gray, and Patrick Gray his son, on the other, it was agreed that Andrew, as son and apparent heir to said Patric
2 CONC k, should marry Elizabeth Hay, daughter of the said Earl, but this marriage does not seem to have been completed.
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) He married first, Janet, only daughter of Robert, Lord Keith, son of William, Earl Marischal, and had issue; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue by first marriage:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Patrick, third Lord.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Isobel, married to Alexander Stratoun of Lauristoun in Kincardineshire, and had issue
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Elizabeth, married, first in 1487, to John, fourth Lord Glamis, as appears from an obligation, of her father to Joh, third Lord Glamis, fo £1000 for the marriage completed and solemnized between John, his son and apparent heir,
2 CONC  and the said Elizabeth, dated 18 May 1487. He died 1500. She was married, secondly, in 1511, to Alexander, third Earl of Huntly, who died at Perth 16 January 1523-24, and was buried in the Blackfriars Church; and thirdly, in 1525
2 CONC , to George, fourth Earl of Rothes, with whom she had a charter of the barony of Ballinbreich 5 june 1525.
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) and married secondly, before 1483, Elizabeth Stewart, third daughter of John, Earl of Atholl (brother uterine of James II), by his first wife Margaret, daughter of Archilbald, Earl of Douglas, and had issue;
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue by second marriage:
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Robert of Leitfle, killed at the battle of Flodden 1513.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Gilbert of Buttergask
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Andrew of Muirtown, died before 13 April 1541. He had a son, Patrick, who had charters of the lands of Balgillo in Forfarshire in liferent 7 June 1540 and 10 April 1543, and possibly another son, Andrew, from whom Sir William G
2 CONC ray of Pittendrum is said to be descended.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Edward, rector of Lundy, built Loretto chapel in Perth, and had a charter of the lands of Inchyra 5 October 1521.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Isobel, married, first, about 1495, to Sir James Scrimgeour of Dudhope, Constable of Dundee, who died about 1503; secondly, before 1510, to Adam Crichton of Ruthven, who died before 18 November 1516; and thirdly, before 1521, t
2 CONC o Sir John Campbell of Lundy, with whom she had a charter of the lands of Boquhan 27 April 1529.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. Janet, married, first, to John Charters of Cathelgurdie; secondly, to (?Alexander) Keith, son of Sir William Keith of Inverugie, with issue; thirdly, to sir David Wemyss of that Ilk, who was killed at Flodden 1513; as Lady Wemy
2 CONC ss she had a charter of Torsoppy in Perthshire 24 February 1523-24; and fourthly, about 1530, to James Campbell of Lawers. She died in October 1539.
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. Marjory, married first to Kinninmont of that Ilk, and secondly, to Silvester Rattray of Craighall.
2 CONT
2 CONT 11. Elizabeth, contracted to Walter, grandson of John, Lord Drummond, which contract was discharged in January 1501-2.
2 CONT
2 CONT and married thirdly, Margaret Houston, widow of his nephew Robert, second Lord Lyle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: THE SCOTS PEERAGE, ed. by Sir James Balfour Paul, Vol IV, Edinburgh, 1906, pp. 276-8
