2 CONT
2 CONT 2020  Thousands of Rare Artifacts Discovered Beneath Tudor Manor’s Attic Floorboards Among the finds are manuscripts possibly used to perform illegal Catholic masses, silk fragments and handwritten music. As part of the site’s £6 
2 CONC million (roughly $7.8 million USD) roof restoration project, workers had lifted the floorboards in the estate’s attic for the first time in centuries. British nobleman Sir Edmund Bedingfeld built the manor house in 1482, reports B
2 CONC BC News. His descendants live in the home to this day. https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/thousands-rare-artifacts-discovered-underneath-attic-floorboards-tudor-manor-180975578/
2 CONT
2 CONT A rare haul of historic items has been discovered beneath the floorboards of Oxburgh Hall in Norfolk, England. The discovery was made during a project to reroof Oxburgh Hall, a moated manor house constructed by Sir Edmund Bedingfe
2 CONC ld after he inherited the estate in 1476.
2 CONT
2 CONT Researchers studying the items said that the variety, age, and significance of the items found, gives a unique insight into the history of the Oxburgh Hall. Independent archaeologist Matt Champion agreed to continue researching th
2 CONC e finds through lockdown on his own, and carried out a careful fingertip search.
2 CONT
2 CONT A page from a rare 15th-century illuminated manuscript was among the items recovered, many dating back to the Tudor period. Finds range from fragments of late 16th century books to high status Elizabethan textiles, as well as more
2 CONC  mundane modern objects such as cigarette packets and an empty box of Terry’s chocolates that date to the Second World War – which may have been hidden after the chocolates were eaten.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Forest the National Trust curator said: “When the boards came up, we could see a wave pattern in the debris which showed it had been undisturbed for centuries. The peak of each wave of dust, debris and objects was highest und
2 CONC er the crack between the boards. It was often inches thick and lay on top of a layer of lime plaster, which drew out the moisture from the debris and resulted in much of it being perfectly preserved over the centuries.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna continued: “One particular challenge was in areas with south facing windows, where hundreds of pins were found, so Matt had to use thick gloves when searching. The rooms, being well lit, had clearly been used for sewing, and 
2 CONC for organising correspondence with evidence of wax seals and fragments of late 18th-century handwritten documents in English and French.
2 CONT
2 CONT “The value of underfloor archaeology to our understanding of Oxburgh’s social history is enormous.”
2 CONT
2 CONT The star find was the 15th-century illuminated manuscript fragment on parchment spotted in the rubble of the eaves by one of the builders. Despite centuries amongst debris, the glimmer of gold leaf and bright blue of the illuminat
2 CONC ed initials was still vibrant.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna said: “The text is distinct enough for us to identify it as part of the Latin Vulgate Psalm 39 (“Expectans expectaui”). We contacted Dr James Freeman, Medieval Manuscripts Specialist at Cambridge University Library, who expla
2 CONC ined that the leaf may be from a Psalter, but its small size – just 8cm x 13cm – suggest it once was part of a Book of Hours. These portable prayer books were for private devotion.
2 CONT
2 CONT “The use of blue and gold for the minor initials, rather than the more standard blue and red, shows this would have been quite an expensive book to produce. It is tantalising to think that this could be a remnant of a splendid man
2 CONC uscript and we can’t help but wonder if it belonged to Sir Edmund Bedingfeld, the builder of Oxburgh Hall.”
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bedingfeld family at Oxburgh Hall were once rising stars of the Tudor Royal Court but Sir Henry Bedingfeld refused to sign the Act of Uniformity in 1559 – which outlawed Catholic Mass. The devout Catholic family stayed true to
2 CONC  their faith over centuries despite being ostracised and persecuted and even had a secret priest hole at Oxburgh to shelter Catholic clergy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The manuscript parchment and other objects found may well have been used in illegal masses and hidden deliberately by the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some tiny pieces of books have been identified including extracts from the 1590 edition of book one of “The ancient, famous and honourable history of Amadis de Gaule.”, a chivalric romance from the Iberian Peninsula, first written
2 CONC  c.1420. It is noted that Catholics of the period tended to read the romances set in Spain, especially since the books mention the mass.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the north west corner of the house, two ancient rats’ nests were found to contain over 200 individual fragments of high quality textiles including silk, velvet, satin, leather, wool and embroidered fabrics, which have been date
2 CONC d to between the second half of the 16th century and the 18th century. The quality of the pieces shows the status of those who lived in the house.
2 CONT
2 CONT The fragments may have been off cuts from clothes being reworked and given new uses. Highlights include a large piece of slashed brown silk shot through with gold, possibly from a sleeve, a woven fabric embellished with delicate w
2 CONC ool blackwork embroidery, a two-tone basket-weave clothing fabric with metallic thread which looks to be late 16th century in date, and pieces of a felted woollen textile which are similar to known examples from Tudor caps and sto
2 CONC ckings.
2 CONT
2 CONT The nests also contained some scraps of handwritten music from the 16th century just large enough to be identifiable as from a cantus or soprano part, possibly from a part book. An active musical life at Oxburgh is already known f
2 CONC rom an inventory of the house dated 1585 in which several musical instruments are listed. Music is likely to have been part of the secret masses held at the house with the presence of a secret chapel during this period at Oxburgh 
2 CONC often suggested.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most recent discovery, spotted in an attic void by a builder, is a complete book called the King’s Psalms dated 1568. Complete with its gilded leather binding, it is almost intact – a few fragments from it had previously been 
2 CONC found in a rats’ nest. Research into the book has just started.
2 CONT
2 CONT Russell Clement, General Manager at Oxburgh Hall said: “We had hoped to learn more of the history of the house during the reroofing work and have commissioned paint analysis, wallpaper research, and building and historic graffiti 
2 CONC recording. But these finds are far beyond anything we expected to see. These objects contain so many clues which confirm the history of the house as the retreat of a devout Catholic family, who retained their faith across the cent
2 CONC uries. We will be telling the story of the family and these finds in the house, now we have reopened again following lockdown.
2 CONT
2 CONT “This is a building which is giving up its secrets slowly. We don’t know what else we might come across – or what might remain hidden for future generations to reveal.”
2 CONT
2 CONT The £6m roof project was already underway when lockdown began and is supported by funds from The National Lottery Heritage Fund, The Wolfson Foundation, as well as the European Agricultural Fund for Rural Development through the L
2 CONC EADER programme, the Sylvia Waddilove Foundation UK and The Constance Travis Charitable Trust, as well as ongoing support from National Trust members and donors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Research into the finds is continuing to identify more of the fragments recovered and understand what they can add to the history of Oxburgh Hall.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:  HeritageDaily is a dedicated, independent publisher of the latest research and discoveries from across the academic community with a focus on archaeology, anthropology, palaeoanthropology and palaeontology.
2 CONT
2 CONT Added by Janet Milburn, 8/29/20
