2 CONT David II Bruce, King of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Father:      Robert I Bruce, King of Scotland (1274-1329)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother:      Lady Elizabeth de Burgh (~1289-1327)
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth:      5 Mar 1324      Dunfermline Abbey, Fife, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT “Anno MCCCXXIII…Anno isto, videlicet, quinto die Martii, die Lunae, in prima ebdomada Quadragesimae, post natus est David, filius regis Roberti et heres Scociae, qui successit patri in regnum, in monasterio de Dunfermlyn post comp
2 CONC letorium”;1 “David was born in Dunfermline Abbey on 5 March 1324, apparently the elder of twins. His younger brother, John, died, probably in infancy, and was buried at Restennet Priory”.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Death:      22 Feb 1371      Edinburgh Castle, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT “Anno Domini MCCCLXX, in festo cathedrae sancti Petri obiit David de Bruysse, rex Scociae, in castro de Edinburgh”;1 “On 22 February 1371 David died suddenly at Edinburgh Castle”.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial:      Holyrood Abbey, Edinburgh
2 CONT
2 CONT “Et sepultus est in monasterio Sanctae Crucis”;1 “He was buried in Holyrood Abbey”.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation:      King of Scotland 1329-1371
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 1:
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Joan ‘of the Tower’ Plantagenet
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage:      17 Jul 1328      Berwick-On-Tweed, Northumberland
2 CONT
2 CONT “Early in July, the queen-mother, accompanied by Mortimer, her son Prince John, her two daughters, Earl Warren, the Bishops of Lincoln, Ely, and Norwich, and attended by a brilliant suite, set forth towards Berwick. Robert Bruce w
2 CONC as represented by two commissioners, the Earls of Douglas and Murray, who had charge of the young bridegroom. The ceremony of betrothal was performed on Sunday, July 17th. The dates assigned to the marriage are various. Gisborne’s
2 CONC  ‘Chronicle of Edward III’, gives July 12th (Cotton MS. Nero D. ii., fol. 272). Several other writers name St Mary Magdalen’s Day, July 22nd, but four independent, contemporaneous authorities: two French chronicles, written in the
2 CONC  time of Edward III (Cotton MSSS., Domitian x., f.85 and Cleop. A. vii., f.84), ‘Chron. Lanercost’, p. 261, and the continuation of Paris, p. 2502, name ‘the Sunday before St Margaret’s Day’, which Sunday fell that year on July 17
2 CONC th. Adam of Murimuth and three other sources all name July 17th”.3
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 2:
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Drummond
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth:      abt 1340
2 CONT
2 CONT “Born  c.1340”.5
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage:      20 Feb 1364      Inchmurdach [Kenly Green], Fife, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT “King David II. married, as his second wife, Margaret, daughter of Sir Malcolm Drummond, and widow of Sir John Logie of that Ilk, at Inchmurdach in Fife, before the 6th of December 1363 (Fordun, Annals, 185; Scotichron, ii. 370, b
2 CONC k. xiv. c.28, p. 379, bk xiv c.34; Wyntoun, ii. 506, bk. viii, c.46, Apr. 1363; Book of Pluscarden, bk. ix. c.46; Scalachron., 203; Cal. Doc. Scot., iv., No. 93; Extracta, 190)”;4 “Margaret then married David II of Scotland at Inc
2 CONC hmurdoch in Fife, on 20 February 1364”.5 Presumably ‘1364’ should be ‘1363’.
2 CONT
2 CONT Divorce:      20 Mar 1369      Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT “King David II. obtained a divorce from his second wife ‘Margareta de Logy,’ about the 20th of March 1369-70 (Scotichron., ii. 379, bk. xiv. c.34; Wyntoun, ii. 506, bk. viii. c.46, l. 7176; Book of Pluscarden, bk. ix. c.46; Peerag
2 CONC e and Consistorial Law, ii. 982-987 [the whole case stated])”.4
2 CONT
2 CONT Death:      1375
2 CONT
2 CONT “She survived the king, and was alive on the 31st of January 1374-5, but seems to have died soon after that date (Cal. Doc. Scot., iv., Preface, pp. xv, xvi, p. 44, Nos. 193, 197; p. 401, No. 17; Foedera, iii. 948; Peerage and Con
2 CONC sistorial Law, ii. 982-987 [the whole case stated])”.4
2 CONT
2 CONT ———————————————————————————————————————————————
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Johannis de Fordun Chronica Gentis Scotorum, William F. Skerne (ed.), Historians of Scotland Volume 1, Edmonston and Douglas (Edinburgh: 1871), pp. 351, 382.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Bruce Webster, “David II (1324–1371)”, Oxford Dictionary of National Biography, 2004.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Mary Anne Everett Green, Lives of the Princesses of England from the Norman Conquest, Volume 3 (London: 1851), pp. 104-105.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Sir Archibald Hamilton Dunbar, Scottish Kings: A Revised Chronology of Scottish History, 1005-1625, With Notices of the Principal Events, Tables of Regnal Years, Pedigrees, Tables, Calendars, etc., David Douglas (Edinburgh: 190
2 CONC 6), pp. 154, 156.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia, “Margaret Drummond, Queen of Scotland”, http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_Drummond_(Queen), 26 February 2013.
