2 CONT 1.  David reigned from 1329 to 1370. Unknown GEDCOM info: MH:N142 Unknown GEDCOM info: D3B12C64-C89E-44AB-8636-3E1E8311D82B
1 NOTE 1. boy King of Scotland; m. as infant to Princess Joan, young sister of King Edward III of England)
1 NOTE He succeeded his father at only five years of age, but soon after his 
2 CONT coronation (1331) he was deposed by Edward de Baliol, an English-backed 
2 CONT claimant to the throne. For the next eight years David lived in exile in 
2 CONT France. In 1341 he returned to Scotland, and five years later went to war 
2 CONT with England as an ally of France. The English defeated both the French 
2 CONT (at Crécy) and the Scots (at Neville's Cross, October 1346), taking David 
2 CONT prisoner and capturing large parts of Scotland and France. David remained 
2 CONT a prisoner in England for 11 years. In 1357 he was freed in return for the 
2 CONT promise of a ransom, and thereafter enjoyed friendly relations with the 
2 CONT English. David was succeeded by his nephew Robert II, founder of the 
2 CONT Stuart dynasty.
1 NOTE David II King of Scotland
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=40089735-2cd5-4bcf-989a-ab7106464198&tid=822673&pid=-1383412952
1 NOTE KNOWN AS "DAVID THE BRUCE"; KING OF SCOTS 1329-8/1332, 12/1332-1333, 1336-1371;
2 CONT EARL OF CARRICK
1 NOTE David II (March 5, 1324  February 22, 1371) king of Scotland, son of King Robert the Bruce by his second wife, Elizabeth de Burgh (d. 1327), was born at Dunfermline Palace, Fife.
2 CONT
2 CONT In accordance with the terms of the Treaty of Northampton he was married on 17 July 1328 to Joan of the Tower (d. 1362), daughter of Edward II of England and Isabella of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT David became king of Scotland upon the death of his father on June 7, 1329. He was crowned at Scone in November 1331.
2 CONT
2 CONT Owing to the victory of Edward III of England and his protégé, Edward Balliol, at Halidon Hill in July 1333, David and his queen were sent for safety into France, reaching Boulogne in May 1334, and being received very graciously
2 CONC  by the French king, Philip VI. Little is known about the life of the Scottish king in France, except that Château-Gaillard was given to him for a residence, and that he was present at the bloodless meeting of the English and Fre
2 CONC nch armies at Vironfosse in October 1339.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile his representatives had obtained the upper hand in Scotland, and David was thus enabled to return to his kingdom in June 1341, when he took the reins of government into his own hands. In 1346 under the terms of the Auld 
2 CONC Alliance, he invaded England in the interests of France, but was defeated and taken prisoner at the Battle of Neville's Cross on October 17 of that year, and remained in England for eleven years, living principally in London and a
2 CONC t Odiham in Hampshire. His imprisonment was not a rigorous one, and negotiations for his release were soon begun. Eventually, in October 1357, after several interruptions, a treaty was signed at Berwick by which the Scottish estat
2 CONC es undertook to pay 100,000 marks as a ransom for their king.
2 CONT
2 CONT David, who had possibly recognized Edward III as his feudal superior, returned at once to Scotland; but owing to the poverty of the kingdom it was found impossible to raise the ransom. A few instalments were paid, but the king sou
2 CONC ght to get rid of the liability by offering to make Edward III, or one of his sons, his successor in Scotland. In 1364 the Scottish parliament indignantly rejected a proposal to make Lionel, duke of Clarence, the next king; but Da
2 CONC vid negotiated secretly with Edward III over this matter, after he had suppressed a rising of some of his unruly nobles.
2 CONT
2 CONT The king died in Edinburgh Castle in 1371. His second wife was Margaret, née Drummond, widow of Sir John Logie, whom he divorced in 1369. At the time of his death he was planning to marry his mistress Agnes Dunbar, daughter of Ag
2 CONC nes Dunbar, 4th Countess of Moray. He left no children, and was succeeded by his nephew, Robert II. David was not one of the more successful monarchs in Scottish history, but he has suffered from comparison with his illustrious fa
2 CONC ther, and he was king under difficult objective conditions
1 NOTE David II (1324-71), king of Scotland, succeeded his father, Robert Bruce, in 1329. After the English victory at Halidon Hill in 1333 he was sent to France (1334), where he remained till 1341. In 1346 he invaded England, but was di
2 CONC sastrously defeated at Neville�s Cross and taken prisoner. [World Wide Illustrated Encyclopedia, 1935]
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl of Carrick before his succession to the crown. He died without issue. {Burke�s Peerage} [GADD.GED]
1 NOTE He ruled from 1329 to 1371.
