1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sir Thomas Erskine of Erskine (<1354 - between 1403–1404), Keeper of Edinburgh Castle and Sheriff of Edinburgh'''
2 CONT s/o Sir Robert Erskine
2 CONT x Mary Douglas, heiress of Sir William Douglas ("Knight of Liddesdale"), died in childbirth.
2 CONT xx Janet Keith or Barclay, widow of Sir David Barclay of Brechin
2 CONT * Sir Robert, his successor.
2 CONT * John
2 CONT * Elizabeth
2 CONT ----
2 CONT =Sir Thomas Erskine of Erskine (d. between 1403–1404)
2 CONT Sir Thomas Erskine, eldest son of Sir Robert Erskine, first appears in records in 1354, likely as the "son and heir" of Sir Robert, when proposed as a hostage for King David II. He served again as a hostage in 1357 upon King David
2 CONC ’s final release, placed in the custody of John de Coupland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1365 and 1370, Thomas was knighted under unusual circumstances: his first wife, '''Mary Douglas, heiress of Sir William Douglas ("Knight of Liddesdale"), died in childbirth.''' A dispute followed with James Douglas, the ne
2 CONC xt heir, over whether the child was born alive. The question of inheritance rights was set to be decided by trial by combat in Edinburgh before the King. Both men were knighted—Thomas by his father and Douglas by Sir Archibald Dou
2 CONC glas—just before fighting. However, the King intervened, and eventually Thomas accepted financial compensation, relinquishing his claim.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1371, Thomas was '''Keeper of Edinburgh Castle and Sheriff of Edinburgh''', and participated in confirming the Act of Succession in favor of the Earl of Carrick. He was granted charters for the barony of Dun in 1376 and receive
2 CONC d annuity payments from customs in Linlithgow and Aberdeen.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1370, he married his second wife, '''Janet Keith or Barclay, widow of Sir David Barclay of Brechin.''' Through Janet, the Erskine family later claimed a right to the Earldom of Mar, asserting she was daughter of Sir Edward Keit
2 CONC h and Christian Menteith, granddaughter of Gratney, Earl of Mar. Though some sources question Janet's parentage, 1447 testimony by Andrew Keith of Inverugie, aged 80, identified her as Edward Keith’s daughter. Additional circumsta
2 CONC ntial support includes Janet’s possession of lands tied to her alleged maternal family and the continued annuity payments after her death to her son, which some argued were linked to lands in Arran once held by Sir John Menteith.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1390–91, during Parliament, Sir Thomas famously protested to King Robert III against any royal endorsement of agreements that might prejudice his wife’s claim to the Earldom of Mar and Lordship of Garioch. The King promised not
2 CONC  to confirm any such arrangements. This was reaffirmed in 1393, declaring that any royal confirmation of a resignation by Countess Isabella Douglas would be null and void if it affected the Erskines’ rightful succession.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Thomas and Janet were granted a portable altar by Pope Clement VII in 1392–93. He was captured at the Battle of Homildon Hill on 14 September 1402 and likely died in English captivity between Martinmas 1403 and Whitsunday 1404
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue of Sir Thomas Erskine
2 CONT By his second wife, Janet Keith or Barclay, he had:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sir Robert Erskine, his successor.
2 CONT * John Erskine, granted the lands of Dun in 1392 by King Robert III; progenitor of the Erskines of Dun and Pittodrie.
2 CONT * Elizabeth Erskine, who married Duncan Wemyss of Rires. The couple received a charter of Pirchock and Ludquhairn between 1398 and 1400.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Thomas’s widow died soon after Whitsunday 1413, after which her annuity passed to their son.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Reference
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun05paul/page/592/mode/2up James Balfour Paul, ed., ''The Scots Peerage'', vol. 5 (Edinburgh: D. Douglas, 1904), p596-600]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT * from http://www.erskinclan.com/gene4.html
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. THOMAS (ERSKINE), LORD ERSKINE, only s. and h., calling himself (like his father) EARL OF MAR. However, by an assize of error, 15 May, confirmed by Parl. 5 Nov. 1457, the Earldom of Mar 6 was declared to have devolved on the Cr
2 CONC own on the death of Earl Alexander in 1435, and the service of Lord Erskine in 1438 as h. to the Countess Isabel was reduced. Knighted before 24 Jan. 1440/1. He had charter of the lands of Dalnotter in Lennox, 5 Jan. 1458/9, as Lo
2 CONC rd Erskine, and, as such, sat in Parl. 14 Oct.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1467. Sheriff of Stirling 1483. He took the part of James III insurrection of 1488. He m., before 1445, Janet Douglas.7 She was living Aug. 1489, when her life rent was reserved. He d.in or shortly before 1493.
2 CONT
2 CONT * from http://thepeerage.com/p2588.htm#i25871
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Erskine, 2nd Lord Erskine was the son of Sir Robert Erskine of that Ilk, 1st Lord Erskine and Elizabeth Lindsay. He married Janet Douglas before 1445.2 He died after 1493.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Erskine, 2nd Lord Erskine gained the title of 2nd Lord Erskine. He succeeded to the title of 13th Earl of Mar [S., c. 1115] between 7 September 1451 and 6 November 1452, de jure.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Thomas Erskine, 2nd Lord Erskine
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Helen Erskine+3
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Thomas Erskine, 2nd Lord Erskine and Janet Douglas
2 CONT
2 CONT # Alexander Erskine, 3rd Lord Erskine+ d. bt 1508 - 1509
2 CONT # Isabel Erskine+
2 CONT # Margaret Erskine+4
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT # [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinc
2 CONC t or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume VIII, page 417. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT # [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume V, page 105.
2 CONT # [S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003). Hereinafter cited as Burke's Peerage and Baronetage,
2 CONC  107th edition.
2 CONT # [S1916] Tim Boyle, "re: Boyle Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger Lundy, 16 September 2006. Hereinafter cited as "re: Boyle Family."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sir Thomas Erskine, knight, succeeded his father, as governor of Stirling castle, and in 1392 was sent ambassador to England. By his marriage with Janet Keith, great-grand-daughter of Gratney, eleventh earl of Mar, he laid the fou
2 CONC ndation of the succession on the part of his descendants to the earldom of Mar and lordship of Garioch.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.venitap.com/Genealogy/WebCards/ps31/ps31_445.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Thomas Erskine, the eldest son of Sir Robert is first named in 1354, if it be he who in that year, as ‘son and heir’ of Sir Robert Erskine, was proposed as one of the hostages for King David II. Three years later, Thomas Erski
2 CONC ne, on King David’s final release, was again a hostage, and was committed to the care of John de Coupland (where not stated), on 2 October 1357. Sometime between 1365 and 1370, he was made a knight under somwhat peculiar circumsta
2 CONC nces.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first wife, the heiress of Dalkeith, of whom later, died at the birth of her first child, and there was a difference of opinion as to whether the child was born living or not. Erskine claimed the liferent of his wife’s estate 
2 CONC on the plea that the child lived, while James Douglas, the next heir, claimed the estates on the ground that the child died, and the partied resolved to decide the matter by personal combat. The duel was to take place at Edinburgh
2 CONC  in presence of the King, and just before they entered the lists the parties were knight, James Douglas by Sir Archibal Douglas, ‘the Grim,’ and Thomas Erskine by his own father. Then they fought, but were separated by the royal c
2 CONC ommand and led out of the lists. No agreement could be effected, and they were again about to engage, when the King’s influence prevailed and the matter was arranged. Sir Thomas Erskine accepted a sum of money in lieu of his right
2 CONC s, and Sir James Douglas obtained his estates in dispute.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Thomas, prior to 1371, was keeper of Edinburgh Castle and also Sheriff of Edinburgh. He was in that year a consenter to the Act of Succession passed in favour of the Earl of Carrick. ... On 8 Nov 1376 he had a royal charter of
2 CONC  the barony of Dun, co. Forfar, resigned to him by his father. He frequently appears as a witness to royal charters, which indicates attendance at court, and on 27 Jan 1398/9, when David, Duke of Rothesay, was made Lieutenant-Gene
2 CONC ral of the kingdom, Sir thomas Erskine was one of those appointed to be his special advisers.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was on 18 March 1390/1 that, during the sitting of Parliament, he made his famous protest to King Robert III on behalf of his then wife, Dame Janet Keith or Barclay. ... On 22 November 1393 the King renewed his promise in a wid
2 CONC er form, granting to Sir Thomas that Although Isabell Douglas, countess of Mar, because of information or agreement with any one, should be willing to resign the earldom or any par of it, or any lands elsewhere to which the heirs 
2 CONC of Thomas ought to succeed, or to make resignations of the same to any one in prejudice of her true heirs, the royal consent or confirmation would not be given, or if given by mistake should be null and void.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 2 February 1392/3 Sir Thomas and his wife wer granted a portable altar by Pope Clement VII.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Thomas was taken prisoner at Homildon on 14 September 1402, and may have remained a captive in England until his death, which took place between Martinmass 1403, when his pension of £100 was paid to himself, and Whitsunday 140
2 CONC 4, when his pension was paid to his widow.
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) Sir Thomas Erskine married, first, about 1365, Mary Douglas, only child and heiress of Sir William Douglas, known as the “Knight of Liddesdale.” She died in childbed, and Sir Thomas was obliged to give up possession of her est
2 CONC ates to the next heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) He married secondly, before 13 Apr 1370, Janet Keith or Barclay, widow of Sir David Barclay of Brechin. It was on her account he protested in 1390/1, regarding the earldom of Mar, that one-half of the earldom of Mar and of the
2 CONC  lordship of Garioch pertained to his wife in right of heritage. She is therefore the most importland between the ancient and the modern Earls of Mar, and it may be well again to examine on what her claim rests. ... [See notes for
2 CONC  Janet Keith]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Thomas Erskine and his second wife had issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Sir Robert who succeeded 2. John, who received the lands of Dun from his father , and had a charter from King Robert III on 25 October 1392. He was the ancestor of the Erskines of Dun and of Pittodrie, 3. Elizabeth, whom it was
2 CONC  proposed to marry to Alexander Seton, afterwards Lord of Gordon, but she was married to Duncan Wemyss of Rires. She and her spouse had from her father and mother a charter of the lands of Pirchock and Ludquharn, dated between 139
2 CONC 8 and 1400. They had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: THE SCOTS PEERAGE, ed. by Sir James Balfour Paul, Vol V, Edinburgh, 1906, pp. 596-601
2 CONT ______________________________________________________________________
