1 NOTE {geni:about_me} >====The Peerage, Database Online: [https://thepeerage.com/p2408.htm#i24078 Andrew Gray, 1st Lord Gray]
2 CONT >Last Edited=30 Apr 2011
2 CONT >M, #24078 
2 CONT >b. circa 1390
2 CONT >d. circa 1469
2 CONT >Andrew Gray, 1st Lord Gray was born circa 1390.{1}
2 CONT >He was the son of Sir Andrew Gray of Broxmouth and Janet Mortimer.{1}
2 CONT >A contract for the marriage of Andrew Gray, 1st Lord Gray and Elizabeth Wemyss was signed on 31 August 1418 at Foulis, Perthshire, Scotland.{2}
2 CONT >He died circa 1469.{1}
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/GLT6-6M1
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Gray-5341
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Andrew Gray, 1st Lord Gray and Elizabeth Wemyss:
2 CONT 1. Margaret Gray+{3}
2 CONT 2. Patrick Gray, Master of Gray+ d. bt 23 Aug 1463 - Sep 1464 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Citations''' 
2 CONT '''1.''' Charles Mosley, editor, ''Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage'', 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 2, page 1642. 
2 CONT '''2.''' G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, ''The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Ex
2 CONC tinct or Dormant'',... Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), Volume VI, page 99.
2 CONT '''3.''' Charles Mosley, editor,'' Burke's Peerage and Baronetage'', 106th edition, 2 volumes (Crans, Switzerland: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 1999), Volume 1, page 133.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Mosley, C. S. (ed.) (1999) ''Burke’s Peerage & Baronetage''. 106th ed. 
2 CONT >Crans, Switzerland: Morris Genealogical Books. Available at: [https://archive.org/details/burkespeeragebar0001unse/page/1210/mode/1up?q= '''Archive.org, p. 1210''']:– "Andrew Gray, 1st Lord Gray (S), so cr by 5 july 1445; b c 139
2 CONC 0; hostage with English for JAMES I 1424, Commr to England 1449 and 1451; Master Household 1452; Warden Marches 1459; m (contract 31 Aug 1418) Elizabeth, dau of Sir John Wemyss of Wemyss (see WEMYSS and MARCH, E), and d c 1469, ha
2 CONC ving had with other issue including at least one dau (Margaret, m 1st William Murray of Tullibardine (see ATHOLL, D), m. 2nd George Clephane of Carslogie):..."
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Wikisource: ''Dictionary of National Biography'', 1885-1900, Volume 23:
2 CONT >'''[https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1885-1900/Gray,_Andrew_(1380%3F-1469) Gray, Andrew (1380?-1469)], by Henry Paton'''
2 CONT
2 CONT "['''Andrew Gray, 1ˢᵗ Lord Gray'''] was the only son of Sir Andrew Gray of Fowlis, Perthshire, by his first wife, Janet, daughter of Sir Roger de Mortimer, whom he married in 1377. He is usually styled second Lord Gray, and the cr
2 CONC eation of the title is said to have taken place in 1437 in the person of his father. But this is now recognised as a mistake (Burke, Peerage, voce ‘Moray’). The title was not created until 1445. Sir Andrew Gray, who died before 17
2 CONC  July 1445, is referred to by his son Andrew in a charter of that date, as well as in a later deed, dated 16 Jan. 1449–50, as deceased, and under the designation merely of Sir Andrew Gray, knight, the rank he held at the time of h
2 CONC is death (Registrum Magni Sigilli, ii. No. 767; Peerage of Scotland, Wood's edit., i. 666).
2 CONT
2 CONT "Andrew Gray the younger of Fowlis was accepted in 1424 by the English government as one of the hostages for the payment of the ransom of James I of Scotland, apparently in place of his father, whose estate is estimated at the tim
2 CONC e as being worth six hundred merks yearly. His father presented a letter to the English government, in which the hostage is said to be his only son and heir, promising fidelity on behalf of his son, and also that he would not disi
2 CONC nherit him on account of his acting as a hostage (Fœdera, Hague ed. iv. pt. iv. 112). Young Gray was then sent to the castle of Pontefract, and was afterwards committed to the custody of the constable of the Tower of London, with 
2 CONC whom he remained until 1427, when he was exchanged for Malcolm Fleming, son of the laird of Cumbernauld. In 1436 he accompanied Princess Margaret of Scotland to France, on the occasion of her marriage to the dauphin. On 1 July 144
2 CONC 5 occurs the first reference to him as Lord Gray of Fowlis (Acts of the Parliaments of Scotland, ii. 60; cf. Exchequer Rolls, v. 198). In June 1444 he is mentioned in the customs accounts as simply Sir Andrew Gray of Fowlis. As th
2 CONC e title of Lord Gray occurs on the union roll of the Scottish peers immediately after that of Lord Saltoun, which was created on 28 June 1445, Sir Andrew Gray was doubtless created a peer by the title of Lord Gray of Fowlis on the
2 CONC  same occasion.
2 CONT
2 CONT "In 1449 Lord Gray was appointed one of a parliamentary committee to examine previous acts of parliament and general councils, and report to next parliament their existing validity. On various occasions between that year and 1460 
2 CONC he was employed as one of the Scottish ambassadors to negotiate treaties of peace and truce with England, and of these treaties he was generally appointed a conservator. He acted too in the capacity of warden of the marches. In 14
2 CONC 51, along with the abbot of Melrose and others, he received a safe-conduct to enable him to make a pilgrimage to Canterbury, and in the following year he became master of the household to James II. On 26 Aug. 1452 the king granted
2 CONC  him a license to build a castle on any part of his lands, and he built Castle Huntly on his estate of Longforgan in the carse of Gowrie. This castle was long the residence of the family. On being sold to the Earl of Strathmore in
2 CONC  1615, its name was changed to Castle Lyon. It was, however, repurchased in 1777 by George Paterson, who married Anne, daughter of John, eleventh baron Gray, and restored the original name to the castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Gray in 1455 was one of the nobles who sealed the process of forfeiture against the Earl of Douglas. In the following year the abbot of Scone sued him for paying the dues of Inchmartin in bad grain. He took an active part in parl
2 CONC iamentary work, and in 1464 was appointed one of the lords auditors for hearing and determining civil causes. He accompanied James III to Berwick, by appointment of parliament, 5 March 1464–5, where he with others had the plenary 
2 CONC authority of parliament to ratify the truce which was being negotiated between the Scottish and English ambassadors at Newcastle. He died in 1469, probably towards the end of that year, being mentioned as deceased in the precept o
2 CONC f clare constat granted by David, earl of Crawford, to his grandson and successor, on 20 Jan. 1469–70.
2 CONT
2 CONT "He married, by contract dated 31 Aug. 1418, Elizabeth, eldest daughter of Sir John Wemyss of Wemyss and Reres, with whom it was stipulated he should receive as dowry a 20l. land in Strathardle, Perthshire. Failure in observing th
2 CONC is condition gave rise to litigation between the two families at a later date (Memorials of the Family of Wemyss of Wemyss, by Sir William Fraser, i. 66, 67, 75). Elizabeth Wemyss survived Lord Gray. They had issue two sons and tw
2 CONC o daughters:
2 CONT (1) Sir Patrick Gray of Kinneff, who married Annabella, daughter of Alexander, lord Forbes, and obtained from his father certain lands in Kincardineshire; he predeceased his father, but left a son, Andrew, who succeeded his grandf
2 CONC ather as second Lord Gray; 
2 CONT (2) Andrew, ancestor of the families of Gray of Schives and Pittendrum; 
2 CONT (3) Margaret, who married Robert, lord Lyle; 
2 CONT (4) Christian, who married James Crichton of Strathurd.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources:''' 
2 CONT ''Acts of the Parliaments of Scotland'', ii. 36–195, xii. 30;
2 CONT ''Acta Auditorum'', pp. 3, 6;
2 CONT ''Registrum Magni Sigilli'', vol. ii. passim;
2 CONT ''Exchequer Rolls of Scotland'', vols. iv–viii.;
2 CONT ''Rotuli Scotiæ'', ii. 245–458;
2 CONT ''Rymer's Fœdera'', Hague ed., iv. pt. iv. 102–30, v. pt. ii. 11–89.
2 CONT
2 CONT >====en.Wikipedia: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Gray,_1st_Lord_Gray Andrew Gray, 1st Lord Gray] (c. 1390–1469):
2 CONT >was a Scottish nobleman, politician and diplomat. He was succeeded in the title by his grandson.[1]
2 CONT >"'''He was the only son''' of Sir Andrew Gray (d. 1445) of Fowlis, Perthshire, by his first wife Janet, daughter of Sir Roger de Mortimer, whom he married in 1377.[2]
2 CONT >"'''In 1424''', he was accepted by the English government as one of the hostages for the payment of the ransom of James I of Scotland, apparently in place of his father, whose estate was estimated at the time as being worth six h
2 CONC undred merks annually. His father presented a letter to the English government, in which the hostage is said to be his only son and heir, promising fidelity on behalf of his son, and also that he would not disinherit him on accoun
2 CONC t of his acting as a hostage. He was sent to Pontefract Castle, and afterwards committed to the Tower of London, where he remained until 1427, when he was exchanged for Malcolm Fleming, son of the laird of Cumbernauld.[3]
2 CONT >"'''In 1436''', he accompanied Princess Margaret, daughter of James I, to France, for her marriage to the Dauphin Louis. In 1449 he was appointed part of a committee of the Estates to examine previous acts of Parliament and gener
2 CONC al councils, and report to Parliament on their existing validity. On various occasions between 1449 and 1460 he was employed as one of the Scottish ambassadors to negotiate treaties of peace with England, and appointed as a genera
2 CONC l conservator of these treaties. He briefly acted as a Warden of the Marches.
2 CONT >"'''In 1451''', along with the abbot of Melrose Abbey, he received a safe-conduct to allow him to make a pilgrimage to Canterbury, and in 1452 he became Master of the Household to James II.
2 CONT >"'''On 26 August that year,''' he was granted permission to build a castle on any part of his lands, and he built Castle Huntly on his estate of Longforgan in the carse of Gowrie (not to be confused with the older Huntly Castle, 
2 CONC in Aberdeenshire). This castle, long the residence of the family, was sold to the Earl of Strathmore in 1615, and the name changed to Castle Lyon. In 1777, it was repurchased by George Paterson, who married Anne Gray, daughter of 
2 CONC the 11th Lord Gray, and restored the original name.
2 CONT >"'''In 1455''', he was one of the nobles who secured the forfeiture of the Earl of Douglas. In the following year, the abbot of Scone sued him for paying the dues of Inchmartin in bad grain. He took an active part in parliamentar
2 CONC y work, and in 1464 was appointed one of the lords auditors for hearing and determining civil causes. He accompanied James III to Berwick, where he had the authority of Parliament to ratify the truce with England being negotiated 
2 CONC at Newcastle. He died in 1469, probably towards the end of the year; he is mentioned as deceased in a document of 20 January 1469–70.
2 CONT >"'''He married, on 31 August 1418, Elizabeth Wemyss''', the eldest daughter of Sir John Wemyss of Wemyss and Reres, with whom it was stipulated he should receive as dowry a £30 land in Strathardle, Perthshire. This condition was 
2 CONC not observed, and gave rise to litigation at a later date. His wife survived him.
2 CONT >"'''They had two sons and two daughters.''' The elder son, Sir Patrick Gray of Kineff, predeceased his father. He had married Anabella Forbes, daughter of Alexander Forbes, 1st Lord Forbes, and had one son, who became Andrew Gray
2 CONC , 2nd Lord Gray. The other three children were the younger son Andrew, and daughters Margaret (married Robert, Lord Lyle) and Christian (married James Crighton/Crichton of Strathurd).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Citations'''
2 CONT '''1.''' ''Ancient Capital of Scotland: The Story of Perth from the Invasion.''.., Volume 1 By Samuel Cowan, p. 186, (New York, 1904).
2 CONT '''2.''' Burke's ''Peerage and Baronetage'', 76th edition, p. 894, (London, 1914).
2 CONT '''3.''' Anderson, William (1877). ''The Scottish Nation: or, The Surnames, Families, Literature, Honours, and Biographical History of the People of Scotland''. Vol. 2. A. Fullarton & Co. Available at Archive.org:  [https://archiv
2 CONC e.org/details/scottishnationor02ande/page/370/mode/2up '''p. 371'''].
2 CONT '''4.''' Paton 1890.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Sources
2 CONT * Anderson, William (1877). ''The Scottish Nation: or, The Surnames, Families, Literature, Honours, and Biographical History of the People of Scotland''. Vol. 2. A. Fullarton & co. p. 371.
2 CONT * Chisholm, Hugh, ed. (1911). "Gray, Patrick Gray, 6th Baron". ''Encyclopædia Britannica''. Vol. 12 (11th ed.). Cambridge University Press. pp. 391–392.
2 CONT * Paton, Henry (1890). "Gray, Andrew (1380?-1469)". In Stephen, Leslie; Lee, Sidney (eds.). ''Dictionary of National Biography''. Vol. 23. London: Smith, Elder & Co. pp. 1–2.
