1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Sir Robert Graham of Morphie1 
2 CONT *M, #109606 Last Edited=4 Sep 2008
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert Graham of Morphie lived at Morphie, Kincardineshire, Scotland.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Sir Robert Graham of Morphie 
2 CONT # Egidia Graham+1 d. a 1606.  Married William Douglas, 9th Earl of Angus 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Pinkie, also known as the Battle of Pinkie Cleugh  took place on 10 September 1547 on the banks of the River Esk near Musselburgh, Scotland. The last pitched battle between Scotland and England before the Union of th
2 CONC e Crowns, it was part of the conflict known as the Rough Wooing. It was a catastrophic defeat for Scotland, where it became known as "Black Saturday".
2 CONT
2 CONT Musselburgh lies just five miles east of Edinburgh 
2 CONT
2 CONT East Lothian  is one of the 32 council areas of Scotland, as well as a historic county, registration county and lieutenancy area. The county was called Haddingtonshire until 1921.  In 1975, the historic county was incorporated for
2 CONC  local government purposes into Lothian Region as East Lothian District, with some slight alterations of its boundaries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Midlothian is a historic county of Scotland. It emerged in the Middle Ages as the territory surrounding the city of Edinburgh within the wider Lothian region, and was formally called the "shire of Edinburgh" or Edinburghshire unti
2 CONC l the 20th century.  Midlothian County Council was abolished in 1975 under the Local Government (Scotland) Act 1973,  Transferred to East Lothian Musselburgh,  Fisherrow, Inveresk, Musselburgh, Wallyford, Whitecraig
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT #http://www.thepeerage.com/p10961.htm#i109606 cites 
2 CONT ## [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extin
2 CONC ct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume I, page 159. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT # https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Douglas,_9th_Earl_of_Angus “ The earl married Giles, daughter of Sir Robert Graham of Morphie, and had six sons and four daughters.”
