2 CONT The PEDIGREE of John 'the Gentle' (4th of Freuchie) GRANT
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd of Rothiemurchus; of MULBEN; (Leslie's 2nd husband) Born: ? Died: 1585
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage brought yet more power for the Grants when Sir John Grant married Maud, the daughter of Gilbert of Glencairnie. Maud was heiress of the cadet branch of the ancient Celtic Stathearn dynasty, an Earldom older than written r
2 CONC ecords. Their eldest son was the first of the Grants of Freuchie, while a younger son was the progenitor of the Tullochgorm branch. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     IDENTITY: Called 'the Gentle.' He was retoured hier to his father in the lands of Glencairnie and Ballindalloch at Elgin on 6 October 1553, aud in the lands of Freuchie and others. On the 25 October 1557 he had a charter of th
2 CONC e lands of Parkhill, co. Fife, on the forfeiture of John Leslie. 
2 CONT     KINSHIP: 1st son.
2 CONT
2 CONT     CHURCH: Appointed successor to his father in the office of Ballie of the Abbey of Kinloss 1554.
2 CONT
2 CONT     TITLES: Laird of Freuchie.
2 CONT
2 CONT     RESIDENCE: Fourth of Freuchie.
2 CONT     RESIDENCE: During his father's life designed 'of Mulben.'
2 CONT     PROPERTY: Retoured heir to his father in the lands of Glencarnie and Ballindalloch at Elgin and in the lands of Freuchie and others 1553.
2 CONT     PROPERTY: Had a charter of the lands of Parkhill, Fifeshire 1557.
2 CONT     PROPERTY: Acting on an order by Queen Mary, he demanded and took the custody of Drummin Castle, the keepers having fled 1562.
2 CONT     PROPERTY: Was bestowed the abbacy of Kinloss 1568/1569.
2 CONT     PROPERTY: By deed, owing to his increasing ill-health he gave his grandson and apparent heir John Grant, a great part of his lands and estates 1584.
2 CONT     POLITICS: Signed the bond in support of the Queen 1568.
2 CONT     PUNISHMENT: Remission> Received from Queen Mary a remission for joining Matthew, Earl of Lennox, on Glasgow Moor, in 1544, 1552.
2 CONT
2 CONT     BURIAL: Will directed burial to be buried in the Kirk of Dowthall in Bogbegis Yll.
2 CONT     PROBATE: Will dated 24 Nov 1584 Baliachastell; will proved 15 Mar 1586 Edinburgh, Midlothian, Scotland.
2 CONT
