2 CONT Notes 	 1 - John Mackenzie of Kintail 'was a great courtier with Queen Mary. He feued much of the lands of Brae Ross. When the queen sent her servants to know the condition of the gentry of Ross, they came to his house of Killin; 
2 CONC but before their coming he had gotten intelligence that it was to find out the condition of the gentry of Ross that they were coming; whilk made' him cause his servants to put ane great fire of fresh arn [alder] wood when they cam
2 CONC e, to make a great reek; also he caused kill a great bull in their presence; whilk was put altogether into ane kettle to their supper. When the supper came, there were a half-dozen great dogs present, to sup the broth of the bull,
2 CONC  whilk put all the house through-other with their tulyie. When they ended the supper, ilk ane lay where they were. The gentlemen thought they had gotten purgatory on earth and came away as soon as it was day; but when they came to
2 CONC  the houses of Balnagowan, and Foulis, and Milton, they were feasted like princes. 'When they went back to the queen, she asked who the ablest men were they saw in Ross. They answered: "They were all able men, except that man that
2 CONC  was her majesty's great courtier, Mackenzie— that he did both eat and lie with his dogs." "Truly," said the queen, "it were a pity of his poverty— he is the best man of them all." Then the queen did call for all the gentry of Ros
2 CONC s to take their land in feu, when Mackenzie got the cheap feu, and more for his thousand merks than any of the rest got for five."
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 - The Mackenzies held their lands of the Earls of Ross and afterwards of the Duke of Ross till 1508, when they were all erected into a barony by King James the Fourth, who gave them a crown charter. In 1543 John Mackenzie acquir
2 CONC ed Kildins, part of Lochbroom, to himself and Elizabeth Grant, his wife, holding blench for a penny, and confirmed in the same year by Queen Mary. [ http://www.fullbooks.com/History-Of-The-Mackenzies1.html ]
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 - Hector Mackenzie, son of Alexander seventh Baron of Kintail. On the death of Kenneth Lord Kintail, his brother and chief, he became guardian to his nephew John. Gathering his own men and those of his nephew together, with his 
2 CONC young chief at their head, he accompanied the King to Flodden, where they were nearly all killed. Hector and his pupil narrowly escaped. [SCOTTISH NOBLEMEN AND GENTLEMEN KILLED AT FLODDEN. BY EGBERT WHITE.]
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 - Another source THE BATTLE OF FLODDEN AND THE RAIDS OF 1513 BY LT-COL. THE HON. FITZWILLIAM ELLIOT pub. 1911 shows Mackenzie of Kintail as having died in the battle. [4]
2 CONT
