1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Lindsay_of_Barnweill '''Alexander Lindsay of Barnweill'''] (died 1308 [(?)], Lord of Barnweill, Byres and Crawford, also known as Alexander de Lindsay, and as '''Sir Alexander Lindsay of Cr
2 CONC awford''', was a Scottish noble.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander was the eldest son of Sir David Lindsay of Barnweill and Margaret de Lindsay. He swore fealty and homage to King Edward I of England on 28 July 1296 at Berwick.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 9 July 1297, Alexander, together with Sir James Stewart, High Steward of Scotland, Sir John Stewart of Bonkyll, Sir Robert de Brus, jure uxoris Earl of Carrick and Robert Wishart, Bishop of Glasgow capitulated at Irvine. [2] He
2 CONC  was on the English side in the Bishop of Durham Antony Bek’s division during the Battle of Falkirk on 22 July 1298 that defeated the Scottish army.[3] For his services, he was granted the former Lindsay lands of Crawford that had
2 CONC  been passed by marriage to the Pinkeneys.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was ordered in September 1305 to leave Scotland for six months for his participation in the uprising of Sir William Wallace and Sir Andrew Moray. He was also a supporter of Robert de Brus. Alexander was captured at the fall of 
2 CONC Kildrummy Castle on 13 September 1306.[4] He was taken to England and held prisoner. Alexander was one of the leaders of the Scottish army that campaigned in Galloway in 1308. He was lastly summoned to the Scottish parliament in 1
2 CONC 309, however apparently had died in 1308.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT >''Birth date estimates seen as 1258 and as 1267.  Conflicting death dates reported: 1307; 1308; March 1312; before 10 December 1314.''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married (Miss) Stewart, daughter of Alexander Stewart, 4th Great Steward.
2 CONT
2 CONT From ''The Scots Peerage, Vol. 3.  “Lindsay, Earl of Crawford.” Page 9.  < [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun03pauluoft/page/9/mode/1up?view=theater Archive.Org] >''
2 CONT
2 CONT The five children of Alexander Lindsay and his wife were: 
2 CONT # Sir David de Lindsay, Lord Crawford, Custodian of Berwick & Edinburgh Castle+ d. 1355.  Married Maria Abernethy, widow of Andrew de Leschelyn (Leslie) and daughter (co-heiress with her sister Margaret, Countess of Angus) of Sir 
2 CONC Alexander de Abernethy.
2 CONT # Alexander,11 who was imprisoned at Carlisle, 1308-1314, became a knight banneret, and was ancestor, as alleged, of the Lindsays of Ormiston, afterwards represented by Cockburn of Langton.
2 CONT # Reginald, prisoner at Carlisle with his brother.
2 CONT # William, Rector of Ayr,1 and Chamberlain of Scotland in 1317, whose close connection with the house of Crawford2 suggests this affiliation.  He died before 5 Kal. January 1339.
2 CONT # Beatrix, d. b 1352,  married, first, to Sir Archibald Douglas, and was mother of the first Earl of Douglas ; 3 secondly, to Sir Robert Erskine of Erskine, Great Chamberlain of Scotland.4
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Lindsay-1321
2 CONT ''Lindsay-1321 created 27 Oct 2013 | Last modified 22 Jul 2023''
2 CONT
2 CONT * His father died in Egypt during the Crusade of St Louis, and Alexander (who was not yet of age) succeeded him in 1279.[1] 
2 CONT * He became the ward of Sir John Comyn of Badenoch and Tynedale, whose mother Alicia may have been a Lindsay.[2]
2 CONT * Alexander attended the parliament of Brigham in 1289 (as a baron)[3] and later was knighted by King Edward I.[1]
2 CONT * When Bruce was forced to flee into the mountains with a small group of friends after the devastating loss at Methven, it was the earl of Atholl (the king's brother) and Sir Alexander Lindsay who were entrusted to lead the queen,
2 CONC  the Lady Marjorie (Bruce's daughter), and the other women in the party safely across the mountains by horseback to the shelter of Kildrummy Castle.[5] 
2 CONT *  In 1309 he was one of the signatories of a letter which was sent to Philippe IV of France from the parliament at St Andrews.[6]
2 CONT *  His unwavering support of Robert Bruce was no doubt strengthened by his marriage...his wife was thought to have been closely related to James the Steward, who was a prominent supporter of King Robert.[6]
2 CONT * The Lord Lyon does not believe that the name of Sir Alexander's wife can be reliably confirmed,[1] and this position is universally upheld among historians, including the family historian, Lord Lindsay.
2 CONT * Sir Alexander Lindsay was present at the parliament of 1308[1] and signed the letter to Philippe IV in 1309.[6]
2 CONT *  There is almost no mention of him after 1309, and he is thought to have died sometime before 10 December 1314.[6]
2 CONT * He was not the Earl of Crawford. The Earldom of Crawford was created for Sir David Lindsay in 1398. (TSP, page 16: < [https://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun03pauluoft#page/16/mode/2up Archive.Org] >
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT From ''The Scots Peerage, Vol. 3.  “Lindsay, Earl of Crawford.” Page 8-9.  < [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun03pauluoft/page/8/mode/1up?view=theater Archive.Org] >''
2 CONT
2 CONT SIR DAVID DE LINDSAY, who acted as one of the Regents of Scotland in 1255,10 was High Chamberlain in 1256. He witnessed a confirmation charter of King Alexander in. to Balmerino as ' David de Lindesay, juniore.' n He joined the Cr
2 CONC usade of St. Louis in 1268, and died in Egypt.12 By his wife, Margaret de Lindesay, probably daughter of Walter de Lindsay, Lord of Lamberton (see above, p. 5) he had issue : —
2 CONT * 1. SIR ALEXANDER.
2 CONT * 2. Sir William, Lord of Symington, who (circa 1310) founded masses at Deer for his two wives Alicia, and M[argaret] Oomyn, Countess of Buchan, and at Newbattle for his father and mother.1 Sir James de Lindesay, Lord of Crawford,
2 CONC  succeeded to Symington,2 and claimed to be Lord of Buchan.
2 CONT * 3. Sir Duncan, brother of Sir William, named c. 1310.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
