2 CONT * '''Pg. 47'''
2 CONT * XV. SIR JOHN de MONTGOMERIE of Eaglesham and Eastwood, and afterwards of Eglinton and Ardrossan, was the son of Sir Alexander. "He married in 1361, Elizabeth, the daughter and heiress of Sir Hugh Eglinton of Eglinton, and by her
2 CONC  obtained the large possessions of that family on the death of her father, which occurred about  the year 1374, together with Ardrossan, the heiress of which estate one of her ancestors had married. Sir John quartered with his own
2 CONC  the Eglinton arms, which were gules, three rings or, gemmed azure."2 . . . . 
2 CONT *''' Pg. 48'''
2 CONT * Sir John de Montgomerie of Eaglesham, greatly distingished himself at the battle of Otterburne, where James Earl Douglas, who was his uncle, was slain.2....Hugh, the eldest son of Sir John, lost his life there, as the ballard ha
2 CONC s it: . . . 
2 CONT * Sir John granted on October, 1396, to William de Blakeford, the lands of Ardbennane, within two years from which time he died; he had by his wife, Elizabeth, who survived him, four sons,
2 CONT ** I. SIR HUGH, who died at the battle of Otterburne, in August 1388, from the good aim of an English archer, in revenging the death of Earl Douglas. . . . 
2 CONT ** '''Pg. 49'''
2 CONT ** '''II. SIR JOHN, who succeeded his father.
2 CONT ** III. ALEXANDER, to whom his mother gave a charter of the lands of Bonnington, in the barony of Ratho. . . . 
2 CONT ** '''Pg. 50'''
2 CONT ** IV. HUGH, who was -- it is presumed-- born after the death of the eldest son. He was tutor to Hugh, the third Lord Montgomerie, and lived to a very advanced age, having survived four generations of the chief of his family.
2 CONT * '''XVI. SIR JOHN de MONTGOMERIE, "Dominus Ejusdem, or of the Ilk," which title shows he was the male heir and chief of the French house of Montgomerie.
2 CONT * '''Pg. 51'''
2 CONT * '''Sir John de Montgomerie " succeeded his father before 1398. In 1402 he formed on the chiefs of the Scotch army which invaded England, and was taken prisoner at the disastrous battle of Halidon Hill." He was not long a captive
2 CONC , however, for two years after he was in . . .
2 CONT * '''Sir John married Margaret, daughter of Sir Robert Maxwell of Caerlaverock, (ancestor of the Earls of Nithsdale,) and dying previous to November, 1429, left by her three sons and three daughters.
2 CONT ** I. ALEXANDER, who succeeded him.
2 CONT ** II. SIR ROBERT MONTGOMERIE, knight, of Giffen.  . . . .
2 CONT ** '''Pg. 52'''
2 CONT ** III. HUGH MONTGOMERIE, is mentioned in a charter of his brother, Lord Montgomerie, Polnoon, July 29, 1452.
2 CONT ** IV. ANNE, married June 16, 1425, to Sir Robert Cuninghame of Kilmaurs, father of the first Earl of Glencairn.... 
2 CONT ** V. JANET, married Sir Thomas Boyd of Kilmarnock, (his second wife,) . . . 
2 CONT ** VI. ISABEL, married Archibald Mure of Rowallan.
2 CONT ** XVII. ALEXANDER de MONTGOMERIE, the first Lord Montgomerie, succeeded his father before November 22, 1429, as on that date "Alexander de Montgomery, lord of that Ilk, is one of the assize in an action  . . . 
2 CONT ** '''Pg. 53'''
2 CONT ** Lord Montgomerie married Margaret, daughter of Sir Thomas Boyd of Kilmarnock, by whom he had four sons and four daughters. He died prior to October 14, 1465, and was succeeded by his grandson. _______________________
2 CONT
2 CONT THIS REFERENCE LISTS DIFFERENT PARENTAGE FOR MARGARET MAXWELL.
