1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT ''Last updated 17 July 2025''
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=John_de_Graham_(died_1337)&oldid=1242196383 Wikipedia] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir '''John de Graham''' of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dalkeith Dalkeith], [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abercorn Abercorn] & [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eskdale_(Scotland) Eskdale] (1278–1337) was a 13th-14th century Scot
2 CONC tish noble.
2 CONT
2 CONT John, born in 1278,[1] was the son of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicholas_de_Graham Nicholas de Graham] of Dalkeith and Abercorn and Mary de Strathearn.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought at the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Bannockburn Battle of Bannockburn] against the English on 23–24 June 1314 and as a result had his Northumberland estates confiscated. King Edward II of England denounced Jo
2 CONC hn as an enemy and rebel, and granted his Scottish lands to Hugh le Despenser. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He signed the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Declaration_of_Arbroath Declaration of Arbroath] in 1320.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === Death
2 CONT
2 CONT John Graham died in Scotland on 25 April 1337; his burial location is unknown. His son and heir, John, became the last mormaer of Dalkeith, Abercorn, and Eskdale, finally resigning Dalkeith in favor of William Douglas on 6 January
2 CONC  1341/2, thus ending the elder branch of the family of Graham through the direct male line.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/page/194/mode/2up The Scots Peerage etc. Volume VI]  p. 194, 195,197.  Article: “GRAHAM, DUKE OF MONTROSE” 
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of [https://www.geni.com/people/Nicholas-de-Graham-of-Dalkeith-Abercorn-and-Eskdale/6000000004533941060?through=6000000026874295420 '''Sir Nicholas'''] [d. 1306] were : —
2 CONT
2 CONT *(a) [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-John-de-Graham-of-Dalkeith-Abercorn-and-Eskdale/6000000026874295420 '''Sir John of Dalkeith, Abercorn, and Eskdale''']. In an inquest at Barnborough with regard to his mother's half of Muscham
2 CONC p, 19 May 1306, he is said to be twenty-eight years of age.10 … he died 25 April 1337, leaving a widow, Isabella, who had a terce of his lands in Crichton, apparently exempted from the forfeiture, and a lease of the other two-thir
2 CONC ds.6   
2 CONT *(b) [https://www.geni.com/people/Isabella-Graham/6000000002166220506?through=6000000026874295420 '''Isabella'''], married (as his second wife) to Walter, High Steward of Scotland, sometime husband of Marjory Bruce, daughter of Ro
2 CONC bert i., and by him, who died 1326, had a son, Sir John Stewart of Ralston, and other children.3
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children were : —
2 CONT * A. [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-John-Graham-of-Dalkeith/6000000026876113073?through=6000000004533941022 '''Sir John de Graham''']  filius, last of Dalkeith, Abercorn, and Eskdale, who parted with those estates, finally resig
2 CONC ning Dalkeith in favour of Sir William Douglas de Laudonia 6 January '''1341-2''' [7].  On his death the direct male line of the elder branch of the family of Graham ended. [d.s.p.  No known wife or issue.]
2 CONT * B. [https://www.geni.com/people/Sybilla-Graham-of-Abercorn/6000000004533941022?through=6000000026874295420 '''Sybilla'''] (?), married to Sir '''William''' More, and, on the resignation of her father or brother, succeeded to the
2 CONC  barony of Abercorn.1 [Was it this Sybilla who married https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-Reginald-Mure-of-Abercorn-and-Cowdams/6000000003827859670?through=6000000004533941022?  Abercorn passed to the Mure family.]
2 CONT * C. [https://www.geni.com/people/Margaret-Graham/6000000083942139277?through=6000000010436556832 '''Margaret'''], said to have been married to Sir William Douglas de Laudonia, the ' Knight of Liddesdale, ' though his only known w
2 CONC ife was named Elizabeth. (See title Morton.) It may, however, have been a marriage with a Graham which carried to the family of Douglas Dalkeith, Kilbucho, and Newlands in Peeblesshire, and the lands of Eskdale.2 [As heir to her b
2 CONC rother John, Dalkeith passed to this Douglas family.]
2 CONT
2 CONT There are no recorded birth dates for these children.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Cawley’s Medlands 
2 CONT SCOTLAND UNTITLED NOBILITY
2 CONT Graham 
2 CONT v5.0 Updated 06 May 2025
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY%20UNTITLED.htm#_Toc100910317
2 CONT
2 CONT Shows the line a bit differently from TSP
2 CONT *  https://www.geni.com/people/Isabella-Graham/6000000002166220506?through=6000000026874295420 as daughter, not sister
2 CONT * no other daughters of this John
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * HENRY [II] de Graham, son of HENRY [I] de Graham & his wife --- (-after 1233).  "Henricus de Graham filius Henrici de Graham" confirmed the donation of "terram de Balnebuth" to Newbattle abbey, made by "avus meus et pater meus P
2 CONC . et H. de Graham", by undated charter[735].  m ---.  The name of Henry’s wife is not known.  Henry [II] & his wife had children: 
2 CONT **1.  Henry [III] & his wife had two children: 
2 CONT ***a)         NICHOLAS de Graham of Dalkeith (-after 20 Jan 1292).  "Nicholaus de Grham miles primogenitus dñi Henrici de Grham" confirmed the donation of "terra…  m MARY of Strathearn, daughter of MALISE Earl of Strathearn & his 
2 CONC first wife Marjory de Muschamp of Wooler ([1250/51]-after 1306).     Nicholas & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT ****i)          '''JOHN Graham of Abercorn''' (-25 Apr 1337).  "Johannes de Graham miles filius et heres Dñi Nicholai de Graham" donated "totam terram de Eskedal" to Melrose abbey by undated charter, dated to the early 14th centur
2 CONC y[744].  "Johannes de Graham dñs de Abrecorn" confirmed "terram…in tenemento de Dalketh" to "Roberto de Lauuedre" by charter dated 4 Mar 1315 witnessed by "…dño Jacobo de Douglas…"[745].  m ISABELLA, daughter of --- (-after Apr 13
2 CONC 37).  John & his wife had two children: 
2 CONT *****(a)       JOHN Graham (-after 6 Jan 1342).  Balfour Paul states that, as the last of the direct male line, he resigned Dalkeith in favour of William Douglas of Laudonia 6 Jan 1343[746]. 
2 CONT *****(b)       ISABEL de Graham.  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not been identified.   '''Balfour Paul records her parentage (as “sister of Sir John Graham of Abercorn”) and marriage (no source c
2 CONC ited)'''[747].  Burnett says that the mother of John Stewart of Ralston “is said to have been Isabel, daughter of Sir John Graham of Abercorn”[748].  m as his second wife, WALTER Stewart, son of JAMES 5th High Steward of Scotland 
2 CONC & his [third] wife Egidia de Burgh ([1292]-9 Apr [1326/29]). 
2 CONT **** b)         HENRY de Graham .  "Henricus de Grahame" donated land by undated charter witnessed by "…Nich de Grahame Henrico de Grahame filio Radulpho de Corry"[749]. 
2 CONT ***2.         NICHOLAS de Graham .  According to Balfour Paul, he was ancestor of the Graham families of Tarbolton and Waiston in Ayrshire[750]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT From John P. Ravilious -soc.genealogy.medieval, at groups - google.com Repository: Name: soc.genealogy.medieval, at groups - google.com Note: Source Medium: Internet Page: 11/16/2005:
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 Sir John de Graham. died on 25 Apr 1337, he was 58.[1],[10] born bef 19 May 1278.[1] laird of Dalkeith b. before 19 May 1278 [an inquisition of 19 May 1306, concerning the Muschamp inheritance, found that he was 28 years of age 
2 CONC [SP VI:195[1], cites Cal. Doc. Scot., iii. 392] supporter of King John (Baliol), capitulated to King Edward I with John Comyn at Strathord, 1303/04 joined with King Robert the Bruce confirmed grants by the Avenel tenants of Eskdal
2 CONC e to Melrose Abbey (confirmed by King Robert I, 25 Dec 1316) [SP VI:196[1 ' John de Graham ', a Surety of the Arbroath Declaration, 6 April 1320 (Barrow pp. 424-8)[11] cf. SP VI:195-6[1] '''Sir John married Isabella.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT IDENTITY: Having joined the fortunes of Bruce, Edward II., twelve days before the battle of Bannockburn, granted to his favourite, Hugh le Dispenser, and Alianora his wife, a charter of all the lands and tenements in Scotland forf
2 CONC eited by John de Graham, the King's enemy and rebel. At first at feud with the monks of Melrose, he eventually granted to them full confirmation and an enlargement of the original grants by the Avenel family of their lands in Eskd
2 CONC ale, which confirmation was ratified by King Robert, by charter dated 25 December 1316. Along with his kinsmen, Sir David de Graham and Sir Patrick deGraham, he affixed his seal to the celebrated letter of the Scots Barons to the 
2 CONC Pope, asserting the independence of their country, 6 April 1320. His estates were forfeited to Edward III
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
