1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John had from his father the lands of Netherpollok, Dryps and Calderwood in Clydesdale; Governor of Dumbarton Castle. [Burke's Peerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Maxwell, Knight, first of Pollok, 1270-1306.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Maxwell, second son of Sir Aymer, and brother of Sir Herbert, Lord of Maxwell and Carlaverock, was provided by his father to the estate of Pollok, from which he and his descendants were designated. It is easy to conjectur
2 CONC e why Sir Aymer thus provided his younger son to Pollok. He had recently acquired the valuable barony of Mearns, but his older baronies of Maxwell and Carlaverock, being situated near the English border, were much exposed to hosti
2 CONC le attacks, and required all his energies for their portection and defense, and he had little time to devote to the care of the distant barionies of Mearns or of Pollok. Here therefore wisely settled his second son in Pollok very 
2 CONC soon after its acquisition; that is, about the year 1270.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Pollok branch thus early planted - the earliest offshoot from the parent stem - became itself and independent tree, whence numerous branches have since sprung, which are now flourishing both in Scotland and in other countries.
2 CONC  In the course of little more than a century, it was enabled, by an alliance with a member of the royal house of Stewart, to provide large estates to a second son, who became the founder of the baronial family of Calderwood.
2 CONT
2 CONT At a later period, when the misfortunes of the parent line of Lords Maxwell had been reduced them from their former affluence, the position of the Pollok branch was such that its representative was solicited by the leading members
2 CONC  of the family to relieve the estates of it s burdens,a nd to assume, on the death of the then chief, his place as head of the house of Maxwell.
2 CONT
2 CONT A race which has been so long established as the Pollok Maxwell family and several of the representatives of which have held positions of prominence and distinction, seems worthy of having its descent recorded. It is the object of
2 CONC  these Memoirs to trace this descent through the seventeen successive generations which have existed between the first Baron of Pollok and his present representative, who is the twenty-first in descent from Undwyn, father of Maccu
2 CONC s. We, therefore, commence these Memoirs of the Pollok line, in continuation of the four generations of the main line already given, with the younger son of the fourth generation, who was Sir John Maxwell, first Baron of Pollok. S
2 CONC ir John Maxwell and his brother Alexander witnessed a charter by their eldest brother, Herbert Maxwell, to the church of Saint James and Saint Mirrin of Paisley, of the lands in Mearns, about the year 1300, in which they are desig
2 CONC nated brothers of Herbert. As Lord of Pollok (lower), Sir John Maxwell also witnessed another charter, about the same year, by Herbert Maxwell, his brother, to the parish church of Merness, of six merks of an annual-rent from the 
2 CONC mills of Merness. It has not been ascertained whom Sir John married. [Memiors of the Maxwells of Pollok I:9-10]
2 CONT
