2 DATE BET APR 1480 AND 14 OCT 1482
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == ROBERT, LORD BOYD ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun05pauluoft/page/142/mode/1up The Scots Peerage V: pp. 142-47]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Boyd (d. c1470) Lord Boyd, was a Scottish statesman.
2 CONT
2 CONT A son of Sir Thomas Boyd (d. 1439), Robert Boyd belonged to an old and distinguished family, of which one earlier Sir Robert Boyd, had fought with Sir William Wallace and Robert The Bruce.
2 CONT
2 CONT Created Lord Boyd in 1454 by James II, he was one of the Regents during the minority of King James III, in 1460. He conspired with his brother, Sir Alexander Boyd, and obtained possession of the King's person in 1466 and was made 
2 CONC by Act of Parliament sole Governor of the Realm.
2 CONT
2 CONT He negotiated the marriage between James and Margaret of Norway in 1469 and secured with it the cession of the Orkney Islands by Norway. He was appointed Great Chamberlain for life, and Lord Justice General in 1467.
2 CONT
2 CONT Conflict broke out between the King and the Boyd family. Robert, and his son Thomas Boyd, 1st Earl of Arran (who was married to Princess Mary), were out of the country involved in diplomatic activities when their regime was overth
2 CONC rown. Robert, 1st Lord Boyd was pronounced guilty of treason and fled firstly to Alnwick, Northumberland. His brother and assistant, Sir Alexander Boyd, was captured and beheaded on 22 November 1469.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert 1st Lord Boyd fought in the English service in the French wars, and died in exile.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Mariotta, daughter of Sir John Maxwell of Calderwood, and had numerous issue. One of his daughters, Elizabeth, married Archibald Douglas, 5th Earl of Angus.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica, Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
2 CONT * Townend, Peter, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage, & Knightage, 105th edition, London, 1970, p.1486.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Boyd, 1st Lord Boyd, so created between 1451 and 18 July 1454; knighted c1451; a Regent for the infant James III 1460; took custody of James III 1466 and got an Act of Parliament passed making himself sole Governor of Scotl
2 CONC and, also Great Chamberlain 1467; attainted (and his peerage forfeited) 1469 while away from court negotiating the transfer of Orkney to Scotland as a dowry for the King of Norway's daughter, whose marriage with James III he had a
2 CONC rranged. [Burke's Peerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Boyd, 1st Lord Boyd, Great Chamberlain of Scotland , and '''Marlot, daughter of Sir Robert Maxwell of Calderwood . [Magna Charta Sureties]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Granted the title Lord Boyd prior to 13 July 1459 by James II, and was one of the commissioners sent to prolong the truce with England, which continued for nine years. In 1468 he was granted full power to visit the courts of Engla
2 CONC nd, Spain, France, Denmark, Burgundy, Savoy, and others to find a wife for King James III. A marriage treaty was concluded with King Christian I of Denmark, who agreed to give his daughter to James III, along with the islands of O
2 CONC rkney and Shetland as dowry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Boyd was later accused of treason and fled to England under sentence of death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes from Sally Walmsley [Geniedash@bigpond.com]:
2 CONT
2 CONT Baron Boyd, Robert Boyd, son and heir of Thomas Boyd of Kilmarnock (who died 9 July 1439). He was knighted, and was created a Peer of Parliament (LORD BOYD [SCT]) by James II at some date between 1451 and 18 July 1454 when he took
2 CONC  his seat, as such, in Parliament. In 1460 he was one of the REGENTS [SCT] during the King's minority. In 1464 he was one of the commissioners for a truce with Edward IV. Having obtained possession of the person of the young King 
2 CONC (for which, as hereafter mentioned, he was eventually condemned for high treason), he was by Act of of Parliament 25 October 1466, made sole GOVERNOR OF THE REALM [SCT]: GREAT CHAMBERLAIN [SCT] 1467. Early in this year he procured
2 CONC  the marriage of his eldest son Thomas, (created Earl of Arran [SCT] for that occasion) with Mary, elder sister of the King, which aroused the jealousy of the other nobles. He obtained the cession of Orkney to Scotland, 8 Septembe
2 CONC r 1468, from Christian, King of Norway, for whose daughter Margaret, he negotiated a marriage with the King. While absent for that purpose he and his said son (the Earl of Arran) and his brother (and coadjutor) Sir Alexander Boyd,
2 CONC  were attainted for high treason, as stated above, whereby his Peerage became forfeited. He married Mariot (or Janet), daughter of Sir Robert Maxwell, of Calderwood. She died after 2 June 1472, apparently early in 1473. He was liv
2 CONC ing Easter 1480/1, and died before 1482, it is said at Alnwick, where he fled in 1469 . (taken from Gen-medieval UTZ@aol.com - 13/Mar/2000)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Boyd,_1st_Lord_Boyd
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT SIR ROBERT BOYD,   THE GREAT LORD KILMARNOCK, was  a  man of great parts, and eminent as a statesman. He was in such great favor with King James II, that he created him  Lord  of  Parliament in 1459.
2 CONT
2 CONT The  eminent abilities of this distinguished nobleman claim a more  extended  notice,  as  they  raised  him to the highest pinnacle of grandeur. Historians have  thought  it sufficient honour  to record of his father that  he  ha
2 CONC d for his son THE GREAT ROBERT BOYD. In what manner the early years of his life were  passed,  or  of  what  age  he was when deprived of theguidance  and  instruction  of his father, we are uninformed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Towards the end of the  reign  of  King James II, he began to make a considerable figure,  and  to  attract much attention. His great penetration and sound judgement rendered him useful at court. His knowledge of mankind was  unsu
2 CONC rpassed by any of his time. His courtesy  and  affability  made him a universal favorite,  so  that  he acquired the esteem and confidence ofall classes of people, as well as the favor of his Prince, by whom he was created a  Baro
2 CONC n, and called to Parliament by the
2 CONT
2 CONT name and  title  of  LORD BOYD OF KILMARNOCK. He added to his grandeur  by   alliances  made  with  many  great  and  noble families,  and  by  the  large additions which he made to his paternal inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT The  first  time  we  find his Lordship engaged in any public employment was in the  year  1459, when he was one of several Lords, Barons, and Prelates,  who were sent to England in the character of Plenipotentiaries  to renew  th
2 CONC e truce with that country,  just  then expired. They prolonged it at Newcastle, for seven years. Upon the unhappy death, in 1460, of James II Lord Boyd was made Justiciary, and was named one of the Lords
2 CONT
2 CONT of Regency,  in whose hands was lodged the administrations of affairs  during  the young kings minority. Buchanan speaks of him as  Lord Chancellor,  but  this  is  a mistake, witness a charter of James II (date Jan 23,1461)  unde
2 CONC r the Great Seal, in  which  Lord  Evandale  is named as a witness, as "ANDREA,
2 CONT
2 CONT DOMINO EVANDALE, CANCELLARIO NOSTRO" 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~confido/book1.htm
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=geolarson2&id=I054139
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Robert Boyd (d. c1470) Lord Boyd, was a Scottish statesman.
2 CONT
2 CONT A son of Sir Thomas Boyd (d. 1439), Robert Boyd belonged to an old and distinguished family, of which one earlier Sir Robert Boyd, had fought with Sir William Wallace and Robert The Bruce.
2 CONT
2 CONT Created Lord Boyd in 1454 by James II, he was one of the Regents during the minority of King James III, in 1460. He conspired with his brother, Sir Alexander Boyd, and obtained possession of the King's person in 1466 and was made 
2 CONC by Act of Parliament sole Governor of the Realm.
2 CONT
2 CONT He negotiated the marriage between James and Margaret of Norway in 1469 and secured with it the cession of the Orkney Islands by Norway. He was appointed Great Chamberlain for life, and Lord Justice General in 1467.
2 CONT
2 CONT Conflict broke out between the King and the Boyd family. Robert, and his son Thomas Boyd, 1st Earl of Arran (who was married to Princess Mary), were out of the country involved in diplomatic activities when their regime was overth
2 CONC rown. Robert, 1st Lord Boyd was pronounced guilty of treason and fled firstly to Alnwick, Northumberland. His brother and assistant, Sir Alexander Boyd, was captured and beheaded on 22 November 1469.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert 1st Lord Boyd fought in the English service in the French wars, and died in exile.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Mariotta, daughter of Sir John Maxwell of Calderwood, and had numerous issue. One of his daughters, Elizabeth, married Archibald Douglas, 5th Earl of Angus.
2 CONT
2 CONT References •This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica, Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. •Townend, Peter, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage, & Knightage, 105th edition, London, 1970,
2 CONC  p.1486.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Boyd, 1st Lord Boyd, so created between 1451 and 18 July 1454; knighted c1451; a Regent for the infant James III 1460; took custody of James III 1466 and got an Act of Parliament passed making himself sole Governor of Scotl
2 CONC and, also Great Chamberlain 1467; attainted (and his peerage forfeited) 1469 while away from court negotiating the transfer of Orkney to Scotland as a dowry for the King of Norway's daughter, whose marriage with James III he had a
2 CONC rranged. [Burke's Peerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Boyd, 1st Lord Boyd, Great Chamberlain of Scotland , and''' Marlot, daughter of Sir Robert Maxwell of Calderwood . [Magna Charta Sureties]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Granted the title Lord Boyd prior to 13 July 1459 by James II, and was one of the commissioners sent to prolong the truce with England, which continued for nine years. In 1468 he was granted full power to visit the courts of Engla
2 CONC nd, Spain, France, Denmark, Burgundy, Savoy, and others to find a wife for King James III. A marriage treaty was concluded with King Christian I of Denmark, who agreed to give his daughter to James III, along with the islands of O
2 CONC rkney and Shetland as dowry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Boyd was later accused of treason and fled to England under sentence of death.
2 CONT
