2 CONT The Bruce Family - The end of the 17th century saw the beginning of the decline of the Bruces of Clackmannan. From the 1650s Sir Henry Bruce developed the extensive coalfields of Clackmannan, being fortunate in having coal seams n
2 CONC ear the harbour, but the cost of draining the seams below the Forth and Black Devon were great. Sir Henry died in 1674 leaving immense debts to his son, David Bruce. David Bruce was the hereditary Sheriff at Clackmannan and a memb
2 CONC er of the British Parliament during the reigns of Charles II and James VII & II. He refused to take the Oaths of allegiance to William and Mary and was removed from Parliament in 1693. The collieries were poorly managed and David 
2 CONC Bruce went bankrupt in 1708. To pay his creditors, he sold the estate and the Sheriffdom to Colonel William Dalrymple, 2nd son of the Earl 0f Stair. David died in Clackmannan in 1712, and his son, Henry, 15th Baron of Clackmannan,
2 CONC  came out for Prince Charles Edward Stuart (Bonnie Prince Charlie) in the 1745 Jacobite uprising. He died in 1772, but his widow Catherine Bruce of Newton continued to live in the old mansion and tower until her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT =='''BRUCE,  EARLS  OF  ELGIN  AND AILESBURY''' [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun03paul/page/466/mode/1up?view=theater Balfour Paul, James, ed. (1906). The Scots Peerage, Vol. III. Edinburgh: David Douglas. pp. 467–468
2 CONC . ISBN 0-9711966-2-1.]
