2 CONT Colin Campbell, 1st Earl of Argyll From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia (28.2.2014)
2 CONT
2 CONT This article needs additional citations for verification. Please help improve this article by adding citations to reliable sources. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed. (February 2013) Colin Campbell, 1st Earl of Argy
2 CONC ll (c. 1433 – 10 May 1493) was a Scottish nobleman. Biography[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Gillespic (Archibald) Campbell, Master of Campbell and Elizabeth Somerville. Colin Campbell succeeded his grandfather Duncan Campbell, 1st Lord Campbell in 1453, and was created Earl of Argyll in 1457 and Lord Lo
2 CONC rne in 1470, after the resignation of his wife's uncle Walter Stewart, 3rd Lord Lorne, who became Lord Innermeath. Campbell had supported King James II against the "Black Douglases", led by the 8th Earl of Douglas, and was given t
2 CONC he earldom by King James III. James also gave him the position of Lord Chancellor of Scotland, but he eventually collaborated in the slaying of James III in 1484. In 1488 he became Lord Chancellor again, this time given by James I
2 CONC V of Scotland. Marriage and issue[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Isabelle Stewart, daughter of John Stewart, 2nd Lord Lorne in 1465, and had issue:[1] Archibald Campbell, 2nd Earl of Argyll.[1]. Thomas Campbell Margaret Campbell, married George Seton, 4th Lord Seton Isabel Campbell, 
2 CONC married William Drummond, Master of Drummond, son to John Drummond, 1st Lord Drummond Mary Campbell married Aonghas Óg Lord of the isles. Helen Campbell, married to Hugh Montgomerie, 1st Earl of Eglinton Elizabeth Campbell, marrie
2 CONC d John Oliphant, 2nd Lord Oliphant Catherine Campbell, married Lachlan Og Maclean References[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT >Balfour Paul, J. ''Scots Peerage'' vol i, pp 334-335
2 CONT
