1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Born in 1455 in Kilchurn Castle, Glenorchy, Argyll, Scotland, and died Sept. 9, 1513 in the Battle of Flodden Field, Branxton, Northumberland, England, and is buried in the Campbell Mausoleum at St. Munn's Church, 
2 CONC Kilmun, Argyll. He married MARGARET DOUGLAS. She was born in 1456 in Kilmaurs, Ayrshire, Scotland, and died in 1472 in Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Flodden<br>
2 CONT The Battle of Flodden or Flodden Field or occasionally Battle of Branxton was fought in the county of Northumberland in northern England on Sept. 9, 1513, between an invading Scots army under King James IV and an English army comm
2 CONC anded by the Earl of Surrey. It ended in victory for the English army, and was the largest battle in numbers fought between the two nations. James IV was himself killed in the battle, becoming the last British monarch to suffer su
2 CONC ch a fate. As the nineteenth century antiquarian John Riddell supposed, nearly every noble family in Scotland would have lost a member at Flodden. The dead are remembered by the song (and pipe tune) "The Flowers of the Forest." Li
2 CONC sten to the tune: http://www.whitestick.co.uk/midi/flowers.mid
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT "Sir Duncan Campbell 2nd Laird of Glenorchy
2 CONT Father: Colin Campbell
2 CONT Mother: Janet Stewart
2 CONT Wife 1: Bef 1468 Margaret Douglas
2 CONT Childern: Archibald "Of Glentyon", Elizabeth, Patrick, Colin "3rd Laird of Glenorchy", Morjory,
2 CONT Wife 2: Aft 1484 Margaret Moncreiff
2 CONT Children: Annabella Margaret, John M "Of Crannich and Bishop of the Isles", and Katherine
2 CONT Died in battle on 9 Sep 1513 at Field of Flowbane, now known as Flodden Field, Northumberland, England
2 CONT Information above extracted form the following sources:
2 CONT The Black Book of Taymouth
2 CONT The Peerage @ thepeerage.com
2 CONT Burke's Landed Gentry of Great Britain @ Google Books" 
2 CONT [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/46794189/duncan-campbell]
