2 TITL The family crest of the St. Clairs of Rosslyn from c.1500
2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE The family crest of the St. Clairs of Rosslyn from c.1500, as depicted in a carving or moulding in the ceiling of the withdrawing or dining room at Rosslyn Castle when building work was being completed by Sir William St. Clair of 
3 CONC Pentland in 1622. It was described by Hay as 'Argent a Cross ingrailed sable; for supporters, a Mermaid on the Dexter, and a Griffin on the Sinister; a Helmet befitting his quality; above which is a Dove Argent, beaked and membere
3 CONC d, Gules. Motto, Credo. The Mermaid has a comb in the right hand, and in the left a branch of some sea weed'.
