1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sir John Stirling of Keir, Knight
2 CONT
2 CONT Seal of Sir John Stirling, Fourth of Keir 1502
2 CONT
2 CONT John, like his father was active in political affairs. He took the side of James IV's widow against some of the nobility that had risen up against her and her son the future King James V. In 1526 John was indicted for treason, and
2 CONC  his lands were forfeited to the brother of the Earl of Angus. There were later restored.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1527 and 1535 Sir John added more than 30 properties to his estates, including Kippendavie which is still under Stirling Family guidance and control to this day. He also added the lands of Blackford, which established the 
2 CONC huge estate and sent the lands past the town of Dunblane and beyond Blackford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perhaps Sir John's methods of growth were somewhat suspect - In 1539 he was killed at Stirling Bridge in revenge for his assassination of Buchanan of Leny and for having stripped Buchanan's daughters of their inheritance. Thus end
2 CONC ed one of the most controversial and colorful periods of this families history. His Son James succeeded him. We'll spend more time looking into Sir John's methods and manners. Some say he was a tyrant, murderer and scoundrel, othe
2 CONC rs say he is merely an example of a large land owner during a time when lawlessness and greed ruled the countryside. 
2 CONT
2 CONT We'll have more on Sir John in a later installment.
2 CONT
2 CONT www.clanstirling.org/Keirline
