1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  
2 CONT Roderick Ruairidh ‘n tartair MacNeil, 35th chief of the Clan MacNeil, 15th laird of Barra
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Roderick (or Rory) An Tartair MacNeil of Barra lived from 1569 to about 1613.  He succeeded as chief sometime after 1598 when it is recorded that John MacNeil, son of his brother Murdo MacNeil was killed by the Campbells of Glenly
2 CONC on.  His Gaelic nickname, “Ruairidh ‘n tartair,” can be variously translated as “Rory The Turbulent,” “Rory The Tartar,” or “Rory of the Noise, Bustle, or Ostentation.” Called a “valiant and stout man of war,” throughout his life 
2 CONC Roderick MacNeil was indeed turbulent and frequently censured by the Scots King’s Council and various Royal Commissions for his depredations and general outlawry. The first documented notice of his life occurred on 28 Mar 1579 whe
2 CONC n he and his father “Rorie Oig MacNeil of Barra” were named by the Bishop of the Isles in an official complaint to the government for various transgressions. On 16 Apr 1587, Roderick, and his brothers John and Murdo, were reported
2 CONC  to the Privy Council for inflicting violence on neighboring MacDonald clansmen. 
2 CONT Battle of Battle of Benbigrie, Islay, 1598
2 CONT
2 CONT Roderick The Turbulent MacNeil of Barra, not unlike his ancestors, was a loyal adherent of the MacLeans of Duart and was frequently involved in that clan’s feuds, mostly against the MacDonalds of Clanranald and Sleat. In 1598, Hec
2 CONC tor Og MacLean, 15th of Duart, enlisted the help of the MacNeils of Barra in avenging the death of Hector Og’s father, Sir Lachlan of Duart, who had been killed fighting the MacDonalds at the Battle of Traigh Ghruinneart in Islay.
2 CONC  [2] In retaliation, the MacNeil, along with Macleod of Harris, Macleod of Dunvegan, Cameron of Lochiel, and some MacKinnons, invaded Islay under the command of Hector Og MacLean of Duart. At Benbigrie, the invaders were confronte
2 CONC d by armed MacDonald clansmen, led by Sir James MacDonald, 9th of Dunnyveg. In the ensuing battle, the MacLean of Duart’s larger force slew almost the entire MacDonald host but, although he was badly wounded, Sir James of Dunyveg 
2 CONC survived and managed to escape to Spain. However, over the next three days, almost the entire civilian population of Islay was physically abused or slain by the MacLeans and their MacNeil allies. [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Raids into Ireland
2 CONT
2 CONT Roderick “The Tartar” MacNeil of Barra, as a young man, likely participated in his father’s raids into nearby Ireland.[3] In 1589, Rory MacNeil, and a large force of Barramen led by his father, raided Erris in Co. Mayo, Ireland. [
2 CONC 5] In summer 1597, Rory and his clansmen again invaded Mayo, and this time were opposed by the famous female leader of the O’Malleys, Granny Ny Maly, or Granuille (anglicized as Grace O’Malley); during this raid Roderick’s brother
2 CONC  Ewen was killed. [3]Granuille and her clansmen may have also raided Barra in retaliation, engaging Roderick MacNeil in a sea battle in the Sound of Barra. [6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Piracy Against English Shipping
2 CONT
2 CONT Roderick The Turbulent MacNeil of Barra frequently committed acts of piracy against English shipping, forcing Queen Elizabeth I of England to formally remonstrate with her cousin, King James VI of Scots. Matters came to a head in 
2 CONC late 1609 when Roderick’s sons illegally boarded and plundered an English ship (out of Bordeaux, carrying Spanish wine) which was peacefully anchored in Castle Bay, Barra. Besides robbing the cargo, the MacNeil’s sons killed two c
2 CONC rewmen and wounded several others. As a result of Queen Elizabeth's protests to King James, Rory the Turbulent received a royal summons to appear before authorities in Edinburgh. Legend has it that Rory The Tartar even appeared in
2 CONC -person before King James VI and confessed to the illegal seizure of English shipping. However, Rory offered a unique defense, cheekily claiming that he had acted out of loyalty to his king, seeking to avenge the harm done by the 
2 CONC Queen of England to James VI's mother, Mary Queen of Scots, executed by Elizabeth in 1587. This tongue-in-cheek explanation supposedly won the grudging admiration of the king (and a personal pardon) but Roderick’s estate was nonet
2 CONC heless forfeited and given to Roderick MacKenzie of Coigeach, the Tutor of Kintail. [2] In fact, the criminal prosecution brought against Roderick MacNeil’s sons involved in the robbery and murder of the Bordeaux ship’s crew dragg
2 CONC ed on in the King’s Privy Council from 16 Jan 1612 to 2 Mar 1613, at which point the case was ultimately dropped. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Loss of Boisdale
2 CONT
2 CONT With the estate of Barra already under the feudal lordship of MacKenzie of Coigeach, Roderick The Turbulent MacNeil suffered further humiliation, this time at the hands of the MacDonalds of Clanranald. In 1601 (or possibly 1604), 
2 CONC Donald MacDonald of Moydart invaded South Uist and after a clan battle at North Boisdale wrested the lairdship of Boisdale from Roderick MacNeil. On 24 Jul 1620 these lands were then included in a Crown Charter to the Captains of 
2 CONC Clanranald; however, Boisdale was later taken away from the MacDonalds and included in new Charters to the MacKenzies, who were already the feudal superiors of nearby Barra. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Raids on the Isle of Lewis
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1605, King James revoked the titles of various MacLeod lairds to their lands in the Isle of Lewis. On 18 Jul 1605, a committee called the King's Justices and Commissioners in the Island of Lewis was appointed to the joint overl
2 CONC ordship of Lewis for one year. They were also given authority over the rest of the Outer Hebrides (including Barra) and the Isle of Skye. The remit of the Commissioners was to extirpate the native clans and colonize the entire Lon
2 CONC g Island with more peaceable Lowland Scots. The Commission thus ordered all the island clan chiefs to give up their castles and weapons or be declared traitors. [2] With other Hebridean clan chiefs, an incensed Roderick MacNeil of
2 CONC  Barra took part in several violent retaliatory attacks on the lowland colonists in the Isle of Lewis; consequently, the King gave orders to “extirpate and rute out” the chief and clan of MacNeil (along with various other MacDonal
2 CONC d and MacLeod chieftains). MacKenzie of Kintail was thus directed, on 30 Sep 1606, to arrest Roderick MacNeil for his depredations in Lewis but the latter was never captured; in fact, “Old Rory” was once again raiding Lewis in Apr
2 CONC  1607. [2] On 9 Jan 1610, a stymied King’s Council ordered Hector MacLean of Duart to apprehend Roderick MacNeil of Barra, for “committing all kynd of barbarite,” but to no avail as Rory The Turbulent remained at large. [1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marital Troubles and Dispute Over Succession to the Chiefship
2 CONT
2 CONT Roderick The Turbulent married twice and had two sets of sons who would later quarrel from 1612 over the chieftainship of the MacNeils. Evidence is contradictory but, in essence, sometime before 1583, Roderick entered into a handf
2 CONC ast relationship (that is, a noncanonical marriage, one not performed in a Roman Catholic church by a priest) with “MacLean of Duart’s sister” (possibly a daughter of Hector Og MacLean, 13th of Duart living 1530-1575). [4][3]This 
2 CONC handfast resulted in the birth of two sons: “Neill Oig McNeill and Gillevuan McNeill, sones natural to the said Rorie unlauchfullie procreat." [1]However, in 1590, Roderick The Tartar formally married (in church by a priest) Moir 
2 CONC (or Marion) MacDonald, the daughter of Allan MacDonald, 9th of Clanranald. The result was that Roderick’s sons by the MacLean lady were thus made illegitimate and not eligible to inherit from their father; conversely, the sons by 
2 CONC the Clanranald MacDonald mother were now the legitimate heirs to the chiefship and lairdship of Barra. The result was an internecine dispute and a long-running (sometimes violent) feud between the two sets of Roderick’s offspring 
2 CONC loyal to their respective mother’s clans. In Oct 1621, Neil Og, as eldest son of the noncanonical relationship, angered by being cut out of the succession to his father’s lands, took drastic action. With his equally illegitimate b
2 CONC rother Gilleonan, and accompanied by 20 armed clansmen, Neil Og attacked Kisimul Castle and seized both his father, Roderick The Turbulent, and half-brother, Gilleonan Og, and incarcerated them in the castle dungeon. Neil Og succe
2 CONC ssfully held Kisimul Castle against several rescue attempts by Neil Uisteach (the rightful heir) and Gilleonan Og MacNeil, his younger half-brothers. The feud lasted over a decade and even reached the authorities in Edinburgh who 
2 CONC unsuccessfully tried to intervene; somehow agreement was finally reached by 28 Jul 1629 allowing that Neil Og (of the illegitimate line) would succeed as chief of the MacNeils and laird of Barra and that the lands of Skirval (in t
2 CONC he north of Barra) would go to Neil Og’s half-brother, Neil Uisteach (the de-jure heir), in compensation for the latter’s de-facto dispossession. [2][1][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and Children
2 CONT
2 CONT As mentioned above, Roderick The Turbulent MacNeil’s marital relationships are subject to debate. However, it is certain that one relationship was recognized by the church and the other was not. Sometime before 1583, Roderick like
2 CONC ly entered into a noncanonical relationship (a handfast) with MacLean of Duart’s sister (possibly a daughter of Hector Og MacLean, 13th of Duart living 1530-1575). [4] [3]This resulted in the birth of two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Neil Og MacNeil (who succeeded his father as chief and laird);
2 CONT Gilleonan MacNeil.
2 CONT Before 11 Mar 1613 Roderick formally married Moir, daughter of Allan MacDonald, Captain of Clanranald. By Moir (or Marion) MacDonald he had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Neil Uisteach MacNeil (who was given the lands of Skirval);
2 CONT John Og MacNeil, on 28 Mar 1579, named with his father and brother, Murdoch; on 6 Feb 1610, before the Privy Council;
2 CONT Murdoch MacNeil;
2 CONT Gillean Og MacNeil. [1]
2 CONT Later Life and Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Roderick “The Turbulent” MacNeil of Barra died before Jun 1629 and was succeeded by his son Neil “Og” MacNeil as next chief of the clan MacNeil and laird of Barra.
2 CONT
2 CONT
