1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "I. <strong>Alexander Mackenzie</strong>, first of this family, was third son of Colin Cam Mackenzie, XI. of Kintail, by his wife Barbara, daughter of John Grant, XII. of Grant, by Lady Marjory Stewart, daughter of John, third Ear
2 CONC l of Athole. In 1616 Alexander has a charter of the lands of Kilcoy, dated 18th July. On the 29th of January, 1618, he has a Crown charter of the Barony of Kilcoy. In July, 1634, he was appointed Commissary of Ross, and in the sam
2 CONC e year, on the 17th of September, he has a charter from King Charles appointing him Principal Sheriff of the shire of Inverness. He married in 161 1 (marriage contract dated 15th August) Jean, daughter of Sir Thomas Fraser of Stri
2 CONC chen, Tutor of Lovat, and widow of Sir James Stewart of Muiren and Kilcoy, with issue — 
2 CONT * Colin, his heir and successor. 
2 CONT * Thomas, who has a sasine in 1678. 
2 CONT * Alexander, I. of Muirton of Kilcoy, who married Marie, daughter of John Cuthbert of Drakies. He has a sasine of "the lands of Muiren" in 1657, and a charter to "Alexander in the Muir" in 1666. By Marie of Drakies he had issue — 
2 CONC (i) Colin, his heir and successor; (2) the Rev. John, successively minister of Kingussie and Laggan, in Badenoch ; (3) Kenneth ; (4) Simon ; (5) Isobell ; and (6) Lilias, who married George Leslie (marriage contract dated 24th Dec
2 CONC ember, 1697). Alexander was succeeded as II. of Muirton by his eldest son, Colin Mackenzie, W.S., who married Anna, daughter of Sir James Grant of Moyness (she married secondly, Hugh Innes, Younger of Rosskeen), with issue— (i) Ke
2 CONC nneth, his heir and successor; (2) Simon, who died abroad ; and three daughters. Kenneth Mackenzie, his eldest son, succeeded as III. of Muirton, and married Mary, second daughter of Charles Mackenzie of Cullen, with issue, a son 
2 CONC — Alexander Mackenzie, IV, of Muirton, who in 1752 excambed the lands of Muirton of Kilcoy for the lands of Wester Fairburn, then the property of his cousin, Colin Mackenzie, VI. of Kilcoy. From this time the lands of Wester Fairb
2 CONC urn were renamed Muirton, after the original possessions of this family in the Black Isle. Alexander, IV. of Muirton, married Margaret, eldest daughter of James Mackenzie, III. of Highfield, with issue — (1) James; (2) Colin, M.D.
2 CONC , who both died unmarried ; and, according to the traditions of the district, (3) Big Simon, who emigrated to New South Wales ; and (4) Alexander, who went to England, married there and had issue ; (5) Mary, who married as his fir
2 CONC st wife, without issue, Roderick Mackenzie, II. of Scotsburn ; (6) Martha, who married Hugh Rose of Cuilich, with issue ; (7) Margaret, who married the " Black " Calder, with issue ; (8) a daughter, who married Alexander Cumming, 
2 CONC with issue ; and (9) Jean, who married James Shaw, Bailie of Inverness, who died on the 21st of January, 1801. Jean and her husband apparently succeeded by will or purchase to the lands of Muirton of Fairburn, for they were undoub
2 CONC tedly in a position at their death to leave them to their eldest son, Alexander Mackenzie Shaw, a minor, only 17 years old when his father died. The management, however, was left in the hands of a Mr Fraser, who squandered the fun
2 CONC ds which should have been invested for the second son William, a Colonel, H.E.I.C.S. (married with issue), and ten daughters, who survived their father ; and to make up the deficiency, not only Muirton of Fairburn, but Waternish i
2 CONC n Skye and Woodside near Fortrose, also the property of Bailie Shaw at his death, had to be sold. Muirton was bought by a Mr Reid, who afterwards resold it to William Mackenzie, W.S., son of Alexander Mackenzie, I. of Portmore. Al
2 CONC exander Mackenzie Shaw, the Bailie's eldest son, was a Captain in the army, and married in 1804, Mary Laing, with issue — (i) Gilbert Shaw, who, born in 1806, was a Judge in Jamaica, and died a few years ago at Tongland, Kirkcudbr
2 CONC ightshire ; (2) Gilbert, who died young ; (3) Henry Bridgwater ; (4) Alexander, Colonel Madras Infantry; (5) John; (6) Mary, who died unmarried ; (7) Hectorina, who married Mr Sprott; and (8) Eleanor, who married Mr Seabank. 
2 CONT * Sir Roderick Mackenzie of Findon, who married Margaret, daughter of Alexander Mackenzie, I. of Ballone, with issue — Alexander, who died in 1693 ; Lilias, who married Sir Kenneth Mackenzie, IV. of Scatwell, with issue ; Isobel, 
2 CONC who married Simon Mackenzie, I. of Allangrange, with issue ; Jean, who married John Chisholm, XX. of Chisholm, with issue ; and Margaret, who married Sir Kenneth Mackenzie, VIII. of Gairloch, with issue. Sir Roderick died in 1692.
2 CONC
2 CONT * Isobel, who married Roderick Mackenzie, III. of Redcastle, with issue. 
2 CONT * Jean, who married David Ross, III. of Pitcalnie, Tutor of David Ross, thirteenth of Balnagown. 
2 CONT * Katherine Beatrice, who married first, Duncan Bayne of Tulloch; and secondly, in 1651, George Munro, Younger of Lemlair. 
2 CONT * A daughter, who married Maclean of Borreray. He also had three natural daughters who married respectively Hector Mackenzie, IV. of Fairburn (marriage contract dated 11th of February, 1637); Neil Bayne, in Uist ; and the Rev. Joh
2 CONC n, son of John Roy Mackenzie, IV, of Gairloch. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander married, secondly, Margaret Dunbar, with issue — a daughter, Barbara, who died unmarried in 1656. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded by his eldest son."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/historyofmackenz00mack#page/580/mode/2up History of the Mackenzies, with genealogies of the principal families of the name]; Alexander Mackenzie; 1894; page 581
2 CONT --------------------
