1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == THOMAS CHISHOLM OF COMAR ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Genealogy ==
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/cc4aq/chisholm02.php Stirnet: Chisholm 02]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>XV. Thomas Chisholm</strong> 
2 CONT
2 CONT Who has a sasine dated the 12th of April, 1578, during his father's life-time, on the occasion of his marriage to Janet Fraser, widow of John Glassich Mackenzie, II. of Gairloch, only daughter and heiress of James Fraser of Phoine
2 CONC as, brother of Hugh fifth Lord Lovat. On the 7th of June, 1539, the lady's father has a charter in his favour of the King's lands of Drumdervale (?Drumderfit) in the lordship of Ardmanoch or Redcastle, in Ross, and on the 25th of 
2 CONC October, 1542, another of the lands of Kinkell, Culbokie, and Pitlundie, in the Black Isle. He was one of the principal men of the name of Fraser who fell at the battle of Blar-nan-leine in 1544, at the head of Loch Lochy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In one of the Gairloch manuscripts it is said that by Janet Fraser her first husband, John Glassich Mackenzie, "got the lands of Kinkell, Kilbokie, Badinearb, Pitlundie, Davochcairn, Davochpollo, and Foynish, with others in the Lo
2 CONC w Country, for which the family [of Gairloch] has been in the use to quarter the arms of Fraser with their own." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Janet had three sons to John Glassich Mackenzie, each of whom became in succession lairds of Gairloch. Her husband having been violently put to death at the instance of John Mackenzie, IX. of Kintail, she, according to an old fami
2 CONC ly manuscript, fled with her eldest surviving son, John Roy Mackenzie, "to Lovat and her Eraser relatives." The same authority says that "she was afterwards married to Chisholm of Comar and heird his family ; here she kept him in 
2 CONC as concealed a manner as possible, and, as is reported, every night under a brewing kettle ; those who, through the barbarity of the times, destroyed the father and the uncles, being in search of the son, and in possession of his 
2 CONC all excepting his mother's dower. He was afterwards concealed by the lairds of Moidart and of Farr, till he became a handsome man, and could put on his weapon, when he had to wait on Colin Cam Mackenzie, laird of Kintail, a most w
2 CONC orthy gentleman who established him in all his lands, excepting those parts of the family estate for which Hector and his successors had an undoubted right by writs."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1574 John Roy Mackenzie acquired from Lord Lovat half the lands of Ardnagrask, partly for the rights he had inherited in Phoineas from his mother. In the disposition of these lands, Lord Lovat designates Mackenzie as " the son,
2 CONC  by her first husband, of his kinswoman, Agnes Fraser." It appears from a charter of alienation by Hugh Fraser of Guisachan, dated the 29th of May, 1582, that John Roy Mackenzie had acquired Davochcairn and Davochpollo from this H
2 CONC ugh Fraser, in 1574, the same year in which he acquired Ardnagrask, and that in 1582 he obtained from him the lands of Kinkell-Clarsach and Pitlundie, in terms of a contract of sale dated the 26th of January, 1581.
2 CONT
2 CONT From this it would seem that part at least of the lands which Agnes Fraser on the occasion of her first marriage carried to her husband, John Glassich Mackenzie of Gairloch, remained with herself, or returned to her Fraser relativ
2 CONC es, and that she did not carry them with her to her second husband, Thomas Chisholm, Hugh Fraser of Guisachan, who conveyed them to Mackenzie, being her father's brother. It would, however, appear from the sasine of 1578, that she
2 CONC  carried some portion of her possessions to her second husband, Thomas Chisholm of Comar, during his father's life-time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This Thomas " Cheisholm of Cummer " is included in "The Roll of the names of the landlordis and baillies of landis in the Hielandis and lies, quhair brokin men hes duelt and presentlie duellis," appended to the Act of Parliament p
2 CONC assed in the year 1587, "for the quieting and keeping in obedience of the disordourit subjects inhabitants of the Bordouris, Hielandis, and His," commonly called The General Band, or Bond.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas died very soon after his father (who was alive in 1584), and before 1590, without surviving male issue, and probably without making up titles, when he was succeeded by his next brother."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/historyofchishol00mackuoft#page/48/mode/2up History of the Chisholms, with genealogies of the principal families of the name]; Alexander Mackenzie; 1891; page 48
