1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===JEAN HAMILTON (aka Jeane HAMILTOUN)===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Jean Hamilton, here treated, is the third daughter by the second marriage of Sir Thomas Hamilton, afterwards Earl of Haddington. She was born on 5 February 1607 [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun04pauluoft/page/314/m
2 CONC ode/1up The Scots Peerage IV: 315] and her baptism was registered on 8 February 1607, at Edinburgh in Midlothian, Scotland. [National Records of Scotland, Edinburgh Baptisms, reference OPR.685/1/1]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriage=== 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Jean Hamilton, here treated married (contract 21 December 1621 and 7 January 1622) John Kennedy, sixth Earl of Cassillis, whom she predeceased, dying about 15 December 1642. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun04pauluo
2 CONC ft/page/314/mode/1up The Scots Peerage IV: 315]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Kennedy,_6th_Earl_of_Cassilis
2 CONT
2 CONT Kennedy's wife, Jean Hamilton, was the daughter of Thomas Hamilton, 1st Earl of Haddington and Margaret Foulis. They married on 7 January 1621/22.[26] They had three children:
2 CONT # Lady Catherine Kennedy (d. c Feb. 1700), who married Lord William Cochrane.
2 CONT # James Kennedy, Lord Kennedy (d. 1642/43)
2 CONT # Lady Margaret Kennedy (d. 1685), first wife of Bishop Gilbert Burne
2 CONT
2 CONT ==="The Gypsy Laddy" and the Countess of Cassilis===
2 CONT "The Gypsy Laddy," an old Scottish ballad about Johnny Faa, king of the “Egyptians,” or “Gypsies,”]] became associated with Jean Hamilton, Kennedy's first wife, in the eighteenth century. The ballad narrates the tale of a gypsy ba
2 CONC nd who carried away the lady loved by Johnny Faa. In the eighteenth century, the people of Ayrshire began to associate the tale with Jean Hamilton, adding elements which placed the kidnapping at a time when the Earl was away at We
2 CONC stminster and included the Earl's revenge against the band of “bonny” gypsies. In 1827, Robert Chambers in his Picture of Scotland added the fiction that the Earl imprisoned his wife for the remainder of her life, and that while s
2 CONC he still lived, he married another woman. These elements have all been disproved, however. Folklorist Francis James Child explained in the nineteenth century that, first of all, Jean Hamilton had died a year before the Earl went t
2 CONC o Westminster; secondly, the Earl's second marriage did not take place until February 1644, more than a year after Lady Hamilton's death; and thirdly, that upon the death of his wife, the Earl wrote a letter of lament to fellow Co
2 CONC venanter the Earl of Eglinton, saying “It hath pleased the Almighty to call my dear bedfellow from this valley of tears to her home (as she herself in her last hour so called it),” and urging Eglinton's attendance at the funeral.[
2 CONC 24][25]
2 CONT
