3 CITY Drumquin
3 STAE Ulster
3 CTRY Ireland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://landedfamilies.blogspot.com/2013/09/78-alexander-families-of-ireland-and.html Nick Kingsley, Sunday, 29 September 2013
2 CONT
2 CONT There are five main branches of the Alexander family based in Ireland. They all trace their descent from John Alexander (?1587-1662), who came over from Scotland c.1613 to rent lands at Eridy (Donegal) from Sir James Cunninghame o
2 CONC f Glengarnock (Ayrshire), who acquired them in 1610 as part of the settlement of Ulster. It is generally assumed that John Alexander was distantly related to the Alexanders of Menstrie, Earls of Stirling, although no connection ha
2 CONC s been shown.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Alexander had five sons, the eldest of whom, Capt. John Alexander (d. c.1690), bought the lands of Enagh and Caw on the east bank of the River Foyle, just north of Londonderry, in 1686, and they remained in his family until t
2 CONC he 20th century. The youngest son, the Rev. Dr. Andrew Alexander, inherited Eridy, and was the ancestor of the Alexanders of Ahilly and Milford, the Cable-Alexander baronets, and the Earls of Caledon. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander, John (?1587-1662), of Eridy (Donegal). Born in Scotland and relocated to Donegal c.1613. He married and had issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) Capt. John Alexander (d. c.1690) (q.v.);
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) Archibald Alexander (b. c.1611; fl. 1665), of Ballybiglimore;
2 CONT
2 CONT (3) William Alexander;
2 CONT
2 CONT (4) Robert Alexander (fl. 1663-90), of Dunvanaddy; took part in the Siege of Derry and for his services there was granted lands at Drumquin, Ardstraw (Tyrone); married and had issue two sons;
2 CONT
2 CONT (5) Rev. Dr. Andrew Alexander (d. 1641); ancestor of the Alexanders of Ahilly, Milford, Caledon and Dublin [see subsequent posts].
2 CONT
2 CONT He rented the lands of Eridy (Donegal) from Sir James Cunninghame of Glengarnock (Ayrshire), who had acquired them in 1610 on condition that he did not 'alienate the premises to no mere Irishman or any other person unless he or th
2 CONC ey first take the Oath of Supremacy'. At his death the lands of Eridy passed to his youngest son.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is said to have died in 1662.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Memorials of the Earl of Sterling and the House of Alexander by Charles Rogers, LL.D., (Edinburgh: William Paterson, 1877), Vol. II, Ch. XV, pp. 59ff.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Alexander of Eredy appears to have had several sons. In the Hearth Tax Roll of Clonmany parish for 1665, is named, as a householder, "John Alexander, jun." In Taghboyne parish Archibald Alexander is, in the Subsidy Roll for 1
2 CONC 662, assessed for 13, 15s.; he is, in 1663, in the Hearth Tax Roll of Taghboyne parish, entered as "Archibald Alexander of Ballybiglimore."
2 CONT
2 CONT In the parish of Clonleigh, in 1663, John Alexander is associated with a "William Alexander," and in the roll of that parish for 1665 he is named along with William Alexander of the parish of Raphoe. In the Hearth Tax Roll of the 
2 CONC parish of Errigal, county Londonderry, in 1663, is named Robert Alexander at Dunvanaddy and Mevoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=128725438
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''Alexander Family Migration To America'''
2 CONT ==
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a well established tradition that seven Alexander brothers, Presbyterians from Scotland who had sojourned a while in the north of Ireland, probably at Raphoe. County, Donegal, and Sligo, Co., came to Somerset Co. Maryland
2 CONC  before going on to Cecil Co. If we may judge from the circumstantial evidence remaining, then along with the brothers came two sisters: one, the wife of Matthew Wallace; the other Jane, who married John McKnitt.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Gardner
2 CONT Spalding
2 CONT Blount Vaughan Baker Carr, 
2 CONT
