1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.ebooksread.com/authors-eng/archibald-allan/history-of-channelkirk-hci/page-34-history-of-channelkirk-hci.shtml
2 CONT
2 CONT "12 May 1607. — The King concedes to George, Master  of Winton — the Earl of Wintoun, with state and title of the  same, the lands, lordship, and barony of Seton and Wintoun  . . . the lands of Hartisheid and Clintis . . . and whi
2 CONC ch, for  service, etc., the King de novo gives to the said George, ex-  tending to ;^83, old extend, viz., Seton and Winton to ;^I5,  Tranent to ;^20 . . . West Niddrie to ;^38, Hartisheid and  Clintis to ;^5 ... and incorporates 
2 CONC the lands of Seton,  Winton, etc., in the constabulary of Haddington, into the 
2 CONT
2 CONT • The Abbot, chap, xxxviii. t Knox's Works, vol. ii., pp. 326, 431.  % Douglas's Peerage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben M. Angel notes: Lundy's picture of George Seton and his family is in fact not of this family, according to the Seton family website: 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www2.thesetonfamily.com:8080/history/7th_Lord_Seton_Epitaph.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT The likely source of the mis-identification, attributed to a 2008 E-mail by Caroline Maubois, comes from the mis-identification of George Seton, 7th Lord Seton, who was the 5th Lord Seton named George. Lundy has him listed as 5th 
2 CONC Lord Seton, apparently a common mistake made by those studying this family. The pic is actually of this person's paternal grandfather, along with this person's father, aunt, and uncles, all of whom were apparently alive after the 
2 CONC death of that generation's George in March 1562. The woman is actually the elder Seton's daughter, not his wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on George Seton, 3rd Earl of Winton:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p2146.htm#i21456
2 CONT
2 CONT George Seton, 3rd Earl of Winton [1]
2 CONT *M, #21456, 
2 CONT *b. December 1584, 
2 CONT *d. from 15 December 1650 to 17 December 1650
2 CONT *Last Edited=24 Mar 2011
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=2.05%
2 CONT
2 CONT George Seton, 3rd Earl of Winton was born in December 1584.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, firstly, Lady Anne Hay, daughter of Francis Hay, 9th Earl of Erroll and Elizabeth Douglas, on 26 April 1609.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, secondly, Hon. Elizabeth Maxwell, daughter of John Maxwell, 6th Lord Herries of Terregles and Elizabeth Maxwell, before 1628.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died from 15 December 1650 to 17 December 1650.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Robert Seton, 1st Earl of Winton and Lady Margaret Montgomerie.[1] 
2 CONT *He succeeded to the title of 8th Lord Seton [S., 1451] on 26 June 1606.[1] 
2 CONT *He succeeded to the title of 3rd Earl of Winton [S., 1600] on 26 June 1606.[1] 
2 CONT *He held the office of Burgess of Glasgow in 1618.1 He held the office of Justice-General of St. Andrews.[1] 
2 CONT *He was Temporary President of the Council between December 1625 and March 1626.[1] 
2 CONT *He held the office of Member of the Council of War in February 1628.[1] 
2 CONT *He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) [Scotland].[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of George Seton, 3rd Earl of Winton and Lady Anne Hay
2 CONT *1. George Seton, Lord Seton+[1] b. 15 May 1613, d. 4 Jun 1648
2 CONT *2. Christopher Seton [1] b. 2 Mar 1617, d. 30 Jun 1618
2 CONT *3. Alexander Seton, 1st Viscount Kingston+[1] b. 1621, d. 21 Oct 1691
2 CONT *4. Lady Elizabeth Seton+[4] b. c 1622, d. 16 Jun 1650
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of George Seton, 3rd Earl of Winton and Hon. Elizabeth Maxwell
2 CONT *1. Lady Isabel Seton [1]
2 CONT *2. Lady Mary Seton+[3] d. 15 Jan 1697/98
2 CONT *3. Christopher Seton [1] b. 28 Jun 1631, d. Jul 1648
2 CONT *4. William Seton [1] b. 8 Jan 1633, d. Jul 1648
2 CONT *5. Lady Anne Seton+[1] b. 30 Sep 1634, d. a 23 Feb 1685/86
2 CONT *6. Sir John Seton of Garleton, 1st Bt.+[1] b. 29 Sep 1639, d. Feb 1686
2 CONT *7. Sir Robert Seton of Windygoul, 1st and last Bt.[1] b. 10 Nov 1641, d. Nov 1671
