2 DATE 1588
2 ADDR
3 CITY Eglinton
3 STAE Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 7 JAN 1661
2 ADDR
3 CITY Eglinton
3 STAE Scotland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Alexander Seton, sixth Earl of Eglintoun, styled Greysteel by his contemporaries, who succeeded, was, as already stated, the cousin-german of his predecessor. He was the third son of Robert Seton, first Earl of Win
2 CONC toun, and of Margaret Montgomerie his Countess was born in 1588, and was before his accession styled Sir Alexander Seton of Foulstruther. On 20 October 1612 he was retoured heir to his cousin in the earldom of Eglintoun, assuming 
2 CONC the surname of Montgomerie, and after the usual infeftment on 30 October 1612, he took the style and title of Earl of Eglintoun, Lord Montgomerie, etc. 
2 CONT A week before this, however, Parliament interfered with his possession of Kilwinning. That abbacy and barony had never been legally dissolved from the Crown, and the Estates now declared it to be in the King's hands, and dissolved
2 CONC  it from the property of the Crown that it might be conferred on Michael, Lord Balfour. Two years later Bal/our received a Crown charter of the lands, but an arrangement was come to between him and the Earl, who paid a sum of 8000
2 CONC  merks, and received a charter on 26 April 1615. 
2 CONT The Earl's assumption of the title of Eglintoun was also objected to by the King, who opposed the transfer of titles by infeftments of entail, but after much trouble and correspondence for about two years, and a formal surrender o
2 CONC f the honours, he obtained the royal recognition of his dignity, with a charter confirming the former grant of 28 November 1611, and of new erecting all his lands, etc., into the earldom of Eglintoun, of date 24 March 1615. After 
2 CONC this the Earl became a favourite with the King, whom he entertained in his house at Glasgow in 1617, while the King was in Scotland. He attended the King's funeral, and became one of the Privy Councillors of King Charles I. The Ea
2 CONC rl carried the spurs at that monarch's coronation, but in the dispute which arose between the King and his subjects he took the popular side and became a prominent adherent of the Covenant. 
2 CONT In 1639 he led a considerable force to join Sir Alexander Leslie near Kelso, in the following year at Newburn, when the Scots army invaded England. He is said to have been in Ireland on duty during the rebellion there in 1641, and
2 CONC  he was in England with the Scots army at York, and in the conflict of Marston Moor. The Earl opposed the Engagement on behalf of King Charles I. in 1648, but he was one of the first Scottish nobles to welcome King Charles II. to 
2 CONC Scotland in 1650. He and many other royalists were expelled from office and military service under the Act of Classes; but in 1651 the Earl was again in the King's service in Dumbartonshire, and was there seized by a party of Crom
2 CONC well's horse, being carried first to Hull then to Berwick, where he was imprisoned until the Restoration. He wrote various letters for mitigation of his imprisonment, but without result. He was released only in 1660, and died on 1
2 CONC 4 January 1661. 
2 CONT The sixth Earl of Eglintoun married, first, on 22 June 1612, Anna, eldest daughter of Alexander Livingstone, first Earl of Linlithgow, by whom, who died on 12 November 1632, he had issue five sons and two daughters; secondly, betw
2 CONC een November 1642 and March 1644, Margaret, daughter of Walter, first Lord Scott of Buccleuch, and widow of James, Lord Ross, without issue. 
2 CONT She died at Hull 5 October 1651.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earl's issue were: 
2 CONT 1.'' Hugh'', who became seventh Earl of Eglintoun.
2 CONT 2.'' Sir Henry'', of Giffen, born 26 June, and baptized on 21 August 1614, Queen Anne being his godmother. He was a student in Glasgow University in February 1628, and travelled abroad in 1632, returning to Scotland in 1634. On 31
2 CONC  July 1636 he had a charter of the lands of Giffen, and on 21 September 1640 (contract dated 25 and 29 January) he married Jean, daughter of Archibald, seventh Earl of Argyll, widow of the first Viscount of Kenmure, but died witho
2 CONC ut issue on 3 May 1644.
2 CONT 3. ''Alexander'', born 8 November 1615. He was educated with his elder brothers at Glasgow and in France, staying abroad until near the close of 1635, when he returned to Scotland and served with the army. He rose to the rank of c
2 CONC olonel, and was made a knight, as he is styled ' Sir Alexander the Golonell. He served in the Scots army against the rebels, and died unmarried in July 1642, leaving issue a natural daughter Katherine.
2 CONT 4. ''James'', who was enrolled as a student in Glasgow University on 1 March 1637. He took military ser- vice and became a colonel. He acquired the estate of Coilsfield from Patrick Houston of that Ilk in the year 1662. He died in
2 CONC  March 1674, and was buried in the New Kirk of Edinburgh on the 18th of that month. He married (contract dated 1 and 6 June 1659) Margaret, daughter of John Macdonald in Kintyre and Elizabeth Stewart his spouse, and by her, who su
2 CONC rvived him, had issue. 
2 CONT 5. ''Robert'', fifth son of sixth Earl, was enrolled as a student at the University of Glasgow on 1 March 1637. He entered the army, distinguished himself in the Civil Wars, and became a major-general. He commanded a brigade at Wo
2 CONC rcester in 1651, and was forced to retreat. He was confined in Edinburgh Castle, from which he escaped in 1658, and retired to Holland, whence he returned with the King. After the Restoration he was appointed one of the Gentlemen 
2 CONC of His Majesty's Bedchamber. He died in December 1684. He married (contract dated 4 September 1662) Elizabeth daughter of James Livingstone, Viscount of Kilsyth, who survived him and married, before 24 January 1688, Captain George
2 CONC  Douglas, brother of Sir James Douglas of Kelhead. They had issue two sons, Alexander and James, and a daughter, who all died without issue.
2 CONT 6.'' Margaret'', born 20 February 1617; married, first, in 1642, to John, first Earl of Tweeddale, as his second
2 CONT wife, secondly, to William, Earl of Glencairn. She died at Edinburgh 27 January 1665.
2 CONT 7. ''Helenor'', born 26 July 1618, but died young.
2 CONT 8. ''Anna'', referred to in 1633, 1642, and 1649. Died unmarried.
2 CONT '''''Source: "The Scots Peerage" Vol 3, page 445.'''''
2 CONT
