2 CONT From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page for Alexander Montgomerie:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p2070.htm#i20698
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Montgomerie, 6th Earl of Eglinton [1]
2 CONT *M, #20698, 
2 CONT *b. 1588, 
2 CONT *d. 7 January 1661
2 CONT *Last Edited=24 Mar 2011
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=2.05%
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Montgomerie, 6th Earl of Eglinton was born in 1588.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, firstly, Lady Anne Livingstone, daughter of Alexander Livingstone, 1st Earl of Linlithgow and Lady Helen Hay, on 22 June 1612.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, secondly, Margaret Scott, daughter of Sir Walter Scott, 1st Lord Scott of Buccleuch and Mary Ker, between November 1642 and March 1644.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 7 January 1661.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was baptised with the name of Alexander Seton.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Robert Seton, 1st Earl of Winton and Lady Margaret Montgomerie.[1],[2] 
2 CONT *He gained the title of 6th Earl of Eglinton [S., 1507] on 4 September 1612.[1] 
2 CONT *He succeeded to the title of 7th Lord Montgomerie [S., 1445] on 4 September 1612.[1] 
2 CONT *On 24 March 1615 he resigned his titles, and had a regrant.[1] 
2 CONT *He fought in the Battle of Marston Moor in 1644, for the Parliamentarians.[1] 
2 CONT *He fought in the Captain in 1650, Horse Guards. for King Charles II.[1] 
2 CONT *Between 1651 and 1660 he was imprisoned.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Alexander Montgomerie, 6th Earl of Eglinton and Lady Anne Livingstone
2 CONT *1. Colonel James Montgomerie+2 d. 18 Oct 1675
2 CONT *2. Maj.-Gen. Robert Montgomerie+4 d. 1684
2 CONT *3. Hugh Montgomerie, 7th Earl of Eglinton+1 b. 8 Apr 1613, d. Feb 1669
2 CONT *4. Sir Henry Montgomerie5 b. 26 Jun 1614, d. 3 May 1643
2 CONT *5. Lady Margaret Montgomerie+4 b. 20 Feb 1616/17, d. 27 Jan 1664/65
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT *1. [S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 1, page 1280. Hereinafter cited as Burke's 
2 CONC Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT *2. [S37] Charles Mosley, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT *3. [S37] Charles Mosley, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition, volume 1, page 560.
2 CONT *4. [S37] Charles Mosley, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition, volume 1, page 1284.
2 CONT *5. [S37] Charles Mosley, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition, volume 1, page 1281.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the biography of Robert Baillie, his regimental chaplain during the Bishop's War:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.british-civil-wars.co.uk/biog/baillie-robert.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his opposition to war against England, Baillie was persuaded to serve as chaplain to the '''regiment of his patron the Earl of Eglinton''' during the Bishops' Wars (1639-40).
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Order of Battle for Marston Moor:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.british-civil-wars.co.uk/military/oob-marston-moor.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT The Scottish Army of the Covenant
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord-General: Alexander Leslie, Earl of Leven (senior Allied commander)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lieutenant-General of the Horse: David Leslie
2 CONT
2 CONT The Horse
2 CONT *Earl of Leven's regiment (Lord Balgonie c.350)
2 CONT *Earl of Dalhousie's regiment (c.350) 
2 CONT *'''Earl of Eglinton's regiment (c.350)''' 
2 CONT *Lieutenant-General David Leslie's regiment (c.350) 
2 CONT *Earl of Balcarres' regiment (c.350) 
2 CONT *Lord Kirkcudbright's regiment (c.350)
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1644: The York March and Marston Moor:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.british-civil-wars.co.uk/military/1644-york-march-marston-moor.htm#marston
2 CONT
2 CONT The Covenanters provided the largest contingent in the Allied army, so Lord Leven was given overall command. The Allies occupied cornfields on the low northern slopes of Marston Hill between the villages of Long Marston and Tockwi
2 CONC th. An area of hedged enclosures and rabbit warrens around Tockwith hampered the deployment of cavalry on the Allied left flank, which was commanded by Lieutenant-General Oliver Cromwell. The first two lines consisted of around 3,
2 CONC 000 men of the Eastern Association horse, including Cromwell's regiment of Ironsides. They were supported by a third line of around 1,000 Scottish horse, commanded by Major-General David Leslie. The Swedish tactic of interspersing
2 CONC  detachments of musketeers between divisions of horse to disrupt attacking cavalry was used by both sides in the battle. A force of 500 Scottish dragoons occupied the extreme left of the Allied position.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Allied centre comprised around 11,000 foot in four lines supported by a few pieces of field artillery. In the first line, soldiers from the three Allied armies were interspersed, with Major-General Lawrence Crawford commanding
2 CONC  the English and Major-General William Baillie commanding the Scots. Four Scottish brigades commanded by Major-General James Lumsden occupied the second line, while Lord Fairfax and the Earl of Manchester commanded their brigades 
2 CONC in the third line. A reserve Scottish brigade occupied the fourth line.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Allied right wing of around 5,000 cavalry and musketeers comprised mainly the horse of Lord Fairfax's Yorkshire army in two lines, the first commanded by Sir Thomas Fairfax, the second by Colonel John Lambert. '''Three Scottis
2 CONC h regiments under Lord Eglinton''' formed a third or reserve line.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT On the (right) wing, however, the Royalists were triumphant. Sir Thomas Fairfax's cavalry came under heavy fire from Goring's musketeers as they struggled to cross the ditch, which was a more significant obstacle on the eastern si
2 CONC de of the battlefield. When Goring's cavalry charged, Fairfax's front line was routed with heavy losses. Colonel Lambert's second line was apparently diverted to another part of the battlefield and was unable to support Fairfax. '
2 CONC ''Although Lord Eglinton's Scottish reserve resisted for some time, the Allied right wing was finally routed''' under the impact of a charge from Sir Marmaduke Langdale and the Northern Horse. While some of the Royalist cavalry sw
2 CONC ept over Marston Hill to plunder the Allied baggage train, Sir Charles Lucas rallied others to wheel right and attack the flank of the Allied infantry. Sir Thomas Fairfax found himself surrounded by the enemy and tore off his fiel
2 CONC d sign to avoid recognition before fighting his way across the battlefield with a few steadfast troopers to join Cromwell on the opposite flank.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From The Engagement, 1647-8:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.british-civil-wars.co.uk/glossary/engager.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT The Whiggamores
2 CONT
2 CONT The defeat at Preston was followed by a Covenanter uprising against the Engagers in south-western Scotland known as the Whiggamore Raid. Towards the end of August 1648, several thousand Whiggamores ("Whigs") marched from Ayrshire,
2 CONC  Kirkcudbright and Wigtownshire towards Edinburgh on a wave of popular support. Noblemen who had opposed the Engagement, including '''Lords Loudoun, Eglinton and Cassillis, put themselves at the head of the uprising'''; Argyll, Le
2 CONC ven and David Leslie declared their support for the movement.
2 CONT
2 CONT Engagers on the Committee of Estates ordered Major-General Monro to gather his forces at Berwick to resist the Whigs while the Earl of Lanark mustered the Scottish home army at Jedburgh. The Committee sent representatives to negot
2 CONC iate with them, but when they refused to halt their advance, the Committee fled from Edinburgh to join Lanark and Monro on the Borders. The Whigs occupied Edinburgh on 5 September. Lanark and Monro were in favour of fighting and w
2 CONC hen terms for a treaty were rejected, Engager forces marched around Edinburgh to capture Linlithgow on 10 September. Two days later, the Engagers routed the Marquis of Argyll's Campbell clansmen and occupied Stirling. By mid-Septe
2 CONC mber 1648, however, Cromwell and the New Model Army, fresh from the victory at Preston, had advanced to the Borders. Cromwell demanded that the Engagers hand back Berwick and Carlisle to the English Parliament or be prepared to su
2 CONC ffer the consequences. The Whigs sent representatives to assure Cromwell that no treaty would be made with the Engagers unless they agreed to disband their forces and relinquish power but this did not prevent Cromwell from marchin
2 CONC g into Scotland on 21 September.
2 CONT
2 CONT Supported by Cromwell and the English army, the Marquis of Argyll and the Whig leaders in Edinburgh demanded the disbandment of Engager forces. Despite the unpopularity of the English occupation, the Engagers gave way and signed t
2 CONC he Treaty of Stirling on 27 September 1648, which brought Engager dominance in Scotland to an end. When the Scottish Parliament met In January 1649, the Act of Classes was passed which excluded supporters of Hamilton and the Engag
2 CONC ers from public office in Scotland and ensured the supremacy of the covenanting Kirk Party.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Whiggamore" is said to be derived from "whiggam", the cry used by the insurgents to encourage their horses. At first only fundamentalist Covenanters were called Whigs but by 1689, the name was applied to the political party that 
2 CONC supported the exclusion of the Catholic James, Duke of York, from the succession to the throne, as distinct from the Tories who supported him.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the 1648 Timeline for the British Civil Wars, Commonwealth, and Protectorate:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.british-civil-wars.co.uk/timelines/1648.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT (Mid-March) A number of duels fought between leading opponents and supporters of the Engagement in Scotland: David Leslie against Lord SInclair, '''Lord Eglinton against Lord Glencairn''', the Marquis of Argyll against Lord Crawfo
2 CONC rd-Lindsay. No serious bloodshed occurs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Revolution and Counter-Revolution in Scotland 1644-1651, David Stevenson (Newton Abbott 1977)
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the 1650 Timeline for the British Civil Wars, Commonwealth, and Protectorate:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.british-civil-wars.co.uk/timelines/1650.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT July 29	
2 CONT *Cromwell advances towards the defensive lines around Edinburgh but cannot draw the Scots out into the open. 
2 CONT ** Sources:
2 CONT ***History of the Commonwealth and Protectorate vol. i, S.R. Gardiner (London 1903)
2 CONT ***Dunbar 1650: Cromwell's most famous victory, Stuart Reid (Osprey 2004)
2 CONT *'''Charles II joins the Earl of Eglinton at Leith near Edinburgh'''. He hopes to win the support of Scottish nobles and army officers against the Kirk Party. 
2 CONT **Source: History of the Commonwealth and Protectorate vol. i, S.R. Gardiner (London 1903)
2 CONT *Commonwealth warships bombard Leith.
2 CONT **Source: Cromwell's Generals, Maurice Ashley (Oxford 1954)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He was given the name of Alexander Seton at birth. He gained the title of 6th Earl of Eglinton on 4 September 1612.1 He succeeded as the 7th Lord Montgomerie on 4 September 1612.1 On 24 March 1615 he resigned his titles, and had a
2 CONC  regrant. He fought in the Battle of Marston Moor in 1644, for the Parliamentarians. He fought in the Captain in 1650, Horse Guards. for King Charles II.1 Between 1651 and 1660 he was imprisoned.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Alexander Montgomerie, 6th Earl of Eglinton and Lady Anne Livingstone
