1 OCCU 9th Earl of Argyll, Peer of the Realm
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===ARCHIBALD CAMPBELL, 9th EARL OF ARGYLL===
2 CONT
2 CONT *Birth: 	Feb. 26, 1629 Dalkeith, Midlothian, Scotland
2 CONT *Death: 	Jun. 30, 1685, Edinburgh, City of Edinburgh, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT The Paper Caves on Lock Eck side were the hiding place of the Campbell estate papers during the rout of Argyll in 1685 
2 CONT
2 CONT Argyll's tradition enemies now could only believe that the rise and fortunes of the Argyll family had surely come to an abrupt halt yet the 9th Earl's son was to have lands and title restored and was made the first Duke of Argyll.
2 CONT
2 CONT The bodies of the Marquis and the 9th Earl are interred in the family mausoleum at Kilmun. After a suitable period of being a grisly warning on spikes at Edinburgh Tolbooth their heads were brought to Kilmun and reunited with thei
2 CONC r bodies.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Paper Caves are associated with this period where the estate papers and titles were hidden to keep them safe till better times and fortunes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oxford Dictionary of National Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Campbell, Archibald, ninth earl of Argyll (1629–1685), politician and clan leader, was born on 26 February 1629 at Newbattle Abbey, Edinburghshire, the son of Archibald Campbell, Lord Lorne and later eighth earl and marquess of Ar
2 CONC gyll (1605x7–1661), and his wife, Lady Margaret (1610–1678), daughter of William Douglas, earl of Morton (1582–1648). At the age of four he was, in accordance with Gaelic tradition, fostered with a kinsman, Sir Colin Campbell of G
2 CONC lenorchy, living mainly at Balloch, Perthshire. After his father had inherited the earldom in 1638 he was known by the courtesy title Lord Lorne, and he returned to his family at Inveraray Castle in 1639. He was sent to the Univer
2 CONC sity of Glasgow in 1642, but he did not graduate.
2 CONT
2 CONT escaping [sic] from Edinburgh Castle on 20 December 1681 with the help of his stepdaughter Sophia Lindsay, the success of his disguise as a page no doubt being helped by his short stature.
2 CONT
2 CONT executed and [sic] As his head fell, his body, in a macabre spasm, jumped upright on its feet, spouting blood ‘like a cascade or jette d'eau' before being held down by the executioners (Lauder, Historical Observes, 194). His head,
2 CONC  like that of his father before him, was displayed on a spike on the top of Edinburgh Tolbooth, while his body was buried at Newbattle Abbey, where he had been born. The head was reunited with the body in 1689 when his eldest son,
2 CONC  Archibald Campbell (d. 1703), was restored as tenth earl, and in 1704 they were taken to his family's burial place at Kilmun.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reputation being the son of ‘the great marquess' brought him many enemies.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1660 to 1681 he fought a long and successful battle for the survival of himself and his family
2 CONT
2 CONT Though successful up to 1681, Argyll was not well liked. He was short-tempered, and never sought popularity
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the 8th Earl's eldest son. He and Mary had seven children. He became the 9th Earl of Argyll on October 16, 1663. He had no issue by his second wife. In June 1685, the 9th Earl of Argyll unsuccessfully tried to invade Scotla
2 CONC nd, and on June 30, 1685 he was executed. As a result of his failure, two of his sons and a nephew who accompanied him were banished, his brother was forced to flee the country, and several others left involuntarily. [Neil Grant, 
2 CONC The Campbells of Argyll, published by Franklin Watts, London, England, 1975, page 55]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lives of the Lindsays By Alexander Crawford Lindsay Crawford pg 153 (letters from the 9th Earl of Argyll the day of his execution - to his daughter in law) pg 152
2 CONT
2 CONT "My dear Lady Sophia, "What shall I say in this great day of the Lord, wherein, in the midst of a cloud, I find a fair sunshine? I can wish no more for you, but that the Lord may comfort you and shine upon you as He doth upon me, 
2 CONC and give you the same sense of His love in staying in the world, as I have in going out of it. 'Adieu! 'ARGYLE 'P.S. My blessing to dearl Earl of Balcarres: the Lord touch his heart, and incline him to His fear!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Married 1st Lady Mary Stewart b 1628 d May 1668 Married 2nd Lady Anne MacKenzie no issue. She was 1st wife of Alexander Lindsay, 1st Earl of Balcarres
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald was succeeded by his son, Archibald Campbell, 10th Earl and 1st Duke of Argyll
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Genealogy===
2 CONT
2 CONT # [http://www.thepeerage.com/p1226.htm#i12256 Darryl Lundy's Peerage: Archibald Campbell, 9th Earl of Argyll]
2 CONT # [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archibald_Campbell,_9th_Earl_of_Argyll Wikipedia: Archibald Campbell, 9th Earl of Argyll]
2 CONT # [https://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GSln=campbell&GSfn=archibald&GSbyrel=all&GSdyrel=all&GScntry=50&GSob=n&GSsr=41&GRid=98051237&df=all& Find A Grave: Archibald Campbell, 9th Earl of Argyll]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archibald_Campbell,_9th_Earl_of_Argyll '''Archibald Campbell''', 9th '''Earl of Argyll'''] was a Scottish peer and soldier.
2 CONT
2 CONT The hereditary chief of Clan Campbell, and a prominent figure in Scottish politics, he was a Royalist supporter during the latter stages of the Scottish Civil War and its aftermath. During the period of the Cromwellian Protectorat
2 CONC e he was involved in several Royalist uprisings and was for a time imprisoned.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Restoration of Charles II, despite his previous Royalism he fell under suspicion due to his hereditary judicial powers in the Highlands and his strong Presbyterian religious sympathies. Condemned to death in 1681 on a hi
2 CONC ghly dubious charge of treason and libel, he escaped from prison and was later to mount "Argyll's Rising", a 1685 Scottish rebellion against James II intended to support the Monmouth Rebellion. The Rising failed, and Argyll was ca
2 CONC ptured and beheaded.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ='''ARCHIBALD CAMPBELL, 9th EARL OF ARGYLL'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Birth: Feb. 26, 1629 Dalkeith, Midlothian, Scotland
2 CONT * Death: Jun. 30, 1685, Edinburgh, City of Edinburgh, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald Campbell, 9th Earl of Argyll was a Scottish peer and soldier. The hereditary chief of Clan Campbell, and a prominent figure in Scottish politics, he was a Royalist supporter during the latter stages of the Scottish Civil
2 CONC  War and its aftermath.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, the 8th. Earl had been executed for treason in 1661, Archibald was restored to the Earldom, but he was also beheaded in 1685.
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke of Argyll (Scottish Gaelic: Diùc Earra-Ghàidheil) is a title, created in the Peerage of Scotland in 1701 and in the Peerage of the United Kingdom in 1892. The Earls, Marquesses, and Dukes of Argyll were for several centuries 
2 CONC among the most powerful noble families in Scotland. As such, they played a major role in Scottish history throughout the 16th, 17th, and 18th centuries. The Duke of Argyll also holds the hereditary titles of chief of Clan Campbell
2 CONC  and Master of the Household of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since 2001, Torquhil Campbell has been Duke of Argyll and is the thirteenth man to hold the title.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The Paper Caves on Lock Eck side were the hiding place of the Campbell estate papers during the rout of Argyll in 1685 
2 CONT
2 CONT Argyll's tradition enemies now could only believe that the rise and fortunes of the Argyll family had surely come to an abrupt halt yet the 9th Earl's son was to have lands and title restored and was made the first Duke of Argyll.
2 CONT
2 CONT The bodies of the Marquis and the 9th Earl are interred in the family mausoleum at Kilmun. After a suitable period of being a grisly warning on spikes at Edinburgh Tolbooth their heads were brought to Kilmun and reunited with thei
2 CONC r bodies.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Paper Caves are associated with this period where the estate papers and titles were hidden to keep them safe till better times and fortunes.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Oxford Dictionary of National Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Campbell, Archibald, ninth earl of Argyll (1629–1685)''', politician and clan leader, was born on 26 February 1629 at Newbattle Abbey, Edinburghshire, the son of Archibald Campbell, Lord Lorne and later eighth earl and marquess
2 CONC  of Argyll (1605x7–1661), and his wife, Lady Margaret (1610–1678), daughter of William Douglas, earl of Morton (1582–1648). At the age of four he was, in accordance with Gaelic tradition, fostered with a kinsman, Sir Colin Campbel
2 CONC l of Glenorchy, living mainly at Balloch, Perthshire. After his father had inherited the earldom in 1638 he was known by the courtesy title Lord Lorne, and he returned to his family at Inveraray Castle in 1639. He was sent to the 
2 CONC University of Glasgow in 1642, but he did not graduate.
2 CONT
2 CONT escaping [sic] from Edinburgh Castle on 20 December 1681 with the help of his stepdaughter Sophia Lindsay, the success of his disguise as a page no doubt being helped by his short stature.
2 CONT
2 CONT executed and [sic] As his head fell, his body, in a macabre spasm, jumped upright on its feet, spouting blood ‘like a cascade or jette d'eau' before being held down by the executioners (Lauder, Historical Observes, 194). His head,
2 CONC  like that of his father before him, was displayed on a spike on the top of Edinburgh Tolbooth, while his body was buried at Newbattle Abbey, where he had been born. The head was reunited with the body in 1689 when his eldest son,
2 CONC  Archibald Campbell (d. 1703), was restored as tenth earl, and in 1704 they were taken to his family's burial place at Kilmun.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reputation being the son of ‘the great marquess' brought him many enemies.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1660 to 1681 he fought a long and successful battle for the survival of himself and his family
2 CONT
2 CONT Though successful up to 1681, Argyll was not well liked. He was short-tempered, and never sought popularity
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the 8th Earl's eldest son. He and Mary had seven children. He became the 9th Earl of Argyll on October 16, 1663. He had no issue by his second wife. In June 1685, the 9th Earl of Argyll unsuccessfully tried to invade Scotla
2 CONC nd, and on June 30, 1685 he was executed. As a result of his failure, two of his sons and a nephew who accompanied him were banished, his brother was forced to flee the country, and several others left involuntarily. [Neil Grant, 
2 CONC The Campbells of Argyll, published by Franklin Watts, London, England, 1975, page 55]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lives of the Lindsays By Alexander Crawford Lindsay Crawford pg 153 (letters from the 9th Earl of Argyll the day of his execution - to his daughter in law) pg 152
2 CONT
2 CONT "My dear Lady Sophia, "What shall I say in this great day of the Lord, wherein, in the midst of a cloud, I find a fair sunshine? I can wish no more for you, but that the Lord may comfort you and shine upon you as He doth upon me, 
2 CONC and give you the same sense of His love in staying in the world, as I have in going out of it. 'Adieu! 'ARGYLE 'P.S. My blessing to dearl Earl of Balcarres: the Lord touch his heart, and incline him to His fear!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Married 1st Lady Mary Stewart b 1628 d May 1668 Married 2nd Lady Anne MacKenzie no issue. She was 1st wife of Alexander Lindsay, 1st Earl of Balcarres
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald was succeeded by his son, Archibald Campbell, 10th Earl and 1st Duke of Argyll
2 CONT
