1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===JEAN CAMPBELL of CLAN CAMPBELL 1st MARCHIONESS-CONSORT of LOTHIAN===
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Jean Campbell was born in Scotland.
2 CONT Her parents were Archibald Campbell the Chief of Clan Campbell, 1st Marquess of Argyll, 8th Earl of Argyll, a member of the Privy Council of Scotland, a member of the Assembly of Divines, and Member of Parliament in 1659 for Aberd
2 CONC eenshire; and his wife Margaret ''Douglass'' Campbell Marchioness-Consort of Argyll, daughter of William Douglas 7th Earl of Morton.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father Archibald the ''de facto'' head of Scotland's government during most of the conflict of the 1640s & 50s known as the ''Wars of the Three Kingdoms'', he was a major figure in the Covenanter Movement that fought for the m
2 CONC aintenance of the Presbyterian religion against the Stuart monarchy's attempts to impose Episcopalian. He is often remembered as the principal opponent of the royalist General James Graham 1st Marquess of Montrose. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean's birthdate is not precisely known. Based upon the dates of her marriage, the birth of their first child, the age of her parents, and a portrait of her that is dated 1654 by the artist, it is speculated that she was born appr
2 CONC oximately 1631.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January of 1660/61, the year of the re-establishment of the British monarchy under King Charles II, Jean married the Scottish Lord Robert Kerr 1st Marquess of Lothian,
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT     William Kerr, 2nd Marquess of Lothian (1661–1722)
2 CONT     Lord Charles Kerr (d. 1735), appointed Director of Chancery in 1703, married Janet Murray, daughter of Sir David Murray, 2nd Baronet, and had issue
2 CONT     Hon. Margaret Kerr (bap. 1670), died young
2 CONT     Hon. Jean Kerr (bap. 1671), died young
2 CONT     Lord John Kerr (bap. 1673 – 8 September 1735), British Army officer
2 CONT     Lady Mary Kerr (bap. 1674 – 22 January 1736), married James Douglas, 2nd Marquess of Douglas
2 CONT     Lord Mark Kerr (bap. 1676–1752), British general
2 CONT     Lady Margaret Kerr (bap. 1678), died young
2 CONT     Hon. James Kerr (bap. 1679), died unmarried
2 CONT     Lady Annabella Kerr (bap. 1682), died young
2 CONT
2 CONT Lothian also had a natural son, Captain John Kerr, who was slain at Douglas Castle by the Duke of Douglas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT other sources say she died on 31 July 1712
