1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://www.thepeerage.com/p10562.htm#i105618]
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Villiers was the daughter of Sir Edward Villiers and Barbara St. John.3,1 
2 CONT
2 CONT A contract for the marriage of Elizabeth Villiers and Robert Douglas, 8th Earl of Morton was signed on 28 April 1627.4 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died before 15 December 1654, from a fever.4
2 CONT
2 CONT *     From 28 April 1627, her married name became Douglas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Elizabeth Villiers and Robert Douglas, 8th Earl of Morton
2 CONT
2 CONT     * William Douglas, 9th Earl of Morton d. 1681
2 CONT     * Lady Anne Douglas1
2 CONT     * Lady Mary Douglas+5
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT *   1. [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, E
2 CONC xtinct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume VIII, page 483. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *   2. [S3409] Caroline Maubois, "re: Penancoet Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger Lundy, 2 December 2008. Hereinafter cited as "re: Penancoet Family."
2 CONT *   3. [S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 2, page 2096. Hereinafter cited as Burke
2 CONC 's Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT *   4. [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume IX, page 296.
2 CONT *  5. [S15] George Edward Cokayne, editor, The Complete Baronetage, 5 volumes (no date (c. 1900); reprint, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 1983), volume II, page 291. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Baronetage.
2 CONT *GIVEN NAME: Anne not Elizabeth - see 'The Complete Peerage', vol. 9 p. 296 fn. e.
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne_Douglas,_Countess_of_Morton 
2 CONT **  Clarendon's regard for Anne's memory is thought to have been one of the reasons for his hatred of her niece Barbara Villiers, mistress of Charles II: he thought it intolerable that so close a relative of his old friend should 
2 CONC disgrace her family in this fashion. This eventually contributed to his downfall, since Barbara returned his hatred, and worked constantly to destroy him.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Countess of Morton
