1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == WILLIAM DOUGLAS, EARL OF MORTON ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William Douglas, Earl of Morton, here treated, is the son of https://www.geni.com/people/Robert-Douglas-8th-Earl-of-Morton/6000000003615653077?through=6000000014013487956 and the grandson of https://www.geni.com/people/William-
2 CONC Douglas-7th-Earl-of-Morton/6000000016992278732. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD34%2f70&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=Earl+of+Morton&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1660&dt=1685&di=y NRS: GD34/70]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Death ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William Douglas, Earl of Morton, here treated, is reported to have died in 1681. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/page/379/mode/1up The Scots Peerage VI: 379]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == First Marriage ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William Douglas, Earl of Morton, here treated, married https://www.geni.com/people/Grizel-Middleton/6000000003391270973?through=6000000014013487956. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun06paul
2 CONC uoft/page/379/mode/1up The Scots Peerage VI: 379] The marriage was booked on 12 June 1662, at Canongate, near Edinburgh, in Midlothian, Scotland. [National Records of Scotland, Canongate Marriages, reference OPR. 685/3]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Second Marriage ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William Douglas, Earl of Morton, here treated, is reported to have married https://www.geni.com/people/Marjory-Foulis/6000000210591480831?through=6000000014013487956. [http://www.thepeerage.com/p2596.htm#i25954 Darryl Lundy's P
2 CONC eerage]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Child of First Marriage ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # ''' https://www.geni.com/people/Charles-Douglas-died-young/6000000210591648842?through=6000000014013487956 '''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/page/379/mode/1up Biographical Summary by The Scots Peerage] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William Doulas, Earl of Morton: "had a new grant in 1662 of the islands of Orkney and Zetland, but as his grandfather had spent a large proportion of his fortune on the support of the royalist cause, he himself was in embarrass
2 CONC ed circumstances, and the grant was taken in the name of his cousin, Viscount Grandison, in trust for the Morton family. In 1669 Parliament ratified a decree of the Court of Session, acting on the instance of the Lord Advocate, re
2 CONC ducing the original disposition of the islands of Orkney and Zetland of 1643 and the confirmation of 1662. The narrative related that 'importunity prevailing with his Majestic and his royal father, their goodness and inclination t
2 CONC o gratify their subjects, they have been induced to give away and part with so great a jewel of the Crown'. This iniquitous Act, one of the most disgraceful ever passed by the Scots Parliament, not only caused grave loss, if not a
2 CONC bsolute ruin, to the Morton family, but involved other creditors who had lent the Earl money on the security of lands, of which he had a valid title from the Crown. Sir Andrew Dick of Craighouse, for instance, lost 100,274 merks, 
2 CONC which he had lent on an apparently absolutely safe investment. On 9 September 1672 the Earl of Morton renounced his right to the style and designation of Lord Dalkeith, in favour of James, Duke of Buccleuch and Monmouth, a deed wh
2 CONC ich was ratified by Parliament the same year. A large portion of the Kinross-shire lands which belonged to the Earl were also parted with to Sir William Bruce of Balcaskie at this time. The Earl died in 1681, having married at Hol
2 CONC yroodhouse, 12 June 1662, Grizel, eldest daughter of John, first Earl of Middleton. By her, who died March 1666, he had issue one son, Charles, born 7, baptized 10, February 1663. He died v.p. [https://archive.org/details/scotspee
2 CONC ragefoun06pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/page/379/mode/1up The Scots Peerage VI: 379]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''12 June 1662: Articles of Matrimonial Contract between William, Earl of Morton and Lady Grizel Middleton, eldest lawful daughter of John, Earl of Middleton, his Majesty's Commissioner in Scotland, with consent of her said noble
2 CONC  father. Witnesses: William, Earl Marischal [Marshall], Lord Privy Seal, Sir John Gilmour of Craigmillar and Sir Archibald Primrose, bart., Clerk Register and Sir John Fletcher, his Majesty's Advocate. [https://catalogue.nrscotlan
2 CONC d.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD33%2f60%2f2&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=grizel+middleton&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1660&dt=1685&di=n National Records of Scotland, Marquess of Aberdeen and Temair. reference GD33/60/2]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''9 March 1666: Letters of Special Charge at the instance of Mr John Hay of Haystoun, one of the clerks of Session, charging [William] Earl of Mortoun to enter heir to the deceased Robert, Earl of Morton, his father and the decea
2 CONC sed [William], Earl of Morton, his grandfather, in the superiority of the lands of Escheilles [Eshiels]; certificate of execution, 11 April 1666. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD34%
2 CONC 2f70&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=Earl+of+Morton&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1660&dt=1685&di=y National Records of Scotland, Papers of the family of Hay of Haystoun, Peeblesshire, reference GD34/70]'''
2 CONT
