1 NAME John /Rutherford/
2 GIVN John
2 SURN Rutherford
2 _MARNM Rutherford
2 NICK Rutherfurd
1 NAME John ‘of that Ilk’ of Edgerston
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1618
2 ADDR
3 CITY Roxburgh
3 STAE Scottish Borders
3 CTRY Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE BEF 1686
2 PLAC Scotland
2 ADDR
1 TITL Lord
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Burkes has https://archive.org/details/genealogicalhera02byuburk/page/1392/mode/2up?view=theater p 1391 -2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''RUTHERFURD    OF    FAIRNINGTON… lineage…'''
2 CONT Among  the  earliest  of  the  names  of  Rutherford are  to  be  found  Robertus,  Dominus  de  Rutherford,  witness to  a  charter  granted  by  David  I.  to  Gervasus  Ridal  1140,  and a  Rutherford  or  Rordesforde  in  the 
2 CONC  charter  of  William  the Lion  1165.  During  the  period  from  1105  to  1249  occur  the names  of  Gregory  and  Nicholas  Rutherford.  Sir  Robert Rutherford,  the  friend  of  Robert  Bruce,  is  mentioned  in Barbour's  H
2 CONC istory  of  that  Prince.  
2 CONT In  1398,  Sir  Richard Rutherford,  of  that  ilk,  was  Ambassador  to  the  Court  of England,  and  in  1400  was  one  of  the  Wardens  of  the  Marches. By  his  wife  Jane  Douglas,  he  had  three  sons;  
2 CONT *James,  who  .s. him,  had  a  charter  of  the  lands  of  Lethbertshiels  and  others; 
2 CONT *John,  ancestor  of  Rutheford  of  Hunt  hill,  who  eventually  enjoyed the  honours  and  titles  of  Lord  Rutherford  and  Earl  of Teviot;  and  
2 CONT *Nichol,  who  obtained  charters  of  the  lands  of Mackerston  and  others,  and  was  ancestor  of  the  Rutherfords of  Hundalee.  James  and  Nichol  were  appointed  guarantees of  a  treaty  with  the  English  1449.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT James  Rutherford,  son  of  the  last-mentioned  James,  obtained, 1457,  a  charter  of  lands  to  him  and  his  spouse,  Margaret Erskine,  dau.  of  Lord  Erskine.  He  had  a  grant  from James  IV.  of  the  lands  of  Edg
2 CONC erston  in  1492,  and  shortly  after of  the  lands  of  Eutherford  and  Wells.  By  his  said  wife  he had  two  sons,  Philip  and  Thomas.    The  elder  son, 
2 CONT >PHILIP  RUTHERFORD,  m.  Elizabeth,  dau.  of  Walter  Ker,  of Cessford,  ancestor  of  the  Dukes  of  Roxburghe,  and  had  (with a  son,  
2 CONT >>Richard,  who  d.  s.  p.)  two  daus.,  the  elder,  
2 CONT >>Helen,  m. twice,  and  d.  s.  p. ;  the  younger.  
2 CONT >>Christian,  m.  James  Stuart, ancestor  to  the  Earls  of  Traquair,  and  through  her  the  estates of  Rutherford  and  Wells  passed  to  the  family  of  Stuart;  and the  Earls  of  Traquair,  in  right  of  this  marria
2 CONC ge,  quartered  the arms  of  Rutherford  with  their  own.  On  the  death  of  the  above Richard  without  issue,  the  lands  and  barony  of  Edgerston reverted  to  his  uncle,  
2 CONT
2 CONT >THOMAS  RUTHERFORD,  heir  male  of  the  family:  and his  son,  
2 CONT >>Robert,  obtained  a  confirmation  of  the  same  under  the Great  Seal  1559.     This  Robert  was  s.  by  his  son,
2 CONT >>>THOMAS  RUTHERFORD,  commonly  called  the  black  Laird  of Eutherford,  renowned  for  the  exploits  of  himself  and  his  nine sons  on  the  borders.     His  son,
2 CONT >>>>Richard  Rutherford,  of  that  ilk,  m.  the  granddau.  of  the Laird  of  Buccleuch,  and  had  a  son,
2 CONT >>>>>ROBERT RUTHERFORD,  who  m.  Margaret  Riddell,  dau.  of  the Laird  of  that  ilk,  and  had  a  son,
2 CONT >>>>>>'''JOHN  RUTHERFORD,  of  that  ilk.  who  acted  a  gallant  and distinguished  part  in  the  Civil  War,  and  was  instrumental  in restoring  Charles  II.  He  m.  Barbara  Abernethy,  dau.  of  the Bishop  of  Caithnes
2 CONC s  (by  a  dau.  of  Murray,  of  Philiphaugh)''', and  had  issue  surviving  sons,
2 CONT >>>>>>>ANDREW,  s.  to  the  estates  in  1686,  and  d.  s.  p.  1713.
2 CONT >>>>>>>THOMAS RUTHERFORD, of that ilk,  of  Edgerston  and  Wells, successor  to  his  brother, acquired  the  lands  of  Bonjedward  and  Mounthooly,  together with  the  lands  of  Hunthill  and  Scraisburgh,  from  Robert, Lord
2 CONC   Rutherford,  who  likewise  assigned  to  Thomas  (with  the estate)  the  title  of  Lord  Rutherford,  in  like  manner  as  it  had been  assigned  to  his  brothers  and  to  him  by  the  Earl  of  Teviot, by  virtue  of  t
2 CONC he  patent,  which  was  the  only  right  they  had. But  Thomas  contented  himself  with  assigning  it  in  like manner  to  his  son  and  successor,  who  likewise  declined claiming  it. 
2 CONT >>>>>>>Robert,   became  Rutherford  of  Bowland,  and  was  ancestor (through  the  female  line)  of   Sir  Walter  Scott,   Bart,  of Abbotsford. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT It  was,  however,  assumed  by  Capt.  John  Rutherford, collateral  heir  male,  descended  from  Richard  Rutherford of  Littlehaugh,  and  since  by  others.  He  m.  Susanna,  dau. and  heiress  of  Riddell,  of  Minto,  and 
2 CONC  had  issue  (with  five daus.)  one  son.
2 CONT >SIR  JOHN  RUTHERFORD,  of  that  ilk,  s.  his  father  1720.  He m.  1st,  1710,  Elizabeth  Cairncross,  heiress  of  the  ancient  and honourable  family  of  Colmslie,  and  had  nineteen  children,  of whom  the  eldest  so
2 CONC n,  
2 CONT >>John,  was  ancestor  (in  the  female  line of William  Oliver-Rutherford,  Esq.  of  Edgerton  ;  the  2nd  son was  
2 CONT >>'''THOMAS''',  of  whose  line  we  treat [below];  another  son,  
2 CONT >>Robert,  a baron  of  the  Russian  Empire,  purchased  back,  1779,  the  old family  property  of  Fairnington ;  and  another  son  
2 CONT >>Walter,  m. Mary,  dau.  of  Gen.  Alexander.  Of  the  daus.,  
2 CONT >>Susanna,  m. Alexander  Pringle,  of  Whytbank;  
2 CONT >>Mary,  m  Sir  Alexander Nisbet,  of  Dean;  
2 CONT >>Elizabeth,  m John  Horsburgh.  of  that  ilk; and  
2 CONT >>Jean  m.  Charles  Inglis,  of  Manorhead.  
2 CONT >Sir  John  m.  2ndly, 1741,  Sarah,  sister  of  Sir  Alexander  Nisbet,  Bart.,  by  whom  he had  also  a  son  and  a  dau.,
2 CONT >>Henry,  of  Hunthill,  m.  Agnes  Lauder,  and  had  issue.
2 CONT >>Christian,  m.  Dr.  Davidson,  of  Hatton  and  Muirhouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT The  2nd  son [see above],
2 CONT >>'''Thomas ''' RUTHERFORD,  Esq.  m 1745,  Martha,  dau.  of  Alder- man Town,  of  York,  and  dying  1749,  left  a  son,
2 CONT >>>John  RUTHERFORD,  Capt.  42nd  regt.,  and  Major  Dumfries Militia,  m.  1st,  1769,  Eleanor,  dau.  of  Thomas  Chalke,  Esq.  of Artane  and  Grange,  co.  Dublin  (by  Anne  Gordon  his  wife), and  by  her  (who  d.  179
2 CONC 5)  had  issue,
2 CONT >>>>John,  b.  1777;  d.  unm.  I15I2.
2 CONT >>>>Charles,  b.  1780;  d.  unm.  1846.        Thomas,  of  Fairnington.
2 CONT >>>>Martha,  m.  Charles  Robson,  Esq.
2 CONT >>>>Eleanor,  m.  William  Elliott,  Esq.  of  Harwood.
2 CONT He  m 2ndly,  Agnes,  dau.  of  J.  Chatto,  Esq.  of  Mainhouse,  and by  her  (who  d.  1850)  had  a  son,  
2 CONT >>>>Walter,  m.  Mary,  dau.  of  Capt. Knight,  R.N.,  and  d.  1856,  leaving  issue  two  sons  and  two daus.     He  d 1830,  and  was  s.  by  his  son,
2 CONT >>>>>Thomas  RUTHERFORD,  Esq.  of  Fairnington,  J. P.,  b.  1784; m.  Caroline  Sanderson,  dau.  of  William  Ball,  Esq.  of  London <by  Lydia  Wyvil  his  wife),  and  had  issue,
2 CONT >>>>>>Henry,  now  of  Fairnington.
2 CONT >>>>>>Thomas  Walter,  b.  4  Sept.  1832,  H.M.  Indian  Staff  Corps.
2 CONT >>>>>>Charles  Ferdinand,  b.  24  Jan.  1834;  1,1.  1  Oct.  18.i9,  Harriet Guilliam,  dau.  of  Rev.  George  S.  Parsons,  Government Chaplain,  and  lncumbent  of  St.  Mary'.s,  Castletown,  Isle  of Man. 
2 CONT >>>>>>John,  b  20  Feb.  1835,  in  H.M.  Indian  Navy.
2 CONT >>>>>>William,  6.  26  April,  1836.       Richard  Capel,  b.  2  April,  1837.
2 CONT >>>>>>Edward,  b.  7  Feb.  1839;  d.  14  Nov.  1870.
2 CONT >>>>>> James,  b.  9  Jan.  1842,  H.M.  Indian  army.
2 CONT >>>>>>George,  b.  6  Sept.  1845;  d  2  Jan.  1851.
2 CONT >>>>>>Caroline,  m.  1863,  Lieut. -Col.  Raynsford,  of  the  E.I.C.S.
2 CONT >>>>>>Ellen.      
2 CONT >>>>>>Eliza  Lydia.        
2 CONT >>>>>>Agnes  Matilda,
2 CONT He  d.  27  March,  1863.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord John Rutherford of Edgerston, Roxburghshire, Scotland 1622 - 11/1686 [other sources say 1681?] married Barbara Abernethy died 1697
2 CONT Lord John succeeded to the Lordship of Edgerston in his father's lifetime in 1639 under the aggreement that he would maintain his full brothers and sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cockburn-Hood shows that there were 4 brothers and 12 sisters, however, only four baptisms appear in the Jedburgh register.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~rutherford/general%20griffith%20line.htm]
