1 NOTE {geni:about_me} CHETWODE, Sir John (d.1412), of Chetwode, Bucks. and Warkworth, Northants., son of Sir Nicholas Chetwode (d.1369) of Chetwode by Elizabeth, da. of Sir John Lyons† of Warkworth.
2 CONT
2 CONT m. (1) bef. 1371, Mary (fl. 1391), 1s. 1da.; (2) bef. 1393 Amabel, (d. 8 Sept. 1430), da. of Sir Thomas Green of Green’s Norton, Northants., 1s. 1da.1 Kntd. bef. Oct. 1386.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of his death, which occurred on 2 Apr. 1412, Chetwode was holding office as verderer of Whittlewood forest. The monumental brass placed over his grave in Warkworth church depicts him as wearing armour, his feet resting
2 CONC  on a lion, his hands lifted as in prayer. The offspring of his first marriage — John and Margery (the wife of John Browning (1397-1420), son of John Browning* of Melbury Sampford, Dorset) — both died in 1420, leaving as heir to t
2 CONC he Chetwode estates their half-brother (Sir) Thomas Chetwode. A few years after Sir John’s death, his widow Amabel married Thomas Strange*.5
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.histparl.ac.uk/volume/1386-1421/member/chetwode-sir-john-1412
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Strange’s marriage to Amabel, niece of Richard II’s unpopular favourite, Sir Henry Green*, and widow of Sir John Chetwode, probably took place before his appointment as escheator of Northamptonshire in 1418, as it was to he
2 CONC r that he owed his influence in the county.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1386-1421/member/strange-thomas-1436
2 CONT
