1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''James Stewart of Kilcattan''' 
2 CONT * Birth: ABT 1445 in Kilcattan, Isle of Bute, Scotland
2 CONT * Death: 1477 in Isle of Bute, Isle of Bute, Scotland
2 CONT * Father: William Stewart of Fennock b: ABT 1395 
2 CONT * Mother: Margaret b: ABT 1425
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT James Stewart of Kilcattan held the office of Sheriff of Bute circa 1468. On 3 November 1474 he received a Crown Charter for one acre for Kilcattan.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of his wife is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT James was Keeper of Rothesay Castle until Martinmas 1465. He received a salary until 1477.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Stewart had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * - James (died soon after 1481 without issue)
2 CONT * - Ninian (c.1460) married 1) Miss Campbell, 2) Jonet Dunlop, 3) Elizabeth Blair.
2 CONT * - Robert married the daughter of John Lamond.
2 CONT * * - James "Secundus" married Marabella Crawford
2 CONT * - Jonet married 1) Archibald or Alexander Campbell, 2) Ninian Bannatyne.1
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p19308.htm#i193074
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilchattan Bay is village on the Isle of Bute, Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It lies the island's southern end, along the coast road at the foot of a steep hill called the Suidhe Chattan which shields the village from the prevailing westerly wind.  The village faces the mainland to the east across the Firt
2 CONC h of Clyde. A sandy bay known locally as the Wee Bay sweeps around to the north.
2 CONT
2 CONT The village is named after the sixth century bishop, Saint Cathan, who established a hermitage at this location in AD 539.
2 CONT  His nephew was Saint Blane and a chapel was established in his honour in the 12th century
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT James obtained, for his good and faithful services, a commission from king James IV. of the heretable constabulary of the castle of Rothsay, in the isle of Bute; a royal seat, wherein king Robert III. died.  The commission is date
2 CONC d anno 1498.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT James obtained, for his good and faithful services, a commission from king James IV. of the heretable constabulary of the castle of Rothsay,in the Isle of Bute, until Martimas 1465; a royal seat, wherein king Robert III. died.  Th
2 CONC e commission is dated anno 1498.
2 CONT
2 CONT JAMES STEWART of Bute, who, died without issue anno 1497, was succeeded by his cousin and heir-male '''James, son of his uncle William of Fynock (Fennock). '''
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
