1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Thomas Erskine, 2nd Lord Erskine (<1440 - <1493)'''
2 CONT s/o Lord Robert Erskine & dau (likely Elizabeth) of David, Earl of Crawford
2 CONT x '''Janet Douglas''', d/o unknown
2 CONT * '''Alexander Erskine''', 3rd Lord Erskine.
2 CONT * '''Helen Erskine''' x  Sir Humphrey Colquhoun of Luss
2 CONT * '''Isabel Erskine''' x Patrick Graham of Kilpont, heir of Malise, Earl of Menteith
2 CONT * '''Elizabeth Erskine''' x Sir Alexander Seton of Touch
2 CONT * '''Mariota Erskine''' x William, 2nd Earl Marischal.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Thomas Erskine, 2nd Lord Erskine (d. by 1493) ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Thomas, second Lord Erskine''' first appears on record on 24 January 1440/41, as a consenting party to a charter by his father, at which time he is styled a knight. He is later referred to during his father’s lifetime, includin
2 CONC g in 1446 as *Master of Mar*, and is first styled *Lord Erskine* in a Crown charter dated at Stirling on 6 November 1452.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 21 March 1452/53, he petitioned the King and General Council at Edinburgh for justice regarding his claim to the '''Earldom of Mar and Garioch'''. The Chancellor replied that the King would adjudicate the matter following Whits
2 CONC unday during a royal progress to the north. However, this promise was not kept, and proceedings resumed only on 5 November 1457 in a Court of Justiciary at Aberdeen.
2 CONT
2 CONT There, jurors who had sworn to the 1438 retour in favour of his father, Robert, Lord Erskine, were re-examined—some in private—and five later gave evidence contradicting the original verdict. A new jury was formed, including six o
2 CONC f the previously examined jurors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Erskine requested the standard '''brieve of inquest and service''', which was granted. He claimed that his father had died vested in half of the earldom of Mar and that he, as the lawful heir of age, was entitled to inherit. 
2 CONC The Chancellor disputed these claims, asserting that King James I had held the earldom by authority of an Act of Parliament. The jury ruled against Lord Erskine, annulling the 1438 retour and declaring that the lands had reverted 
2 CONC to the Crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT After this defeat, Lord Erskine largely retired from public life. He received a pension of £100 from the revenues of Aberdeen, attended sessions of Parliament, and witnessed various charters. He is also said to have supported King
2 CONC  James III during the conflict of 1488. He died in or shortly before 1493, as his son Alexander received sasine of the lands of Sintoun at that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married '''Janet Douglas''', who is named in a charter dated 12 August 1489. Although sometimes identified as a daughter of James, 1st Earl of Morton, and Lady Joan Stewart (daughter of King James I), this is now considered imp
2 CONC robable, and her precise parentage remains uncertain.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Children ===
2 CONT * '''Alexander Erskine''', who succeeded as 3rd Lord Erskine.
2 CONT * '''Helen Erskine''', married Sir Humphrey Colquhoun of Luss; mother of Sir John Colquhoun of Luss.
2 CONT * '''Isabel Erskine''', married (by papal dispensation dated 24 January 1465/66) Patrick Graham of Kilpont, heir of Malise, Earl of Menteith; had issue.
2 CONT * '''Elizabeth Erskine''', married Sir Alexander Seton of Touch; had issue.
2 CONT * '''Mariota Erskine''', married William, 2nd Earl Marischal.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Reference
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun05paul/page/592/mode/2up James Balfour Paul, ed., ''The Scots Peerage'', vol. 5 (Edinburgh: D. Douglas, 1904), p605-7]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p2588.htm#i25871
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Erskine,_2nd_Lord_Erskine
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Erskine, 2nd Lord Erskine (d. 1494), de jure 14th Earl of Mar, was a Scottish peer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Erskine was the son of Robert Erskine, 1st Lord Erskine, and Elizabeth, daughter of David Lindsay, 1st Earl of Crawford. He married Janet Douglas. They had several children. One daughter, Helen, married Humphrey Colquhoun of Luss,
2 CONC  son of Sir John Colquhoun of Luss. Another daughter, Margaret, married William Keith, 1st Earl Marischal.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Erskine died in 1494 and was succeeded by his son, Alexander.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.erskinclan.com/gene4.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Erskine, the King's bodyguard, foiled the Gowrie Conspiracy and was enobled as Lord Erskine by James II in 1460.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.instirling.com/sight/tower.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I2484&tree=CC
2 CONT
2 CONT The identity of Thomas Erskine's wife is not certain. She may not have been a daughter of the 1st Earl of Morton although that is often stated, sometimes as Elizabeth but perhaps more often as Janet. Cunningham gedcom source says 
2 CONC this Janet died without male issue which would seem to promote Elizabeths claim to the position of wife of Thomas Erskine and mother of his heir and probably other children.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.genealogy.com/forum/surnames/topics/erskine/378/
2 CONT
2 CONT [These are the dates for Thomas, father of the 1st Lord !]
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: Sir Robert Erskine 1st Lord Erskine and Elizabeth Lindsay.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st wife: Janet Douglas, heiress of Dalkeith, who died at the birth of her first child[This Janet Douglas was the 1st wife ofThomas, father of the 1st Lord]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Janet Douglas before 1445 in Scotland. Douglas' Peerage Vol. 5, pg. 105 - "He married, before 1445, Janet Douglas States in Douglas' Peerage she is the daughter of James, Earl of Morton, granddaughter of King Jam
2 CONC es I.
2 CONT
2 CONT [This is impossible, Janet dau of James, Earl of Morton by Joan, dau of King James I cannot have been born before 1459 – the date of her parent’s marriage.She married Bef. 1 Feb 1480/1 to Patrick Hepburn, 1st Earl of Bothwell, and
2 CONC  died Bef. 1491.IfJanet Douglas, wife of Thomas, 2nd Lord Erskine was of the Morton family then she came from a previous generation, nothing can be stated with any certainty.She was his wife in Aug 1489]
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd wife: Janet Keith or Barclay, widow of Sir David Barclay; It was Janet's heritage that connected the Erskine family with the Earldom of Mar.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Janet Keith was the 2nd wife ofThomas, father of the 1st Lord – vide supra]
2 CONT
2 CONT children:[These are misplaced - children of Sir Thomas, father of the 1st Lord] i. - Sir Robert Erskine ii. - John Erskine - ancestor of the Erskines of Dun and of Pittodrie iii. - Elizabeth Erskine - married to Duncan Wemyss of R
2 CONC ires iv. - Margaret Erskine - married to Lord James Rutherfurd[Two of her ?!?]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children were: Alexander Erskine 3rd Lord Erskine Helen Erskine Isobel Erskine Elizabeth Erskine Mariota Erskine Margaret Erskine - married Lord James Rutherfurd II[Not shown in SP V, p. 606-7.If their first child – Peter was b Ab
2 CONC t. 1450 then she CANNOT have been born after 1435.Her supposed parents were married Bef .1445 – vide supra.Thomas d in or Abt. 1493, his parents m Aft. 1400, he was no doubt of age in 1441 – therefore his birth had to occur betwee
2 CONC n 1401-20, probably much towards the end of that range given his death date]
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://books.google.com/books?id=2ss6AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA75&lpg=PA75&dq=thomas+erskine+married+elizabeth+douglas&source=bl&ots=fTTYYpbPpY&sig=y10ksRk3JJdeT3HuYk7H3nnSzjc&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwj_itX5uP7eAhWmj4MKHRIbBpk4ChDoATADegQICBAB#
2 CONC v=onepage&q=thomas%20erskine%20married%20elizabeth%20douglas&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas, second Lord Erskine, who married Lady Elizabeth Douglas, daughter of James, Earl Of Morton, and dying before 1494, had Alexander, third Lord Erskine, guardian to K. James the IVth.
