1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_Douglas
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Catherine Douglas'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine Douglas, later Catherine "Kate" Barlass, was a historical figure who tried to prevent the assassination of King James I of Scotland on February 20, 1437. She was a lady-in-waiting to his queen, Joan Beaufort.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a member of the powerful Clan Douglas. Her '''mother was a daughter of David Lindsay, 1st Earl of Crawford and his wife Elizabeth Stewart daughter of Robert II''', making her a first cousin once removed of the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legend has it that during the King's stay at a Dominican chapterhouse in Perth, a group of men led by Sir Robert Graham came to the door searching for the King in order to kill him. The King's Chamberlain, Robert Stewart, Master o
2 CONC f Atholl, aware of the plot against his life, had taken the precaution of removing the bolt from the door of the room in which James and his queen were staying.
2 CONT
2 CONT James fled into a sewer tunnel as the queen and her ladies quickly replaced the floorboards to hide his location.[1] Catherine sprang to the door and placed her arm through the staples to bar the assassins' entrance.[2] However, t
2 CONC hey forced the door open anyway, breaking Catherine's arm, and discovered and killed the King. From that point on, according to the story, Catherine took the surname of "Barlass". Dante Gabriel Rossetti recounted the story of Cath
2 CONC erine Douglas in verse in 1881, under the title "The King's Tragedy". This poem contains the line "Catherine, keep the door!" - possibly the origin of the idiomatic phrase "Katy, bar the door!" (a warning of the approach of troubl
2 CONC e).
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Marshall. p. 52.
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ John Stewart Of Ardvorlich: The Grahams, W. & A. K. Johnston & G. W. Bacon, Ltd (1958), Page 9. [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT * Marshall, Rosalind (2003) Scottish Queens, 1034-1714. Tuckwell Press.
2 CONT * Rossetti, Dante Gabriel, The King's Tragedy: James I of Scots--20 February, 1437 (The "Catherine, keep the door" action begins at line 546.).
