1 NAME Cecilia /de Dunbar/
2 GIVN Cecilia
2 SURN de Dunbar
2 _MARNM de Dunbar
1 SEX F
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Lady Cecelia DE DUNBAR-MARCH married James (Seumas) STEWART 5th High Steward of the Scots, son of Alexander LE STEWARD 4th High Steward of Scotland and his wife before 1293 in Scotland 1970.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of Patrick III, 7th Earl of Dunbar[1] (c. 1213 – 24 August 1289)  and (perhaps) his wife Cecile Fraser; Patrick had no issue by his 1st wife, Cecily fitz Robert.
2 CONT
2 CONT (James (Seumas) STEWART 5th High Steward of the Scots was born about 1260 in Dundonald Castle, Irvine, Ayrshire, Scotland 1970 and died on 16 Jul 1309 in Dundonald Castle, Irvine, Ayrshire, Scotland 1970.)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''This marriage is uncertain.  No issue of this marriage.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===comments===
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patrick_III,_Earl_of_Dunbar
2 CONT
2 CONT Although some sources show Christiana de Brus as Patrick's wife and the mother of his children, other sources are in disagreement with this. According to The Scots Peerage, 'Cecilia filia Johannis' was his only recorded wife and m
2 CONC other of his sons, based on a charter by her eldest son.[15] The Complete Peerage shows 'Cecil, da. of John' as his wife and states that Christian Bruce was erroneously assigned as his wife in Wood's Douglas.[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===supporting data===
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://books.google.com/books?id=8JcbV309c5UC&lpg=PA356&ots=kvoLIVOOb2&dq=sir%20james%20stewart%20of%20preston%20giles%20stewart&pg=PA356#v=onepage&q&f=false page 356] of Magna Carta Ancestry: A Study in Colonial and Medieval 
2 CONC Families, 2nd Edition, 2011 by Douglas Richardson.  "Giles de Burgh, married (as his 3rd wife) James [Stewart] (or James fitz Alexander), Knt., 5th Stewart of Scotland, son and heir of Alexander Stewart, Knt., 4th Stewart of Scotl
2 CONC and, of Dundonlad.  They had four sons, Andrew, Walter, Knt. [6th Steward of Scotland], John, Knt., and James, Knt., [of Durrisdeer], and one daughter, '''Giles''' (wife of Alexander de Meyners or Menzies, Knt.). ...."
2 CONT * [http://fmg.ac/library/3976-the-wives-of-sir-james-the-steward-d-1309 "The Wives of Sir James the Steward (d.1309)"] Author: MacEwen, Andrew B W Publisher: Foundation for Medieval Genealogy Year published: 2011 Status: See notes
2 CONC  Description / Notes: "This paper rehabilitates the traditional year of Sir James the Steward’s birth, names his first two wives, explains the late date of his third marriage to Giles de Burgh, and provides information about their
2 CONC  four known children. It posits a double marriage alliance between the Stewarts and Comyns about 1240, clarifies the date of birth of Robert II, and establishes the circumstances of his mother’s death in 1317. It incidentally brin
2 CONC gs forward some overlooked information on the Muschamp family and shows when Sir William, earl of Mar, actually died. Lastly it draws together what little is known concerning Sir Nicholas Campbell (d.1305), whose representation pa
2 CONC ssed to the Lochawe branch."
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patrick_III,_Earl_of_Dunbar
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Stewart,_5th_High_Steward_of_Scotland
2 CONT * Burke, Messrs., John and John Bernard, The Royal Families of England Scotland and Wales, with Their Descendants etc., London, 1851, volume 2, page xlvi.
2 CONT * [http://www.archive.org/stream/calendarofdocume02grea#page/222/mode/2up Cal.Doc Scot. vol ii, no 847. ] It appears more likely that the Earl of Ulster's daughter was his spouse given the name of his daughter
