1 OCCU Co-Regent of Scotland, 4th High Steward of Scotland, 4th High Stewart
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Alexander Stewart''', 4th High Steward of Scotland Alexander Stewart (died 1283), also known as ''Alexander of Dundonald'', was 4th hereditary High Steward of Scotland from his father's death in 1246.   He died 
2 CONC in 1282, and was succeeded by his son James.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Coat of arms of the High Stewart of Scotland: Or, a fess chequy argent and azure 
2 CONT *Tenure 1246–1283 
2 CONT *Predecessor Walter Stewart, 3rd High Steward of Scotland 
2 CONT *Successor James Stewart, 5th High Steward of Scotland 
2 CONT *Died 1283 
2 CONT *Nationality Scottish 
2 CONT *Parents Walter Stewart, 3rd High Steward of Scotland and Bethóc (Beatrix) Mac Gille Críst
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a son of Walter Stewart, 3rd High Steward of Scotland by his wife Bethóc, daughter of Gille Críst, Earl of Angus.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Career 
2 CONT
2 CONT He is said to have accompanied King Louis IX of France on the Seventh Crusade (1248–1254).[1] In 1255 he was one of the councillors of King Alexander III of Scotland, though under age.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the principal commander under King Alexander III at the Battle of Largs, on 2 October 1263, when the Scots defeated the Norwegians under Haakon IV. The Scots invaded and conquered the Isle of Man the following year, which w
2 CONC as then, together with the whole of the Western Isles, annexed to the Crown of Scotland.[3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT ''The identity of Alexander's wife is unknown. Traditionally she was Jean of Bute, the heir of Bute and Arran, but Lord Lyon David Sellars has shown her parents are likely a later myth to justify the Stewart takeover.''
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean married Alexander Stewart 4th High Steward of Scotland, son of Walter Stewart 3rd High Steward of Scotland and Beatrix of Angus. No date for this marriage is given in Burke or by Thompsett, so have to assume that it occurred 
2 CONC before the birth of first child. He was  b. circa 1214, d. circa 1283.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children are variously given.
2 CONT
2 CONT Medlands as of June 2022 shows these six:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTLAND.htm#JamesStewartPearstondied1333A
2 CONT
2 CONT # James, died young 
2 CONT # James Stewart 5th High Steward of Scotland+ b. 1243, d. 16 Jul 1309.  Married 1) Cecilia de Dunbar 2) Muriel of Strathearn 3) Egida de Burgh.
2 CONT #Elizabeth Stewart+ b. c 1250 4. Married Sir William "Le Hardi" Douglas of that Ilk.
2 CONT # Andrew Stewart (d after 1350) Married Beith.  (The chronology of this entry is extremely shaky and it is unlikely that Andrew was the son of Alexander.)
2 CONT #  Sir John Stewart of Jedworth + b. c 1240, d. 22 Jul 1298 2.  Married Margaret de Bonkyl.
2 CONT # daughter .  Her parentage and marriage are confirmed by a charter dated [Oct] [1299] under which "Robert de Feltone" reported to Edward I King of England damage caused by raids on castle Lochmaben whose constable was "Robert de 
2 CONC Conigham…vallet of the Steward of Scotland, whose sister his uncle had married"[1285].  m --- Cunningham, son of ---. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia currently (31 March 2025) shows:
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife is said to have been named Jean,[2] and they had four documented children:
2 CONT
2 CONT #     James Stewart, 5th High Steward of Scotland[1][2]
2 CONT #     Sir John Stewart of Bonkyll[2]
2 CONT #     Elizabeth Stewart, who married Sir William le Hardi, Lord of Douglas.[2]
2 CONT #     Hawise Stewart, who married John de Soules[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Previously Geni showed also shows:
2 CONT * 1.	Hawise Stewart 2. Married John de Soulis.
2 CONT *  2.	Beatrix Stewart Countess of Crawford+ 2,6. also found as "daughter Stewart.". Married Sir Alexander Lindsay of Crawford and Luffness.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Through their eldest son James they were great-grandparents of King Robert II, the first Stewart to be King of Scots, and thus ancestors of all subsequent Scottish monarchs and the later and current monarchs of Great Britain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through their second son John, they were the direct ancestors in the male line of Henry Stuart, Lord Darnley, and of the Stuart monarchs of Scotland and England from Darnley's son James VI and I onwards.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through their third son Andrew they were the 9x great grandparents of Oliver Cromwell, the Lord Protector.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT *1 - Principal commander under King Alexander III at the Battle of Largs 2 Oct 1263 when the Scottish army defeated the Norwegians. In 1264 he invaded the Isle of Man.
2 CONT *2 -He commanded the right wing of the Scots army at the battle of Largs, 1263. He had two sons In the thirteenth century, the 4th Stewart of Scotland (a crusader) married the heiress of the Lord of Bute of the royal House of Isle
2 CONC s
2 CONT * 3 - Battle of the Largs  1 October 1283: Invasion of Scotland by Haakon, King of Norway, attacked on the beaches by gathering Scottish forces, beginning the Battle of the Largs.
2 CONT * 5 October 1283: Norwegians abandon invasion of Scotland, leaving ships and wounded on the beach. Despite the victory of Brian Boru over the Danes in Ireland in 1014, the Scandinavian incursions into the Celtic nations took a lon
2 CONC g time to fade away. It would be nearly 270 years after the Battle of Clontarf before the ScandinaviansÙu last hurrah in the spectacular Battle of the Largs.
2 CONT * In the year 1283 the English were just consolidating their conquest of Wales with the execution on 3 October of Dafydd, the last native Prince of Wales. The power of Norway still dominated the North Sea and reached around the co
2 CONC ast of Scotland into the Irish Sea, hedging the growing power of Scotland with a chain of island possessions that included the Orkneys, Shetland, the Hebrides, and the Isle of Mann.
2 CONT * When Scotland's boy king Alexander III turned 21 in 1262, one of his first acts was to try to purchase the Hebrides from Norway. The offer was refused, but when the Earl of Ross led a bloody raid on the Norwegian-held Isle of Sk
2 CONC ye, the Norwegian King Haakon prepared for an armed showdown with Scotland.
2 CONT * Haakon assembled a fleet of 100 ships, the largest armada yet seen in those waters, and was joined by Magnus, the King of Mann, along with other Scandinavian jarls. But after wasting the summer in fruitless sparring and maneuver
2 CONC ing, Haakon divided his forces, sending most of the Manx fleet off on coastal raids and dispatching 40 other ships to be dragged overland and floated in Loch Lomond, a novel if pointless tour de force.
2 CONT *King Alexander in the meantime was biding his time, keeping his field armies intact behind a defensive screen of castles. The opportunity he was waiting for came at last on 1 October, when the first storms of autumn forced Haakon
2 CONC  to decide between abandoning the campaign or chancing a risky landing on the Scottish coast. Haakon chose to go for the landing. The Norwegians struggled through the storm-roiled surf on the west coast of Scotland only to be met 
2 CONC on the beaches by a Scottish vanguard of archers and mailed knights, who commenced a running battle with the Norwegians on 2 October.
2 CONT *The bedraggled Norwegians were in no shape to deal with a hot landing zone, but found themselves unable to put back out to sea due to the worsening weather. They were equally unable to gain a secure beachhead for themselves in th
2 CONC e face of the growing numbers of Scots that Alexander dispatched from their inland bases as soon as he learned of the Norwegian predicament. After some 72 hours of debilitating and almost continuous combat, the weather lifted just
2 CONC  enough to enable the remaining Norwegians to make a hasty evacuation, leaving most of their dead and wounded on beaches lit by the burning hulks of their ships.
2 CONT *The Battle of the Largs marked the rise of independent Scotland and the terminal decline of Norway's North Sea hegemony. The victory was followed by the death of Haakon, Norway’s cessation of the Hebrides to Scotland, and the Sco
2 CONC ttish takeover of the Orkneys and the Kingdom of Mann and the Isles. Scotland eventually gained Shetland too, as a wedding present, but that is a story for another day.
2 CONT
