1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sir  SIMON of Glendoning. Etc.   He made a great figure in Scotland, and was highly efteemed by king James II. from whom he had the honour of knighthood.    He commanded  a brave and warlike race of men well respec
2 CONC ted on the borders.   Vefted with the moft extended jufticiary powers within his own lands, particularly in Glendoning, and with privilege of regality in the adjacent country, of which he was hereditary bailie, and his family havi
2 CONC ng made some most noble alliances by marriages  ; he maintained a character of authority, which made him on many honourable occafions stand in the rank of the most potent barons of the kingdom. He was one of the guarantees of a tr
2 CONC eaty of peace with King Henry VI. of England, together with the Earls of Douglas, Angus, Ross, Murray, Crawford Etc.  anno 1449 ; and is amongst the several great lords who were guarantees of peace in the years 1451-53-57-59, etc.
2 CONC  He got a charter under the great seal, of  a twenty pound land in the barony of AuldRoxburgh, anno 1450. Upon his own resignation, he obtained another from King James II.  Simoni de Glendoning de eodem, militi, totas terras baron
2 CONC ie de Partone in fenefcallatu de Kirdcudbright, cum omnibus curiis, furia, foffa; etc. una cum advocatione eccleftæ ejufdem, etc. dated 23d February 1458. He was again guarantee of a treaty with the Englifh, together with the earl
2 CONC s of Athole, Crawford, Huntly, Argyle, Etc. Etc. anno 1464, where he is designed Sir Simon Glendoning of Parton, by which title this family have been often promiscuously designed with that of Glendoning. He married lady Elifabeth 
2 CONC Lindsay, daughter of Alexander earl of Crawford (omitted in the Peerage, page 157) by whom he had four sons. 1. John, his heir. 2.  Alexander, who probably was progenitor of the Glendonings of Drumrask. 3. Archibald. 4.  Matthew, 
2 CONC who got a charter under the great feal, of lands of Quhitfdale, Glenkirk, etc. dated 18th March 1489.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p23119.htm#i231185
