1 OCCU Prevost of St. Giles
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====BOOK PASSAGE
2 CONT * Clerk, G. and Welwood, A.M. (1859) ''Registrum Cartarum Ecclesie Sancti Egidii de Edinburgh: A Series of Charters and Original Documents Connected with the Church of St. Giles, Edinburgh. M.CCC.XLIV.-M.D.LXVII''. Edited by D. La
2 CONC ing. Edinburgh, Scotland: Bannatyne Club. [https://archive.org/details/registrumcartaru00bann/page/n61/mode/2up?view=theater&q=Provost Archive.org]''':'''
2 CONT ** “Mr. William Forbes, Perpetual Vicar, was advanced to the Provostship of the newly erected Collegiate Church. He was educated at St. Andrews, his name occurring among the Determinants in 1448, and of the Licentiates In 1451 in 
2 CONC that University. In the year 1464, he was elected Rector.^ After his appointment as Provost of St. Giles, Forbes obtained a charter under the Great Seal, dated 2d of October 1478,[See Appendix, No. I.] confirming to him for life a
2 CONC n annual pension or stipend of 220 marks, which had been granted by the Provost, Bailies, and Community of Edinburgh ; and in an old inventory of the town's charters, &c., there is entered, — "Item, ane discharge granted be Mr. Wi
2 CONC lliam Forbess, Provost of the Colledge Kirk of St. Geill, wherby he dischairges Thomas Yair, Thesaurer of Edinburgh, and Johne Quhythed, fermorer of the Kirk of Dunbarnie, the soume of ane hundreth and ten merkis for the Witsunday
2 CONC  termes maill of the fermes of Dunbarnie." This discharge is dated the penult of September [1469].”[Guthrie's MS. Inventory, 1638.]
2 CONT ** “But we find that two years later he was obliged to institute a suit in Parliament against the Magistrates for recovery of his stipend, amounting to [(220 marks)]. The Lords Auditors, on seeing the obligation of the Magistrates
2 CONC , with the King's confirmation, ordained them to pay the Provost's stipend on pain of imprisonment in Blackness Castle.^ The name of Forbes, as Provost of St. Giles, appears so late as the year 1496, in the grant of part of his gl
2 CONC ebe for the enlargement of the cemetery; and he may have survived till the commencement of the sixteenth century, as there appears no intermediate person as Provost between himself and his successor, who afterwards became Bishop o
2 CONC f Dunkeld, and holds a distinguished place in early Scottish literature.”
