1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===JAMES DOUGLAS, EARL OF MORTON===
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Death===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''A date of death cannot be identified for James Douglas, Earl of Morton. He was alive on 22 June 1493, [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/page/355 The Scots Peerage VI: 355] but he probably died before 10 Se
2 CONC ptember 1493, when his son James is noticed as the brother german of John, Earl of Morton. [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD150%2f237&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=john+earl+of+morton&ko
2 CONC =p&r=&ro=s&df=1450&dt=1513&di=n NRS: GD150/237]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Evidence from the National Records of Scotland===
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''10 June 1493: Crown precept directed to Mr. George Crechtoun, James Giffert, Mr. William Giffert and David Broun, for the infeftment of John Douglas, son and heir apparent of James, earl of Mortone, in the barony of Dalkeith, t
2 CONC he baronies of Caldorclere, Garmyltoun, Donnyng and Quhittinghame, the lands of Dechment and Holdene in the barony of Wester Caldar, the baronies of Kilbothok, Newlandis and Lyntoun, the said James's part of the barony of Kirkurde
2 CONC , Lochurde, Eschellis, Qwyht and Fithane, £6,13s.4d. sterling of annualrent from the lands of Horsbruke and the same from the lands of Ester Hopkelzath, lands of Eschellis, £4 sterling of annualrent from the lands of Newby in the 
2 CONC Sheriffdom of Peblis, barony of Robertoun, barony of Abirdoure, lands of Wodfield, Tyrie and Seyfield, two Balbartanys, barony of Mortoun, barony of Mordingtoun, lands of Ganachy and Pettulane, fishing of Water of Tay, barony of P
2 CONC restoun, lands of Borg, tenement of Hutoun, and lands of Moffetdale, resigned by said earl. [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD150%2f234&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=&ko=p&r=GD150%2f234&r
2 CONC o=s&df=1450&dt=1513&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Earls of Morton, reference GD150/234]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Comment: James, Earl of Morton, was evidently still alive on or shortly before 10 June 1493.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''10 September 1493: Instrument of sasine following on GD150/234 Done at the outer door of the castle of Dalkeith. Witnesses: James Douglas, brother german of John, earl of Morton, John Inglis of Langlanhill, Andrew Broune of Thr
2 CONC ipland, David Broune and Andrew Broune, brothers german, masters and sirs William Gifferd and William Colden, prebendaries of Spittalhaucht and Lochurd, John Wode, vicar of Dalkeith, John Chalmer, Walter Symsone, Alexander Cole an
2 CONC d David Fethane, priests, Alexander Glendonwyn and Patrick Greif. Notary - William Henryson, priest, prebendary of Horsbruce in the collegiate church of Dalkeith. [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx
2 CONC ?reference=GD150%2f237&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=john+earl+of+morton&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1450&dt=1513&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Earls of Morton, reference GD150/237]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Primary Sources
2 CONT 1463/4. (7 Jan.) St. Peter's, Rome. (f. 175.) <br>
2 CONT To the bishop of St. Andrews. Mandate to absolve '''James Douglas, lord of Dalketh and Morton, and Janet (Joneta) Stewart''', of his diocese, from sentence of excommunication incurred by contracting marriage per verba legitime de 
2 CONC presenti, by the persuasion of the late James king of Scots, they not being ignorant that they were related in the second and third degrees of kindred, and by having offspring, enjoining penance, and thereafter, and after temporar
2 CONC y separation, to dispense them to contract marriage anew and remain therein, decreeing legitimate the offspring already born, and that to be born of the said marriage. Oblate nobis. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Lateran Regesta 594: 1463', in Calendar of Papal Registers Relating To Great Britain and Ireland: Volume 12, 1458-1471, ed. J A Twemlow (London, 1933), pp. 204-206. British History Online http://www.british-history.ac.uk/cal-papa
2 CONC l-registers/brit-ie/vol12/pp204-206 [accessed 22 September 2017].
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''James Douglas''', (1426 - 22 October 1493), the 4th Lord of Dalkeith, was created the 1st Earl of Morton in 1458.
2 CONT
2 CONT from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Douglas,_1st_Earl_of_Morton Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of James Douglas, 2nd Lord of Dalkeith and Elizabeth Gifford, daughter of James Gifford of Sheriffhall.[2] His father resigned all his estates to James in 1456 when James became the 4th Lord of Dalkeith.[3] James wa
2 CONC s created Earl of Morton in 1458 upon his marriage to Joan Stewart (1428-1493), the daughter of James I, King of Scots. She was a deaf-mute.
2 CONT
2 CONT They were ancestors of the later Earls of Morton, extinct in the male line in 1548. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===family===
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his wife Joan were the parents of:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sir John Douglas, 2nd Earl of Morton.
2 CONT * James, appeared in several writs 1466-1480.
2 CONT * Janet, married bef. 1 February 1490-1 to Sir Patrick Hepburn, 1st Earl of Bothwell.
2 CONT * Elizabeth, she was mentioned in a charter of 1479.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===comments===
2 CONT
2 CONT According to [https://archive.org/stream/historyofhouseof01maxw#page/n308/mode/1up page 308] of 
2 CONT "The House of Douglas":
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Morton died about the year 1504, leaving two sons -- (1) John; who succeeded to the Earldom; (2) James; and one daughter, Janet, who married Patrick, Earl of Bothwell."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Douglas,_1st_Earl_of_Morton
2 CONT * http://www.douglashistory.co.uk/history/james1stearlofmorton.htm
2 CONT * [https://archive.org/stream/historyofhouseof01maxw#page/n309/mode/2up Page 240] of "A history of the house of Douglas from the earliest times down to the legislative union of England and Scotland" by Maxwell, Herbert Eustace, Si
2 CONC r, bart., 1845-1937
2 CONT * [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun06paul#page/356/mode/2up The Scots Peerage,] Founded on Wood's Edition of Sir Robert Douglas's Peerage of Scotland, Vol. VI, ed. James Balfour Paul (Edinburgh: David Douglas, 1909), p. 
2 CONC 356
2 CONT * [http://www.cdss.ca.gov/cdssweb/entres/pdf/ODA/DeafWithoutLimitsDeafandHOHPioneers.ppsx DEAF WITHOUT LIMITS - DEAF AND HARD OF HEARING PIONEERS] CDSS Office of Deaf Access
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Douglas,_2nd_Earl_of_Morton
