1 NOTE {geni:about_me} George Gordon (Seton), 2nd Earl of Huntly is my 16th great grandfather.
2 CONT  Thanks for accepting my request to be added as a manager of this profile.
2 CONT Janet Milburn 12/25/22
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT George Gordon, 2nd Earl of Huntly (died after 8 June 1501) was a Scottish nobleman and Chancellor of Scotland from 1498–1501.
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Gordon,_George_(d.1502%3F)_(DNB00)_ Dictionary of National Biography] 
2 CONT
2 CONT GORDON, GEORGE, second Earl of Huntly (d. 1502?), lord high chancellor of Scotland, was the eldest son of Alexander de Seton, lord of Gordon, and first earl of Huntly, by his third wife, Elizabeth, eldest daughter of William, lord
2 CONC  Crichton, lord high chancellor of Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The father, after receiving a grant of Strathbogie and other lands, and being in 1449 created Earl of Huntly, defeated the Earl of Crawford at Brechin, 18 May 1452. By his second marriage he had a son, Sir Alexander, ancestor of t
2 CONC he Setons of Touch, but the succession to the earldom of Huntly was settled by charter on the issue of the third marriage, who took the surname of Gordon. George, the eldest son by this marriage, succeeded to the earldom and the b
2 CONC ulk of the estates on the death of his father, 13 July 1470.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 13 May 1491 he was appointed king's lieutenant north of the Esk, until the king should reach the age of twenty-five. In connection with a scheme for bringing the highland regions more directly under legal control, Huntly was ap
2 CONC pointed in 1492 with other commissioners to drive out ‘broken men’ from forfeited estates, and let them for five yeare to ‘true men.’ On 4 March 1498 he was appointed lord high chancellor (Reg. Mag. Sig. i. 2389). He was supersede
2 CONC d in this office in 1501 by George, duke of Orkney. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Apparently on this account he is represented by the historian of the house of Gordon, who states that he was buried in the chancel of the abbey church of Cambuskenneth, as dying on 8 June 1501, but he was alive on 11 July 1502 (ib
2 CONC .2656), and died some time between that date and 30 Jan. 1502-3 (ib. 2689). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Marriage & Children==
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=46660107 Find A Grave Memorial# 46660107]
2 CONT
2 CONT First married Elizabeth Dunbar, younger daughter and co-heiress to James Dunbar, Earl of Moray and widow of Archibald Douglas, Earl of Moray. They married by contract dated 20 May 1455 and had no issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Secondly, husband of Annabel of Scotland, daughter of King James I and Joan de Beaufort. They were married before 10 Mar 1460, the date of the grant. They were divorced before 12 May 1466. with the final divorce announcements occu
2 CONC rring in 1471. They had one daughter:
2 CONT # Isabel, wife of William Hay, Earl of Erroll
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir George married a third time to Elizabeth Hay, daughter of Sir William Hay, Earl of Erroll and Constable of Scotland. They were married after 12 May 1466 by dispensation dated 25 June 1466,as they were related by 3rd and 3rd de
2 CONC grees, and four sons and six daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Sir Alexander, Earl of Huntly
2 CONT # Adam, Earl of Sutherland
2 CONT # William
2 CONT # James
2 CONT # Janet, wife of Alexander Lindsay, Master of Crawfurd
2 CONT # Elizabeth, wife of William, Earl Marischal
2 CONT # Margaret, wife of Patrick Hepburn, Earl of Bothwell
2 CONT # Catherine
2 CONT # Eleanor, wife of William Sinclair and David Hepburn
2 CONT # Agnes, wife of Sir Gilbert Hay
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir George had two illegitimate children:
2 CONT # Alexander
2 CONT # Agnes, wife of James Ogilvy.
2 CONT
