1 NOTE 2ND EARL OF HUNTLY
1 NOTE Chancellor of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Huntly 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aberdeenshire 
2 CONT
2 CONT A livestock market town in Strathbogie, NW Aberdeenshire, 
2 CONT situated at the confluence of the River Bogie and the River
2 CONT  Deveron on the main road and rail route to Inverness. 
2 CONT Occupying a strategic position on a low-lying plain 
2 CONT surrounded by hills, it lies at the junction of ancient 
2 CONT routes linking Moray with Strathdon and Aberdeen. The town 
2 CONT developed around a defensive site that became the power 
2 CONT centre of the Catholic clan Gordon and was given burgh 
2 CONT status in 1545. From 1776 Huntly was extended by the 
2 CONT Duke of Gordon who laid it out in a regular grid pattern.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are tourist and sporting facilities including an 
2 CONT 18-hole golf course, a Community Centre, a library and 
2 CONT museum, a livestock market and walkways out of town to 
2 CONT Ba'hill, Battlehill and Clashmach Hill. St Margaret's 
2 CONT Catholic Church (1834) has fine Spanish decorative panels 
2 CONT and on the south bank of the River Deveron stand the remains 
2 CONT of Huntly Castle with its 12th-century Norman motte and
2 CONT bailey, medieval L-plan tower house and defensive earthworks
2 CONT  dating from the Civil War. Destroyed by the Earl of Moray 
2 CONT in 1452, the castle was rebuilt in a new 'palace' style 
2 CONT that was not completed until 1602 when the George Gordon, 
2 CONT the 1st Marquis of Huntly (c.1563 - 1636) returned from 
2 CONT exile after supporting the Catholic rebellion against 
2 CONT James VI in 1594. It was subsequently abandoned in the 
2 CONT 17th century when the Duke of Gordon moved to Fochabers.
