1 NOTE {geni:about_me} There is no documentary evidence available currently to affirm that Elizabeth, daughter of https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-Alexander-Seton-of-Winton-Lord-Gordon/6000000001013134949?through=6000000003615927699 & htt
2 CONC ps://www.geni.com/people/Lady-Elizabeth-Gordon/6000000003615927692?through=6000000003615927699 & sister of https://www.geni.com/people/Alexander-Gordon-1st-Earl-of-Huntly/6000000001644776831?through=6000000003615927699, is the sam
2 CONC e person as the wife of https://www.geni.com/people/Alexander-MacDonald-9th-Lord-of-the-Isles-12th-Earl-of-Ross/6000000185694300967?through=6000000003615927699 (this profile).  Do not merge with https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabe
2 CONC th-Seton/6000000185753832867?through=6000000003615927699.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Elizabeth''' is seen in peerage reports as Elizabeth Seton. She was dead by 2 November 1467, when Alexander's son John granted a charter confirming a gift to the monastery of Fearn "for the salvation of the souls of his parents
2 CONC , Alexander earl of Ross and '''Elizabeth''' his wife." (See Munros, at 143.) <[https://digital.nls.uk/scottish-history-society-publications/browse/archive/126627435#?c=0&m=0&s=0&cv=235&xywh=-320%2C-1%2C2343%2C2825 link]>
2 CONT
2 CONT Apparently she was the first “legal” wife of Alexander MacDonald (d 1449), 9th Lord of the Isles, 12th Earl of Ross, son of Donald MacDonald, 8th Lord of the Isles and Mary (Marion) de Leslie, and  married by 1431, when his mother
2 CONC  appears as the elder Lady of the Isles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander’s sons Celestine Macdonald of Lochalsh and Hugh of Sleat were older than his heir John of the Isles, and had different mothers, but they are not considered to have been born illegitimately.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children	
2 CONT
2 CONT # John MacDonald, 13th Earl of Ross, Last Lord of the Isles+3 b. c 1435, d. between 10 Jan 1502/3 and 5 Feb 1503 as a pensioner, Lodging House In Dundee, Scotland.  Married Elizabeth Livingston.
2 CONT
2 CONT # (perhaps called) Margaret MacDonald [b c 1442?] married John Sutherland, 8th Earl Sutherland, son of John Sutherland, 7th Earl Sutherland and Margaret Baillie.  died circa 1490 at Unes, Scotland; Murdered. (Seen as 1st daughter;
2 CONC  mother uncertain, marriage uncertain) 
2 CONT # Flora or Florence MacDonald+6 [b. c 1449?] married Duncan MacIntosh, 11th Laird of MacIntosh, 12th Chief of Clan Chattan (Seen as 2nd daughter; mother uncertain, marriage uncertain)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/ss4tz/stewart23.php  mentions that https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabeth-Gordon/6000000003615405093?through=6000000007980977365, wife of https://www.geni.com/people/Neil-Stewart-2nd-of-Fo
2 CONC thergill-and-Garth/6000000003615380394?through=6000000003615405093  might possibly be a sister of the [https://www.geni.com/people/Alexander-Gordon-1st-Earl-of-Huntley/6000000001644776831?through=6000000003615927699 Earl of Huntly
2 CONC ].  So some studies suggest the widowed https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabeth-Seton/6000000003615927699  married Niel Stewart as her 2nd husband.  The sources do not seem to support this, however.
2 CONT ===Identity
2 CONT
2 CONT Highland papers by MacPhail, J. R. N. (James Robert Nicolson), 1858-1933 (1914).  Page 92. <[https://archive.org/details/highlandpapers01macp/page/93/mode/1up?q=Maclaide&view=theater Archive.Org]>
2 CONT
2 CONT Vll.  Papal Mandate to admonish Alexander, Earl of Ross and Lord of the Isles, and Christina, his Concubine. 24th March 1445-6. (Archivio Vaticaiio. 1-145-0. An. xvi. Eug. iv. Vol. 426, fol. 297 v.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander, Earl of Ross and Lord of the Isles, is generally said to have married Elizabeth, daughter of Sir Alexander Seton and Elizabeth Gordon, and thus sister of the first Earl of Huntly. This statement made by Crawford (''Peer
2 CONC age'', p. 232) on the authority of Ferrerius is not supported by the terms of any known charter or other contemporary writ, but there seems- no reason for doubting its accuracy, unless indeed from the fact that in a charter dated 
2 CONC 5th October 1442 by Alexander to Huntly (then Sir Alex.inder Seton), of the barony of Kynedward, there is no hint of any relationship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Very little is known about this lady. Hugh Macdonald (cf. ''ante'', p. 35) plainly confuses her with her son's wife ; but the anonymous author of the marginal note on p. 35, besides accepting her identity, asserts that he has seen
2 CONC  her name in a charter, and that that charter proves her to have been the mother of Hugh of Sleat as well as of his brother John.
2 CONT
2 CONT The document now printed shows that her Christian name was '''Elizabeth''', so far corroborating the tradition, and ''it also shows that in their later years she and her husband had become seriously estranged.'' According to Hugh 
2 CONC Macdonald there were three sons of the marriage, and there were certainly daughters also, facts which entirely coincide with the assertion that the spouses had lived together until Alexander, ''damnabiliter'' ''illectus'', by the 
2 CONC wiles of Christina Maclaide [Macleod] had practically repudiated his wife and installed Christina in her place. Information would be welcome on many points, e.g. the identity of Christina — whether she was the mother of Hugh, Dona
2 CONC ld and Alexander, whose legitimation has just been discussed, and what was the upshot of the whole matter. If the petition on which the mandate proceeds is still extant, it and perchance other documents preserved in the Vatican ar
2 CONC chives may throw more lighten the history of Alexander and his family.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is interesting to note the wholesome respect entertained for the power of Alexander, and the apprehension that any attempt to inflict personal admonition on him or his paramour might have grave consequences for the adventurous 
2 CONC ecclesiastic.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://ancestorium.com/tng/getperson.php?personID=I037488&tree=1
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Clan Donald Genealogy Database at http://www.clandonald.org.uk/Genealogy/d0003/g0000070.html#I0172 (dead link) shows mother of most of his children to be Elizabeth SETON. Neither Burke's nor The Clan Donald Genealogy Database 
2 CONC mention a "Jacobella".
2 CONT
2 CONT The case of the identity of Alexander's wife, who was mother of his heir John and two daughters, illustrates some of the difficulties faced in identifying spouse's maiden names and their parents. Even such meticulous and experienc
2 CONC ed researchers as Jean Munro, Ph.D., and R. W. Munro can apparently be led astray, as the two letters from Douglas Hickling below illustrate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The "note of a discharge" mentioning "Dame Elizabeth Haliburton, countess of Ross," is dated 40 years after Isabel Stewart, of Albany first husband's death and close to that after her second marriage. Given that, and the differenc
2 CONC es in first names, this may not be the end of this discussion. So Elizabeth (probably of Dirleton) Halyburton shown here as possibly one of his wives may or may not have existed.  (If she was a “Halyburton of Dirleton, then she co
2 CONC uld have been a daughter of https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-Walter-Halyburton-of-Dirleton-1st-Lord-Halyburton/6000000002187742736?through=6000000000174589576).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st letter from Douglas Hickling based on research of Jean Munro, Ph.D., and R. W. Munro.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I am going to comment on the name of Alexander's wife. Virtually all of the authorities state, without any documentary proof, that Alexander's wife was Elizabeth Seton, a daughter of Sir Alexander Seton and his wife Elizabeth Gor
2 CONC don. The Munros in their ACTS OF THE LORDS 0F THE ISLES, at 302-303 and elsewhere provide convincing evidence that Elizabeth's surname was Haliburton. At, 63, the Munros set forth a 1443 note of a discharge to Sir John Scrymgeour 
2 CONC by "Dame Elizabeth Haliburton, countess of Ross," apparently the only contemporary record which gives Elizabeth's surname. The Munros, at 241, set forth a reference to a papal indult, dated 19 October 1433, to Alexander and Jacobe
2 CONC lla, his wife, for a portable altar. There seems to be no other record of this earlier marriage or of any children resulting therefrom. '''On 2 November 1467''', Alexander's son John granted a charter confirming a gift to the mona
2 CONC stery of Fearn "for the salvation of the souls of his parents, Alexander earl of Ross and Elizabeth his wife." (See Munros, at 143.) This shows that Elizabeth, not Jacobella, was John's mother. BURKE'S PEERAGE & BARONETAGE (106th 
2 CONC edition), at 328, accepts "Elizabeth Halyburton, probably of Dirletoun," as Alexander's wife. "
2 CONT
2 CONT Douglas Hickling
2 CONT (August 2003)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd letter from Douglas Hickling
2 CONT
2 CONT Based on my previous e-mail to you, you now show that the wife of Alexander Macdonald ID: I37488, and the mother of John, Lord of the Isles, was Dame Elizabeth Haliburton.
2 CONT
2 CONT A correction seems to be needed. In the fall 2003 issue of THE GENEALOGIST, there is an article by Andrew B. W. MacEwen, beginning at  222, on Cristina de Brus, Countess of Dunbar. At page 225, the author,  as an illustration of t
2 CONC he difficulty in sometimes determining a woman's  maiden name, says:
2 CONT
2 CONT >And the twice married Isabella Stewart of Albany in a discharge dated 10 December 1443 was styled "Dame Elizabeth Haliburton, countess of  Ross," taking the rank of her first husband, Alexander Lesley, Earl of  Ross, and the surn
2 CONC ame of her second husband, Sir Walter Haliburton of  Dirleton--and seriously misleading such acute modern scholars as the  Munros.
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnote 13 says: "Jean Munro, Ph.D., and R. W. Munro, eds. ACTS OF THE LORDS OF THE ISLES 1336-1493, Scottish History Society, 4th ser., 22 (1986); 63, No. 41. Corrected in WEST HIGHLAND NOTES & QUERIES  [Isle of Coll. Argyll], s
2 CONC er. 2, No. 19 (March 1999): 24-25."
2 CONT
2 CONT The foregoing means that Isabel, daughter of Robert Duke of Albany, married (in 1398) Alexander, Earl of Ross (died 1402). She subsequently married Sir Walter Haliburton of Direlton. The wife of Alexander, Lord of the Isles and Ea
2 CONC rl of Ross (died 1499) [? date should be 1449?]  and the mother of their son John, the last earl of Ross and lord of the Isles, cannot be named with certainty. As the Munros point out at p. 63 in their comment regarding the note o
2 CONC f a discharge No. 41, and in a subsequent comment at p. 303, Elizabeth Seton, daughter of Sir Alexander Seton and Elizabeth Gordon, and sister of the first earl of Huntly, has been accepted as the wife of Alexander, but she is not
2 CONC  so named in any contemporary record. Her place as the wife of Alexander, Lord of the Isles and Earl of Ross depends upon a 16th century Gordon genealogy by John Ferrerius . Ferrerius is generally well-regarded and I suggest that 
2 CONC you may wish to show Elizabeth Seton as the earl's wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Munros were not alone in failing to discern the true identity of  "Dame Elizabeth Haliburton, Countess of Ross," referred to in the note of a discharge cited above. Complete Peerage V. 11, p. 151, published in 1949, makes the 
2 CONC same mistake.
2 CONT
2 CONT Doug Hickling
2 CONT (November 2003)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Basis for identification of Elizabeth as a Haliburton of Dirleton:
2 CONT
2 CONT Acts of the lords of the Isles, 1336-1493 (1986). Page 303, Appendix D. <[https://digital.nls.uk/scottish-history-society-publications/browse/archive/126627435#?c=0&m=0&s=0&cv=395&xywh=-320%2C-1%2C2343%2C2825 link]>
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000185751670858&size=large}}
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000185751928822&size=large}}
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT According to genealogist Andrew B. W. MacEwen (2003), the woman in the transaction was Alexander’s relative, https://www.geni.com/people/Lady-Isobel-Stewart/6000000000174589576, styling herself as “Elizabeth” (rather than Isabel),
2 CONC  using her second married name of “Haliburton”, and her former title of “countess of Ross.”  She is not described as Alexander’s wife in the discharge.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Posible daughters 
2 CONT
2 CONT Volume 22 - Acts of the lords of the Isles, 1336-1493 (1986).  Page 309.  Appendix D.  <[https://digital.nls.uk/scottish-history-society-publications/browse/archive/126627435#?c=0&m=0&s=0&cv=401&xywh=-49%2C106%2C1841%2C2220 link]>
