1 NAME Ian /Mackay of Aberach/
2 GIVN Ian
2 SURN Mackay of Aberach
2 _MARNM Mackay of Aberach
2 NICK John
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1417
2 PLAC Aberach, Scotland
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ian Aberach was the eldest son of Angus Du by his second wife, a daughter of Alexander Carrach Macdonald of Keppoch, as shall be afterwards shown ; and was fostered by maternal relatives in Lochaber, hence his name
2 CONC  Ian Aberach, John the Lochaber man. We shall "here relate a tradition regarding the meeting of father and son on the eventful eve of the battle of Drum nan Coup. When Ian arrived at Tongue from Lochaber, his father determined to 
2 CONC test his spirit in a quaint fashion. He ordered food to be spread for his son in a room where a large boar-hound was placed, The tierce brute, looking upon the food as under its charge, prepared to~show fight as soon as the Aberac
2 CONC h entered ; but Ian nothing daunted drew his dirk, closed with the hound, and soon dispatched it. The father delighted with his son's intrepidity exclaimed, " Dhearbh thu fuil do chridhe," you have proved the blood of your heart. 
2 CONC It may well be imagined his clansmen caused these words to ring throughout Strathnaver after the further proof they had of his valour on the field of Drum nan Coup. Be that as it may, this incident is traditionally reported to hav
2 CONC e occasioned the slogan " Dearbh do chridhe," prove thy heart, with which the Aberach Mackays were afterwards wont to rush into battle. That of the other branches of the clan is " Bi treun," be valiant. The latter slogan, in the L
2 CONC atin form " Maim forti," is now the motto of Mackay. - foot note pg. 59
2 CONT
2 CONT I. The  Aberach Mackays 
2 CONT
2 CONT I. Ian Aberach, son of Angus Du VII. of Strathnavev by his second wife, a daughter of Alexander Carrach Macdonald of Keppoch, son of John, Lord of the Isles, by his wife, a daughter of King Robert II., was the first of this branch
2 CONC . We quoted already at page 61 from the Knock MS. that " Mackay of Stratlmaver was taken, who married thereafter a daughter of Alexander Macdonald of Keppoch, of whom descended the race of Mackays called Slioc Ean Abrich." In vari
2 CONC ous encounters, already sufficiently described, he gallantly led the Mackays, and for some years during his elder brother's absence governed Strathnaver. Of his banner, now preserved in the National Museum, Edinburgh, we shall hav
2 CONC e something to say in an addendum to this Account. We show on page 242 the arms of the Aberach Mackays, as heraldically drawn from the representation on the said banner. The seat of the Aberach chieftains was at Achncss, about two
2 CONC  miles below Loch Naver, on the east side of the river. Their burial place was at Gnubeg, on an eminence close by the northern shore of Loch Naver, and in a special part of the cemetery there carefully walled oft' from the rest. '
2 CONC ''According to the Bute MS., Ian Aberach m. a dau. of Macintosh of Macintosh, and had by her known issue two sons :—
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William Du, of whom follows ; and  John. pg. 242-244
2 CONT
2 CONT  THE BOOK OF MACKAY BY ANGUS MACKAY, M.A. (St. Andrews University) Minister at Westerdale, Caithness EDINBURGH : NORMAN MACLEOD, -25 GEORGE IV. BRIDGE CANADA : EDWARD MACKAY, ST. PETER'S MANSE, MADOC, ONT. M DCCC
2 CONT
2 CONT =================
2 CONT
2 CONT Then again John Aberigh settled himself into the country of Strathnaver, where he continued until the death of King James the First, that his brother Neil- Wass Mackay was relieved out of the Bass, by the means of the lady of that
2 CONC  place, who was his near kinswoman. And at Niel his return into Strathnaver, John Aberigh willingly surrendered unto him all his lands within the country. Yet Niel gave unto his brother John the lands about Lochnaver, as a possess
2 CONC ion to dwell in during his days ; which lands his posterity, the Sleaght-ean Aberigh," i. e. the descendants of John Abrach, "do possess and inhabit at this day." pg. 67 
2 CONT
2 CONT IN consequence of the signal and decisive victory he had obtained at
2 CONT Drimnacoub, his name became famous in his time, and to posterity. He governed the country in peace, during the absence of his brother Niel ; was much beloved by the people, who viewed him as their common father, while he considere
2 CONC d himself as their guardian, acting for his absent brother. As he possessed great strength of body, and courage and fortitude of mind, so he excelled in all the social virtues. He was unambitious of encroaching on his neighbours, 
2 CONC and they were too much aware of his prowess to provoke him, or give him any ground to retaliate. The common opinion is, that Kiel's detention was for seven years in which case, his return must have been in 1434; but Sir Robert say
2 CONC s he only returned upon the death of King James, which was in 1437. John was distinguished by the appellation of Abrack or Aberigh  some say, from his having in his youth resided for some time with relations of his in Lochaber; an
2 CONC d others, from his bold resolute disposition; but the former is by far the most common and probable opinion. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His descendants have always been, and to the present time are, termed Slighd-ean-Abereigh, i. e. the offspring of John-Abrach. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They were  the most populous and powerful branch of the Mackays. It is said, that during his brother Niel's long absence, John was solicited by the people to assume the chieftainship, but he declined to interfere with his brother'
2 CONC s right. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon Niel's return, he gave to John and his heirs, in fee, the lands of Brechat, or heights of Sutherland, together with those round Lochnaver, consisting of Achness, Truderscag, Corrihuran, Achoul, Rhihalavag, Clibrig, Mowdale, T
2 CONC ubeg, Grubmore, Grubeg, and, as some say, the Strath of Naver, as far down as Rosshall. As these lands bounded with Sutherland, John and his descendants became wardens of Mackay's country ; and as they were in consequence, frequen
2 CONC tly opposed to Sutherland, during the two following centuries, Sir Robert is liberalin complimenting them after his own manner.
2 CONT
2 CONT John married first, a daughter of the laird of Mackintosh, by whom  he had two sons, William-Dow, who succeeded him, and John; and,  secondly, a daughter of Hector Mackenzie of Garloch, brother of the  laird of Kintail, by whom he
2 CONC  had a son, Hector
2 CONT
2 CONT HISTORY THE HOUSE AND CLAN MACKAY,BY ROBERT MACKAY, 1829
2 CONT
2 CONT ============
2 CONT
2 CONT THE CLAN-ABRACH BRANCH OF MACKAYS.
2 CONT
2 CONT John-Abrach Mackay of Achness, Breachat, &c. son of Angus-Dow, married 1st,
2 CONT a daughter of Mackintosh of Mackintosh, by whom he had two sons, 
2 CONT * William-Dow,who succeeded him, and 
2 CONT * John; and
2 CONT
2 CONT  2nd, a daughter of Hector Mackenzie of Garloch,brother of the laird of Kentail, by whom he had a son 
2 CONT *Hector  pg. 558 HISTORY THE HOUSE AND CLAN MACKAY,BY ROBERT MACKAY, 1829
