1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Lachlan Og /Maclean/ 8Th Chief Of MacLean birth about 1432 in Duart Castle, Lochdon, Isle of Mull, Argyll and Bute, Scotland 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents Lachlan Bronnach /Maclean/ 7th Chief Of MacLean and Margaret/Stewart/
2 CONT
2 CONT first wife Catherine /Campbell/ married:1498 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT children
2 CONT
2 CONT #Anne Maclean b. about 1474 in Duart Castle, Lochdon, Isle of Mull, Argyll and Bute, Scotland 
2 CONT
2 CONT #Hector /Maclean b. about 1478 in Duart Castle, Lochdon, Isle of Mull, Argyll and Bute, Scotland, d.1541 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Lachlan Og, Eighth Chief Of MacLean.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lachlan Og, or Young Lachlan, so called in order to distinguish him from his father, was lord of Duard and Chief Of MacLean during that eve when great political changes were about to take place in the Western Isles.  The western c
2 CONC hiefs had thought themselves practically independent of the king, and were obedient only to the Lord Of The Isles, who ruled in princely style.  In 1448, John MacDonald became Fourth Lord Of The Isles, and soon evinced himself as 
2 CONC one of the strongest opponents of the king's party.  In 1462, he entered into a compact with the banished house of Douglas and the commissioners of England, in which it was stipulated that John, with his vassals and such auxiliari
2 CONC es as would be furnished by Edward IV., and the assistance that might be given by the Douglases, should enter upon the conquest of Scotland; that John, Donald Balloch, and John, the sons and heirs of MacDonald, upon the payment of
2 CONC  a stipulated sum of money (John, Å200 sterling annually in time of war, and one hundred marks in time of peace; to Donald Balloch, Å40, and to John, his son, Å20, in time of war, and in times of peace half these sums respectively
2 CONC ), should become forever the sworn vassals of England, and assist in the wars in Ireland and elsewhere.  In the event of the conquest of Scotland, then that kingdom should be equally divided between John, Donald Balloch, and the E
2 CONC arl of Douglas.  While these negotiations were still pending, he Lord Of The Isles assembled a large force, captured the castle of Inverness, and commenced to assert authority over Inverness, Nairn, Ross, and Caithness.  How this 
2 CONC extraordinary rebellion was suppressed is uncertain.  He was summoned before parliament for treason; and that on failing to appear, the process of forfeiture against him was suspended for a time, and he was allowed to retain undis
2 CONC turbed possession of his estates for about fifteen years. The treaty of 1462 did not come to light until 1475, when the Scottish government determined to proceed against John as an avowed traitor and rebel.  Commission was given t
2 CONC o Colin Campbell, first Earl of Argyle, to prosecute a decree of forfeiture against him; and on the appointed day, failing to appear, sentence was passed upon him.  So great were the preparations now made against him, that MacDona
2 CONC ld sued for pardon, and with much humility surrendered to the king's mercy.  He was pardoned and restored to his forfeited estates on July 1, 1476.  Soon after, the earldom of Ross and the lands of Kintyre and Knapdale became inal
2 CONC ienably annexed to the crown.  This caused great dissatisfaction on the part of Angus, the bastard son of MacDonald, a man who was early accustomed to rebellion.  The division of the lands also divided the vassals of the Lordship 
2 CONC of the Isles, the MacLeans, MacLeods, and MacNeils adhering to John, while the various branches of the Clan MacDonald made common cause with the turbulent heir of the lordship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lachlan Og was called upon to pilot the clan during these troublous times, being beset both within and without.  The seanachaids have recorded but very little of his life; but they declare him "to have been a good and pious man." 
2 CONC  Enough is recorded to show that he was possessed of rare judgment, and maintained a peaceful demeanor.  He lived and died one of the most peaceful of his race.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Catherine, daughter of Colin Campbell, first Earl of Argyle.  His father-in-law and his accomplices were successful in involving the Lord Of The Isles in difficulties, and although he did not scruple to misrepres
2 CONC ent his son-in-law and brand him as a recreant chief, yet Lachlan's judgment and skill warded off every blow Argyle attempted to inflict.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, the records do not show when he became chief and when he died.  He was living in 1478, for in that year, at Edinburgh, on December 22d, he was witness to a charter confirmed by the king to David, Earl of Crawford.(*
2 CONC )  He probably died soon after.  By his wife, Catherine Campbell, he had Hector, his heir and successor; Fionnaghal, who was married to Celestine MacDonald, lord of Lochalsh and Lochcarron; and Anne, who was married to Sir Robert 
2 CONC Munro of Fowlis.  The marriage of Fionnaghal must have taken place in 1462, for in that year (at the castle at Dingwall, February 2d), John of the Isles grants to his brother "Celestine De insulis of the lands of Lochalsh, etc., t
2 CONC o hold to the said Celestine and the heirs to be gotten between him and Finvola, daughter of Lachlan McGilleone of Dowart."
2 CONT
2 CONT The records show that Lachlan McGilleain was witness to a "charter granted by Alexander, Lord Of The Isles, to his esquire, Gilleownan of Roderic of Murchard, Makneill, of the island of Barra, etc., and failing the said Gilleownan
2 CONC , then to the surviving brother of him gotten between the said Roderic Makneill and the daughter of Ferchard Makgilleoin.  Dated at the Isle of St Finlagan in Isla, on the vigil of St John the Baptist, 1472."
2 CONT
2 CONT Rolland Makclane of Dowart and Hector McClane of Carnlochboy are witnesses to a charter "granted by John Lord Of The Isles to John Davidson, of the lands of Grenare in Carrick.  Dated at Isla 20th August 1746."?? This same charter
2 CONC  appears to have been duplicated in 1478, with "Rolland Maklane of Dowart" again as a witness. [A History of the Clan Maclean pp48-50]
2 CONT
2 CONT (Finvola has been attached to her brother in this case.  The Lachlan that was her father is one generation previous to this. Chronologically it would have been impossible for her to be daughter of Catherine Campbell)
2 CONT
2 CONT ... Fuentes (3) 1. A History of the Clan Maclean J.P. Maclean  2. The Scots Peerage Sir James Balfour Paul, Lord Lyon King of Arms, editor  3. Isiaha Lee 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
