1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Wikipedia gives the date of death for this Malise as 1357, assigning the death at Neville's Cross to his brother in law Maurice de Moray.  (See https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malise_V,_Earl_of_Strathearn and https:/
2 CONC /en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maurice_de_Moravia,_Earl_of_Strathearn.)  Here, we follow MedLands: See below.
2 CONT
2 CONT MALISE ([1275/80]-before 1329).  Andrew Wyntoun´s Cronykil records that "Jhon and Alysandyre" had five sisters, the second of which married "Off Stratherne the Erle Malys" by whom she had "a swne…Malys"[732].  He succeeded his fat
2 CONC her in 1313 as Earl of Strathearn.  m firstly AGNES, daughter of --- (-after Aug 1320).  John of Fordun´s Scotichronicon (Continuator) records that "dominus Willelmus de Sowlis et comitissa de Strathern" were convicted of high tre
2 CONC ason at a parliament in Scone in Aug 1320 and sentenced to life imprisonment[733].  m secondly ([1323]) as her first husband, JOAN Menteith, daughter of Sir JOHN Menteith of Rusky & his wife --- (-after 20 Mar 1367).  She married 
2 CONC secondly John Campbell Earl of Atholl, thirdly Maurice Moray Earl of Strathearn, and fourthly (before 9 Nov 1347) as his second wife, William Sutherland 5th Earl of Sutherland.  Earl Malise & his first wife had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT i)          MALISE (-killed in battle Neville´s Cross 17 Oct 1346).  He succeeded his father as Earl of Strathearn.  He succeeded his cousin Magnus as Earl of Caithness and Orkney in [1323/29].  He resigned the earldom of Strathea
2 CONC rn [24 Sep/16 Dec] 1332 into the hands of Edward Balliol, was tried for treason in 1344 for so doing, but found innocent[734].  John of Fordun´s Scotichronicon (Continuator) records that "Johannes Ranulphi comes Moraviæ ac etiam c
2 CONC omes de Strathern constabularius Scotiæ…David filius et heres David de Lindsay…Willelmus Fraser…" were killed at Durham 17 Oct 1346[735].  [736]'''m firstly MARY, daughter of ---.'''  '''m secondly ([1325/28]) MATILDA [Marjory] of
2 CONC  Ross''', daughter of HUGH Earl of Ross & his first wife Matilda Bruce (-after 1350).  Earl Malise & his '''first wife''' had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT (a)       MATILDA .  m --- de la Arde . 
2 CONT
2 CONT (b)       EUPHEME . 
2 CONT
2 CONT (c)       AGNES (-before 1360).  m ERNGILS, son of ---.  He was granted the title of Earl of Orkney by the king of Norway in right of his wife but forfeited it in 1375[737]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (d)       ISABEL .  m Sir WILLIAM St Clair, son of ---.  Lord St Clair. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ii)         MARY .  "Malisius comes Strathern" granted "terram de Abbercarny…que fuit Roberti filii Magni dicti de Strathern" to "Johanni de Moravia de Dromsygart…[et] heredibus suis de Maria filia nostra" by undated charter[738].
2 CONC   [739]m ([1319/22]) as his second wife, Sir JOHN Moray of Drumsagard, co. Lanark, son of ---. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY.htm#MaliseStrathearndied1271A
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Malise Earl of Strathearn [68, The ancient earls of Strathearn, vol VIII, pp. 252-4], [68, Ancient earls of Caithness, Vol II, pp. 319-320], [68, Sinclair earls of Orkney article, vol VI, pp. 568-9], 21G Grandfather
2 CONT
2 CONT Death 	bef 1357
2 CONT
2 CONT General 	8th earl of Strathearn, which he resigned in 1344. Also earl of Caaithness.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Malise Earl of Strathearn (<1280-<1329)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	N. N. (poss. Marjory)
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouses
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 	Marjorie de Ross [68, Ancient earls of Caithness, Vol II, p. 320], 21G Grandmother
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Hugh de Ross Earl of Ross (-1333)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Maud de Bruce (->1323)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 	Isabella
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Malise Earl of Strathearn
2 CONT
2 CONT dspm.
2 CONT
2 CONT His daughters were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Matilda, m. to Watland de Ard, whose son seems to have been Alexander del Ard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella, as shown,
2 CONT
2 CONT Agusta, m. Arnglis Sunneson
2 CONT
2 CONT N. N., m. Gutform Sperra and whose son seems to have been Malise Sperra lord of Skaldale.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT VIII MALISE V, 8th Earl. From Dean Gules translation of the Orcadian Diploma we ascertain that King Magnus of Norway had directed the Lawman and Commons of Orkney to deliver to Earl Malise all charters, evidents, and letters of pr
2 CONC ivilege pertaining to him concerning the Earldom of Orkney. Very little is known of this Earl. In 1334 he had endeavoured to recover Stratherne, which Earldom King Edward III of England had bestowed on Earl John de Warrenne, broth
2 CONC er-in-law of Malise. He is supposed to have visited Norway about the same year, and in 1344 failing heirs-male, makes a special destination of the Earldom of Caithness to his daughter Isabella, who presently married Sir William St
2 CONC .Clair of Roslin, and, surviving all other issue of her father, died post 1404, transmitting her claim to that Earldom to her son Earl Henry II of Orkney, who allowed it to remain dormant, but the claim was successfully revived in
2 CONC  the person of his son, Earl William, who thus became first of his line Earl of Caithness, anno 1455. The Diploma states that Earl Malise married, first, Joanna, daughter of the Earl of Menteith (perhaps his cousin), by whom he ha
2 CONC d - 
2 CONT
2 CONT MATILDA, married Weyland de Ard, and had an only child, ALEXANDER DE ARD, EARL OF CAITHNESS (1375) without issue
2 CONT
2 CONT And secondly, Marjory, daughter of Hugh, Earl of Ross, by whom he had - 
2 CONT
2 CONT ISABELLA, married to Sir William St.Clair of Roslin, and had, with other issue HENRY ST.CLAIR, EARL OF ORKNEY, 1379. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ANNOT (or MERETTA), married Erengisle Suneson, Earl of Orkney by right of his wife, 1353-57, without issue 
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter, married Gothorm Spar, and had SIR MALISE SPAR, CLAIMANT OF ORKNEY, slain without issue 
2 CONT
2 CONT EUPHEMIA, died without issue 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earldom of Stratherne was apparently a male fief. Earl Malise is presumed to have died before 1345, in which year [his brother-in-law and cousin], Sir Maurice Moray, was created Earl of Stratherne, who falling at Durham the fo
2 CONC llowing year, the Earldom was given to Robert Stuart, first of his line King of Scotland, who, later on when he ascended the throne, assigned it to his brother David. 
