2 CONT
2 CONT John de Striwelyne is presumed to have been taken prisoner at Halidon Hill on 19th. July 1333, where his cousin of the same name was slain. John de Striwelyne married Mary, the aunt of John of Argyll, Lord of Lorn, the last of the
2 CONC  male line of the ancient Lords of Lorn, who claimed descent from Dugal, the son of Somerled of Argyle and the Isles. John of Lorn granted to Mary, his aunt, the wife of John de Striwelyn, the lands of Rathoran, and others, in Lor
2 CONC n, to be held of the granter for payment of a pair of spurs. This charter is dated at Perth on the Nativity of St. Mary 1338; and it was perhaps granted to the lady on the occasion of her marriage. In the following year, 1339, Joh
2 CONC n de Striwelyne and Alan Boyd, styled by Fordum "valentes armigeri", commanded the Archers, under the Steward of Scotland, at the siege of Perth, when they were both slain. The death of John de Striwelyne at Perth is also recorded
2 CONC  by Wyntoun in his Cronykil. He was the father of William of Strivelyn of Rathoran.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: "The Stirlings of Keir" by William Fraser pg. 15 - 1858 - 2nd, ed. 2005 _____________________________________________________________
