1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Kilmarnock, industrial town, East Ayrshire council area, historic county of Ayrshire, southwestern Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===MARGARET STEWART===
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Stewart is the bastard daughter of James IV, King of Scots, and Margaret Drummond. [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun01paul/page/22/mode/1up The Scots Peerage I: 22]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Stewart, daughter of James IV King of Scots and Margaret Drummond (a), married, as her 3rd husband, her 2nd cousin, Sir John Drummond, III.  They had the same Grandfather, Sir John Drummond, I. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Stewart was born illegitimately circa 1497.1 She married, firstly, John Gordon, Lord Gordon, son of Alexander Gordon, 3rd Earl of Huntly and Lady Jean Stewart, in November 1512.2 She married, secondly, Sir John Drummond o
2 CONC f Innerpeffray on 20 January 1530/31.2
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of James IV, King of Scots and Margaret Drummond.1 From November 1512, her married name became Gordon.2 From 20 January 1530/31, her married name became Drummond.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Margaret Stewart and Sir John Drummond of Innerpeffray
2 CONT
2 CONT ># Agnes Drummond+3 d. 21 Jan 1589/90
2 CONT ># Margaret Drummond+
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Margaret Stewart and John Gordon, Lord Gordon
2 CONT
2 CONT ># Alexander Gordon2
2 CONT ># James Gordon4
2 CONT ># George Gordon, 4th Earl of Huntly+2 b. 1513, d. 22 Oct 1562
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT #     [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 240. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
2 CONT #     [S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 2, page 2011. Hereinafter cited as Burke'
2 CONC s Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT #     [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Ex
2 CONC tinct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume XI, page 624. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT #     [S37] Charles Mosley, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition, volume 2, page 2012.
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://www.thepeerage.com/p10832.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p10351.htm#i103505
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Footnotes:''' (a) On 5/1/2015, Sharon Stuart McRee shares, as a reference only (no sources given), of Margaret TUDOR as the mother of Margaret Stewart because she was the wife of James IV, even though Margaret's Stewart's biolo
2 CONC gical mother was Ms. Drummond, with this reply of a professional genealogist (name unspecified);  "The line you ask about (parentage of Margaret Stewart) would be biologically her step-mother's [Margaret TUDOR's] line of ancestry,
2 CONC  which can also be used as Margaret STEWART's line, since in those days it was common practice for the higher men to have mistresses & or concubines, ostensibly to serve as child bearers for a wife who couldn't or didn't have a ma
2 CONC le heir. Biologically, as you can see in the chart below (chart not added), Margaret TUDOR had 6 children with him but only 1 would survive past infancy [James V], but of course they would not have known this the time; that child 
2 CONC could have died too. The security of the male line was not a sure thing. Whatever the motives of the husband, it was very common for these mistresses to act as surrogate mothers for his wife. Years ago as I was first learning this
2 CONC  I did not understand the overarching importance of passing the family name down, hoping it was a son, having control of the land properties & personal properties. With the royals, this was HUGE. I even researched in biblical days
2 CONC , and indeed this was routine. It only seems odd to us because our modern customs of recording family birth data in most cases wouldn't attribute an illegitimate child to a wife,but would call the wife the step-mother. James IV an
2 CONC d Margaret Tudor probably made little or no distinction."
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Stewart1,2,3,4,5,6 F, #37550, 
2 CONT b. circa 1497, d. after 1562 
2 CONT Father	James IV, King of Scots 4,5,6 b. 17 Mar 1472, d. 9 Sep 1513 
2 CONT Mother	Margaret Drummond3,4,5,6 d. May 1502
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Stewart was born circa 1497.3,5 
2 CONT
2 CONT She married John Gordon, Lord Gordon, Master of Huntly, son of Alexander Gordon, 3rd Earl of Huntly, Sheriff of Aberdeen & Inverness and Jean Stewart, before 26 April 1510; They had 3 sons (George, 4th Earl Huntly, Earl of Moray; 
2 CONC Alexander, Bishop of Galloway; & James, Chancellor of Moray).3,4,5,6 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Stewart and Alexander Stewart, Bishop of Moray were engaged after 5 December 1517; They did not marry, but did have a daughter Margaret, who married David, 2nd Lord Drummond.3,5 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Stewart and Sir John Drummond, 2nd Laird of Innerpeffray obtained a marriage license on 20 January 1531; Date of Papal Dispensation. They had 5 daughters (Agnes, wife of Sir Hugh Campbell, of Hugh Montgomery, 3rd Earl of 
2 CONC Eglintoun, and of Patrick, Lord Drummond; Isabel, wife of Sir Matthew Campbell; Margaret, wife of Robert, 3rd Lord Elphinstone; Jean (Jane), wife of Sir James Chisholm; & Elizabeth, wife of Malcolm Drummond.7,3,4,5,6 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Stewart died after 1562.3,5 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 1 	Alexander Stewart, Bishop of Moray d. 19 Dec 1537 
2 CONT
2 CONT Child	
2 CONT
2 CONT #    Margaret II Stewart3 d. bt 1539 - Dec 1543
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 2 	Sir John Drummond, 2nd Laird of Innerpeffray b. c 1500, d. a 4 Aug 1554 Children	
2 CONT
2 CONT #     Margaret Drummond+7
2 CONT #     Isabel Drummond+8
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 3 	John Gordon, Lord Gordon, Master of Huntly d. 5 Dec 1517 Children	
2 CONT
2 CONT #     Alexander Gordon, Archbishop of Athens, Bishop of the Isles, Bishop of Galloway+2,3,5 d. 11 Nov 1575
2 CONT #     James Gordon, Chancellor of Moray9
2 CONT #     George Gordon, 4th Earl of Huntly+ b. c 1515, d. 28 Oct 1562
2 CONT
