1 NOTE {geni:about_me} George Gordon, fourth Earl of Huntly, “the most powerful subject in Scotland, “who was killed at Corrichie, near Aberdeen in 1562.  (Apparently the cause of death is now determined to have been stroke while being h
2 CONC eld prisoner.)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on George Gordon, 4th Earl of Huntly
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Gordon,_4th_Earl_of_Huntly
2 CONT
2 CONT George Gordon, 4th Earl of Huntly (1514 – 28 October 1562) was a Scottish nobleman. He was the son of John Gordon, Lord Gordon, and Margaret Stewart, daughter of James IV.[1] George Gordon inherited his earldom and estates in 1524
2 CONC  at age 10. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As commander of the King's Army he defeated the English at the Battle of Haddon Rig in 1542, was a member of the council of Regency under James Hamilton, 2nd Earl of Arran and Cardinal Beaton and succeeded as Chancellor on the mur
2 CONC der of Beaton in 1546. He was captured at the Battle of Pinkie Cleugh in 1547, but escaped and in 1550 accompanied Mary of Guise to France. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He joined the Lords of the Congregation in 1560 and was prepared to accept Mary, Queen of Scots, until she transferred the Earldom of Moray, which had been given to the Earl of Huntly in 1549, to her half-brother Lord James Stewar
2 CONC t, at which point he withdrew to his estates in the North-East of Scotland.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary, Queen of Scots, toured the northeast in August 1562, and was refused entry to Inverness Castle on Gordon's orders. The Queen's forces captured the Castle before moving to Aberdeen where she issued a summons for Gordon. He re
2 CONC fused to answer and was outlawed. He marched on Aberdeen but was defeated by James Stewart, 1st Earl of Moray at the Battle of Corrichie in October 1562. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died of apoplexy after his capture, and his son, Sir John was executed in Aberdeen. Huntly was posthumously forfeited by parliament in May 1563.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death his body and the goods from Strathbogie Castle were shipped from Aberdeen to Edinburgh. The body stood for the earl at his trial. The goods were taken to Holyrood Palace. When Mary was imprisoned at Lochleven, she 
2 CONC was given the earl's cloth-of-estate.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT On 27 March 1530 he married Elizabeth Keith, daughter of Robert Keith, Master of Marischal, by whom he had nine sons and three daughters, including;
2 CONT *1. Thomas Gordon
2 CONT *2. George Gordon, 5th Earl of Huntly
2 CONT *3. Lady Margaret Gordon
2 CONT *4. Lady *Jean Gordon, Countess of Bothwell (1546 - 14 May 1629)
2 CONT *5. Lady Elizabeth Gordon (died 1557)
2 CONT *6. Alexander Gordon, Lord Gordon
2 CONT *7. Sir John Gordon, executed 1563
2 CONT *8. Adam Gordon, (born 1545) [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT *1. ^ He was the grandson of Alexander Gordon, 3rd Earl of Huntly by his wife, Lady Jean Stewart, daughter of John Stewart, 1st Earl of Atholl, by his wife Margaret Douglas. Margaret Douglas was the daughter of Archibald Douglas, 
2 CONC 5th Earl of Douglas by his wife Euphemia Graham. Euphemia Graham was a maternal great-granddaughter of Robert II of Scotland and Euphemia de Ross) and Margaret Stewart, Illegitimate daughter of King James IV of Scotland and Margar
2 CONC et Drummond.
2 CONT *2. ^ "Chapter III - Earldom and Earls of Huntly", Electric Scotland.com
2 CONT *3. ^ Robertson, (1863), xxii.
2 CONT *4. ^ http://thepeerage.com/p10922.htm#i109218
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Gordon, George (1514-1562)". Dictionary of National Biography. London: Smith, Elder & Co. 1885–1900.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonner, Elizabeth, 'The Earl of Huntly and the King of France, 1548: Man for Rent', English Historical Review, vol. 120, no.485 (Feb 2005), 80-103.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robertson, Joseph ,Inventaires de la Royne Descosse, Banntayne Club, (1863), xxii-xxv, 49-56.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peerage of Scotland
2 CONT *Earl of Huntly (1524–1562)
