2 CONT He was called "Mailach or "High-Brows". He was of Carrick and the 3rd Earl of Argyll. He was Master of the Kings Household in 1513/14 and High Justice of Scotland and was vice-regent to the King in1517. He served as Lieutenant of 
2 CONC the Borders and Warden of the Marches and Sheriff of co. Argyll.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''Colin Campbell, 3rd Earl of Argyll''' (c. 1486 – 9 October 1529) was a Scottish nobleman and soldier.
2 CONT
2 CONT from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colin_Campbell,_3rd_Earl_of_Argyll
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Life===
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Archibald Campbell, 2nd Earl of Argyll and Elizabeth Stuart, daughter of John Stewart, 1st Earl of Lennox. In 1506/07 he married Lady Jean Gordon, the eldest daughter of Alexander Gordon, 3rd Earl of Huntly.
2 CONT
2 CONT Campbell led an army against the insurrection of various Highland chieftains; a few years later, he joined the court of King James V. He was given the position Lord Warden of the Marches and in 1528, Lord Justice General of Scotla
2 CONC nd.[1] He is buried at Kilmun Parish Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family===
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Colin Campbell, 3rd Earl of Argyll and Jean Gordon:
2 CONT * Archibald Campbell, 4th Earl of Argyll d. bt 21 August 1558 – 2 December 1558, married three times.
2 CONT * John Campbell, 1st of Lochnell, killed at the Battle of Langside.[2]
2 CONT * Lady Elizabeth Campbell d. c 1548, married; firstly, James Stewart, 1st Earl of Moray, an illegitimate son of King James IV; secondly, John Gordon, 11th Earl of Sutherland[3]
2 CONT * Lady Agnes Campbell (1526–1601), married James MacDonald and Turlough Luineach O'Neill.
2 CONT
2 CONT Argyll's sister, Lady Catherine Campbell, survived a murder attempt by her husband, Lachlan Maclean of Duart in 1527. Maclean rowed out to Lady's Rock in the Firth of Lorne one night at low tide and left his wife stranded.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Colin Campbell: This nobelman was appointed, in 1528, lieutenant of the borders, warden of the marches, heritable sheriff of Argyllshire, justice-general of Scotland, and Master of the king's household. His lordship marr
2 CONC ied Jane, daughter of Alexander, 3rd Earl of Huntly and had issue, 1.	ARCHIBALD, 4th earl. 2.	John, ancestor of Campbell of Lochnell, Campbell now of Barbreck, Campbell of Balerno, and Stonefield. 3.	Alexander, Dean of Moray. 1.	E
2 CONC lizabeth married 1st, James, Earl of Moray, illegitimate son of JAMES IV., King of Scots, and by him (who died 12 June, 1544) had a daughter. She married 2ndly, John, 10th Earl of Sutherland, s.p.  The earl died in 1529, and was s
2 CONC ucceeded by his eldest son, ARCHIBALD, 4th EARL OF ARGYLL. This nobleman was the first person of importance in Scotland who embraced the Protestant religion, and he became subsequently a strenuous advocate for the Reformation. He 
2 CONC married 1st, Helen, daughter of James. Earl of Arran, and had one son, 1.	ARCHIBALD, his successor. He married 2ndly, Margaret, daughter of William Graham, Earl of Monteith, by whom he had, 2.	COLIN, who succeeded as 6th Earl. 1.	
2 CONC Margaret, married James, Lord Doune. 2.	Janet, married Hector Maclean, of Doward His lordship died in 1558, and was succeeded by his eldest son,
2 CONT
2 CONT ARCHIBALD, 5TH EARL OF ARGYLL. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Notes for Colin Campbell: This nobelman was appointed, in 1528, lieutenant of the borders, warden of the marches, heritable sheriff of Argyllshire, justice-general of Scotland, and Master of the king's household. His lordship marr
2 CONC ied Jane, daughter of Alexander, 3rd Earl of Huntly and had issue, 1. ARCHIBALD, 4th earl. 2. John, ancestor of Campbell of Lochnell, Campbell now of Barbreck, Campbell of Balerno, and Stonefield. 3. Alexander, Dean of Moray. 1. E
2 CONC lizabeth married 1st, James, Earl of Moray, illegitimate son of JAMES IV., King of Scots, and by him (who died 12 June, 1544) had a daughter. She married 2ndly, John, 10th Earl of Sutherland, s.p. The earl died in 1529, and was su
2 CONC cceeded by his eldest son, ARCHIBALD, 4th EARL OF ARGYLL. This nobleman was the first person of importance in Scotland who embraced the Protestant religion, and he became subsequently a strenuous advocate for the Reformation. He m
2 CONC arried 1st, Helen, daughter of James. Earl of Arran, and had one son, 1. ARCHIBALD, his successor. He married 2ndly, Margaret, daughter of William Graham, Earl of Monteith, by whom he had, 2. COLIN, who succeeded as 6th Earl. 1. M
2 CONC argaret, married James, Lord Doune. 2. Janet, married Hector Maclean, of Doward His lordship died in 1558, and was succeeded by his eldest son,
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ='''Colin Campbell, 3rd Earl of Argyll'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT Colin Campbell, 3rd Earl of Argyll was a Scottish nobleman and soldier. He was also known as ''"Cailen Malloch"''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Colin Campbell was the son of Archibald Campbell, 2nd Earl of Argyll and Lady Elizabeth Stuart, daughter of John Stewart, 1st Earl of Lennox. In 1506/07, he married Lady Jean Gordon, the eldest daughter of Alexander Gordon, 3rd Ea
2 CONC rl of Huntly by his first wife, Lady Jean Stewart. He succeeded as Earl of Argyll upon the death of his father on 9 September 1513.
2 CONT
2 CONT Campbell led an army against the insurrection of various Highland chieftains; a few years later, he joined the court of King James V of Scotland. He was given the position of Lord Warden of the Marches, and in 1528, Lord Justice G
2 CONC eneral of Scotland.[1] He died on 9 October 1529, and was buried at Kilmun Parish Church in Cowal, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Colin Campbell was succeeded by his son, Archibald Campbell. The Campbell family resided at Castle Campbell, near Dollar, Clackmannanshire, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Colin Campbell, 3rd Earl of Argyll and Lady Jean Gordon:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Archibald Campbell, 4th Earl of Argyll''' (d. bt 21 August 1558 – 2 December 1558), married three times.
2 CONT * '''John Campbell, 1st of Lochnell''' (d. 13 May 1568), was killed at the Battle of Langside.[2]
2 CONT * '''Lady Elizabeth Campbell''' (d. c. 1548), married: firstly, James Stewart, 1st Earl of Moray, an illegitimate son of King James IV of Scotland; secondly, John Gordon, 11th Earl of Sutherland[3]
2 CONT * '''Lady Agnes Campbell''' (b. 1526, d. 1601), married: firstly, James MacDonald, 6th of Dunnyveg; secondly, Sir Turlough Luineach O'Neill of Tír Eoghain, Ireland. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agnes_Campbell]
2 CONT
2 CONT Campbell's sister, Lady Catherine Campbell, survived a murder attempt by her husband, Lachlan Maclean of Duart, in 1527. Maclean rowed out to Lady's Rock in the Firth of Lorne one night at low tide and left his wife stranded.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Refrences'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Stephen, Leslie, ed. (1886). "Campbell, Colin (d.1530)" . Dictionary of National Biography. 8. London: Smith, Elder & Co.
2 CONT
