2 CONT 5. Margaret Ogilvy+6
2 CONT 6. Agnes Ogilvy6
2 CONT 7. Marion Ogilvy+7 b. b 1520
2 CONT 8. Thomas Ogilvy of Westercraig+5 b. b 1547, d. b 1577
2 CONT 9. Alexander Ogilvy6 b. b 1548
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinc
2 CONC t or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume I, page 314. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT 2.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume I, page 70.
2 CONT 3.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume VIII, page 140.
2 CONT 4.[S323] Sir James Balfour Paul, The Scots Peerage: founded on Wood's edition of Sir Robert Douglas's The Peerage of Scotland (Edinburgh, Scotland: David Douglas, 1904), volume I, page 117. Hereinafter cited as The Scots Peerage.
2 CONT 5.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume I, page 71.
2 CONT 6.[S323] Sir James Balfour Paul, The Scots Peerage, volume I, page 118.
2 CONT 7.[S37] BP2003 volume 2, page 1642. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT From: https://www.thepeerage.com/p10924.htm#i109232
2 CONT ______
2 CONT Henry Sinclair (died 1513) was a Scottish noble and the 4th Lord Sinclair.[1] In The Scots Peerage by James Balfour Paul he is designated as the 3rd Lord Sinclair,[2] but historian Roland Saint-Clair designates him the 4th Lord Si
2 CONC nclair and references this to an Act of the Scottish Parliament in which he was made Lord Sinclair based on his descent from his great-grandfather, Henry II Sinclair, Earl of Orkney, the first Lord Sinclair.[1] Bernard Burke, in h
2 CONC is a Genealogical and Heraldic Dictionary of the Peerage and Baronetage of the British Empire, agrees with Roland Saint-Clair and says that Henry Sinclair was "in reality" the fourth holder of the title of Lord Sinclair.[3]
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT He was the son of William Sinclair, 3rd Lord Sinclair and Christian Leslie, daughter of George Leslie, 1st Earl of Rothes. Immediately after the death of his father, an Act was passed by the Scottish Parliament in his favour and w
2 CONC hich recognised him as "Chief of yat blude" and willing "yarfor that he be callit Lord Saintclair in tyme to cum", dated 26 January 1488–89. This Act was only a recognition of the Barony of St. Clair existing in the person of his 
2 CONC ancestor, Henry II Sinclair, Earl of Orkney and did not constitute a new creation.[1]
2 CONT Notable offices
2 CONT Henry Sinclair, 4th Lord Sinclair sat as a Baron of Parliament on 14 January 1488. On 4 December 1488, Confirmation issued to him and his wife Margaret, the lands of Cousland, house and fortalice, and Ravenscraig with the adjacent
2 CONC  lands of Woolston, Carberry and Dubbo.[1]
2 CONT Lord Sinclair is mentioned with frequent involvement in the Orcadian dominions. As the Tacksman of the Isles he granted an annuity to the Bishop of Orkney on 6 August 1485. On 28 May 1489 Lord Sinclair received several grants incl
2 CONC uding a 13-year lease of Orkney and Shetland, the custody of Kirkwall Castle with the fortalices, and the Justicary, Folderie and Balliatus for 13 years.[4] It is also likely that through his influence an Act of the Scottish Parli
2 CONC ament was passed in 1503 for the annulment of all foreign laws within that realm and to spare the native laws of Orkney and Shetland.[1] In 1503, he had sasine of the lands of Newburgh.[2]
2 CONT Lord Sinclair was made Master of the Artillery on 13 March 1510, for a fee of £100 a year, deducted from his Orkney accounts. His main residence was at Ravenscraig Castle. He was on bad terms with the inhabitants of Dysart as show
2 CONC n by a complaint made against him by John Wynde, burgess of Dysart. In 1512, he was the captain of the Scottish flagship, the Great Michael.[1]
2 CONT Shortly before his death his sold eight pieces of ordance to the King for £100 which was paid to his widow. He was killed at the Battle of Flodden on 9 September 1513.[2][5][3][6]
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Henry Sinclair, 4th Lord Sinclair, married Margaret, daughter of Adam Hepburn, Master of Hailes and sister of Patrick Hepburn, 1st Earl of Bothwell.[3] They had the following children:[1]
2 CONT 1. William Sinclair, 5th Lord Sinclair, heir and successor.
2 CONT 2. Catherine Sinclair, who in 1512 married Sir David Wemyss of Wemyss, ancestor of the Earls of Wemyss.[7]
2 CONT 3. '''Helen Sinclair, who married James Ogilvy, 4th Lord Ogilvy of Airlie.'''
2 CONT 4. Jean Sinclair, who married Sir Alexander Lindsay, Master of Crawford and son of David Lindsay, 8th Earl of Crawford.
2 CONT 5. Agnes Sinclair, who married Patrick Hepburn, 3rd Earl of Bothwell.
2 CONT 6. William Sinclair, a natural son who was legitimised on 20 February 1539–40. He was Chaplain, Rector of Olrig and later Vicar of Latheron.
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Sinclair%2C_4th_Lord_Sinclair
2 CONT _____
2 CONT Earl of Airlie
2 CONT Earl of Airlie is a title of the peerage in Scotland created on 2 April 1639 for James Ogilvy, 7th Lord Ogilvy of Airlie, along with the title "Lord Ogilvy of Alith and Lintrathen". The title "Lord Ogilvy of Airlie" was created on
2 CONC  28 April 1491.
2 CONT etc. 
2 CONT Family history
2 CONT The family was probably descended from Gillebride, Earl of Angus, who received lands from William the Lion. Sir Walter Ogilvy (died 1440) of Lintrathen, Lord High Treasurer of Scotland from 1425 to 1431, was the Son of Sir Walter 
2 CONC Ogilvy of Wester Powrie and Auchterhouse, a man, says Andrew of Wyntoun, "stout and manfull, bauld and wycht", who was killed in 1392. He built a castle at Airlie in Forfarshire and left two sons. The elder of these, Sir John Ogil
2 CONC vy (D.C 1484), was the father of Sir James Ogilvy (c. 1430–c. 1504), who was made a Lord of Parliament in 1491; and the younger, Sir Walter Ogilvy, was the ancestor of the Earls of Findlater. The Earldom of Findlater, bestowed on 
2 CONC James Ogilvy, Lord Ogilvy of Deskford, in 1638, was united in 1711 with the Earldom of Seafield and became dormant after the death of James Ogilvy, the 7th Earl, in October 1811.[3]
2 CONT Sir James Ogilvy's descendant, James Ogilvy, 5th Lord Ogilvy of Airlie (c. 1541–1606), a son of James Ogilvy, master of Ogilvy, who was killed at the Battle of Pinkie in 1547, took a leading part in Scottish politics during the re
2 CONC igns of Mary — Queen of Scotts, and of James VI.[3] In June 1562, the 5th Lord Ogilvy was badly injured in a duel with John Gordon of Findlater in Edinburgh. John Gordon was imprisoned until Ogilvy recovered.[4]
2 CONT The 5th Lord Ogilvy's grandson, James Ogilvy (c. 1593–1666), was created Earl of Airlie and Lord Ogilvy of Alith and Lintrathen by Charles I at York on 2 April 1639. A loyal partisan of the king, he joined Montrose in Scotland in 
2 CONC 1644 and was one of the royalist leaders at the Battle of Kilsyth. The destruction of the Earl's castles of Airlie and of Forther in 1640 by the Earl of Argyll, who "left him not in all his lands a cock to crow day", gave rise to 
2 CONC the song The Bonnie House of Airlie.[5] His eldest son, James, the 2nd Earl (c. 1615–c. 1704) also fought among the royalists in Scotland; in 1644 he was taken prisoner, but he was released in the following year as a consequence o
2 CONC f Montrose's victory at Kilsyth. He was again a prisoner after the Battle of Philiphaugh and was sentenced to death in 1646, but he escaped from his captivity at St. Andrews and was afterward pardoned. Serving with the Scots again
2 CONC st Cromwell, he became a prisoner for the third time in 1651 and was in the Tower of London during most of the years of the Commonwealth. He was a fairly prominent man under Charles II and James II, and in 1689 he ranged himself o
2 CONC n the side of William of Orange. 
2 CONT etc
