2 CONT '''Euphemia Drummond'''1 
2 CONT F, #137692, '''d. 1501'''
2 CONT Last Edited=1 Dec 2018
2 CONT Consanguinity Index=0.04%
2 CONT '''     Euphemia Drummond was the daughter of''' John Drummond, 1st Lord Drummond and Lady Elizabeth Lindsay.1 '''She married John Fleming, 2nd Lord Fleming.2 She died in 1501, poisoned.3 She was buried in 1502 at Dunblane Abbey, 
2 CONC Scotland'''G.3
2 CONT '''     Her married name became Fleming.'''2
2 CONT '''Children of Euphemia Drummond and John Fleming, 2nd Lord Fleming'''
2 CONT 1. Margaret Fleming+4
2 CONT 2. Malcolm Fleming, 3rd Lord Fleming+2 b. c 1494, d. 10 Sep 1547
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1.[S1224] Derek Hughes, "re: 1st Lord Drummond," e-mail message to Darryl Lundy, 22 December 2004, 13 February 2005 and 2 March 2005. Hereinafter cited as "re: 1st Lord Drummond."
2 CONT 2.[S37] BP2003 volume 3, page 3103. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT 3.[S1224] Derek Hughes, "re: 1st Lord Drummond," e-mail to Darryl Lundy, 22 December 2004, 13 February 2005 and 2 March 2005, says 1501.
2 CONT 4.[S6286] Clan MacFarlane and associated clans genealogy, online http://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info. Hereinafter cited as Clan MacFarlane.
2 CONT From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p13770.htm#i137692
2 CONT _____
2 CONT John Drummond, first Lord Drummond (died 1519), was a Scottish statesman.
2 CONT Drummond, ninth successive knight of his family, was the eldest son of Sir Malcolm Drummond of Cargill and Stobhall, Perthshire, by his marriage with Mariota, eldest daughter of Sir David Murray of Tullibardine in the same county.
2 CONC  etc.
2 CONT He died at Drummond Castle, Strathearn, in 1519, and was buried in the church of Innerpeffray. He was succeeded by his great-grandson David. In Douglas's ‘Peerage of Scotland’ (ed. Wood, ii. 361) Drummond is absurdly stated to hav
2 CONC e married ‘Lady Elisabeth Lindsay, daughter of David, duke of Montrose.’ His wife was Elizabeth Lindsay, daughter of Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford, and by her he had three sons and six daughters. Malcolm, the eldest son,
2 CONC  died young; David, master of Drummond, is not mentioned in the pedigrees, but is now believed to have been the chief actor in the massacre of members of the Murrays at Monivaird Church (Monzievaird), for which he was executed aft
2 CONC er 21 Oct. 1490.[5] William was living in March 1503; and John was ancestor of the Drummonds of Innerpeffray and of Riccarton.
2 CONT Of the daughters, Margaret Drummond, mistress of James IV, was poisoned in 1501; Elizabeth married George Douglas, Master of Angus, and was great-grandmother of Henry, Lord Darnley, Beatrix, it has been said, married James Hamilto
2 CONC n, 1st Earl of Arran; Annabella married William Graham, 1st Earl of Montrose; '''Eupheme, the wife of John Fleming, 4th Lord Fleming, was poisoned in 1501'''; and Sibylla shared a like fate, the sisters were buried at Dunblane Cat
2 CONC hedral. Drummond was the common ancestor of the viscounts of Strathallan and of the earls of Perth and Melfort.
2 CONT From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Drummond,_1st_Lord_Drummond 
2 CONT _______________________
2 CONT DRUMMOND, JOHN, first Lord Drummond (d. 1519), statesman, ninth successive knight of his family, was the eldest son of Sir Malcolm Drummond of Cargill and Stobhall, Perthshire, by his marriage with Mariot, eldest daughter of Sir D
2 CONC avid Murray of Tullibardine in the same county. etc.He died at Drummond Castle, Strathearn, in 1519, and was buried in the church of Innerpeffray. He was succeeded by his great-grandson David. In Douglas's ‘Peerage of Scotland’ (e
2 CONC d. Wood, ii. 361) Drummond is absurdly stated to have married ‘Lady Elisabeth Lindsay, daughter of David, duke of Montrose.’ His wife was Elizabeth Lindsay, daughter of Alexander, fourth earl of Crawford, and by her he had three s
2 CONC ons and six daughters. Malcolm, the eldest son, died young; David, master of Drummond, is not mentioned in the pedigrees, but is now believed to have been the chief actor in the outrage on the Murrays at Monivaird Church, for whic
2 CONC h he was executed after 21 Oct. 1490 (Exchequer Rolls of Scotland, ed. Burnett, vol. x. p. 1, with which cf. Accounts of the Lord High Treasurer, Scotland, ed. Dickson, vol. i. pp. cii–civ); William was living in March 1502–3; and
2 CONC  John was ancestor of the Drummonds of Innerpeffray and of Riccarton. Of the daughters, Margaret [q. v.], mistress of James IV, was poisoned in 1501; Elizabeth married George, master of Angus, and was great-grandmother of Henry, l
2 CONC ord Darnley; Beatrix married James, first earl of Arran; Annabella married William, first earl of Montrose; '''Eupheme, the wife of John, fourth lord Fleming, was poisoned in 1501'''; and Sibylla shared a like fate. Drummond was t
2 CONC he common ancestor of the viscounts of Strathallan and of the earls of Perth and Melfort.
2 CONT [Douglas's Peerage of Scotland (Wood), ii. 360–1; Malcolm's Memoir of the House of Drummond, pp. 67–86; Registrum Magni Sigilli Regum Scotorum (Paul), 1424–1513, (Paul and Thomson) 1513–46; Exchequer Rolls of Scotland (Burnett), v
2 CONC ols. vii–x.; Accounts of the Lord High Treasurer, Scotland (Dickson), vol. i.; Cal. State Papers, Scottish Ser. (1509–89), p. 1; Letters and Papers of Hen. VIII (Brewer), 1509–16.]
2 CONT G. G.
2 CONT From: http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Drummond,_John_(d.1519)_(DNB00) 
2 CONT ____________
2 CONT
2 CONT Before 5 May 1496 John Fleming married '''Eupheme Drummond''', daughter of John, 1st lord Drummond, and Lady Elizabeth Lindsay. However, in May 1502 she was poisoned when she was at her parents' home. She died together with two of
2 CONC  her sisters; one of these, Margaret Drummond, was King James IV's mistress and at the time it was rumoured the King intended to marry her. Several years later Margaret Tudor, who by then was the wife of King James IV, blamed John
2 CONC  Fleming and spread the story that 'the Lord Fleming, for evil will that he had to his wife, caused to poison three sisters and one of them was his wife and this is known of truth in all Scotland'. )
2 CONT
2 CONT John Fleming married (1/2) bef. 5 May 1496 Eupheme Drummond, 5th dau. of John [Drummond], 1st Lord Drummond, by his wife Lady Elizabeth Lindsay, only dau. of Alexander [Lindsay], 4th Earl of Crawford.  On 3 May 1502 she died, by p
2 CONC oisoning, along with her sisters Margaret, one of four mistresses of James IV, and Sybella (d. unm.). 
2 CONT
2 CONT > Euphemia Drummond, married, in 1495, to John, 2nd Lord Fleming, by “whom he had two sons and five daughters.” [SIC] ("Genealogical memoir of the most noble and ancient house of Drummond" by David Malcolm pub. 1808). <BR>
2 CONT >John Fleming is known to have had two daughters, unnamed by Balfour Paul and likely born before 1502 when Agnes Drummond was poisoned. (Wikitree)
2 CONT
2 CONT (Possibly) the children of John Fleming and Euphemia Drummond:
2 CONT * 1.  Malcolm Fleming, later 3rd Lord Fleming; ( b.12 Jan 1493 - d. 10 Sep 1547). Killed in Action during the Rough Wooing at the Battle of Pinkie Cleugh.   Married Janet Stewart.   
2 CONT *2.  Jean Fleming.  Married David Crawfurd, 5th of Kerse 
2 CONT *3.  Elizabeth Fleming. Married  William Crichton, 5th Lord of Sanquhar.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
