2 CONT '''     Elizabeth Drummond was the daughter of''' John Drummond, 1st Lord Drummond and Lady Elizabeth Lindsay.2,1 '''She married, firstly, Sir David Fleming before 1485.1 She married, secondly, George Douglas, Master of Angus''', 
2 CONC son of Archibald Douglas, 5th Earl of Angus and Elizabeth Boyd, '''between July 1485 and 31 January 1489.1 She died after 21 August 1514.'''1
2 CONT '''     From before 1485, her married name became Fleming.1 From before March 1488, her married name became Douglas.'''
2 CONT '''Children of Elizabeth Drummond and George Douglas, Master of Angus'''
2 CONT 1. Elizabeth Douglas+3
2 CONT 2. William Douglas3 d. 2 Oct 1528
2 CONT 3. Alison Douglas+3
2 CONT 4. Janet Douglas+3 d. 17 Jul 1537
2 CONT 5. Margaret Douglas+3
2 CONT 6. Archibald Douglas, 6th Earl of Angus+1 b. c 1490, d. Jan 1556/57
2 CONT 7. Sir George Douglas+4 b. 1490, d. Aug 1552
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinc
2 CONC t or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume I, page 157. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT 2.[S2] Peter W. Hammond, editor, The Complete Peerage or a History of the House of Lords and All its Members From the Earliest Times, Volume XIV: Addenda & Corrigenda (Stroud, Gloucestershire, U.K.: Sutton Publishing, 1998), page 
2 CONC 27. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage, Volume XIV.
2 CONT 3.[S8] BP1999 volume 1, page 1282. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S8]
2 CONT 4.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume I, page 158.
2 CONT From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p10957.htm#i109569
2 CONT ______
2 CONT '''George Douglas, Master of Angus''' (1469 – 9 September 1513) was a Scottish Nobleman. The son of Archibald Douglas, 5th Earl of Angus and Elizabeth Boyd, daughter of Robert Boyd, 1st Lord Boyd, he was born at Tantallon Castle a
2 CONC nd died at the Battle of Flodden.
2 CONT Overview
2 CONT In 1489 the 5th Earl of Angus, Douglas's father resigned his lordships of Tantallon, Douglasdale, Liddesdale, Ewesdale, Eskdale, Selkirk, and Jedburgh Forest to the crown. James III then granted a new charter in favour of '''the M
2 CONC aster.'''
2 CONT During the stand off between James III and the party backing his son James, then Duke of Rothesay, '''the Master''' attended the last parliament of the king before his death in the Battle of Sauchieburn. It is not recorded to whic
2 CONC h faction the younger Angus adhered to. He attended parliament again under the newly crowned James IV in 1490.
2 CONT '''The Master''' did not take an active part in Public affairs until 1499 when he took formal control over his lordships of Eskdale and Ewesdale. These lordships had become renowned for lawlessness and banditry, and the Master was
2 CONC  appointed by the King as warden of Eskdale. In his capacity of Warden he met with his English counterpart Lord Dacre at Canonbie to hear grievances and fix punishments.
2 CONT '''The Younger Angus''' was not a particularly effective at restoring law and order. His undue leniency to his own tenantry caused the King to deprive him of office in 1506, granting it instead to Alexander Home, 3rd Lord Home. Ge
2 CONC orge did however, gain the Barony of Crawford-Lindsay in 1510, and his father made over his estates of Kirriemuir, Abernethy, and Horsehopecleugh in the same year.
2 CONT Death at Flodden Field
2 CONT In late August 1513 '''the Master of Angus''' rode out with his father the Earl, and his younger brother Sir William Douglas of Glenbervie at the head of a large Douglas contingent and their adherents. On reaching the mustering po
2 CONC int at the Ellemford, north of Duns, they joined with the largest and most modern army that Scotland had ever fielded. The army proceeded under King James into England where it eventually met with the army of the Earl of Surrey at
2 CONC  Flodden Edge.
2 CONT A petulant Earl of Angus, having had his advice snubbed by King James, left the field and returned to Scotland, leaving '''the Master''' and his brother in charge of the Douglas contingent.
2 CONT Nothing more is recorded of '''the Master of Angus''' except an anecdote recorded by David Hume of Godscroft recording the last moments of King James. When Sir Edward Stanley had broken the Scottish left under the Earl of Lennox a
2 CONC nd Earl of Argyll. King James in the centre dismounted and prepared to make his stand amongst his spearmen. On noticing '''the Master of Angus''' still on horseback, he cried to him, "asking if it had been in the manner of his rac
2 CONC e to remain mounted while their sovereign fought on foot". To which '''the Master''' replied asking whether "it was the fashion of the King of Scots to wear his mail and armorial bearings while fighting on foot". '''The master''' 
2 CONC hit a raw nerve in James's chivalric mind and he replied "I dare fight upon my feet as well as you or any subject I have, and that without coat-armour or royal cognisance."
2 CONT The English billmen now closed on the Scottish centre and King James was found within a spear length of Surrey. Whether Godscroft's anecdote is true or not, that '''the Master''' of Angus's taunts drove him to his death, '''the Ma
2 CONC ster''' was equal to the King in reckless gallantry. '''The Master's corpse''' was found amongst the twelve Scottish Earls and seventeen Lords who died. According to Godscroft over 200 men of the name of Douglas also died.
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT In 1485 '''the Master of Angus''' was contracted in marriage to Margaret, daughter of Laurence Oliphant, 1st Lord Oliphant. '''It appears that this contract was not fulfilled as George was wed in 1488 to Elizabeth Drummond''', dau
2 CONC ghter of John Drummond, 1st Lord Drummond. '''The marriage ran afoul of the strictures of a small feudal society such as Scotland: it was found out some years after the marriage that it was within the prohibited degrees of consang
2 CONC uinity. The Master of Angus applied for and successfully obtained Papal dispensation in 1495.'''
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT '''By Elizabeth Drummond, George Master of Angus had three sons and four daughters:'''
2 CONT *Archibald Douglas, 6th Earl of Angus
2 CONT *Sir George Douglas of Pittendreich
2 CONT *William Douglas, Prior of Coldingham and Abbot of Holyrood
2 CONT *Elizabeth Douglas, married John Hay, 3rd Lord Yester
2 CONT *Alison Douglas, married 1) Robert Blackadder of that Ilk (died 1513) and then David Home of Wedderburn
2 CONT *Janet Douglas, (1498-1537) {executed by James V) married John Lyon, 6th Lord Glamis
2 CONT *Margaret Douglas, married James Douglas of Drumlanrig
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Douglas,_Master_of_Angus
2 CONT _______
2 CONT DRUMMOND, JOHN, first Lord Drummond (d. 1519), statesman, ninth successive knight of his family, was the eldest son of Sir Malcolm Drummond of Cargill and Stobhall, Perthshire, by his marriage with Mariot, eldest daughter of Sir D
2 CONC avid Murray of Tullibardine in the same county. etc.  In 1514 Drummond gave great offence to many of the lords by promoting the marriage of his grandson, Archibald Douglas, sixth earl of Angus, with the queen-dowager Margaret. etc
2 CONC . He died at Drummond Castle, Strathearn, in 1519, and was buried in the church of Innerpeffray. He was succeeded by his great-grandson David. In Douglas's ‘Peerage of Scotland’ (ed. Wood, ii. 361) Drummond is absurdly stated to h
2 CONC ave married ‘Lady Elisabeth Lindsay, daughter of David, duke of Montrose.’ His wife was Elizabeth Lindsay, daughter of Alexander, fourth earl of Crawford, and by her he had three sons and six daughters. Malcolm, the eldest son, di
2 CONC ed young; David, master of Drummond, is not mentioned in the pedigrees, but is now believed to have been the chief actor in the outrage on the Murrays at Monivaird Church, for which he was executed after 21 Oct. 1490 (Exchequer Ro
2 CONC lls of Scotland, ed. Burnett, vol. x. p. 1, with which cf. Accounts of the Lord High Treasurer, Scotland, ed. Dickson, vol. i. pp. cii–civ); William was living in March 1502–3; and John was ancestor of the Drummonds of Innerpeffra
2 CONC y and of Riccarton. Of the daughters, Margaret [q. v.], mistress of James IV, was poisoned in 1501; '''Elizabeth married George, master of Angus, and was great-grandmother''' of Henry, lord Darnley; Beatrix married James, first ea
2 CONC rl of Arran; Annabella married William, first earl of Montrose; Eupheme, the wife of John, fourth lord Fleming, was poisoned in 1501; and Sibylla shared a like fate. Drummond was the common ancestor of the viscounts of Strathallan
2 CONC  and of the earls of Perth and Melfort.
2 CONT [Douglas's Peerage of Scotland (Wood), ii. 360–1; Malcolm's Memoir of the House of Drummond, pp. 67–86; Registrum Magni Sigilli Regum Scotorum (Paul), 1424–1513, (Paul and Thomson) 1513–46; Exchequer Rolls of Scotland (Burnett), v
2 CONC ols. vii–x.; Accounts of the Lord High Treasurer, Scotland (Dickson), vol. i.; Cal. State Papers, Scottish Ser. (1509–89), p. 1; Letters and Papers of Hen. VIII (Brewer), 1509–16.]
2 CONT G. G.
2 CONT From: http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Drummond,_John_(d.1519)_(DNB00) 
2 CONT ____________
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Drummond of Glamis is my/our 16th great grandmother.
2 CONT Janet Milburn 4/28/22
2 CONT
