1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===EUPHEMIA ELPHINESTONE (aka EUFAMIAM ELPHINSTOUN)===
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphemia Elphinestone is the daughter of Alexander, Lord Elphinestone, and his wife Elizabeth Barlow. She was born on 11 May 1509. [https://archive.org/stream/elphinstonefamil01fras#page/50/mode/1up The Elphinestone Family Book, i
2 CONC , p. 50] The place of her birth is not mentioned in the Elphinestone Family Book  [https://archive.org/stream/elphinstonefamil01fras#page/50/mode/1up Ibidem] and Sir James Balfour Paul appears to have been unaware of it.  [https:/
2 CONC /archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefou02paulgoog#page/n550/mode/1up The Scots Peerage, iii, p. 531] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Bastard Son===
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphemia Elphinstone had a bastard son named Robert Stewart by James V, King of Scots. He and his mother are mentioned in an entail in a charter by which his father confirmed that he had given the lands of Temptalloun and others t
2 CONC o his bastard son James by Margaret Erskine: "Rob. S. ejus fratri, filio naturali inter regem et Eufamiam Elphinstoun procreato." The charter was issued under the Great Seal of Scotland on 31 August 1536. [https://archive.org/stre
2 CONC am/registrummagnisi03scot#page/360/mode/1up Register of the Great Seal of Scotland, A.D. 1513-1546, charter number 1620 on pp. 360-61]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===First Marriage===
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the birth of her son Robert in the Spring of 1533, Euphemia Elphinestone was married to John Bruce of Cultmalindie with whom she had issue. [https://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefou02paulgoog#page/n550/mode/1up The Sco
2 CONC ts Peerage, iii, p. 531] The marriage contract, between Alexander, Lord Elphinestone, her brother, and John Bruce of Cultmalindie, her future husband, was signed at Elphinestone on 13 April 1540. [https://archive.org/stream/elphin
2 CONC stonefamil01fras#page/82/mode/1up The Elphinestone Family Book, i, p. 83]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Second Marriage===
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Francis J. Grant, following the death of her husband in March 1547, Euphemia Elphinestone married for a second time to John Bruce of Kennet but he offered no proof.  [https://archive.org/stream/TheCountyFamiliesOfTheS
2 CONC hetlandIslands/The%20County%20Families%20of%20the%20Shetland%20Islands#page/n12/mode/1up The County Families of the Shetland Islands]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biographical Accounts===
2 CONT
2 CONT # [https://archive.org/stream/elphinstonefamil01fras#page/82/mode/1up The Elphinestone Family Book, i, p. 83]
2 CONT # [http://www.undiscoveredscotland.co.uk/usbiography/e/euphemiaelphinstone.html Undiscovered Scotland: Euphemia Elphinstone]
2 CONT # [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Elphinstone-11 Wikipedia: Euphemia Elphinstone]
2 CONT # [http://www.thepeerage.com/p10834.htm#i108337 Darryl Ludy's Peerage: Euphemia Elphinstone]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biographical Account by Wikipedia===
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphemia Elphinstone (also written Euphame or Eupheme; 11 May 1509 – either 1542 or after 1547) was a mistress of James V of Scotland and the mother of his son Robert Stewart, 1st Earl of Orkney, born in 1532, as well as another r
2 CONC oyal bastard who died in childhood. One of her sons with her husband John Bruce was Laurence Bruce of Cultmalindie (1547–1617), the builder of Muness Castle. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euphemia_Elphinstone
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the second daughter of Alexander Elphinstone (born c. 1480, died 9 September 1513, at the Battle of Flodden Field), created Lord Elphinstone in 1510, and Elizabeth Barlow (Berclay) of Aberdeenshire (c. 1476 – 10 September 
2 CONC 1518). The family hailed from Elphinstone in Stirling. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euphemia_Elphinstone
2 CONT
2 CONT Her brother Alexander, Lord Elphinstone, died at Edinburgh following the Battle of Pinkie Cleugh, and is the ancestor of the Elphinstone family. Euphemia married John Bruce of Cultmalindie (c. 1507 – March 1546/7), a descendant of
2 CONC  Robert the Bruce, and had five children: Laurence Bruce, Robert Bruce (born c. 1536), Henry Bruce (born c. 1538), James Bruce (born c. 1540) and Euphamie Bruce (born c. 1542). She died either in 1542, or after 1547: there is no f
2 CONC irm evidence for any death date.https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euphemia_Elphinstone
2 CONT
2 CONT Euphemia appears to have been, in her own right, rather unimportant: the daughter of a minor noble, she married a minor member of the Bruce family, and many consider her to be only a passing fancy to James V (although if she conce
2 CONC ived by him on two separate occasions then this would appear slightly misleading). However, her children and descendants were to be important in the history of the Shetland Islands and Orkney Islands. Her two most significant chil
2 CONC dren, Laurence Bruce and Robert Stewart and their families were to become closely entwined in the Shetland Islands, of which Laurence Bruce was appointed Sheriff by his half-brother. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euphemia_Elphinst
2 CONC one
2 CONT
2 CONT According to legend, many people in the Shetland Islands are descended from her; in particular, those named Bruce are supposed to be descended from her children by John Bruce. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euphemia_Elphinstone
