1 NOTE {geni:about_me} AI generated info 5/13/26
2 CONT
2 CONT John de Ness (also recorded as John de la Glen) was a medieval noble recognized in Scottish genealogical history as the father of Sir Richard de Glen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Historical Identity
2 CONT
2 CONT Name Variations: John de la Glen, John de Ness, or John de la Glen (de Ness).
2 CONT
2 CONT Origin and Lineage: Born around 1185 in Shrewsbury, England, he was part of the Anglo-Norman de Ness (or Nase) family lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Glen Connection: His family held land under the High Stewards of Scotland. His descendants formally adopted the territorial surname "de Glen" from the Lordship of Glen in Renfrewshire, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Scotland.Notable Offspring: His son, Sir Richard de Glen, became the Lord of the Glen and served as a subordinate lord to the High Steward.Lineal HeritageHistorical charters (such as the 1292 land confirmations) link John de Ness'
2 CONC s family to earlier regional predecessors named Robert Nase and Cubinus. Through subsequent generations, his direct descendants became prominent figures in Scottish history, eventually expanding their holdings to Balmuto in Fife a
2 CONC nd marrying into families close to the Scottish Crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT - - -
2 CONT
2 CONT AI Overview         Based on records, John de Ness (often referred to as John de la Glen or John de Glen, active c. 1200–1230) is associated with the Scottish Glen family originating from Shropshire, England. He is frequently iden
2 CONC tified as the father of Richard de le Glen (Laird of the Glen), rather than being the same person as his descendant, John de le Glen who died in 1292.
2 CONT
2 CONT Key Familial Details
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: Various genealogies, such as those on Geni.com, suggest his father was Richard Henry de Ness and '''his mother was Lady Unknown de Ness.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Background: The de Ness family hailed from Ness (Ness Strange) near Shrewsbury, England, and accompanied Walter Fitz Alan to Scotland, with Henry de Ness holding lands under the Steward in the lordship of Glen around 1180.Identity
2 CONC : John de Ness is, in some records, identified as the Constable of Dunoon, who was seized of lands in Kildun.Descendants: He is the progenitor of the Scottish Glen family, with his descendant John de le Glen (c. 1275–1292) holding
2 CONC  lands at Lochwinnoc in Renfrewshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: There are multiple, distinct individuals named John de Glen/de Ness across the 13th and 14th centuries in these records, which can cause confusion regarding exact parentage between different generations.
