2 CONT 1.  Stammtafeln says that Isobel was Robert's mother as does the Complete Peerage vol.V,p736 chart, another source showed Christina as Roberts mother, but Royal and Noble source lost a note of which it was. Weir does not show Chri
2 CONC stina as offspring. Unknown GEDCOM info: MH:N244 Unknown GEDCOM info: BA5176E6-058C-4CD6-917F-4A7D3549B20B
1 NOTE In 1251, on the death of his Mother, he did homage to Henry III for her lands in England. He was made Sheriff of Cumberland and Constable of Carlisle. His claim to the crown based on a verbal nomination (tanistry) by Alexander III
2 CONC . In 1290, on the death of the Maid of Norway, he and John BALIOL claimed the succession. The arbitrator, King Edward I of England, decided in favour of John BALIOL. To avoid swearing fealty to his rival, Robert resigned Annandale
2 CONC  to his eldest son. {Burke�s Peerage and Chamber�s Biographical Dictionary} [GADD.GED]
2 CONT Nominated heir presumptive to the crown in 1238, Competitor to the crown 1292
2 CONT Died 1294
1 NOTE He laid claim to the crown of Scotland 1291-1292. He claimed that he had been nominated by Alexander III if Margaret, the Maid of Norway, failed to produce a child. He worked with King Edward I of England in the hopes it would fac
2 CONC iitate his crowning. However, Edward chose John Baliol instead because he though Baliol would be easier to control. As a result, Bruce took the side of King Edward, when Baliol rose in 1296. Still, he did not receive the crown he 
2 CONC expected. Bruce then went against the wishes of his father and joined the revolt, which ended in defeat at Falkirk in 1298. The end result was that it left two ment in joint guardianship in Scotland, Robert Bruce and John Comyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert murdered John Comyn in Greyfriars Church in Dumfries in February 1306. The Pope excommunicated him for sacrilege and Bruce had to fight the Baliol factions of which Comyn had been leader. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was crowned King of Scots in March or April 1306, but lost two battles after which he went into hiding. He was exhausted from the continuing battles and in despair and grief as his family had been hunted down and killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a Scottish legend that Bruce was inspired to continue the struggle against the English while hiding out in a cave. He watched a spider trying to swing across the ceiling. The spider kept trying to swing, over and over aga
2 CONC in, until at last he reached the other side and was able to anchor his web there. Bruce adopted the motto "Try, try, and try again" and spent the next eight years fighting the Engish occupiers of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bruce is remembered most for finishing the 30 year war of Independence by finally driving the English out of Scotland. Bruce defeated a huge army led by King Edward II at Stirling. He defeated an army of 20,000 men with his 6,000.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was accepted by the Pope as King of the Scots and his excommunication was lifted.
2 CONT
2 CONT His heart was carried in the Crusades and brought back to Scotland to be buried in Melrose Abbey.
1 NOTE He laid claim to the crown of Scotland 1291-1292. He claimed that he had been nominated by Alexander III if Margaret, the Maid of Norway, failed to produce a child. He worked with King Edward I of England in the hopes it would fac
2 CONC iitate his crowning. However, Edward chose John Baliol instead because he though Baliol would be easier to control. As a result, Bruce took the side of King Edward, when Baliol rose in 1296. Still, he did not receive the crown he 
2 CONC expected. Bruce then went against the wishes of his father and joined the revolt, which ended in defeat at Falkirk in 1298. The end result was that it left two ment in joint guardianship in Scotland, Robert Bruce and John Comyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert murdered John Comyn in Greyfriars Church in Dumfries in February 1306. The Pope excommunicated him for sacrilege and Bruce had to fight the Baliol factions of which Comyn had been leader. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was crowned King of Scots in March or April 1306, but lost two battles after which he went into hiding. He was exhausted from the continuing battles and in despair and grief as his family had been hunted down and killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a Scottish legend that Bruce was inspired to continue the struggle against the English while hiding out in a cave. He watched a spider trying to swing across the ceiling. The spider kept trying to swing, over and over aga
2 CONC in, until at last he reached the other side and was able to anchor his web there. Bruce adopted the motto "Try, try, and try again" and spent the next eight years fighting the Engish occupiers of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bruce is remembered most for finishing the 30 year war of Independence by finally driving the English out of Scotland. Bruce defeated a huge army led by King Edward II at Stirling. He defeated an army of 20,000 men with his 6,000.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was accepted by the Pope as King of the Scots and his excommunication was lifted.
2 CONT
2 CONT His heart was carried in the Crusades and brought back to Scotland to be buried in Melrose Abbey.
