2 CONT ----
2 CONT NEIL Campbell, son of COLIN Campbell & his wife --- (-[26 Apr 1315/1316]).  "…Nigello filio Colini Campbell…" witnessed the charter dated 17 Oct 1282 under which "Willelmus de Kynmonde et filius et heres Agnetis filie quondam Will
2 CONC elmi dicti de Ketilstoune" donated "terre de Badyndath" to Cambuskenneth priory[214].  As noted above, MacEwen highlights the separate existence of “Nicholas Campbell” and his brother “Neil Campbell”[215].  MacEwen suggests that t
2 CONC he latter was illegitimate (the basis for this suggestion has not been ascertained).  The 17 Oct 1282 charter suggests that Neil would have been about the same age as his brother Nicholas.  However, Neil is not recorded again in t
2 CONC he sources for many years.  One explanation for the long gap could be that the earlier Neil died and was later replaced by another son who was given the same name, maybe born to a different mother.  Neil’s absence from the Ragman 
2 CONC Roll may be explained by his youth, which would be consistent with this hypothesis of two sons being named Neil. King Edward I granted the custody of lands in Cumberland of the heir of "Sir Hubert de Multon...also letters-patent e
2 CONC mpowering Margaret, Hubert’s widow, to marry him if she will" 13 Feb 1302 (N.S.) but revoked the grant before 8 Jan 1304 (N.S.) when the custody was with “Robert de Langeton”[216].  "Chevaliers...Neel Cambel..." is named in a roll
2 CONC  of magnates who served under King Edward I at the siege of Stirling, dated 1303[217].  Neil is named in the documents dated early Nov 1303 quoted below under his [second] wife.  He defected from the English king to join Robert Br
2 CONC uce some time in [1304/early 1306] as confirmed by an order dated 20 Jun 1306 which granted custody of the Multon heir to “Robert de Cardoill...[in] the king’s hands by reason of Nigel Cambel, to whom the said custody was formerly
2 CONC  granted, having betaken himself to Scotland against the king”[218].  The Ane accompt records details of the career of “Sir Neill Campbell” in service to the Scottish king which have been unverified elsewhere[219].  Safe conduct w
2 CONC as granted 21 Aug 1309 to "John de Menteth and Nigel Cambel knights, the Scottish envoys coming to treat with Richard de Burgo earl of Ulster"[220].  Robertson indexes an undated charter under which Robert I King of Scotland grant
2 CONC ed "all the lands quhilks were David Earl of Athole’s" to "Sir Neill Campbell and Mary his spouse sister to the king and John their son"[221].  Balfour Paul records that Neil “was one of the great Barons in the Parliament that met
2 CONC  at Ayr 26 Apr 1315, when the succession to the crown of Scotland was settled” and says that “Sir Neil is said to have died before 1316” (no sources cited)[222]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly --- (-before Dec 1301).  The name of Neil’s first wife is not known, but the fact of this first marriage is confirmed by the likely birth date of his eldest son Colin before [1295/1300?] as shown below.  Her early death 
2 CONC is confirmed by the king’s licence dated 13 Dec 1301 for "Margaret late the wife of…Hubert de Multon" to marry "Nigel Cambel, if she will", at the same time as the grant of custody of the heir of the deceased to Neil Campbell[223]
2 CONC .  The Ane accompt says that “this Sir Neill Campbell” married “also Margaret daughter of Sir John Cameron of whom he had a son called Dugald Campbell” (see below)[224].  This statement has not been verified, but the Ane accompt i
2 CONC s clearly confused about Neil’s family as it also says that Mary Bruce was mother of his son Colin which is chronologically impossible.  
2 CONT
2 CONT [m secondly ([Nov 1303] [annulled?]) --- de Crawford, daughter of ANDREW de Crawford & his wife ---.  A document dated early Nov 1303 records "Robert de Kethe" requesting the king to confirm his holding “the ward and marriage of t
2 CONC he two daughters of Andrew de Crawford”, which he had bought from John Balliol King of Scotland, but which was endorsed “the king gave it to Sir Nigel Cambel” while another attached document records “the said Robert prosecuted his
2 CONC  petition” and the king appointing auditors who “prohibited Nigel from marrying the younger daughter till it should be discussed by the Council...but Nigel having married thereafter, was called before the Council for contempt” whi
2 CONC ch he defended[225].  It is not known whether this supposed marriage was annulled after the hearing before the Council.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT m [thirdly] ([1305] or [1313]) as her first husband, MARY de Bruce, daughter of ROBERT de Brus Lord of Annandale & his first wife Margaret Ctss of Carrick ([1280/83?]-before 22 Sep 1323).  Follow her hyperlink for sources which re
2 CONC cord her imprisonment, firstly at Roxburgh, later at Newcastle-upon-Tyne.  It is assumed that Mary’s first marriage took place after her release from custody, but it is not impossible that she was married before her imprisonment, 
2 CONC which would date the marriage to [1305]: the career of the couple’s son John, noted below, would seem tight chronologically if he was born [1313/14].  She married secondly Alexander Fraser.  Her second marriage and her date of dea
2 CONC th are confirmed by the 22 Sep 1323 charter under which Robert I King of Scotland granted land at Auchincarnie to "Alexandro Fraser militi" and "heredibus suis inter ipsum et quondam Mariam de Brwce sponsam suam, sororem nostram"[
2 CONC 226]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Neil & his first wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         COLIN Campbell ([before 1295/1300?]-before 2 May 1343).  Robertson indexes a charter [dated 10 Feb 1315[227]] under which Robert I King of Scotland granted "the lands of Lauchaw, Ardscodniche" to "Colini fil Nigelli Cam
2 CONC pbell"[228].  This date suggests Colin’s birth some time before 1300, to his father’s first wife.  Balfour Paul records that Colin accompanied King Robert I to Ireland in 1316 and in 1334 "recovered from the English the castle of 
2 CONC Dunoon, in Cowal, for which David II rewarded him with the hereditary keepership of the castle" (citing “a charter in the possession of the Duke of Argyll is quoted by Douglas”)[229].  Balfour Paul records that Colin died "before 
2 CONC May 1343" (no source cited)[230].  m ---.  Balfour Paul states that Colin Campbell "is said to have married Helena, daughter of Sir John Mor, son of the Earl of Lennox"[231].  This is presumably based on the Ane accompt which says
2 CONC  that Colin married “Helena daughter to Sir John More whom Colvin supposes to have been the son of trhe Earle of Lennox”[232].  The accuracy of this suggestion is unknown, but the Ane accompt is clearly confused as in a later pass
2 CONC age it records that [her son] “Gillespig More or Sir Archibald” married “Isobella daughter to Sir John Lamont, called Sir John More”[233].  “Sir John Mor/More” has not been identified in the Lennox family (see the document SCOTLAN
2 CONC D, EARLS CREATED 1162-1398).  Colin & his wife had three children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         DUGAL Campbell (-after 3 Aug 1323).  Balfour Paul names Dugal Campbell as son of “Neil Campbell”[237].  The Ane accompt says that Neil married “also Margaret daughter of Sir John Cameron of whom he had a son called Duga
2 CONC ld Campbell”[238].  On the other hand, MacEwen, when highlighting the separate existence of “Nicholas Campbell” and his brother “Neil Campbell” (see above), records Mariot Campbell (wife of Colin Campbell of Lochow, see below) as 
2 CONC “great-granddaughter and sole heiress” of “Nicholas Campbell”[239].  However, the following document indicates that Dugal was in fact the son of “Neil” (assuming that the name Neil/Nigellus is correctly distinguishable in Latin fr
2 CONC om Nicholas, as discussed above under Gillespic’s wife): Robertson indexes a charter dated 3 Aug 1323 which records an agreement between "Dugallum Campbell filium Colin Campbell, militis" and "Dugallum filium Nigelli" concerning l
2 CONC and "de Ardscodniche et advocationem ecclesie de Kilmartin"[240].  m MARGARET Glasreth, daughter of --- Glasreth of Glassary & his wife ---.  Balfour Paul states that Dugal Campbell, son of Colin Campbell, "married Margaret, siste
2 CONC r of John Glasreth of Glassary, and received with her lands in Glassry"[241].  It seems that Balfour Paul has incorrectly identified her husband’s father: MacEwen confirms that Mariot Campbell (wife of Colin Campbell of Lochow, se
2 CONC e below) was “heiress of Menstrie and Ardskeodnish as well as of one third of Glassary (through her paternal grandmother)”, presumably indicating Margaret Glasreth[242].  Dugal & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Neil & his [third] wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.         JOHN Campbell ([1305/06] or [1313/14]-killed in battle Halidon Hill 19 Jul 1333).  Robertson indexes an undated charter under which Robert I King of Scotland granted "all the lands quhilks were David Earl of Athole’s" t
2 CONC o "Sir Neill Campbell and Mary his spouse sister to the king and John their son"[248].  His alternative birth dates are estimated from the possible marriage dates of his parents (see above), although if he was born in [1313/14] he
2 CONC  would have been young for marriage in [1329].  Balfour Paul indicates that John succeeded his parents in the lands of Atholl, adding that "there can be no doubt he held the title of Earl of Atholl" based on his undated charter gr
2 CONC anting “the lands of Ballandro and Inverbervy, in the sheriffdom of the Mearns or Kincardine” to “Sir Roger Mortimer”, later confirmed by charter dated 31 Jan [1360][249].  m ([1329]) as her second husband, JOAN Menteith, widow of
2 CONC  MALISE Earl of Strathearn, daughter of JOHN Menteith of Rusky & his wife --- (-after 20 Mar 1367).  The Complete Peerage records her first marriage[250].  Her second marriage is confirmed by a Papal dispensation dated 11 Jul 1339
2 CONC  for her third marriage, which notes that it was required because of the relationship between her second and third husbands[251].  She married thirdly (Papal dispensation 11 Jul 1339) Maurice Moray Earl of Strathearn, and fourthly
2 CONC  ([Nov 1346/Oct 1347], post-nuptial dispensation 9 Nov 1347[252]) as his second wife, William [IV] Sutherland Earl of Sutherland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY%20UNTITLED.htm#NeilCampbelldied1316
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Niall mac Cailein (died 1316) also known as Sir Neil Campbell was a nobleman and warrior who spent his life in the service of King Robert I of Scotland, His Gaelic name means "Niall, Colin's son" since he was the son of Cailean Mó
2 CONC r. His services to the king elevated the Campbells into the higher ranks of the Scottish nobility. Master Niall 
2 CONT
2 CONT By later Campbell tradition, Niall was the elder son of Cailean Mór; however, contemporary evidence seems to suggest that his brother Domhnall enjoyed this distinction. Niall's earliest appearance in the sources occurs in 1282 on 
2 CONC a witness list to a royal charter in favour of Cambuskenneth Abbey. Niall disappears for 20 years, unless the "Master Niall" active in the service of the then Earl of Carrick, Robert, in the 1290s can be identified with Niall mac 
2 CONC Cailein. This seems likely, because one official source styles him Mestre Neel Cambell. Another of the sources for "Master Niall" tells us that he came from the "county of Ayr"; this would tie in with the known background of the C
2 CONC ampbells of the era, and with Niall's later affiliation with King Robert. In 1293, Niall was sent to Norway to deliver personal items to Robert's sister, Isabella Bruce, Queen of Norway. In 1296, this Master Niall swore fealty to 
2 CONC King Edward I of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Wars of Independence'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Niall mac Cailein who appears again in the source in 1302 was still in the service of the English crown. Until 1306, he remained on the side of the officially Bruce-backed English regime. Niall served in the warband of Richard
2 CONC  Og de Burgh, 2nd Earl of Ulster and in the "English" army which besieged Stirling Castle in 1305. Niall and his brother Domhnall were rewarded for their services. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1302, Niall was given lands in Cumberland. In the same year, Niall and his brother Domhnall received the guardianship of the heiresses of Andrew de Crawford, lord of the Baronies of Loudoun, Lochmartnaham and Draffan. However, 
2 CONC Niall and Domhnall, like their lord the Earl of Carrick, were drifting towards rebellion.  Niall was at Westminster in 1305, because his rights were being challenged by a knight called Robert Keith. In Spring 1305, Edward decided 
2 CONC that in favour of Keith, judging "to allow [Keith] to have these children and to distrain Sir Dovenald Chambel and Sir Nel Chambel by their lands and bodies". In the same year, Edward granted some Campbell lands to an English knig
2 CONC ht, Sir John Dovedale. Such judgments were both a cause and effect of deteriorating relations with the English crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT So when Robert decided to rebel in 1306, it is not surprising that Niall and Domhnall were among the would-be king's first adherents. Niall was present at Scone in March 1306 when Robert was crowned King of Scots. After the defeat
2 CONC s Robert suffered at the Battle of Methven and Battle of Dalrigh, Niall was one of the few men who stuck by Robert, as John Barbour testified later in the century. All the evidence suggests that Niall remained in Robert's warband 
2 CONC for the years to come, fighting both the English-side generally and the MacDougalls in the west of Scotland. Niall even acted as a representative of Robert in negotiations with the English crown, on two occasions, in 1309 and 1314
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage and family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT As a reward for his loyal services to King Robert, Niall received his sister, Mary Bruce, in marriage. By this marriage, Niall and Mary parented a son, Iain. Robert granted the couple the lands confiscated from David Strathbogie, 
2 CONC almost certainly so that Iain would eventually become the Earl, which is indeed what happened. This was part of a general policy by Robert of redistributing lands and titles to his close king. Niall, however, had been married prev
2 CONC iously, to an unknown woman, by whom he had at least two sons, Cailean and Dubhghall. In 1315, King Robert granted the barnonies of Loch Awe and Artscotnish to Cailean for the service of a 40-oared galley for 40 days per annum. Th
2 CONC is grant, in the view of the most recent historian of the subject, is the real beginning of the Campbell lordship of Lochawe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Niall probably died in 1316, leaving a strong legacy of heroism and royal favour, from which his offspring would benefit enormously.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Neil Campbell of Lochow was the son of Sir Colin Campbell of Lochow. He married, firstly, unknown daughter Crawford, daughter of Andrew Crawford, before 1303.2  He married, secondly, Margaret Cameron between 1303 and 1312. He 
2 CONC married, thirdly, Lady Mary Bruce, daughter of Sir Robert le Brus, 1st Lord Brus and Margaret, Countess of Carrick, circa 1312. He died circa 1316
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Neil Campbell of Lochow was also known as Nigel Campbell. He was invested as a Knight circa 1285. In 1296 he joined Robert the Bruce in the struggle against the English. Robert the Bruce granted him, his (third) wife and their
2 CONC  son John, all of the lands of David de Strathbogie, 11th Earl of Atholl.2 He lived at Lochow, Argyllshire, Scotland. On 26 April 1315 he was one of the great Barons of Parliament which met to fix the succession to the Crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT Neil (or Nigel) Campbell of Lochow, Knight, joined Robert Bruce in 1296 and fought for him in almost every action between Methven and Bannockburn.  He married 1st ca.1303 the daughter of Andrew Crawford, whose wardship had been gr
2 CONC anted to him by King Edward I, and had with other issue: Colin, his heir He married 2nd (it has been said) Margaret Cameron, possibly the widow of Hubert de Multon, and had by her issue: Duncan Campbell Macdhonnachie of Inverawe, 
2 CONC progenitor of the Campbells of Inverawe He married 3rd, ca 1312, Mary, sister of King Robert I, daughter of Robert Bruce, Lord of Annandale, and Earl of Carrick (in right of his wife),
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT He joined Robert Bruce in 1296 and fought for him in almost every action between Methven and Bannockburn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Neil appears in a charter to the abbey of Cambuskenneth on 17 Oct. 1282. Niall was active in the service of the then Robert, Earl of Carrick in the 1290's. In 1293 Niall was sent to Norway to deliver personal items to Robert's sis
2 CONC ter, Isabella Bruce, Queen of Norway. He swore allegiance to King Edward at Berwick 28 Aug. 1296 and on 10 Sept. he is mentioned as one of the Barons of Argyll and the King's bailee for Lochow and Ardskeodnish. He was a witness to
2 CONC  a charter confirmed by Edward I on 10 Oct. 1296.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was one of the Earl of Ulster's knights when he brought over a contingent from Ireland in 1303 to help King Edward who granted him the ward and marriage of the two daughters of Andrew Crawford. He obtained from Edward a grant 1
2 CONC 3 Feb. 1301/2 of the custody of land in Cumberland belonging to the heir of Sir Hubert de Multon until the heir becomes of age an also letters patent empowering Hubert's widow Margaret to marry him if she will, however by 8 Jan. 1
2 CONC 304 the King had taken back his gift and he gives John de Dovedale a charter of Neil's lands and the ward and marriage of his son which was confirmed at Westminster 24 Oct. 1305. This unusual procedure was probably due to the fact
2 CONC  that Neil had joined Robert the Bruce as on 20 June 1306 it is stated that the ward and marriage of Hubert de Multon's heir is in the King's gift "by reason of Nigel Cambel to whom the said custody was formerly granted having bet
2 CONC aken himself to Scotland against the King".
2 CONT
2 CONT Neil was a staunch adherant of Robert the Bruce and fought with him in almost every battle from the battle of Methven to Bannockburn. He was present at Scone in March 1306 when Robert was crowned King of Scots. After the defeats a
2 CONC t the Battle of Methven and the Battle of Dalrigh, Niall was one of the few knights who stuck by King Robert fighting against the English as well as the MacDougalls in the west of Scotland. He was part of the envoy to Richard de B
2 CONC urgo, Earl of Ulster who had been appointed Edward's commissioner to treat with Robert for terms of peace for which he obtained a safe conduct from Edward 21 Aug. 1309. After Bannockburn, Neil was one of the Commissioners sent to 
2 CONC York in Sept. 1314 to negotiate a peace with the English. He was one of the great Barons in the Parliament at Ayr 26 Apr. 1315.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ref The Life of His GRACE John Duke of ARGYLE etc – 1765 by Robt Campbell'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Neil .....was knighted in the latter end of the reign of K. Alexander III and was in the same commission with his Father in the fore-mention'd Arbitration, and so far acquiesced in the Decision of Edward, in favour of John de 
2 CONC Baliol, that he remained faithful to that weak and unhappy Prince, until by an inglorious Surrender of the Independency of Scotland and acknowledging to hold the Sovereignty in Vassalage of Edward of England, he had forfeited that
2 CONC  title his blood gave him, and declared himself unworthy of that Crown he was born to. Upon this emergency, and so soon as Robert de Bruce had formed a rational scheme to make good his pretensions to the Throne, this Sir Neil, who
2 CONC m Buchanan calls Sir Neil Campbell, joined heartily with those few patriots who had courage and honesty to assert the liberty and independency of that ancient kingdom, against the usurpation of the English Edward, owned King Rober
2 CONC t's title and raised a considerable number of his vassals to make head against McDougald of Lorn who had a personal pique at and was declared enemy of Robert. He soon reduced McDougald and all his adherents and brought his country
2 CONC  and al the shire of Argyle to submit to the new King. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From this action he went to Scoon and assisted at the solemnity of King Robert's Coronation, in Anno 1306; after which he continued steadfast in his loyalty and gave many signal marks of his attachment to that monarch even in the 
2 CONC lowest ebb of his fortune and when he was but yet tottering on his throne he entered into that famous association with Sir Gilbert Hay and Sir Alexander Seton; where in a most solemn manner, they bound themselves to defend with th
2 CONC eir lives and fortunes the liberty of their country and the right of Robert Bruce their king against all mortals, French, English or Scotch. To this association they appended their seals at the Abby of Cambuskeneth, Sept 8, 1308. 
2 CONT
2 CONT So early we find this family appearing in the interest of their country against the usurpation of foreigners and the weak concessions of an hereditary Prince. A spirit which seems hitherto intailed upon the successors of this grea
2 CONC t man which we hope will continue without a breach till latest ages. This Sir Neil was one of the commissioners sent from Scotland to England to treat of peace, Anno 1314, which did not take effect. The ensuing year he was one of 
2 CONC the barons of the parliament held at Air which intailed the crown upon King Robert and his heirs and in the close of that year he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT FamilySearch []https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/LY35-7MC?1=1&spouse=LZ6F-BGP
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Niall 'MacCailean Mór' Campbell, 10th Laird of Lochawe (1258-1315/6)'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Niall Mac Cailein (died 1316), also known as Neil Campbell or Nigel Campbell, was a nobleman and warrior who spent his life in the service of King Robert I of Scotland, His Gaelic name means "Niall, Colin's son" since he was t
2 CONC he son of Cailean Mór. His services to the King elevated the Campbells into the higher ranks of the Scottish nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Biography:'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Master Niall:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT By later Campbell tradition, Niall was the elder son of Cailean Mór; however, contemporary evidence seems to suggest that his brother Domhnall enjoyed this distinction.[1] Niall's earliest appearance in the sources occurs in 1282 
2 CONC on a witness list to a royal charter in favour of Cambuskenneth Abbey.[2] Niall disappears for 20 years, unless the "Master Niall" active in the service of the then Earl of Carrick, Robert, in the 1290s can be identified with Nial
2 CONC l mac Cailein. This seems likely, because one official source styles him "Mestre Neel Cambell". Another of the sources for "Master Niall" tells us that he came from the "county of Ayr"; this would tie in with the known background 
2 CONC of the Campbells of the era, and with Niall's later affiliation with King Robert. In 1293, Niall was sent to Norway to deliver personal items to Robert's sister, Isabella Bruce, Queen of Norway. In 1296, this Master Niall swore fe
2 CONC alty to King Edward I of England.[3] Issued with a safe passage through England, on 12 June 1297, to return to Scotland.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Wars of Independence:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Niall Mac Cailein who appears again in the source in 1302 was still in the service of the English crown. Until 1306, he remained on the side of the officially Bruce-backed English regime. Niall served in the warband of Richard
2 CONC  Óg de Burgh, 2nd Earl of Ulster and in the "English" army which besieged Stirling Castle in 1305.[5] Niall and his brother Domhnall were rewarded for their services. In 1302, Niall was given lands in Cumberland.[6] In the same ye
2 CONC ar, Niall and his brother Domhnall received the guardianship of the heiresses of Andrew de Crawford, lord of the Baronies of Loudoun, Lochmartnaham and Draffan. However, Niall and Domhnall, like their lord the Earl of Carrick, wer
2 CONC e drifting towards renewing their war against the English conquest. Niall was at Westminster in 1305, because his rights were being challenged by a knight called Robert Keith. In Spring 1305, Edward decided in favour of Keith, jud
2 CONC ging "to allow [Keith] to have these children and to distrain Sir Dovenald Chambel and Sir Nel Chambel by their lands and bodies".[7] In the same year, Edward granted some Campbell lands to an English knight, Sir John Dovedale. Su
2 CONC ch judgments were both a cause and effect of deteriorating relations with the English crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Robert de Bruce decided to raise the Scottish banner in 1306, it is not surprising that Niall and Domhnall were among the would-be king's first adherents. Niall was present at Scone in March 1306 when Robert was crowned King 
2 CONC of Scots. After the defeats King Robert suffered at the Battle of Methven and Battle of Dalrigh, Niall was one of the men who remained faithful, as John Barbour testified later in the century.[8] All the evidence suggests that Nia
2 CONC ll remained in King Robert's warband for the years to come, fighting both the English-side generally and the MacDougalls in the west of Scotland. Niall also acted as a representative of King Robert in negotiations with the English
2 CONC  crown, on two occasions, in 1309 and 1314.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage and Family:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Niall married Robert de Bruce's sister, Mary Bruce. The date of their marriage is unknown. Niall and Mary had a son, Iain. King Robert granted the couple the lands confiscated from David Strathbogie, almost certainly so that Iain 
2 CONC would eventually become the Earl, which is indeed what happened. This was part of a general policy by Robert of redistributing lands and titles to his extended kin. Niall, however, had been married previously to Alyse de Crawford,
2 CONC [9] by whom he had at least two sons, Sir Colin Og Campbell of Lochawe and Dubhghall. In 1315, King Robert granted the baronies of Loch Awe and Ardscotnish to Cailean for the service of a 40-oared galley for 40 days per annum. Thi
2 CONC s grant, in the view of the most recent historian of the subject, is the real beginning of the Campbell lordship of Lochawe.[10]. In 1326, King Robert created the post of sheriff of Argyll, and granted it to Niall's son, Dougall.
2 CONT
2 CONT Niall probably died in 1316, leaving a strong legacy of heroism and royal favour, from which his offspring would benefit enormously.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT # Stephen Boardman, The Campbells, 1250-1513, (Edinburgh, 2006), p.21
