1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==brief biography and family==
2 CONT From an unidentified source:
2 CONT
2 CONT ><This> Sir John Oliphant of Aberdalgie and Kellie, son of the last, is mentioned in 1388. He married, first, a daughter of Borthwick de eodem, by whom he had William, who succeeded him in the Perthshire estates; he married, secon
2 CONC d, a daughter of Home de eodem, by whom he had Thomas, the first of the house of Kellie.  (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==sources==
2 CONT * ''The East Neuk of Fife: its history and antiquities'' (Google eBook).  Walter Wood.  D. Douglas, 1887 - Fife (Scotland) - 586 pages.  [https://books.google.com/books?id=B6UHAAAAQAAJ&ots=0LFApx0zB1&pg=PA249#v=onepage&q&f=false P
2 CONC age 249], [https://books.google.com/books?id=BvEvAAAAMAAJ&lpg=PA259&ots=0LFApx0zB1&dq=John%20Oliphant%20of%20kellie&pg=PA258#v=onepage&q=John%20Oliphant%20of%20kellie&f=false Pp 258-259]
2 CONT * ''A genealogical and heraldic History of the Commoners of Great ...'', Volume 1 By John Burke.  [https://books.google.com/books?id=uo9AAAAAcAAJ&lpg=PA494&ots=3tGXwgMRDs&dq=sir%20John%20Oliphant%20of%20Kelly&pg=PA494#v=onepage&q=
2 CONC sir%20John%20Oliphant%20of%20Kelly&f=false Page 494]
2 CONT * Garty, Lisa Jayne. “The Dynastic and Architectural Development of Kellie, Fife, c. 1150-c. 1613.”  University of St Andrews - St Andrews Research Repository.   (30 September 1992) < [https://research-repository.st-andrews.ac.uk/
2 CONC bitstream/handle/10023/22488/LisaGartyMThesis1993_original_C.pdf?sequence=1&isAllowed=y PDF] > page 23-24.  “Joseph Anderson's work ''The Oliphants in Scotland'' (1879) has been the foundation for much of the subsequent research o
2 CONC n the Oliphant family - including Wood's ''East Neuk of Fife'' (1887). In its turn Lorimer's ''Red Book'' and his genealogy of the Oliphants of Kellie relied heavily on both works.  … This Sir John, we are told, married first a da
2 CONC ughter of Sir William Borthwick, producing a son and heir William, and secondly a daughter of Sir Thomas Home of Home by whom he had Thomas, "from whom are derived the Oliphants of Kellie".(8) From this point of departure Anderson
2 CONC  continues with the senior line of the family - giving details of William Oliphant, his son Sir John II, and the successive lords Oliphant.(9) … page 27-28.  “… although they contain some sustainable evidence, it would seem pruden
2 CONC t to err on the side of caution when using these seventeenth and eighteenth century genealogies as a source for the early descent of the Oliphant family and the origin of the Oliphants of Kellie.” … “ Whatever the exact relationsh
2 CONC ip between John I and Walter Oliphant of Kellie, we may conclude that the two families diverged c.1388-99 and the nineteenth and that both the seventeenth and eighteenth century genealogies century works, are erroneous in naming t
2 CONC he first Oliphant of Kellie as Thomas. From this point of departure the Oliphants of Kellie became a secondary branch of the family and a line of succession can be reconstructed from the surviving documentary evidence and this is 
2 CONC represented in Appendix 1.” (Appendix One: Family Tree, follows page 103, or PDF Page 113.)
2 CONT
