3 CTRY Scotland
1 OCCU Appointed Governor of the Castle of Dumbarton by King James I
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===SIR JOHN COLQUHOUN OF LUSS===
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sometime Governor of Dumbarton Castle===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir John Colquhoun of Luss is the second son of Sir Humphrey Colquhoun of Luss. The name of his mother is not known. He succeeded his older brother Robert in or before 1408. Sir John Colquhoun of Luss was murdered on the island
2 CONC  of Inchmurrin in Loch Lomond on 24 September 1439, where he had gone to treat with Lachlan Maclean and his followers. He married Lady Jean Erskine, daughter of Robert, Lord Erskine, and co-heir with Lyle of Duchal of the ancient 
2 CONC Earls of Mar. They had two children who follow. [https://archive.org/stream/chiefsofcolquhou01fras#page/25/mode/1up The Chiefs of Colquhoun and their Country, i, pp. 25-32]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children===
2 CONT
2 CONT # https://www.geni.com/people/Malcolm-Colquhoun/6000000002979906210?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1478884806901&through=6000000005062819077
2 CONT # https://www.geni.com/people/Isabel-Colquhoun/6000000003714021236?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1478884864216&through=6000000005062819077
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Genealogical Accounts===
2 CONT
2 CONT # [https://archive.org/stream/chiefsofcolquhou01fras#page/n6/mode/1up The Chiefs of Colquhoun and their Country (Ednibugh, 1869), i, 416 pp.]
2 CONT # [https://archive.org/stream/chiefsofcolquhv200fras#page/n4/mode/1up The Chiefs of Colquhoun and their Country (Edinburgh, 1869), ii, 442 pp.]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Dumbarton Castle'''
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dumbarton_Castle#cite_note-5 Wikipedia] we find this reference to Sir John in reference to Dumbarton Castle: "In 1425 the castle was attacked by James the Fat, youngest son of Murdoch Stewart, Du
2 CONC ke of Albany, who had been imprisoned by King James I of Scotland on charges of treason. James the Fat became a rallying point for enemies of the King, and raised a large rebellion against the crown. He marched on the town of Dumb
2 CONC arton and burned it, but was unable to take the castle, whose defender John Colquhoun successfully held out against James' [the Fat's] men."
2 CONT
2 CONT Another resource that shows the location of the castle and of others in the area is [http://www.stravaiging.com/history/castle/dumbarton-castle Stravaiging.com]
