2 TITL Our Royal, Titled, Noble, and Commoner Ancestors & Cousins - Person Page - 143
1 NOTE Sir John Colquhoun was appointed governor of the castle of Dumbarton, by King James I, for his fidelity to that king during his imprisonment in England. From his activity in punishing the depredations of the Highlanders, who often
2 CONC  committed great outrages in the low country of Dumbartonshire, he rendered himself obnoxious to them, and a plot was formed for his destruction. He received a civil message from some of their chiefs, desiring a friendly conferenc
2 CONC e, in order to accommodate all their differences. Suspecting no treachery, he went out to meet them but slightly attended, and was immediately attacked by a numerous body of Islanders, under two noted robber-chiefs, Lachlan Maclea
2 CONC n and Murdoch Gibson, and slain in Inchmurren, on Loch Lommond, in 1439.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sheriff of Dumbarton Castle in 1427. Governer of Dumbarton Castle in the minority reign of King James II. He was lured into ambush at Inchmurrin on Sept 24th , 1439 and was assassinated, along with 120 of his men. Thus ending his 
2 CONC term of office as Head of the Clan Colquhoun and of Luss
2 CONT
2 CONT The clan chieftain's coat of arms originated with the exploits of Sir John. The coat of arms is connected with the Cross saltire of the Earls of Lennox But the chief's heraldic supporters of the two greyhounds together with their 
2 CONC crest of a red stags's head and motto "Si je puis" are linked to and old tale. The story goes that the king asked the Colquhoun chief to recover Dumbarton Castle from his enemies. The Gaelic-speaking chief replied thus in the Norm
2 CONC an-French fashionable in Court at the time, and then pursued a stag with his hounds past the castle gates. The garrison opened the gates tojoin in the chase, whereupon his clansmen rushed the castle and captured it for the king. T
2 CONC he legend may well be true, for in 1424, at the height of heraldry, King James I decided to overthrow the too-powerful  Lennox family and chose John Colquhoun to be his governor of Dumbarton Castle and wrest it from them.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Colquhouns went to war, their chief dipped a charred wooden cross in goat's blood and sent this fiery cross by relay throughout  the entire district of Luss. The bearer shouted out the name of the fathering place as he pa
2 CONC ssed. The muster place was usually at Cnoc Elachan, the armoury hill near Rossdhu. They were issued arms if they had none and badges of hazel, the lucky plant of the clan, to wear in their bonnets.
2 CONT "The Calhouns of Glencoe, Illinois" by Richard F. Calhoun
2 CONT
2 CONT Name Suffix:<NSFX> 4th Laird Of Luss, Sir
2 CONT Sir John Colquhoun of Luss; Governorof Dumbarton Castle during minori
2 CONT ty of James II.  Murdered by marauding Highlanders.  [Burke's Peerage]
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT --------------------------------------- --  -------------
