1 NOTE  Younger of Colquhoun and Luss, 1410-1439.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of his father's death, Malcolm was being held for the ransom of King James I and was unable to succeed to the title of chief.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fraser, in the Chiefs of Colquhoun, states that Malcolm predeceased hid Father and that his son, John, succeeded his grandfather in the estates of Colquhoun and Luss.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a charter dated 13 Nov 14333, he is designated Malcolm Colquhoun, son and heir apparent of Sir John Colquhoun of Luss. Died before 1439, v. p. Gives dates 1410-1439.
2 CONT
2 CONT Malcolm was held hostage for ransom of King James and died between 1433 and 1439 before his Father. His son , John succeeded to the chieftainship upon his grandfather's death in 1439
