2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/64/63/b2/ef/534448393b3f4848/wemyss_castle1521a_original.jpg?hash=c6e4ed6b6e8123283fc4cf5b628245bd9f56533dda25ed688148fd6d4ee93d43.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
1 NOTE Copied from Drumry history website,templum.freeserve.co.uk/history/drumry.htm:
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early 15th century, there is the first mention of St. Mary'sChapel in Drumry. At this time Robert Livingston, as the laird of Drumry,was patron of the church. He also granted lands in Fife to the Abbot ofDunfermline in 1437
2 CONC . The fortunes of the Livingstons were to rise withSir Alexander being regent to the young king but in 1449, with the regentaway in England, James II, arrested many of the prominent Livingstons andlater executed two - one of whom 
2 CONC was Robert Livingston of Drumry.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT It is peculiar that Burke's Peerage indicates the following on page 2975when noting that one of Michael Wemyss's daughter married a Livingstonand received a portion of Wemyss's estates (presumably Easter Wemyss) indowry:
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael Wemyss d 1342, leaving, with two other daughters (from whosedescendants Robert Livingston and John Boswell the Sir John Wemyss whodied c1428 bought back their share of the former Wemyss lands. [Burke'sPeerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT Which would indicate that this Robert Livingston sold Easter Wemyss backto the Wemyss family before Sir John Wemyss died in 1428. Yet at leasttwo more Livingston descendants after this are identified as "Lord ofEaster Wemyss" by B
2 CONC urke's Peerage.
