0 @N6000000005940222655@ NOTE IX-2
1 CONT of Calderwood.  See Calderwood branch, page 460
1 CONT Robert Maxwell. These lands were by Charles II., in 1667 erected into the Barony of Calderwood, etc. Robert Maxwell was the son of Isabella Linsey, his father's first wife. He married first Elizabeth Danielson.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Castles of Scotland by Maurice Lindsay
1 CONT page 376,377
1 CONT Newark Castle - ... Thelands of Newark were originally owned by the Danielstouns of that Ilk. One of Sir Robert Danielstoun's two heiresses married Sir Robert Maxwell of Calderwood in 1402.  The Maxwells were therefore the builder
1 CONC s of the entire castle as it now stands.  James IV was a frequent visitor. Records for the year 1497 detail four shillings and eight pence for 'ane bote to fetch wine fra the schip twys, quhenshe lay at the New Werk'; and another,
1 CONC  dated the following year for six shillings to carry 'the Kingis gere...to the New Werk' from 'Dumbertone', on which rock , almost opposite, there then stood a now largely-vanished royal castle.  Sir Patrick sold the lands around 
1 CONC the castle to the magistrates of Glasgow in 1668, the name of the hamlet, Newark being changed to Port Glasgow ....
1 CONT
1 CONT page 431-432
1 CONT Stanley Castle - ...The lands of Stanley, or Stanelie, belonged to the fourteenth century to the Dennistouns (or Danielstouns) of that Ilk, but the present castle dates from its Maxwell ownership, brought about by the marriage of 
1 CONC one or two Danielstoun heiresses. The Maxwells also ownded, among others, the castles of Newark, Haggs, Pollock, Dargarvel, and Calderwood. ...
0 @N6000000005940222661@ NOTE IX-3
1 CONT of Aikenhead.  See Aikenhead branch, page 495.
0 @N6000000005940228283@ NOTE XVII-2
1 CONT of Caerlaverock, was restored and became ninth Lord Maxwell. Created Earl of Nithsdale, August 29, 1620. M. Elizabeth Beaumont. D. May, 1646. Had XVIII Robert second, Earl of Nithsdale, tenth Lord maxwell. D., unm., October 5, 166
1 CONC 7.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Castles of Scotland by Maurce Lindsay
1 CONT page 107
1 CONT Caerlaverock Castle - stands near the mouth of the River Nith seven miles south-east of Dumfries. Formerly surrounded by marshland, it was known locally as the 'Island of Caerlaverock' - and with the surging tide of the Soway as a
1 CONC n additional water defence, it must have been a formidable fortress. It is 'one of the most splendid examples of baronial architecture in Scotland', in the words of W. Douglas Simpson, which 'includes masonry of every age between 
1 CONC the thirteenth and the seventeenth centuries.' In addition, it has the most impressive gatehouse in Scotland.  There was an earlier castle, now a square mound to the south of the present building. This later structure dates from 1
1 CONC 290 and 1300. Whether it was intended to act as a bridgehead for a sea-based English invasion, or to sustain defence against such an eventuality, is uncertain. Certainly, it was besieged and taken by theEnglish not long after it w
1 CONC as completed, the event being commemorated in a poem in medieval French, Le Siege de Kalavreock, translated into English and published in 1828.  ...  Externally changes were made in the fifteenth century, when heavy machicolations
1 CONC  were added to the front and to the courtyard.  There were changes again in the seventeenth century, when in 1638, Robert, first Earl of Nithsdale, erected against the east wall one of the most sophisticated Renaissance palaces to
1 CONC  go up in Scotland.  The castle has been involved in much warlike action  Apart from the siege already mention, several of the Lords Maxwell used ita s a base from which to harry the English. At the time of the rout of the Scots a
1 CONC t Solway Moss in 1542, James V was staying at Caerlaverock. In 1545 it was forcibly delivered up to the English by Lord Maxwell, but was recovered a few months later.  The English took it again in 1572, partly destroying it.  It w
1 CONC as probably when repairing the damage done from this siege that the Earl of Nithsdale added his splended domestic range. Two years later the Convenanters, under Lieutenatn Colonel Home, besieged it again.  The Earl insisted on an 
1 CONC inventory of the contents being signed before handing it over. The Maxwells then moved their seat to Terregles, on the west side of Dumfries, later moving on to Traquair House, Peebleshire, where the present laird is their lineal 
1 CONC successor, and Caerlaverock fell into ruin. It is now and Ancient Monument.
