0 @N6000000003615380459@ NOTE On his coronation took the name of Robert III 
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert III was born in 1337 as John Stewart of Carrick. His father, Robert Stewart (1316-1390), was related within the forbidden degrees of kinship to his mother, Elizabeth Mure. John and his brothers had already been born when th
1 CONC eir parents asked the pope for a dispensation and were finally properly married in the late 1440s. The uncertainty surrounding John's legitimacy undermined his authority as King and even led to a bitter conflict between his descen
1 CONC dants and the unquestionable legitimate descendants of Robert Stewart's second marriage.
1 CONT The elder Robert Stewart was a son of Marjorie Bruce of Scotland (1297-1316). He had fought in the Scottish independence wars and on occasion he had acted as Guardian of the Realm. After the death of Marjorie's half-brother in 137
1 CONC 1, he became Robert II, King of Scots. By then, his youthful vigour had deserted him and he was nearly blind with 'red bleared eyes'. His control of the government progressively weakened until he appointed his eldest son, John of 
1 CONC Carrick, in 1384 to enforce authority on his behalf. John ruled Scotland until 1389, when he was crippled by a riding accident. Afterwards he was unable to engage in military pursuits and trusted the management of the government e
1 CONC ntirely to his brother, the ambitious Robert of Fife (1341-1420).
1 CONT
1 CONT A year after his accident John of Carrick succeeded his senile father as King of Scots. John assumed the style of "Robert III", because the Scottish King John Balliol had been a rival of King Robert I Bruce and - even worse - a va
1 CONC ssal of King Edward I of England. The new Queen was his wife, Annabella Drummond (1350-1401), who had given birth to seven children. Their eldest son, Robert, died in infancy. In 1378 David was born and a third son, James, followe
1 CONC d in 1394.  Sickly and limping John Stewart had always been a gentle, kind and warm-hearted man, but he was hesitant. He had the best of intentions and he look dignified with his long snowy beard, but he lacked the ruthlessness ne
1 CONC eded to control the Scottish nobles. On his accession he was already a chronic invalid and a depressive. Living in troublesome medieval Scotland, his invalidity undermined Robert III's authority and probably worsened his depressio
1 CONC n. Gradually he became an ailing recluse. Once, when discussing his end with his wife, Robert III asked to be buried in a dunghill, beneath the epitaph "Here lies the worst of Kings and the most miserable of men".
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon it became apparent that the ruthless regent Robert of Fife was mainly concerned with his own advancement and turbulent Scotland plunged into total disorder. Another brother of the King, the ruffianly Alexander "the Wolf of Ba
1 CONC denoch"1, was Justiciar of the North. He entertained his guests by burning down parts of the great Ruthiemurchus Forest to drive out and hunt down deer, wild boar, wolves and clansmen. In his castle on an isle of Loch Indorb, the 
1 CONC Wolf had a dark dungeon with about three feet of icy water, so that incarcerated suspects had to remain standing, or drown. If they survived a few days and nights, they were believed to be innocent. His wife had brought the Wolf t
1 CONC he Earldom of Ross, but Alexander refused to be separated from his mistress and contemplated a divorce. When he was reprimanded by the bishop of Moray in an ecclesiastical court, The Wolf took revenge in 1390 by burning down the b
1 CONC ishop's beautiful cathedral, two monasteries and much of the town of Elgin.
1 CONT A notorious tournament was held in 1396 on the North Inch of Perth as an entertainment before the King and court and vast crowds. Thirty men of the Clan Chattan fought against thirty men of another clan. They were clothed in a sho
1 CONC rt kilt and armed with sword, dirk, axe, crossbow and three arrows each. To the music of the pipes they slaughtered each other until after a long time only a dozen survived - all badly wounded. As a result of the acceptance of the
1 CONC  outcome of this judicial combat and the slaughter of so many local champions in it,  the following years the central highlands were more peaceful.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert III's eldest son, David, was a high-spirited and charming, but spoiled young man. He rivalled for power with his uncle, Robert of Fife. In 1399 the Queen persuaded her husband to proclaim their eldest son Duke of Rothesay a
1 CONC nd delegate Royal authority to him. However, David was wild and irresponsible and often lacked good judgement. He was betrothed, but repudiated the girl, turning her father into an enemy. Moreover, Robert of Fife was not so easily
1 CONC  set aside. In 1402, a few months after the Queen's death, he persuaded Robert III to order his own son's arrest. David was incarcerated in his uncle's Falkland Palace, where he died. Historians assume he succumbed to dysentery, p
1 CONC ossibly as a result of neglect, but many contemporaries believed he had actually been starved to death.
1 CONT Robert III began to fear for the fate of his only surviving son, James. In February 1406 he had James taken in secrecy to Dirleton Castle to wait for a ship to transport him to France.  Robert of Fife sent a large force after the 
1 CONC Crown Prince and when a battle was fought near-by, James was put in a rowing boat and ferried to the Bass Rock (to the right) in the Firth of Forth. The 11-year-old heir to the throne and his guardians were left for about a month 
1 CONC on the tiny, windswept, rocky island among the boiling seas, before a ship arrived to bring James to France. Robert of Fife informed the English King, who arranged the ship's interception. Thus James became a prisoner of the King 
1 CONC of England for 18 years. When Robert III heard of his son's capture, he became even more depressed. He refused any food and died within a few days. At his own request he was not buried with the other Kings at Scone but in nearby P
1 CONC aisley abbey, because he did not believe himself fit for such an honour.
1 CONT
1 CONT Copyright  1997-2000 by J.N.W. Bos. All rights reserved.
1 CONT
1 CONT " A Prince for Peace, that had for Mars no Mind,
1 CONT Abhorring Warrs and all Intestine Strife.
1 CONT Noght to be Cross'd with Kingly Cares Inclined,
1 CONT Bot loving more a calme and quiet Life:
1 CONT A King indeed, and yet in Sho bott sitts,
1 CONT For to his Brother he the Care Committs. "
1 CONT
1 CONT Bibliography
1 CONT Tranter, N.: The story of Scotland, Neil Wilson Publishing, 1987
1 CONT Prebble, J.: The Lion in the North, Penguin Books
1 CONT Ross, S.: Monarchs of Scotland, Lochar Publishing, 1990
1 CONT Linklater, E.: The royal house of Scotland, Sphere Books Limited, 1972
1 CONT Bingham, C.: Kings and Queens of Scotland, Dorset Press, 1976
1 CONT Horan, M.: Scottish executions, assassinations and murders, Chambers, 1990
1 CONT Mackie, J.D.: A history of Scotland, Dorset Press, 1978
1 CONT Sutherland, E.: Five Euphemias (Women in Medieval Scotland 1200-1420), Constable, 1999
1 CONT Mure Mackenzie, A.: The Rise of the Stewarts, Olvier & Boyd Ltd., 1957
1 CONT Hallam, E. (ed.): The Plantagenet Encyclopedia (An alphabetical guide to 400 years of English history), Weidenfeld and Nicolson, 1990
1 CONT Weir, A.: Britain's royal families (The complete genealogy), Pimlico, 1996
1 CONT Ashley, M.: British Monarchs (The Complete Genealogy, Gazetteer and Biographical Encyclopedia of the Kings & Queens of Britain), Robinson, 1998
1 CONT Dictionary of British Kings and Queens, Bookhampton Reference, Geddes & Grosset, 1995
1 CONT Moncreiffe of that Ilk, Sir. I.: The Highland Clans (The dynastic origins,..), Barrie & Rockliff, 1967
1 CONT Last edited: October 22, 2000. Content: Joan Bos. Design: Klaas Vermaas. Email: madmonarchs@xs4all.nl.
0 @N6000000003615615345@ NOTE Robert Stewart or Stuart (ca.1339 - September 3, 1420) was the first duke of Albany, starting from 1398. He was also earl of Fife, earl of Atholl, earl of Buchan and earl of Menteith.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was son of king Robert II of Scotland, by his wife Elizabeth of Rowallan, and younger brother of Robert III (whose name was actually John). Robert III trusted his namesake brother and gave him not only the duchy of Albany, but 
1 CONC also several other titles. Robert Stuart was also prime minister and governor of Scotland. In 1361 he married Mary Graham, countess of Menteith, and with her he had several daughters and Murdoch, the future duke of Albany. Robert 
1 CONC married a second time, with Muriella de Keith, from whom he had John, the earl of Buchan, and Robert, the earl of Ross.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1389, Robert was nominated by his father guardian of the kingdom owing to Robert III's age and infirmity. By 1399, owing to the king Robert III's "sickness of the body", the control of the kingdom passed on to David Stuart, duk
1 CONC e of Rothesay and heir to the throne. This event was followed by an English invasion of Scotland, by serious differences between Robert and his nephew David, and finally in March 1402 by Rothesay's mysterious death by starvation a
1 CONC t Falkland Palace. After the death of Robert III, the duke of Albany became regent to James I of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT He died in Stirling Castle and is interred in Dunfermline Abbey in Fife.
1 CONT
1 CONT
