0 @N6000000004533926376@ NOTE Robert's elder brother John became Robert III of Scotland.  [Burke'sPeerage]
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany, so created 1398 (Albany beingGaelic for Scotland north of Forth), the same day as his nephew, theheir apparent to the throne, they being the first two dukes evercreated in Scotland; also Earl of
1 CONC  Menteith in right of his wife andEarl of Fife by entail 1371; Great Chamberlain of Scotland 1382-1408,invaded England with Douglas 1385, Regent 1388-1420; imprisoned hisnephew, who died mysteriously 1402, invaded England 1417; re
1 CONC builtDoune Castle; married 1st (papal dispensation 9 Sep 1361) Margaret (d.c1380), Countess of Menteith in her own right (through whom heinherited Doune Castle), daughter of John Graham, Earl of Menteith,and widow of (a) Sir John 
1 CONC Murray, (feudal) Lord of Bothwell, (b) 9thEarl of Mar, and (c) Sir John Drummond of Concraig, and died 2 Sep1420; married 2nd (papal dispensation May 1380) Muriel (died May1449), daughter of Sir William Keith, Marshal of Scotland.
1 CONC   [Burke'sPeerage]
1 CONT
1 CONT -------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert Stewart, Earl of Fife, Earl of Menteith, Duke of Albany, GreatChamberlain, Regent of Scotland, b. 1339, d. 2 Sep 1420; m. (1) c 1360Margaret, d. between 20 July 1372 and 4 May 1380, suo juris Countessof Menteith.  [Magna Ch
1 CONC arta Sureties]
1 CONT
1 CONT -------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT DUKEDOM of ALBANY (SCT) (I) 1398
1 CONT
1 CONT EARLDOM of ATHOLL (SCT) (XVIII, 1) 1403-1406
1 CONT
1 CONT EARLDOM of BUCHAN (SCT) (X, 2) 1406?
1 CONT
1 CONT EARLDOM of FIFE (SCT) (XII, 1) 1371
1 CONT
1 CONT EARLDOM of MENTEITH (SCT) (X, 1) in right of wife, Margaret Graham1361
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert Stewart, 3rd, but 2nd surviving son of Robert, High Steward ofScotland, afterwards (1370-90) Robert II, by his 1st wife, Elizabeth,daughter of Sir Adam Mure, of Rowallan, was b. about 1340, and while ayoung man (1361), beca
1 CONC me, in right of his wife, Earl of Menteith.  Byagreement, 30 Mar 1371, with Isabel, suo jure Countess of Fife, hebecame, on her resignation, Earl of Fife, by which later title he wasgenerally known.  Great Chamberlain 1383-1407.  
1 CONC He mad two successfulraids into England, one in 1385, and another in 1388.  On 1 Dec 1388the King (his father), being aged, and his eldest brother (afterwardsRobert II) infirm, he was made, by Parliament, Guardian of the Realm,and
1 CONC , as such, agreed to a treaty with the English in 1389; but on 27Jan 1398/9, he was superseded, by the appointment of David, the heirapparent of the throne, as "King's Lieutenant" with as ample powers ashis (David's) uncle (the sa
1 CONC id Duke) had as "Guardian of the Realm". Onthe death, sp. legit, of his brother Alexander Stewart, Earl ofBuchan, he appears to have been considered to have succeeded to thatEarldom, (recognized to the said Alexander, 25 July 1382
1 CONC ) which heresigned 20 Sep 1406.  In Mar 1398 he, with his said nephew David, hadan interview at Haudenstank with John, Duke of Lancaster, and otherEnglish Commissioners, and shortly afterwards, he and his said nephewwere each adva
1 CONC nced to a Dukedom (the first Dukes ever made inScotland), he being, on 28 Apr 1398, created Duke of Albany, in asolemn Council held at Scone.  For his complicity in the arrest of hissaid nephew, David, then Duke of Rothesay (who d
1 CONC . a prisoner inAlbany's Castle of Falkland, 27 Mar 1402), he received a remissionfrom Parliament.  After his nephew's death, the Duke assumed the thenvacant office of "King's Lieutenant"; and by Charter, 2 Sep 1403, wascreated Ear
1 CONC l of Atholl, during the life of the reigning King, withremainder (should he die before the said King) to his 2nd son John. Bythe death of the said King, 4 Apr 1406, this Earldom consequentlybecame extinct.  At the council held Jun
1 CONC e 1406, after the death of hisbrother Robert III, he was made Regent, the King (his nephew James I),being then a prisoner in England, which Kingdom accordingly he againinvaded in 1417, but on this occasion without success.
1 CONT
1 CONT He m., 1stly (dispensation 9 Sep 1361), Margaret, suo jure Countess ofMenteith (who had previously been the wife of Sir John Moray ofBothwell (dsp. 1352), of Thomas Erskine, 9th Earl of Mar, and of SirJohn Drummond of Concraig), d
1 CONC aughter (only surviving child & heir) ofJohn Graham, jure uxoris Earl of Menteith, by Mary, suo jure Countessof Menteith.  She d. about 1380, between 21 Jul 1372 and 4 May 1380.He m. 2ndly (Papal dispensation 4 May 1380), Muriel, 
1 CONC 1st daughter ofSir William Keith, Marischal of Scotland, by Margaret, daughter andheir of John Fraser of Zouch (sic. Touch?) Fraser, son and heir of SirAlexander Fraser, High Chamberlain.  He d. 3 Sep 1420, at StirlingCastle, aged
1 CONC  above 80, and was buried in Donfermline Abbey.  His widowd. shortly before Whitsunday (1 June) 1449.  [Complete PeerageI:77-79, XIV:15]
1 CONT
1 CONT Note: Albany is that part of Scotland which lies north of the Firthsof Clyde & Forth, including all of the highlands, which was theancient kingdom of Albans.  Also note that James I was a prisonerbecause his father (Robert III) fe
1 CONC lt the need to send him to France inorder to protect him from the Duke of Albany (otherwise he would havesuffered the same fate as his brother David).  But the ship he was on,bound for France, was captured by the English, and Jame
1 CONC s was takenprisoner for a very long time.  The Duke of Albany was sometimesblamed for not ransoming James earlier, but apparently the fault laywith the English, who had no desire to free him.
0 @N6000000002187730868@ NOTE Robert III of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert III ("circa" 1340 – April 4, 1406), king of Scotland (reigned 1390 - 1406), the eldest son of King Robert II by his mistress, Elizabeth Mure, became legitimised with the formal marriage of his parents about 1349. (They had 
1 CONC previously married in 1336, but some had criticized that ceremony as uncanonical.)
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1368 his great-uncle King David II of Scotland created him Earl of Carrick, and he took some part in the government of the kingdom until about 1387, when a kick from a horse disabled him. Probably in consequence of this acciden
1 CONC t his brother Robert, Earl of Fife, and not the crown prince himself, became guardian of the kingdom in 1389; but the latter succeeded to the throne on his father's death in May 1390.
1 CONT
1 CONT At this time he changed his baptismal name of John - unpopular owing to its connection with John de Baliol - for that of Robert, and became crowned at Scone in August 1390 as King Robert III. Although he probably attended several 
1 CONC parliaments, the new king seed only nominally as the ruler of Scotland, the real power remaining in the hands of his brother, the Earl of Fife.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1399, however, owing to the king's "sickness of the body," his elder son, David, Duke of Rothesay, gained appointment as lieutenant of the kingdom; but there followed an English invasion of Scotland, serious differences between
1 CONC  Rothesay and his uncle, Robert, now Duke of Albany, and finally in March 1402 Rothesay's mysterious death by starvation at Falkland Palace.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fearing for the safety of his surviving son, James, the king had the boy hidden at Dirleton Castle, with a view to smuggling him from there to France. However, a month later, in 1406, Englishmen captured the young James en route, 
1 CONC whereupon King Robert died, probably at Rothesay. The king allegedly died from grief over the capture of James. Robert even asked to be buried under a dunghill with the epitaph: Here lies the worst of Kings and the most miserable 
1 CONC of men. Instead he was interred at at Paisley instead of Scone, the traditional burial ground of the Scottish kings, as he did not consider himself worthy of the honor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert III married Annabella Drummond (c. 1350 - 1402), daughter of Sir John Drummond of Stobhall, and by her, in addition to the two sons already mentioned, had four daughters (Margaret, Mary, Egidia, and Elizabeth). He also had 
1 CONC an illegitimate son, James Stewart of Kilbride.
1 CONT
1 CONT This entry was modified from the 1911 Encyclopedia Britannica.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT Scottish monarchs' family tree 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Robert II King of Scots Succeeded by:
1 CONT James I 
0 @N6000000001783571811@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany was born circa 1340. He was the son of Robert II Stewart, King of Scotland and Elizabeth Mure of Rowallan.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a result of his marriage to Margaret Graham, Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany was styled as Earl of Menteith on 28 February 1361.3. He was created 1st Earl of Fife [Scotland] on 30 March 1371. He resigned as Earl of Fife on 6
1 CONC  March 1372.3 He held the office of Great Chamberlain [Scotland] between 1383 and 1407. In 1385 he made a successful raid into England. In 1388 he made another successful raid into England. He held the office of Guardian of the Re
1 CONC alm [Scotland] between 1 December 1388 and 27 January 1399. He succeeded to the title of 2nd Earl of Buchan [S., 1382] on 24 July 1394. He was created 1st Duke of Albany [Scotland] on 28 April 1398. He was created 1st Earl of Atho
1 CONC ll [Scotland] on 2 September 1403, for the life of King Robert III only. He was deposed as Earl of Atholl on  April 1406. He held the office of Regent of Scotland in June 1406. He resigned as Earl of Buchan, in favour of his secon
1 CONC d son John, and a special remainder to his third and fourth sons, Andrew and Robert on 20 September 1406. In 1417 he again invaded England, but without success this time.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT He married, firstly, Margaret Graham, Countess of Menteith, daughter of Sir John Graham and Mary Menteith, Countess of Menteith, after 9 September 1361, by Papal dispensation. Children of Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany and Mar
1 CONC garet Graham, Countess of Menteith were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Janet Stewart
1 CONT Mary Stewart
1 CONT Margaret Stewart
1 CONT Joan Stewart
1 CONT Beatrice Stewart d. c 14248
1 CONT Isabella Stewart
1 CONT Lady Marjorie Stewart d. b Aug 14329
1 CONT Murdoch Stewart, 2nd Duke of Albany b. 1362, d. 24 May 1425
1 CONT
1 CONT He married, secondly, Muriel Keith, daughter of Sir William Keith and Margaret Fraser, after 4 May 1380, by Papal dispensation. Children of Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany and Muriel Keith were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Andrew Stewart d. c 14139
1 CONT Sir Robert Stewart d. a 14319
1 CONT Elizabeth Stewart+ 10
1 CONT John Stewart, 3rd Earl of Buchan+ b. c 1381, d. 17 Aug 14248
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT He died on 3 September 1420 at Stirling Castle, Stirling, Stirlingshire, Scotland. He was buried at Dunfermline Abbey, Dunfermline, Fife, Scotland.
1 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p10211.htm#i102101
0 @N6000000002187730602@ NOTE Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert Stewart or Stuart, 1st Duke of Albany (c. 1340 – 3 September 1420) a member of the Scottish royal house, served as regent (at least partially) to three different Scottish monarchs. He also held the titles of Earl of Menteit
1 CONC h (28 February 1361), Earl of Fife (1361; resigned in 1372), Earl of Buchan (1394; resigned in 1406) and Earl of Atholl (1403, for the duration of Robert III's life only), in addition to his 1398 creation as Duke of Albany.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert Stewart, an illegitimate son of the future King Robert II of Scotland and of Elizabeth Mure of Rowallan, became legitimated in 1349 upon his parents' marriage. The elder Robert had many children, the eldest of whom, John St
1 CONC ewart, became Earl of Carrick in 1368. In 1361, Robert Stewart married Mary Graham, Countess of Menteith, with whom he had several daughters and one son, Murdoch Stewart, 2nd Duke of Albany; his sister-in-law's claim to the Earldo
1 CONC ms of Menteith and Fife allowed him to assume those titles after marriage. Robert subsequently married Muriella de Keith, with whom he had two sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the reign of their infirm father as King Robert II (1371 - 1390), the future Duke of Albany and Lord Carrick functioned as regents, with Albany serving as High Chamberlain of Scotland; during this time, he also led several 
1 CONC military expeditions and raids into the Kingdom of England. However, in 1389, Lord Carrick became incapacitated in an accident. After Carrick nevertheless acceded to the throne as Robert III (1390), this "sickness of the body" cau
1 CONC sed control of the kingdom to devolve in 1399 to the new king's son and heir apparent, David Stewart, Duke of Rothesay (who held the first dukedom created in the Scottish Peerage). However, the English soon invaded Scotland, and s
1 CONC everal important events took place, fomenting serious differences between Albany and Rothesay, and in 1402 the latter duke mysteriously died of starvation at Falkland Palace. After the death of his brother Robert III in 1406, Alba
1 CONC ny served as regent to his young nephew James I.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Duke of Albany died in Stirling Castle and lies buried in Dunfermline Abbey in Fife.
0 @N6000000001783406514@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret Graham, Countess of Menteith was born before 1334. She was the daughter of Sir John Graham and Mary Menteith, Countess of Menteith. She succeeded to the title of Countess of Menteith on 29 April 1360, suo jure.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT She married, firstly, Sir John Moray, Lord of Bothwell, son of Sir Andrew Moray and Lady Christina Bruce, after 21 November 1348. From after 21 November 1348, her married name became Moray
1 CONT
1 CONT She married, secondly, Thomas, 9th Earl of Mar, son of Donald, 8th Earl of Mar and Isabella Stewart, between 15 August 1352 and 29 May 1354. Her married name became Erskine
1 CONT
1 CONT She married, thirdly, Sir John Drummond circa 1359. She and Thomas, 9th Earl of Mar were divorced circa 1359. From 1359, her married name became Drummond.
1 CONT
1 CONT She married, fourthly, Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany, son of Robert II Stewart, King of Scotland and Elizabeth Mure of Rowallan, after 9 September 1361, by Papal dispensation. From after 9 September 1361, her married name bec
1 CONC ame Stewart.
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Margaret Graham, Countess of Menteith and Robert Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany
1 CONT
1 CONT Janet Stewart
1 CONT Mary Stewart
1 CONT Margaret Stewart
1 CONT Joan Stewart
1 CONT Beatrice Stewart d. c 14245
1 CONT Isabella Stewart
1 CONT Lady Marjorie Stewart b Aug 14326
1 CONT Murdoch Stewart, 2nd Duke of Albany b.1362, d. 24 May 1425
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT She died between 20 July 1372 and 4 May 1380. She was buried at Inchmahone Priory, Perthshire, Scotland.2
1 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p10795.htm#i107947
