1 CONT Robert II Stewart, King of Scotland was the son of Walter Stewart, 6th High Steward of Scotland and Margorie Bruce, Princess of Scotland. He was born on 2 March 1316 at Paisley, Renfrewshire, Scotland.2 He married, firstly, Elizab
1 CONC eth of Rowallan Mure, daughter of Sir Adam of Rowallan Mure <p10247.htm> and Jannet Mure <p466.htm>, on 22 November 1347, by Papal dispensation, which legitimised their previously born children.3 He married, secondly, Eupheme de R
1 CONC oss, Countess of Moray <p10532.htm>, daughter of Hugh de Ross, 4th Earl of Ross <p10790.htm> and Margaret Graham <p10809.htm>, on 2 May 1355, by Papal dispensation.4 He died on 19 April 1390 at age 74 at Dundonald Castle, Ayrshire
1 CONC , Scotland.4 He was buried at Scone Abbey, Scone, Perthshire, Scotland.5  He succeeded to the title of 7th High Steward of Scotland on 9 April 1327.3 He fought in the Battle of Halidon Hill on 19 July 1333 at Halidon Hill, Scotlan
1 CONC d, where he was in command.3 He held the office of Regent of Scotland between 1338 and 1341.3 He was created 1st Earl of Atholl [Scotland] on 16 February 1341/42.3 He held the office of Regent of Scotland between 1346 and 1357.3 H
1 CONC e was created 1st Earl of Strathearn [Scotland] in 1358.3 He abdicated as Earl of Atholl on 31 May 1367.1 He abdicated as Earl of Strathearn on 18 April 1369.1 He gained the title of Earl of Strathearn on 4 April 1370.1 He succeed
1 CONC ed to the title of King Robert II of Scotland on 22 February 1371.3 He was crowned King of Scotland on 26 March 1371 at Scone Abbey, Scone, Perthshire.1  Before his accession, he had been successively joint and sole regent in Davi
1 CONC d II's absence. During his reign, from 1384, his two sons were the real rulers for their unmilitary father.
