1 CONT From Burkes Peerage, Part Three, House of Stewart
1 CONT Ro bert II (March 2, 1316- April 19, 1390), king of Scotland, called "the
1 CONT Stewar d", a title that gave the name to the House of Stewart (or Stuart).
1 CONT Robert was the sole son of Walter, the 5th High Steward of Scotland (d.
1 CONT 1326), and Marj orie (d. 1316 in childbirth), daughter of King Robert the
1 CONT Bruce.
1 CONT In 1318 the Scottish parliament decreed that if King Robert died without
1 CONT sons the crown should pass to his grandson; but the birth of a son
1 CONT afterwards, King David II , to Bruce in 1324 postponed the accession of
1 CONT Robert for nearly forty-two yea rs. Soon after the infant David became
1 CONT king in 1329, the Steward began to tak e a prominent part in the affairs
1 CONT of Scotland. He was one of the leaders of t he Scottish army at the battle
1 CONT of Halidon Hill in July 1333; and after gainin g some successes over the
1 CONT adherents of Edward Balliol in the west of Scotland , he and John
1 CONT Randolph, 3rd Earl of Moray (d. 1346), were chosen as regents o f the
1 CONT kingdom, while David sought safety in France.
1 CONT The colleagues soon quar relled; then Randolph fell into the hands of the
1 CONT English and Robert became so le regent, meeting with such success in his
1 CONT efforts to restore the royal auth ority that the king was able to return
1 CONT to Scotland in 1341. Having handed ove r the duties of government to
1 CONT David, the Steward escaped from the battle of N eville's Cross in 1346,
1 CONT and was again chosen regent while the king was a capt ive in England. Soon
1 CONT after this event some friction arose between Robert and his royal uncle.
1 CONT Accused, probably without truth, of desertion at Neville's C ross, the
1 CONT Steward as heir-apparent was greatly chagrined by the king's propos al to
1 CONT make Edward III of England, or one of his sons, the heir to the Scottis h
1 CONT throne, and by David's marriage with Margaret Logie.
1 CONT In 1363 he rose in re bellion, and after having made his submission was
1 CONT seized and imprisoned toget her with four of his sons, being only released
1 CONT a short time before David's de ath in February 1371. By the terms of the
1 CONT decree of 1318 Robert now succeeded to the throne, and was crowned at
1 CONT Scone in March 1371. His reign is unimport ant. Some steps were taken by
1 CONT the nobles to control the royal authority. In 1 378 a war broke out with
1 CONT England; but the king took no part in the fighting, which included the
1 CONT burning of Edinburgh and the Scottish victory at the Battl e of
1 CONT Otterbourne in 1388.
1 CONT As age and infirmity were telling upon him, the es tates in 1389 appointed
1 CONT his second surviving son Robert, Earl of Fife, afterw ards Duke of Albany,
1 CONT guardian of the kingdom. The king died at Dundonald in 1 390, and was
1 CONT buried at Scone.
1 CONT His first wife was Elizabeth, daughter of Sir Robert Mure of Rowallan, a
1 CONT lady who had formerly been his mistress. Robert ha d married her in 1336,
1 CONT but as the marriage had been criticised as uncanonical , he remarried her
1 CONT in 1349. By her he had at least four sons, the eldest of w hom was his
1 CONT successor, King Robert III, and six daughters. By his second wife ,
1 CONT Euphemia, daughter of Hugh, 6th Earl of Ross, and widow of the 3rd Earl
1 CONT o f Moray, formerly his colleague as regent, he had two sons and several
1 CONT daught ers.
1 CONT The confusion about the circumstances of his first marriage would later
1 CONT lead to conflict amongst the descendants of his first marriage (which
1 CONT includ ed James I of Scotland) and the unquestionably legitimate
1 CONT descendants of his second marriage.
