0 @N6000000004533926408@ NOTE Robert III Stewart (called John at birth), b. 1337, legitimated(dispensation) 1347, d. 4 Apr 1406, Earl of Carrick 22 June 1368, Kingof Scotland 1390-1406; m. in or before 1367 (dispensation 13 Mar1365/6), Annabella, d. 1401, daug
1 CONC hter of Sir John Drummond and Mary(perhaps Montefichet), of Stobhall.  [Magna Charta Sureties]
1 CONT ------------------------
1 CONT Robertt III..., after having ruled Scotland in the name of his father,Robert II, from 1384 to 1388, physically disabled by a kick from ahorse, he was never the real ruler of Scotland during the years of hiskingship.
1 CONT
1 CONT The eldest son of Robert the Steward and Elizabeth Mure, he waslegitimized by their marriage several years after his birth.  In1362-3 he joined his father in a futile revolt against King David II,who both imprisoned him and create
1 CONC d him Earl of Carrick in 1368.  (Hehad been created Earl of Atholl in 1367.)  Robert II became king in1371; in 1384, because of his advanced age, he turned over thegovernment to Carrick. After his injury in 1388, however, Carrick 
1 CONC wassupplanted by his brother Robert, Earl of Fife.
1 CONT
1 CONT On his accession, probably on April 19, 1390, he changed his name toRobert III from John to avoid reminding others of John de Balliol,King of Scotland from 1292 to 1296, who was not favourably remembered.Fife, created Duke of Alba
1 CONC ny in 1398, continued to govern throughoutthis reign, except for three years (1398-1402) when Robert III'seldest son, David Duke of Rothesay, took his place.  The dissoluteRothesay died in March 1402 while imprisoned in Albany's c
1 CONC astle ofFalkland, Fife. Perhaps in an attempt to save his remaining son, James(afterward James I King of Scotland) from death at Albany's hands,Robert III sent the boy to France, but James was captured by Englishsailors, a shock t
1 CONC o the aging king.  [Encylclopedia Britannica]
