1 CONT Robert III Stewart, King of Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Born: 1337, Scotland, England 631,766
1 CONT     * Marriage: Annabella Drummond in 1367 766
1 CONT     * Died: 4 Apr 1406, Rothesay Castle, Isle of Bute, Argyllshire, Scotland at age 69 766
1 CONT     * Buried: 1406, Paisley Abbey, Renfrewshire, Scotland 766
1 CONT
1 CONT    Another name for Robert was John Stewart.
1 CONT
1 CONT   General Notes:
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert III (c. 1340-1406), king of Scotland from 1390 to 1406, son of Robert II. The principal events in Robert's reign were the invasion of Scotland in 1400 by Henry IV of England, who penetrated as far as Edinburgh, and the reta
1 CONC liatory expedition of the Scotch, two years after, under Archibald Douglas, which resulted in the terrible disaster at Homildon Hill. [The Home University Encyclopedia, 1946]
1 CONT
1 CONT John Stewart. Created Earl of Carrick in 1368. Succeeded to the throne as Robert III.{Burke's Peerage} [GADD.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT   Noted events in his life were:
1 CONT
1 CONT  Title: Earl of Carrick, 1368.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Title: King of Scotland, 1390-1406.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Alt. Death, 4 Apr 1406, Dundonald Castle, Ayrshire, Scotland. 631
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert married Annabella Drummond, daughter of Sir John Drummond and Unknown, in 1367.766 (Annabella Drummond was born about 1350,766 died in Oct 1401 in Scone Palace, Perthshire, Scotland 766,788 and was buried in 1401 in Dunferm
1 CONC line, Fife, Scotland 766.)
0 @N6000000003403243977@ NOTE Earl of Buchan
0 @N6000000003615380429@ NOTE
1 CONT Ross, Euphemia, Countess of Ross
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Alexander Stewart and Euphemia De Ros are:
1 CONT    i.   Earl of Mar Alexander Stewart, born Abt. 1400; died Unknown.
1 CONT   2660624 ii.   Robert Stewart, born Abt. 1385; died Abt. 1388.
1 CONT    iii.   James Stewart, born Abt. 1387; died Unknown; married Janet Menzies; born Abt. 1389; died Unknown.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Alexander 'The Wolf' STEWART (Earl of Badenach)
1 CONT 18th Gt. Grandfather
1 CONT
1 CONT Sex: M
1 CONT
1 CONT Title: Earl of Badenach
1 CONT
1 CONT Ref. No.: I35114
1 CONT
1 CONT Birth: ABT. 1341
1 CONT
1 CONT Death: 24 JUL 1394
1 CONT
1 CONT History: Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Father:  Robert II of Scotland STEWART (King of Scotland) [b. 2 MAR 1315/16; d. 19 APR 1390]
1 CONT Mother:  Elizabeth of Rowallan MURE [b. ABT. 1320; d. BET. 1355 - 1358]
1 CONT
1 CONT Family 1 #16240
1 CONT Partner:  Margaret ATHEYN
1 CONT
1 CONT Child 1:  Andrew STUART (Sir)
1 CONT Child 2: +  Margaret STUART [b. ABT. 1373; d. ABT. 1439]
1 CONT Child 3: +Alexander of Mar STUART (Earl of Mar) [b. ABT. 1375; d. ABT. 1435]
1 CONT
1 CONT Family 2 #16241
1 CONT Wife:  Euphemia ROSS (Countess of Ross) [b. ?; d. ABT. 20 FEB 1394/95]
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage: AFT. 1382
1 CONT
1 CONT No known issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT "GARTH CASTLE was probably built Alexander Stewart, earl of Buchan, who is better known to history as the Wolf of Badenoch. Son of the first Stewart king, Robert II, and grandson of Robert Bruce he was a law unto himself
1 CONT north of the Highland line and an uncomfortable man to have in any position of authority. So much so that the Gaels called his descendants 'Children of the Accursed Whelp' and there were plenty of them. David Stewart, he of the
1 CONT statue, estimated in 1820 that four thousand people in Atholl descended from the Wolf's son who settled at the castle.
1 CONT
1 CONT For a century the family were pillars of the community, slaughtering only the king's enemies, until the time of Neil Gointe. A free translation of his name would be Neil the Bitter and Twisted. Soon after inheriting Garth he fell 
1 CONC out with his neighbours, particularly Sir Robert Menzies who was charged by the king to keep order in Rannoch, but Neil protected the gangs of outlaws who preyed on the people and incited them to direct their assaults on Sir Rober
1 CONC t and his dependents. Finally, in 1502, Neil and his henchmen attacked Castle Menzies, burnt it to the ground and pillaged Weem, Camserney, and other Menzies properties. Sir Robert was stuffed into the dungeon of Garth Castle and 
1 CONC starved in order to encourage him to sign over to Neil local estates that he coveted.
1 CONT
1 CONT King James IV then came north to sort out the affair. Neil kept his head by surrendering his captive once he had extracted a document in which the prisoner forgave his captor. Having been deprived of control of much of his
1 CONT estates Neil's later years are obscure but he survived until 1554. He was suspected of murdering his wife by dropping a rock on her head when she was down in the burn below the castle. Tradition has it that his last nine years aft
1 CONC er her death were spent in the dungeon of his own castle.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is interesting to compare this, the oral tradition's vilification of Neil, with documented history. The dispute was about land and Neil was supported by the Stewarts of both Grantully and Bonskeid. Three years after the burning
1 CONC  of the Menzies stronghold, the king confirmed Neil in the lands he inherited. And the Menzies never did win possession. The Earl of Huntly, married to Neil's sister-in-law, became titular lord of the disputed estates but Neil ret
1 CONC ained control. His second wife was killed by a stone falling from above but the man responsible is named as Alexander Stewart and the use of 'negligently' in the contemporary report would, at worst, indicate a culpable homicide ra
1 CONC ther than murder. The tradition that Neil lived his last nine years in his own dungeon would seem to be a confusion with his ancestor 'The Wolf' who built Garth Castle and did indeed spend a considerable time imprisoned by his fat
1 CONC her, the king, after the burning of Elgin Cathedral."
1 CONT
1 CONT The above written by James Irvine ROBERTSON of Aberfeldy Perthshire Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir JAMES STEWART of Garth, born abt 1377 in Dundonald Ayr to
1 CONT ALEXANDER , Prince of Scotland and Earl of Buchan and
1 CONT his longtime mistress, Margaret de ATHEYN(E).
1 CONT
1 CONT JAMES was ancestor of the Stewarts of Atholl, Balnakeily
1 CONT Bonskeid, Cammoch, Castle Stewart, Chesfield, Clunie, Derculich,
1 CONT Drumchary, Drumchuine, Dundee, Duntanlich, Easter Kinnaird,
1 CONT Edradynate, Fincastle, Forthergill, Foss, Garth, Grainnich, Inchgarth,
1 CONT Innersianie, Killiechassie, Kinnakile, Kynachin, Ladywall, Polcack,
1 CONT Rotmell, St. Fort, Shiergiass, Tillinyrish, Tulloch, Urrard, Wester Cluny.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Janet MENZIES (b abt 1379),
1 CONT the daughter & heiress of
1 CONT Alexander de MENZIES0 of Fothergill & Jean de ATHOLIA.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT John Gorm STEWART of Fothergill & Garth.
1 CONT Born abt 1405. He was killed on Midsummer Day in 1443.
1 CONT
1 CONT John STEWART of Garth, Fothergill, Drumcharry, etc.
1 CONT Died at Garth Castle on Dec 10 1475.
1 CONT He married Marion/Mariot ~ They were the parents of:
1 CONT
1 CONT * (1) Neil Gointe STEWART ~ his descendants became proprietors of Duntanlich about 1600
1 CONT and had some claim to Drumcharry as well.
1 CONT (2) Alexander STEWART, 1st Earl of Bonskeid.
1 CONT He received a charter of the lands of Bonskeid on Dec 31 1474 from his father.
1 CONT He died in 1501.
1 CONT (3) Maud STEWART married Thomas STEWART, 2nd Laird of Grantully.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT * Neil Gointe STEWART, Laird of Garth, married Elizabeth STEWART,
1 CONT daughter of Alexander STEWART, Laird of Grantully & Margaret HAY of Tulliebodie.
1 CONT (She was also the sister of Thomas STEWART, 2nd Laird of Grantully,
1 CONT who married her husband Neil's sister Maud!!)
