0 @N6000000002187730895@ NOTE Walter Stewart, 6th High Steward of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Walter Stewart or Steward (1293 -1326) was the 6th High steward of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was son to James Stewart, 5th High Steward of Scotland and Cecilia de Dunbar. His maternal grandparents were Patrick de Dunbar, 7th Earl of Dunbar and Marjory de Comyn. Marjory was a daughter of Alexander Comyn, 2nd Earl of Buc
1 CONC han.
1 CONT
1 CONT Walter married Marjorie Bruce, only daughter of Robert I of Scotland by his first wife Isabella of Mar.
1 CONT
1 CONT Their son Robert was heir to the House of Bruce; he eventually inherited the Scottish throne when his maternal uncle David II died childless in 1371.
1 CONT
1 CONT Walter commanded one of the schiltrons at the Battle of Bannockburn, but because of his youth and inexperience, its effective leader appears to have been Bruce's lieutenant James Douglas the Black. Walter Stewart and James Douglas
1 CONC  were first cousins.
1 CONT
1 CONT His descedants include the House of Stuart and all their successors at the throne of Great Britain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT James Stewart High Steward of Scotland Succeeded by:
1 CONT Robert II of Scotland 
0 @N6000000003403245073@ NOTE [huntingdonhenry_descendents10_fromrootsweb_bartont.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT 6th High Steward of Scotland. His (1) wife is said by Douglas to have been Alicle, dau. Sir John Erskin by whom a dau. Jean, m. to Hugh, Earlof Ross No proof) After rteh death of Lady Marjorie, Walter the Steward m. (2) or (3) Isa
1 CONC bel, sis. of Sir John Graham by whom he had issue. (Weis 252-31-h).
0 @N6000000003615400281@ NOTE The name "Malcolm" is an honorable one in Scotttish history, ShubealDouglas Malcolm ,who long made a study of the derivation of names,asserts the name is derived from Mael-Columbia, meaning follower andservant of Columbia 521-597,
1 CONC  missionary to the Iona.
1 CONT There were four Malcolms, Kings of Scotland in early history.  TheMalcolm clan were Highlanders.  They were strenous fighters and thestory is told of that the Clan was so reduced in numbers and powerthey were forced to seek allian
1 CONC ce with their neighbors the Campbellsof Argyllshire.  At one time, through continual warfare with neighborsand quarrels amongst themselves, the Malcolms were reduced to onlyLaird and two sons.  Fearing total extinction the Laird o
1 CONC rdered histwo sons to mount their horses, and giving each a pannier sent themoff in opposite directions with orders to continue riding until thepanniers dropped form their horses and at that spot they were toabide, which they did.
1 CONC   Years later, both families decided to visitthe old home.  The two groups of about thirty men each met at a rivercrossing and joined in battle.  Only two survivors, one of eachfamily, were left.  Taking a much needed rest before 
1 CONC continuing thebattle, they then discovered they were both Malcolms.
1 CONT The Malcolms evidently not only loved a good battle, but were oftentimes on the wrong side.  In 1745, the Young Pretender, Bonnie PrinceCharles Stuart, in an effort to place his father, the pretender JamesIII on the British throne
1 CONC , came to Scotland from France to raise anarmy to invade England.  After not finding the support from thecountryside he had expected, Charles turned back at Derby, but waspursued by the forces of the Duke of Cumberland, son of Kin
1 CONC g GeorgeII.  They engaged in a great battle at Culloden Moor (5 miles east ofInverness, northern Scotland.) The Duke's forces numbered 8811including 1100 cavalry and 18 guns. Stuart forces numbered about 7000.This battle fought Ap
1 CONC ril 16, 1746 was the last land battle to befought in Great Btitian.  Stuart's army was destroyed, and because ofthe ferocity with which the Duke of Cumberland executed the wounded,he was given the nickname "the Butcher."
1 CONT Some escaped.  Stuart wandered in the Highlands as a refugee until heleft for France in September.  Some escaped to Ulster and some to theAmerican Colonies.
1 CONT
1 CONT Our emigrant ancestor, Duncan Malcolm was one of these refugees to theAmerican Colonies.  He had joined his fortune with that of CharlesStuart, grandson of Charles I of England and great grandson of MaryQueen of Scots.  The battle
1 CONC  fought at Culloden Moors shattered thehopes of Prince Charles and the fortunes of Duncan Malcolm.  In 1774,he came to America with his ten children.  No record of his wife.
1 CONT He settled first at Watermill, near Southhampton, Long Island, NY,removed to Satbrook, CT in 1776, and then later to Middlefield, CT.After the Battle of Long Island, Aug. 27, 1776 between the British andthe American forces, all of
1 CONC  Long Island was surrendered to theBritish. The people generally were required to take an Oath ofAllegiance to his Majesty, King George III of Great Britian.
1 CONT   To avoid taking the oath, many thousands became refugees.  The stateof New York impressed vessels to carry them and their goods from LongIsland to Connecticut.  Duncan sailed Sept., 1776 on the sloop Dove ofSaybrook, CT.  The fa
1 CONC mily settled at Middlefield, CT.  About thistime, Duncan's son Finley, our ancestor, went to Penobscot, Maine (nowcalled Castine.) There he was active as a pioneer settler, ship'sCaptian, active Troy and finally an evacuee to Nova
1 CONC  Scotia.Penobscot, also called Castine and Ft. George had a log fort that wasoccupied by the British troops, many of whom were Scots.  The fort wasunder a long siege, but did not fall. Dr. Calif, medical officer ofthe fort kept a 
1 CONC diary during the siege. "Finley Malcolm, on HisMajesty's Service was the only civilian admitted to the fort without apass."  He was recorded in His Majesty's Service at Ft. George duringthe siege of the Continentals in 1779.  He r
1 CONC ecieved a grant of landfor his adherance to the King during the American Revolution. In1798, he sold several hundreds of acres of land at Penobscot and movedto Upper Canada.  There he met and married Tryphena Wardwell, daughterof 
1 CONC Capt. Daniel Wardwell of York Maine.  The ancestry of theWardwell's is equally important as that of the Malcolms and isrecorded in both England and America.
1 CONT It will be noted on the historical notes on Duncan's children, that atleast two sons, Malcolm and Daniel fought on the American side in theRevolution.  In 1778, the British reoccupied Ft. George at Penobscot,Maine once abandoned, 
1 CONC to serve as a place of refuge for the Loyalistsof Maine. About 647 people, members of the Association of PenobscotLoyalists were active there.  After the war, they became refugees.Many were forced to leave their possessions and fl
1 CONC ee to Canada andelsewhere. Not less than 35,000 destitute poured into Nova Scotia in1783.  Great Britian spent nearly 3 million lbs sterling and gave over3,000,000 acres of land exemot from taxes to help the desperaterefugees.
1 CONT Finley Malcolm, our ancestor, was granted land in Nova Scotia, butafter 15 years he sold his holdings and moved.  He set sail by way ofthe Gulf and St. Lawrence River for Upper Canada.  On Aug. 1, 1798, ason was born "on the water
1 CONC " enroute on the St. Lawrence.  Finley andhis six sons and four daughters settled in Scotland, Ontario.  Theywere a very enterprising family and over the years they established 5mills on the creek between Scotland and Oakland - a 
1 CONC woolen mill, agrist mill, and a saw mill at Scotland and grists mills at Scotlandand Oakland.  The creek was called Malcolm's Creek and for years theVillage of Scotland was known as Malcolm's Mills.  The sons weresettled on farms 
1 CONC in the vicinity of Scotland, except for John andFinley who settled at Oakland.  Daniel later moved to Washington Twp.near St. Thomas.  Hugh was killed at a barn raising while still ayoung man.  Eliakin's farm ajoined Duncan's. It 
1 CONC is the only one of thefarms owned and operated now by one of the Malcolm's of Scotland.Eliakin was described in one book as being "extremely acquisitive."
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: History and Genealogy of the Malcolm Family of the UnitedStates and Canada by John Karl Malcolm, Ann Arbor, MI 1950
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Walter Stewart Sixth High Stewart of Scotland
1 CONT Walter, sixth High Stewart of Scotland, was born in 1292. Inconjunction with Sir James Douglas he commanded one of the divisionsof the Scottish army at Bannockburn in 1314, and in the following yearreceived from Robert I, The Bruc
1 CONC e ( King of Scotland ), the hand ofhis daughter the Lady Marjory Bruce, in marriage. He took part in allthe principal episodes in the War of Independence against Eddward II,notable in the defense of Berwick against a great English
1 CONC  army, andacted as Regent of Scotland during King Robert's absence in Ireland.He died on April 9, 1326, at the early age of thirty- three.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Walter Stewart was the son of James Stewart, Lord High Stewart ofScotland, and Egidia Burgh, sister of Richard de Burgh, Earl ofUlster. ( The Scots Peerage, Edited by Sir James Balfour SPaul, 1904,Vol I, pp 13,14)
