0 @N6000000002187742881@ NOTE James I of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT James I (December 10, 1394 – February 21, 1437) reigned as king of Scotland from April 4, 1406 until February 21, 1437. However, from 1406 to 1424 he was king in name only.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born on December 10, 1394, the son of Robert III and Annabella Drummond. He had an eventful childhood. In 1402 his elder brother, David, was starved to death in prison at Falkland in Fife. Before the death of his father in 
1 CONC 1406 James was sent to France for safety.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the way there, the English captured him and handed him over to Henry IV of England who imprisoned him and demanded a ransom. Robert III was said to have died from grief over the capture of James. His uncle, Robert Stewart, 1st 
1 CONC Duke of Albany who became Regent on the death of Robert III, was in no hurry to pay for his release. Robert secured the release of his son Murdoch, who was captured at the same time, but not so with James. So for the next 18 years
1 CONC , James was imprisoned and educated in Windsor Castle and in secure large country houses near London.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of his uncle in 1420, the ransom of £40,000 was finally paid, and in 1424 James returned to Scotland to find a country in chaos. He took his bride with him – he had met and fallen in love with Joan Beaufort while i
1 CONC mprisoned. He married her in London in February 2, 1423. They would have eight children, including the future James II of Scotland, and Margaret, wife of Louis XI of France. He is believed during his captivity to have written The 
1 CONC Kingis Quair, an allegorical romance and one of the earliest major works of Scottish literature extent.
1 CONT
1 CONT James was formally crowned King of Scotland at Scone Abbey, Perthshire, on May 2 or 21, 1424. He immediately took strong actions to regain authority and control. One such action was to execute the Albany family, who had opposed hi
1 CONC s actions. The execution of Murdoch, Duke of Albany, and two of Murdoch's sons took place on May 24, 1425 at Castle Hill, Stirling.
1 CONT
1 CONT He proceeded to rule Scotland with a firm hand, and achieved numerous financial and legal reforms. For instance, for the purpose of trade with other nations, foreign exchange could only be exchanged within Scottish borders. He als
1 CONC o tried to remodel the Parliament of Scotland along English lines. However, in foreign policy, he renewed the Auld Alliance, a Scottish-French (and therefore anti-English) alliance, in 1428.
1 CONT
1 CONT His actions throughout his reign, though effective, upset many people. During the later years of his reign, they helped to lead to his claim to the throne coming under question.
1 CONT
1 CONT James I's grandfather, Robert II, had married twice and the awkward circumstances of the first marriage, from which James was descended, led to it being disputed. Conflict broke out between the descendants of the first marriage an
1 CONC d the unquestionably legitimate descendants of the second marriage over who should be on the Scottish throne. Matters came to a head on February 21, 1437, when James was assassinated by a group of Scots led by Sir Robert Graham wh
1 CONC ile staying at the Friars Preachers Monastery in Perth. He attempted to escape his assailants through a sewer. However, three days previously, he had the other end of the drain blocked up because it was connected to the tennis cou
1 CONC rt outside, and balls were in the habit of getting lost in it. (See also: Catherine Douglas.)
1 CONT
1 CONT A wave of executions followed in March, 1437, of those who were part of the plot. Among those executed were James's uncle, Walter, Earl of Atholl, and his grandson, Robert, Master of Atholl (both of whom were descended from Robert
1 CONC  II's second marriage).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT Scottish monarchs family tree 
1 CONT [edit]
