0 @N6000000002188078222@ NOTE Domhnall of Islay, Lord of the Isles
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Donald or properly Domnhall of Islay, Lord of the Isles († 1423) was the son and successor of John of Islay, Lord of the Isles. The Lordship of the Isles was based in and around the Scottish west-coast island of Islay, but under J
1 CONC ohn of Islay had come to include most of the other islands of the west coast, as well as Morvern, Garmoran, Lochaber, Kintyre and Knapdale. While it is customary to portray the Lords of the Isles as divorced from the mainstream of
1 CONC  Scottish political life, and as representatives of an alternative brand of lordship, this view largely obscures the fact that Domhnall was only one of many magnates in late 14th and early 15th century Scotland who held large lord
1 CONC ships with little interference from the crown. The Douglas kindred of southern Scotland and the Albany Stewarts easily parallel Domhnall's lordship. Domhnall was, moreover, the grandson of King Robert II of Scotland and first cous
1 CONC in of King Robert III and took pride in his royal blood, even adopting the royal tressure to surround his coat of arms.
1 CONT
1 CONT Domhnall spent some of his first years as Lord of the Isles suppressing a revolt by his brother John Mór. John was Domhnall's younger brother, and resented his meagre portion of his father's inheritance. Although he was recognized
1 CONC  as heir-apparent (tanaiste), he only received patches of land in Kintyre and Islay in the inheritance. The rebellion started in 1387 and went on into the 1390s, and John found the support of the MacLean (MacGill-Eain) kindred. Ho
1 CONC wever, John and the MacLeans were eventually forced to submit to Domhnall, and by 1395 John Mór had been forced into Ireland where he entered the service of King Richard II of England and later established a MacDonald lordship in 
1 CONC Antrim.
1 CONT
1 CONT Suppression of the revolt enabled Domhnall to turn his attention northwards and eastwards. Most of the area to the north and east of the Lordship, that is Skye, Ross, Badenoch and Urquhart was controlled by Alexander Stewart, Earl
1 CONC  of Buchan, famously known as the "Wolf of Badenoch". Alexander had been building up a powerbase in the central Highlands and north of Scotland since the death of John Randolph, 3rd Earl of Moray in 1346. He had acquired control o
1 CONC f the lordship of Badenoch, the earldom of Buchan and the Justiciarship of Scotia, as well as effectively taking the place of the earls of Moray and Ross as "Lieutenant of the North". He was at once the de facto ruler of northern 
1 CONC Scotland as well as the means by which the crown itself exercised control.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, there had been complaints the activities of his caterans (warbands), and more importantly, Alexander's position was threatening not only to the crown, but also to the Euphemia I, Countess of Ross, her son Alexander and th
1 CONC e titular Dunbar Earl of Moray. Late in 1388, soon after becoming Guardian of the Kingdom, Robert Stewart, Earl of Fife (created Duke of Albany in 1398) deprived Alexander of the Justiciarship. The assault of Alexander's position 
1 CONC continued into the 1390s. Domhnall and his brother Alexander of Lochaber were in a perfect position to benefit. In 1394 the latter entered a 17-year agreement with the titular Earl of Moray taking over Alexander Stewart's role as 
1 CONC "protector" of the wealthy comital and episcopal lands in the Moray lowlands. The MacDonalds were is possession of Urquhart by the end of 1395, and gave control of the castle to the MacLean (MacGill'Eain) kindred of Duart.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, the Guardian soon turned his hostility against the MacDonalds. Alexander of Lochaber had been using his role as "protector" to further his own lordship, including granting episcopal lands to his military followers. In 139
1 CONC 8, Robert Stewart (now Duke of Albany) was called upon to take action, but the well-prepared expedition in the end came to nothing. Lochaber continued his activities, and in a raid of 1402 burned the burgh of Elgin, for which he w
1 CONC as excommunicated by William Spynie, bishop of Moray. However, later in the year Alexander visited Spynie to seek forgiveness.
1 CONT
1 CONT Domhnall himself was causing still further concern when in the same year, following the death of Alexander Leslie, Earl of Ross, Domhnall pressed the claims of Mariota, Alexander Leslie's sister and Domhnall's wife, to the possess
1 CONC ion of Ross, and attempted to gain control of the earldom. Sometime after 1405 but before 1411, Domhnall gained control of Dingwall Castle, the chief seat of the Earldom. In the year after the death of the nominal king Robert III 
1 CONC (1406), in August 1407 Domhnall sents emissaries to England to the heir of the throne, the captive James Stewart. King Henry IV of England sent his own emissaries the following year to negotiate an alliance against Albany.
1 CONT
1 CONT With control over the principle seat of the earldom of Ross and support of the exiled heir to the Scottish throne, in 1411 Domhnall felt strong enough to march against Albany's main northern ally, Alexander Stewart, Earl of Mar. A
1 CONC t the Battle of Harlaw Domhnall failed to inflict a decisive victory, and withdrew back to the western highlands. In the aftermath, Albany was able to retake Dingwall and seize control of Easter Ross. In 1415, the heir of Alexande
1 CONC r Leslie, Euphemia II, resigned the earldom to Albany. Domhnall prepared for war and proclaimed himself "Lord of Ross". Although Albany appointed his own son John Stewart to the earldom, Domhnall's wife continued to regard herself
1 CONC  as the rightful Countess.
1 CONT
1 CONT Domhnall died in 1423 on Islay, the historic centre of his Lordship. He was succeeded by his son Alexander.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Boardman, Stephen, The Early Stewart Kings: Robert II and Robert III, 1371-1406, (East Linton, 1996) 
1 CONT Brown, Michael, James I, (East Linton, 1994) 
1 CONT Oram, Richard, "The Lordship of the Isles, 1336-1545", in Donald Omand (ed.) The Argyll Book, (Edinburgh, 2005), pp. 123-39 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT John Lord of the Isles
1 CONT 1386-1423 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Alexander 
0 @N6000000003615405954@ NOTE self styled 9th Earl of Ross.
1 CONT
1 CONT ----------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Donald MacDonald, Lord of the Isles (so designated as successor by his father in preference to his elder half-brothers (by 1st wife Amie dau of Roderick MacRuari) under the Celtic system called tanistry) and self-styled 9th Earl o
1 CONC f Ross in right of his wife; either in support of his claim to the Earldom or as a sovereign ruler in alliance with England or France he invaded the Scottish mainland with an army made up of men of the Isles and Ross but was check
1 CONC ed at Harlaw 24 July 1411 in a pyrrhic victory by the Regent of Scotland who despite his "victory" failed to prevent the MacDonald's styling themselves Earls of Ross for at least two generations as well as promulgating charters fr
1 CONC om Dingwall Castle, the chief place of the territory over which the Earls of Ross were supposed to hold sway, though in the immediate aftermath of the Battle of Harlaw the Regent captured the Castle.  [Burke's Peerage]
1 CONT
1 CONT ----------------
1 CONT
1 CONT LORD OF THE ISLES (VII)
1 CONT
1 CONT Donald, Lord of the Isles, who m. Marry, suo jure Countess of Ross, daughter of Sir Walter Leslie and Eupheme, sur jure Countess of Ross.  In right of his marriage Donald claimed the Earldom of Ross, and the battle of Harlaw (1411
1 CONC ) was fought in consequence.  He d. about 1423.  [Complete Peerage VII:76-7]
