0 @N6000000001968479060@ NOTE The 2nd Earl was born in 1358 and succeeded his father at a time of much border warfare between Scotland and England. Due to the old age of King Robert II, the Douglases were left in charge of the defense of the kingdom. They lite
1 CONC rally became "the shield of Scotland". In 1388 the 2nd Earl led a plundering sweep into England in retaliation for the devastation caused by King Richard's army three years earlier. While on this raid, Douglas met and defeated the
1 CONC  renown English knight Henry "Hotspur" Percy in personal combat. In triumph he carried off Percy's pennon. 
0 @N6000000002330688058@ NOTE James, 2nd Earl of Douglas
1 CONT The 2nd Earl was born in 1358 and succeeded his fat her at a time of much border warfare between Scotland and England. Due to the o ld age of King Robert II, the Douglases were left in charge of the defense of t he kingdom. They l
1 CONC iterally became "the shield of Scotland". In 1388 the 2nd Ear l led a plundering sweep into England in retaliation for the devastation caused by King Richard's army three years earlier. While on this raid, Douglas met an d defeate
1 CONC d the renown English knight Henry "Hotspur" Percy in personal combat. In triumph he carried off Percy's pennon.
1 CONT
1 CONT In an effort to regain his pennon, and his honor, Percy pursued and engaged Douglas at the Battle of Otterburn. D ouglas, although mortally wounded in the battle, directed his captains to carry his standard, sound his battle cry a
1 CONC nd rally his troops. Even in death, he car ried the field. James left one illegitimate son, William, 1st Lord of Drumlanri g, who was the sire of the Queensberry cadet branch. As no legitimate heir was left, the earldom passed to 
1 CONC the "base born" son of Sir James "The Good".
1 CONT
1 CONT Ott erbourne
1 CONT
1 CONT A Scottish ode to James, 2nd Earl of Douglas and Sir Hugh Montgomer y, his nephew, who fought and defeated Henry "Hotspur" Percy on the field at Ot terburn in 1388.
1 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------- ------------- ------
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT It fell about the Lammas tide,
1 CONT When the muir-men win their hay,
1 CONT The doughty Douglas bound him to ride
1 CONT Into England, to drive a pr ey.
1 CONT
1 CONT He chose the Gordons and the Graemes,
1 CONT With them the Lindesays, light an d gay;
1 CONT But the Jardines wald not with him ride,
1 CONT And they rue it to this day.
1 CONT
1 CONT And he has burned the dales of Tyne,
1 CONT And part of Bambrough shire,
1 CONT And thre e good towers on Reidswire fells,
1 CONT He left them all on fire.
1 CONT
1 CONT And he marched up to Newcastle,
1 CONT And rode it round about:
1 CONT "O wha's the lord of this castle?
1 CONT Or wha's the lady o't?"
1 CONT
1 CONT But up spake proud Lord Percy then,
1 CONT And O but he sp ake hie!
1 CONT I am the lord of this castle,
1 CONT My wife's the lady gay.
1 CONT
1 CONT "If thou'rt the lord of this castle,
1 CONT Sae weel it pleases me,
1 CONT For, ere I cross the Border fells,
1 CONT The tane of us shall die."
1 CONT
1 CONT He took a lang spear in his hand,
1 CONT Shod with the metal free,
1 CONT And for to meet the Douglas there
1 CONT He rode right furiousl ie.
1 CONT
1 CONT But O how pale his lady looked,
1 CONT Frae aff the castle-wa,
1 CONT When down befo re the Scottish spear
1 CONT She saw proud Percy fa.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Had we twa been upon the gre en,
1 CONT And never an eye to see,
1 CONT I wad hae had you, flesh and fell;
1 CONT But your swo rd sall gae wi me."
1 CONT
1 CONT The Otterbourne's a bonnie burn;
1 CONT 'Tis pleasant there to be;
1 CONT But there is nought at Otterbourne
1 CONT To feed my men and me.
1 CONT
1 CONT "The deer r ins wild on hill and dale,
1 CONT The birds fly wild frae tree to tree;
1 CONT But there is neither bread nor kale
1 CONT To fend my men and me."
1 CONT
1 CONT "Yet I will stay at Otterbo urne,
1 CONT Where you shall welcome be;
1 CONT And, if ye come not at three dayis end,
1 CONT A fause lord I'll ca thee."
1 CONT
1 CONT "Thither will I come," proud Percy said,
1 CONT "By the might of Our Ladye;"
1 CONT "There will I bide thee." said the Douglas,
1 CONT "My troth I plight to thee."
1 CONT
1 CONT They lighted high on Otterbourne,
1 CONT Upon the bent sae brown;
1 CONT They lighted high on Otterbourne,
1 CONT And threw their pallions down.
1 CONT
1 CONT And he t hat had a bonnie boy
1 CONT Sent out his horse to grass;
1 CONT And he that had not a bonni e boy
1 CONT His ain servant he was.
1 CONT
1 CONT But up then spake a little page,
1 CONT Before the peep of dawn:
1 CONT "O waken ye, waken ye, my good lord,
1 CONT For Percy's hard at hand."
1 CONT
1 CONT "Ye lie, ye lie, ye liar loud!
1 CONT Sae loud I hear ye lie:
1 CONT For Percy had not men yestreen
1 CONT To dight my men and me."
1 CONT
1 CONT "But I have dreamed a dreary dream,
1 CONT Beyond the Isle of Skye;
1 CONT I saw a dead man win a fight,
1 CONT And I think that man w as I."
1 CONT
1 CONT He belted on his guid braid sword,
1 CONT And to the field he ran,
1 CONT But he forgot the helmet good,
1 CONT That should have kept his brain.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Percy with th e Douglas met,
1 CONT I wat he was fu fain;
1 CONT They swakked their swords, till sair the y swat
1 CONT And the blood ran down like rain.
1 CONT
1 CONT But[Belcher.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [My Family.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT James, 2nd Earl of Douglas
1 CONT The 2nd Earl was born in 1358 and succeeded his fat her at a time of much border warfare between Scotland and England. Due to the o ld age of King Robert II, the Douglases were left in charge of the defense of t he kingdom. They l
1 CONC iterally became "the shield of Scotland". In 1388 the 2nd Ear l led a plundering sweep into England in retaliation for the devastation caused by King Richard's army three years earlier. While on this raid, Douglas met an d defeate
1 CONC d the renown English knight Henry "Hotspur" Percy in personal combat. In triumph he carried off Percy's pennon.
1 CONT
1 CONT In an effort to regain his pennon, and his honor, Percy pursued and engaged Douglas at the Battle of Otterburn. D ouglas, although mortally wounded in the battle, directed his captains to carry his standard, sound his battle cry a
1 CONC nd rally his troops. Even in death, he car ried the field. James left one illegitimate son, William, 1st Lord of Drumlanri g, who was the sire of the Queensberry cadet branch. As no legitimate heir was left, the earldom passed to 
1 CONC the "base born" son of Sir James "The Good".
1 CONT
1 CONT Ott erbourne
1 CONT
1 CONT A Scottish ode to James, 2nd Earl of Douglas and Sir Hugh Montgomer y, his nephew, who fought and defeated Henry "Hotspur" Percy on the field at Ot terburn in 1388.
1 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------- ------------- ------
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT It fell about the Lammas tide,
1 CONT When the muir-men win their hay,
1 CONT The doughty Douglas bound him to ride
1 CONT Into England, to drive a pr ey.
1 CONT
1 CONT He chose the Gordons and the Graemes,
1 CONT With them the Lindesays, light an d gay;
1 CONT But the Jardines wald not with him ride,
1 CONT And they rue it to this day.
1 CONT
1 CONT And he has burned the dales of Tyne,
1 CONT And part of Bambrough shire,
1 CONT And thre e good towers on Reidswire fells,
1 CONT He left them all on fire.
1 CONT
1 CONT And he marched up to Newcastle,
1 CONT And rode it round about:
1 CONT "O wha's the lord of this castle?
1 CONT Or wha's the lady o't?"
1 CONT
1 CONT But up spake proud Lord Percy then,
1 CONT And O but he sp ake hie!
1 CONT I am the lord of this castle,
1 CONT My wife's the lady gay.
1 CONT
1 CONT "If thou'rt the lord of this castle,
1 CONT Sae weel it pleases me,
1 CONT For, ere I cross the Border fells,
1 CONT The tane of us shall die."
1 CONT
1 CONT He took a lang spear in his hand,
1 CONT Shod with the metal free,
1 CONT And for to meet the Douglas there
1 CONT He rode right furiousl ie.
1 CONT
1 CONT But O how pale his lady looked,
1 CONT Frae aff the castle-wa,
1 CONT When down befo re the Scottish spear
1 CONT She saw proud Percy fa.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Had we twa been upon the gre en,
1 CONT And never an eye to see,
1 CONT I wad hae had you, flesh and fell;
1 CONT But your swo rd sall gae wi me."
1 CONT
1 CONT The Otterbourne's a bonnie burn;
1 CONT 'Tis pleasant there to be;
1 CONT But there is nought at Otterbourne
1 CONT To feed my men and me.
1 CONT
1 CONT "The deer r ins wild on hill and dale,
1 CONT The birds fly wild frae tree to tree;
1 CONT But there is neither bread nor kale
1 CONT To fend my men and me."
1 CONT
1 CONT "Yet I will stay at Otterbo urne,
1 CONT Where you shall welcome be;
1 CONT And, if ye come not at three dayis end,
1 CONT A fause lord I'll ca thee."
1 CONT
1 CONT "Thither will I come," proud Percy said,
1 CONT "By the might of Our Ladye;"
1 CONT "There will I bide thee." said the Douglas,
1 CONT "My troth I plight to thee."
1 CONT
1 CONT They lighted high on Otterbourne,
1 CONT Upon the bent sae brown;
1 CONT They lighted high on Otterbourne,
1 CONT And threw their pallions down.
1 CONT
1 CONT And he t hat had a bonnie boy
1 CONT Sent out his horse to grass;
1 CONT And he that had not a bonni e boy
1 CONT His ain servant he was.
1 CONT
1 CONT But up then spake a little page,
1 CONT Before the peep of dawn:
1 CONT "O waken ye, waken ye, my good lord,
1 CONT For Percy's hard at hand."
1 CONT
1 CONT "Ye lie, ye lie, ye liar loud!
1 CONT Sae loud I hear ye lie:
1 CONT For Percy had not men yestreen
1 CONT To dight my men and me."
1 CONT
1 CONT "But I have dreamed a dreary dream,
1 CONT Beyond the Isle of Skye;
1 CONT I saw a dead man win a fight,
1 CONT And I think that man w as I."
1 CONT
1 CONT He belted on his guid braid sword,
1 CONT And to the field he ran,
1 CONT But he forgot the helmet good,
1 CONT That should have kept his brain.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Percy with th e Douglas met,
1 CONT I wat he was fu fain;
1 CONT They swakked their swords, till sair the y swat
1 CONT And the blood ran down like rain.
1 CONT
1 CONT But
