0 @N6000000004533169958@ NOTE 2nd Earl of Douglas.
0 @N6000000002188101959@ NOTE James Douglas, 2nd Earl of Douglas
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir James Douglas, 2nd Earl of Douglas and Mar (c.1358 - 14 August 1388), was an influential lord in the Kingdom of Scotland. He was son and heir of William Douglas, 1st Earl of Douglas and Margaret, Countess of Mar.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1385 he made war on the English with the assistance of a French contingent under John de Vienne. He allowed the English to advance to Edinburgh, wisely refusing battle, and contented himself with a destructive counter-raid on C
1 CONC arlisle. Disputes soon arose between the allies, and the French returned home at the end of the year.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1388 Douglas captured Henry 'Hotspur' Percy's pennon in a skirmish near Newcastle. Percy sought revenge in the Battle of Otterburn in August of that year, although the Scots were victorious, and Hotspur and his brother were cap
1 CONC tured. James Douglas was killed in the fight. The battle, as narrated by Jean Froissart, forms the basis of the English and Scottish ballads The Ballad of Chevy Chase and The Battle of Otterburn.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage and issue
1 CONT Douglas married Isabel Stewart, a daughter of King Robert II of Scotland. He left no legitimate male issue. His natural sons William and Archibald became the ancestors of the families of Douglas of Drumlanrig (see Marquess of Quee
1 CONC nsberry) and Douglas of Cavers. His sister Isabel, inherited the lands and earldom of Mar, and the unentailed estates of Douglas.
1 CONT
1 CONT The earldom and entailed estates of Douglas reverted by the patent of 1358 to Archibald Douglas, called "The Grim", cousin of the 1st Earl and a natural son of "good" Sir James Douglas.
1 CONT
1 CONT Peerage of Scotland 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT William Douglas Earl of Douglas
1 CONT 1384-1388 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Archibald Douglas 
1 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Battle of Otterburn
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Battle of Otterburn 
1 CONT Part of Anglo-Scottish border conflicts 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Date 19 August 1388 
1 CONT Location 1 mile northwest of Otterburn, Northumberland 
1 CONT Result Scottish victory 
1 CONT
1 CONT Combatants 
1 CONT Scotland England 
1 CONT Commanders 
1 CONT James Douglas, 2nd Earl of Douglas† Henry "Hotspur" Percy 
1 CONT Strength 
1 CONT around 6000 around 8000 
1 CONT Casualties 
1 CONT around 500 1,800 + killed, wounded, and captured 
1 CONT The Battle of Otterburn took place on 19 August 1388, as part of the continuing border war between England and Scotland. Partly fought in moonlight, it was a victory for the Scots, led by James Douglas, 2nd Earl of Douglas over Ha
1 CONC rry Hotspur, son of the Earl of Northumberland. Douglas was killed in the battle, though his victory added to the prestige of his house, foremost among the border fighters of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 To Newcastle 
1 CONT 2 To Otterburn 
1 CONT 3 Hotspur's Attack 
1 CONT 4 'A Douglas!' 'A Douglas!' 
1 CONT 5 A Dead Man's Victory 
1 CONT 6 References 
1 CONT 7 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] To Newcastle
1 CONT When the latest truce with England ended in the high summer of 1388 the Scots began attacks on both the western and eastern marches, taking advantage of growing divisions between the Percies and the Nevilles, the English wardens. 
1 CONC In August the Earl of Douglas led a particularly bold move against the port of Newcastle. This was risky: he was not equipped to carry out a siege; Newcastle was one of the main muster points for English troops in the north, so it
1 CONC  was likely there would be more soldiers inside the town defending than outside attacking; and with the Earl of Northumberland at Alnwick, there was always a danger that his retreat would be cut off. But the very audacity of Dougl
1 CONC as' move had the effect of convincing the English that his force was only the vanguard of a much larger army close by. Frequent skirmishes took place at the outer defences of the western wall. In the account of Jean Froissart Doug
1 CONC las is said to have captured Hotspur's own pennon, though this story reads as if it has been added to provide some romantic colour, a technique in which the chronicler excels.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Hotspur woke on the morning of the 18th it was to find that his opponent had vanished in the night. By now the deception was clear, and he determined to set off in pursuit with all haste, before the enemy had a chance to slip
1 CONC  back across the border. Hotspur had at his disposal some 8000 troops and, true to his impetuous nature, he decided to set off at once, rather than wait for reinforcements promised by John de Fordham, the Bishop of Durham.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] To Otterburn
1 CONT After leaving Newcastle Douglas moved in a north-westerly direction, making for the valley of the River Rede, intending to take the same route back to Scotland by which he had entered England. He was in no particular hurry, despit
1 CONC e the obvious dangers of his situation. His force, of course, was weighed down with livestock and other booty; but when he reached the tower of Ponteland, a few miles from Newcastle, he paused to attack this unimportant obstacle, 
1 CONC thus alerting Hotspur to the direction of his retreat.
1 CONT
1 CONT By the evening of the same day he reached the valley of the Rede at a place called Otterburn. Here Douglas set up camp across the road, with his right flank close to the river and his left stretching out on the slope leading up to
1 CONC  the moors, approximately one mile beyond Otterburn Tower. Most of the following day-Wednesday 19 August-was spent in unsuccesful assaults on the tower. With his men tired by their exertions Douglas prepared to settle down for a s
1 CONC econd night on the banks of the Rede. Believing himself safe from attack he did not even take the precaution of posting sentries, an action suggesting a dangerous degree of overconfidence. But for the outcome of what was to follow
1 CONC  history is likely to have passed a very different verdict on the second Earl of Douglas.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Hotspur's Attack
1 CONT Hotspur made good progress in his march from Newcastle, but it is likely that he believed the enemy to be further ahead. When he entered the valley of the Rede in the dying summer light of the 19th he was simply looking for a plac
1 CONC e to camp: his men were tired and stretched out in a long column reaching back to Ponteland. But there, a short distance to the front, were the Scots. Two choices were open to him: to wait for the morning, allowing his men to rest
1 CONC  and regroup before beginning the battle, thus allowing the usual English superiority in the longbow to have its full effect; or to take the high-risk strategy of beginning an immediate attack, hoping to gain the advantage of surp
1 CONC rise. Hotspur would not wait for dawn: battle would be joined at once. To prevent the Scots slipping away he detached part of his force on a wide sweep to the north, past the Scottish left flank and then, in the word's of John Har
1 CONC dyng's Chronicle, to "holde them in that they fled not away" while the main body of the army launched a frontal attack.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] 'A Douglas!' 'A Douglas!'
1 CONT With the sudden approach of the English in the fading light there was considerable confusion in the Scottish camp, taken by complete surprise. The Chronicle of Pluscarden describes the scene thus;
1 CONT
1 CONT They rose at once and rushed to arms, but scarcely could a bare half of them arm themselves. The Earl of Douglas also rose, and in his haste could hardly put on his armour or fasten it with the buckles, owing to the confusion of t
1 CONC he sudden onslaught of the enemy; so he rushed forward with uncovered face to marshall the line of the battle.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gathering as many men as he could Douglas began a counter-attack that was to win him a battle and immortality. He approached Percy's right flank to the north, racing swiftly along a wooded hillside, with a slight depression coveri
1 CONC ng his approach for the last two hundred yards, before falling on the astonished Englishmen by the light of the autumn moon with loud cries of 'A Douglas!' 'A Douglas!' The ensuing battle was one of the strangest in all the Anglo-
1 CONC Scottish wars. Because of the poor visibility Percy was unable to make effective use of his archers. Each man fought in a grim hand-to-hand contest, with only enough light to see for a short distance around him. The spectral comba
1 CONC t ceased whenever clouds flitted across the face of the moon, allowing all a welcome rest in the darkness, only to begin again with renewed vigour when the wind carried them past. In these conditions the combat continued for sever
1 CONC al hours, amidst the shrieks of the wounded and dying, over ground slippery with blood.
1 CONT
1 CONT At some point during the night Earl James was killed, but by whom and in what manner is unknown, despite Froissart's theatrical account. Andrew of Wyntoun, the Scottish chronicler, simply says; "Bot Errl James thar was slane, that
1 CONC  na man whist on quhat manner." His body was found the following morning, stripped of his armour and with a great wound in his neck. Unaware of his death his comrades fought on, steadily pushing the English downhill. As dawn broke
1 CONC  Hotspur's army began to crumble, with men fleeing the field in increasing numbers. Hotspur was taken prisoner, as was his brother, Ralph, who had been badly wounded. Altogether over 1800 men were slain or captured. A number of Sc
1 CONC ots were also taken prisoner in their over-hasty pursuit of the English.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] A Dead Man's Victory
1 CONT When the Battle of Otterburn was being fought the Bishop of Durham was on his way from Newcastle with 2000 cavalry and 5000 infantry. They arrived at Ponteland on the morning of 20 August, where they met groups of men fleeing from
1 CONC  the battlefield, which had such a demoralising effect that the whole force retired.
1 CONT
1 CONT The principal cause of the English defeat is simply stated-Hotspur was a brave soldier but a bad commander, a truth summarised by the Westminster Chronicle;
1 CONT
1 CONT The calamity that befell our countrymen on this occasion of Otterburn was due in the first place to the heady spirit and excessive boldness of Sir Henry Percy, which caused our troops to go into battle in the disorder induced by h
1 CONC aste: and in the second place because the darkness played such tricks on the English that when they aimed a careless blow at a Scotsman, owing to the chorous of voices speaking the same language, it was an Englishman that they cut
1 CONC  down.
1 CONT
1 CONT When news of the defeat reached London the search for scapegoats began immediately. The obvious candidate was the Bishop of Durham, who was criticised by the Royal Council for arriving too late to help Hotspur. Curiously, no offic
1 CONC ial blame was attached to the commander himself for his military incompetence. He was generally perceived as a rather heroic figure, with King Richard and Parliament both contributing towards the cost of his ransom.
1 CONT
1 CONT The body of Douglas was taken back to Scotland and buried with all honour beside that of his father at Melrose Abbey. Of such renown was the battle of Otterburn that several ballads were later composed in its honour including The 
1 CONC Battle of Otterburn and The Ballad of Chevy Chase (Child ballads 161 and 162). Chevy Chase rather mangles the history of the battle and may be confusing it with other conflicts at around the same time, but it is still cited as one
1 CONC  of the best of the ancient ballads. In The Battle of Otterburn Douglas has a premonition of his death;
1 CONT
1 CONT Last night I dreamed a dreary dream,
1 CONT
1 CONT From beyond the Isle of Skye,
1 CONT
1 CONT I saw a dead man win a fight,
1 CONT
1 CONT And I think that man was I.
1 CONT
1 CONT A dead man had indeed won a fight.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT PRIMARY
1 CONT
1 CONT Froissart, Jean, Chronicles, 1903 ed. 
1 CONT Hardyng, John, Chronicles, 1812. 
1 CONT Pluscarden, the Book of, ed. F. H. Skene, 1880. 
1 CONT The Westminster Chronicle, 1381-1394, ed. and trans. by L. C. Hector and B. F. Harvey, 1982. 
1 CONT Wyntoun, Andrew of, Origynal Chronicle of Scotland, ed F. J. Amours, 1908. 
1 CONT SECONDARY
1 CONT
1 CONT Grant, A., The Otterburn War from a Scottish Point of View, in War and Border Societies in the Middle Ages, ed A. Goodman and A. Took, 1992. 
1 CONT Robson, J., Border Battles and Battlefields, 1897. 
1 CONT Tyson, C, The Battle of Otterburn: When and Where was it Fought?, in War And Border Societies, see above. 
1 CONT Walton, R. H., The Otterburn Story, in History of the Berwickshire Naturalist Club, vol. 35, 1961. 
1 CONT Wesencraft, C. F., The Battle of Otterburn, 1988. 
1 CONT White, R. H., The History of the Battle of Otterburn, 1857. 
