0 @N6000000002187911984@ NOTE William Douglas, 8th Earl of Douglas
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT William Douglas, 8th Earl of Douglas, 2nd Earl of Avondale (1425–February 22, 1452), was a Scottish nobleman. He was the eldest son of James Douglas, 7th Earl of Douglas and Beatrice Sinclair.
1 CONT
1 CONT The power of the 'Black' Douglases, lost after the execution of the 6th Earl, was restored by the 8th Earl, who recovered the lordships of Wigtown, Galloway and Bothwell by marriage (by papal dispensation) to his cousin, the Fair 
1 CONC Maid of Galloway (daughter of the 5th Earl. He was soon high in favour with James II, and procured the disgrace of Sir William Crichton, presumed murderer of his kinsman the 6th Earl, by an alliance with his rival, Sir Alexander L
1 CONC ivingstone.
1 CONT
1 CONT James II raided the Douglas lands during his absence on a pilgrimage to Rome. Despite this, their relations seemed outwardly friendly until in 1452 the king invited Douglas to Stirling Castle under a safe-conduct, in itself, howev
1 CONC er, a proof of strained relations. There James demanded the dissolution of a league into which Douglas had entered with Alexander Lindsay, the 'Tiger' earl of Crawford. On Douglas' refusal the king murdered him with his own hands,
1 CONC  the courtiers helping to despatch him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dying without issue, his titles passed to his brother James.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT James Douglas Earl of Douglas
1 CONT 1443–1452 Succeeded by:
1 CONT James Douglas
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT James Douglas Earl of Avondale
1 CONT 1443–1452 Succeeded by:
1 CONT James Douglas
0 @N6000000002187742527@ NOTE John Stewart, 1st Earl of Atholl
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Stewart, 1st Earl of Atholl (born c. 1440– September 19, 1512), also known as Sir John Stewart of Balveny, was created the 1st Earl of Atholl in around 1457.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the oldest child of Joan Beaufort, widowed Queen of James I of Scotland, and Sir James Stewart, the Black Knight of Lorn.
1 CONT
1 CONT He having married twice, first to Margaret (Beatrix) Douglas also known as the 'fair Maid of Gallowy' with whom he had 2 daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Janet Stewart 
1 CONT Elizabeth Stewart. 
1 CONT From his second marriage to Eleanor St Clair, nine children were born:
1 CONT
1 CONT John Stewart, 2nd Earl of Atholl 
1 CONT Andrew Stewart. the Bishop of Caithness 
1 CONT Lady Jean Stewart 
1 CONT Catherine Stewart 
1 CONT Elspeth Stewart (who married Sir Robert Innes of Innermarky) 
1 CONT Marjory Steart 
1 CONT Margaret Steart 
1 CONT Isabel Steart 
1 CONT Lady Anne Stewart 
1 CONT He is believed to have had a hand in suppressing the rebellion of the John, Earl of Ross, the last of the Lord of the Isles, and it is reported that it from this that his nephew King James III took these words as his motto.
