0 @N6000000006444307105@ NOTE [Brøderbund WFT Vol. 27, Ed. 1, Tree #0482, Date of Import: Aug 16, 2003]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Stewarts of Appin are a West Highland clan descended from Sir
1 CONT      John Stewart of Bonkyl, the son of Alexander, High Steward of
1 CONT      Scotland. His younger son, Sir James fought at Bannockburn and was
1 CONT      eventually killed at the Battle of Halidon Hill in 1333. His grandson
1 CONT      gained the Lordship of Lorn through marriage to the heiress. A later
1 CONT      descen dant, Sir John Stewart was murdered at Dunstaffnage castle
1 CONT      in 1463 and his son Dugald became the first chief of Appin. He
1 CONT      unsuccessfully tried to recover the Lordship of Lorn and was
1 CONT      supported by the Maclarens of Balquidder. The clan fought at Flodden
1 CONT      in 1513 and Pinkie in 1547; during the 1644-45 campaign they
1 CONT      supported the Marquis of Montrose. Because of this the chief of Appin
1 CONT      was outlawed and had his lands forfeited. These were later returned at
1 CONT      the Restoration. The clan joined Dundee's campaign in 1688 and
1 CONT      supported the Jacobites in the Risings of 1715 and 1745. After the
1 CONT      battle of Culloden in which over 100 of the clan were killed the banner
1 CONT      of the Appin Regiment was one of the few saved from destruction.
1 CONT      Clan feuds with the Campbells continued even after the rebellion was
1 CONT      quashed. Colin Roy Campbell was murdered in Appin in 1752 ; this
1 CONT      was the famous Appin murder on which R L Stevenson based his
1 CONT      novels "Kidnapped" and "Catriona". Since the Campbells demanded
1 CONT      blood for this outrage, James 
1 CONT
1 CONT      Stewart of the Glens was hanged although clearly innocent. This was the last death in Scotland resulting from a
1 CONT      clan feud; a cairn marks the site of his execution. Dugald, 9th chief sold Appin in 1765 and on his death was
1 CONT      succeeded by his cousin, Duncan Stewart, 6th of Ardshiel who became 10th Chief of Appin. The chiefship
1 CONT      remains in this line today. 
