0 @N6000000002329575523@ NOTE George Douglas
1 CONT
1 CONT George Douglas, 1st Earl of Angus, was born c1376. He married on 24 May 1387 to Lady Mary Stewart, (daughter of King Robert III of Scotland). They had three children:
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Elizabeth Douglas (b. c1397; m. Alexander Forbes)
1 CONT
1 CONT    2. William Douglas
1 CONT
1 CONT    3. Mary Douglas (m. Sir David Hay [1421/34-by 1Mar1478]; son:)
1 CONT
1 CONT          1. John Hay (1st Lord Hay of Yester)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT George, 1st Earl, is said to have died of the Plague sometime after 04 September 1402 in England. After his death, Lady Mary remarried several times.
0 @N6000000002187899678@ NOTE George Douglas, 1st Earl of Angus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT George Douglas, 1st Earl of Angus (1380–1403) was born at Tantallon Castle, East Lothian, Scotland. The bastard son of William, 1st Earl of Douglas and Margaret Stewart, Dowager Countess of Mar & Countess of Angus in her own right
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT He was seen as the product of incest as his mother was the widow of Earl William's wife's brother, Thomas, 13th Earl of Mar.
1 CONT
1 CONT Earl William's legitimate wife Margaret of Mar had already produced an heir for her Lord in 1358, James, 2nd Earl of Douglas and Mar inherited upon his father's death in 1384
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1389, Margaret of Angus relinquished her title in favour of her son, but he did not assume it until his betrothal in 1397 to the princess Mary, daughter of King Robert III. Margaret of Angus' influence must have been considerab
1 CONC le- in addittion to obtaining a royal bride for her illegitimate son, she peruaded King Robert to confirm him in his style of Earl of Angus, and also to bestow upon him the lordships of Abernethy, (Perthshire) and Bonkill, (Berwic
1 CONC kshire); and to "to endow him and his spouse with the justiciary fees of the County of Forfar, to ratify all gifts, entails, and leases made or to be made by Isabel, Countess of Mar, to the said Jorge her brothir" -(Maxwell).
1 CONT
1 CONT James 2nd Earl of Douglas, was killed without issue in 1388, at the Battle of Otterburn, and the Earldom of Mar, and all non-entailed Douglas possessions passed to his sister Isabel. The earldom of Douglas passed to a cousin, a ba
1 CONC stard son of The Good Sir James Douglas, the aptly named Archibald the Grim.
1 CONT
1 CONT The descendants of Archbald formed the famed Black line, and those of George the equally famed and longer lived Red Line.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT A History of the House of Douglas, Rt.Hon.Sir Herbert Maxwell. Freemantle, London 1902 
1 CONT Ane Historie of the House and Race of Douglas and Angus, Hume of Godscroft. Edinburgh 1646 
1 CONT The Black Douglases, Michael Brown. Tuckwell press, East Linton, East Lothian 1998 
1 CONT From the Bloody Heart, Oliver Thomson. Sutton, Stroud, Gloucs. 2003 
0 @N6000000003043224917@ NOTE George was the illegitimate son of William, Earl of Douglas by his first cousin, the Countess of Angus and Mar. He inherited his mother's title and possessions in 1389 making him the 1st Earl of Angus.
