0 @N6000000002187746044@ NOTE Joan Beaufort, Countess of Westmoreland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan Beaufort, Countess of Westmorland, (c.1379 - 13 November 1440), was the fourth child (and only daughter) of John of Gaunt and his mistress Katherine Swynford. Joan married Robert Ferrers, 3rd Baron Ferrers of Wemme, and they 
1 CONC had two daughters before he died. Along with her three brothers, Joan was privately declared legitimate by their cousin Richard II of England in 1390, but for some reason their father secured another such declaration from Parliame
1 CONC nt in January 1397. Perhaps the reason was that on 3 February 1397, when she was 18, Joan married Ralph Neville, 1st Earl of Westmorland, who had also been married once before. They had at least ten children, one of whom was Cecil
1 CONC y Neville (1415 - 1495) ("Proud Cis"), who married Richard, Duke of York (1411 - 1460), and two of their children became Edward IV of England and Richard III of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan died on 13 November 1440 and was entombed next to her mother in the sanctuary of Lincoln Cathedral. Joan's is the smaller of the two tombs; both were decorated with brass plates -- full-length representations of them on the t
1 CONC ops, and small shields bearing coats of arms around the sides -- but those were damaged or destroyed in 1644 during the English Civil War. A 1640 drawing of them survives, showing what the tombs looked like when they were intact, 
1 CONC and side-by-side instead of end-to-end, as they are now
0 @N6000000007329673284@ NOTE [Enc. Brit.] granddau. of John of Gaunt and Catherine Swynford.
0 @N6000000008630295669@ NOTE Ahnetafel Chart. Gerry Hill File.
0 @N6000000002134131886@ NOTE Joan Beaufort   (1407-1445)
1 CONT Born circa 1407
1 CONT Died 15 July 1445 Dunbar Castle
1 CONT Married (1) 2 February 1424 St.Mary Overy, Southwark
1 CONT James I, King of Scots 1406-1424-1437, son of Robert III,
1 CONT King of Scots 1390-1406 and Annabella Drummond
1 CONT Born December 1394 Dunfermline
1 CONT Died 21 February 1437 Perth (murdered)
1 CONT Married (2) 21 September 1439 *
1 CONT Sir James Stewart, "the Black Knight of Lorne", son of Sir
1 CONT John Stewart, Lord of Lorne and Innermeath, 1st of
1 CONT Grandtully and Isabel de Ergadia
1 CONT
1 CONT During his imprisonment in England, James I, King of Scots, fell in love with her and wrote of her in his poem "The Kingis Quair" ('The King's quire-filling poem', or one of 24 sheets---a quire).
1 CONT Eventually, by the Treaty of London in December 1423, it was agreed that James I should be released for a ransom of ?40,000. In February 1424, before his return to Scotland, he married her and they became the parents of eight chil
1 CONC dren.
1 CONT After an imprisonment of eighteen years, James I was determined to enforce the rule of law, which meant disaster for those who had ruled in his absence. Resentment grew against his tough style of
1 CONT government and, in February 1437 when the King and his court were in residence in Perth, a plot led by Sir Robert Graham caused the murder of one of Scotland's ablest rulers.
1 CONT Queen Joan, who had been badly wounded while trying to protect her husband, took a terrible revenge. Sir Robert Graham and his fellow conspirators were soon captured and put to death with indescribable
1 CONT barbarity, unusual even for that cruel age.
1 CONT In 1439, probably against her will, she married Sir James Stewart, known at "the Black Knight of Lorne", and they produced three more sons.
0 @N6000000002322286664@ NOTE This union with her was an unhappy one, a union formed solely for political
1 CONT reasons. Father Charles and son Louis became wholly estranged after the death
1 CONT of Margaret in 1445, to whom the father had been attached. Detected in a plot
1 CONT against Breze (advisor to Charles), Louis was exiled to Daulphine, France,
1 CONT never to see his father again. In Daulphine, young Louis served his
1 CONT apprenticeship as a ruler, eagerly awaiting his turn as King of France,
1 CONT keeping himself posted by spies with every detail of his father's ill health.
