0 @N6000000002322291803@ NOTE James II reigned from 1437-1460. His father, James I, fell victim to
1 CONT conspiracy and James II, age 6, became King, 1437-1460. James II was killed
1 CONT in an Artillery demonstration, hit by "a piece of mis-framed gun that brak in
1 CONT the shooting", leaving a young James III under regency of Mary Guildres, then
1 CONT Bishop James Kennedy, and finally unscrululous Lord Boyd of Kilmarnock, high
1 CONT justican, after whom James III assumed control in 1469 when Lord Boyd died.
0 @N6000000002187788067@ NOTE James II of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT James II of Scotland (October 16, 1430 – August 3, 1460) was king of Scotland from 1437 to 1460.
1 CONT
1 CONT James was son of James I of Scotland and Joan Beaufort, daughter of John Beaufort, 1st Earl of Somerset and Margaret Holland. He had an elder twin, Alexander Stewart, Duke of Rothesay, who lived long enough to be knighted, but die
1 CONC d in infancy. He was later father of James III. He was known as "Fiery face" because of a conspicuous vermilion birthmark on his face. He also had six sisters, who were married into the royal dynasties of Europe.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Child King 
1 CONT 2 Struggles with the Douglases 
1 CONT 3 Effective ruler 
1 CONT 4 Marriage and children 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Child King
1 CONT Inheriting the throne at under seven years old, most of James' reign saw the government in the hands of others. James I's death had been an attempt to usurp power by Walter Stewart, 1st Earl of Atholl, but it failed miserably and 
1 CONC Atholl and his allies were captured and executed in the months after the assassination.
1 CONT
1 CONT The government was led (1437-1439) by Archibald Douglas, 5th Earl of Douglas, as lieutenant-general of the realm. After his death, and with a general lack of high status earls in Scotland because of death, forfeiture or their youn
1 CONC g age, power was shared uneasily between William, 1st lord Crichton, Lord Chancellor of Scotland, sometimes in cooperation with James 'the gross' Douglas, Earl of Buchan, and Sir Alexander Livingston of Callendar, who had possessi
1 CONC on of the young king in his stronghold of Stirling Castle.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1440 Stirling was also the location for the 'Black Dinner' where the young William Douglas, 6th Earl of Douglas and his brother were summarily executed. The blame has tended to be put at the feet of Crichton, Livingston and par
1 CONC ticularly Buchan, as Douglas's death brought him the earldom of Douglas (as 7th earl of Douglas), and the position as the most powerful magnate in Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT The precise details of who ran the government year by year between 1439 and 1445 are complex and far from certain, but in 1445 the Livingstons cooperated with William Douglas, 8th Earl of Douglas, son of the recently deceased 7th 
1 CONC Earl, to throw Crichton from power. Douglas now took on the lead in governing Scotland until 1449, placing his brothers and other family members in positions of power. Yet the wily Crichton soon returned to influence, now cooperat
1 CONC ing with the Douglases.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Struggles with the Douglases
1 CONT In 1449 James II emerged into adulthood, yet in many ways his 'active kingship' was little different from his minority. The Douglases used his emergence into adulthood as a way to throw the Livingstons out of the shared government
1 CONC , as the young king took revenge for the brief arrest of his mother (in turn as a means to remove her from political influence) that had taken place in 1439. Douglas and Crichton continued to dominate political power, and the king
1 CONC 's ability to rule without them was arguably limited.
1 CONT
1 CONT But James was a not a king to put up with this situation without argument, and between 1451 and 1455 he struggled to free himself from the power of the Douglases. Attempts to curb Douglas power were seen in 1451, when Douglas was 
1 CONC out of the country, and culminated with the murder of the 8th Earl of Douglas at Stirling Castle on February 22, 1452.
1 CONT
1 CONT The king had accused the earl, probably with justification, of making bonds with John Macdonald, 11th Earl of Ross (aka the Lord of the Isles) and Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Crawford. This bond would have created a dangerous a
1 CONC xis of powerful and independently-minded men that was a major threat to royal power. When, allegedly, Douglas refused to break the bond, James flew into a temper and stabbed Douglas to death. His court officials (many of whom woul
1 CONC d rise to great influence in later years, often in former Douglas lands) then joined in the bloodbath, one even striking out the earl's brains with an axe.
1 CONT
1 CONT This murder did not remove the power of the Douglases, but rather created a state of intermittent civil war between 1452 and 1455. James attempted to seize Douglas lands, but was forced into repeated climb-downs, whereby he return
1 CONC ed the lands to James Douglas, 9th Earl of Douglas and a brief and uneasy peace ensued.
1 CONT
1 CONT Military campaigns ended indecisively, and it has been argued by some that James was in serious danger of being overthrown, or having to flee the country. But James' patronage of lands, titles and office to allies of the Douglases
1 CONC  saw their allies begin to change sides, most tellingly the Earl of Crawford, and in 1455 James was finally able to make a decisive blow against the Douglases, and they were finally defeated at the battle of Arkinholm in May 1455.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the months that followed the extensive Douglas lands were forfeited and permanently annexed to the crown by the Parliament of Scotland, as were many other lands, finances and castles. The earl fled into a long English exile. Ja
1 CONC mes was finally free to govern as he wished, and it can be argued that his successors as kings of Scots never faced such a powerful challenge to their authority again. Along with the forfeiture of the Albany Stewarts in reign of J
1 CONC ames I, the destruction of the Black Douglases saw royal power in Scotland take a major step forward.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Effective ruler
1 CONT Between 1455 and 1460 James II proved to be an active and interventionist king. Ambitions to take Orkney and Shetland and the Isle of Man to Scotland were not successful. The king travelled the country, and seems to have originate
1 CONC d the practice of raising money by giving remissions for serious crimes. In 1458 an act of parliament seems to criticise the king, but it is difficult to say how his reign would have developed had he lived longer.
1 CONT
1 CONT James was an enthusiast for modern artillery, which he used with some success against the Black Douglases. His ambitions to increase Scotland's standing saw him besiege Roxburgh castle in 1460, one of the last Scottish castles sti
1 CONC ll held by the English after the Wars of Independence. On August 3 James was killed when one of his cannons exploded. The Scots carried on with the siege and took the castle.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lindsay of Pitscottie, about 1728, wrote concerning the accident that befell King James II as he was standing near a piece of artillery, that "his thigh-bone was dung in two with a piece of misframed gun that brake in shooting, by
1 CONC  the which he was stricken to the ground and died hastily."
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Marriage and children
1 CONT He married Mary of Gueldres at Holyrood Abbey, Edinburgh, on July 3, 1449. They had seven children:
1 CONT
1 CONT An unnamed son. Both born and died on 19 May 1450). 
1 CONT James III of Scotland (1451/1452 - 1488). 
1 CONT Alexander Stewart, 1st Duke of Albany (c. 1454 - 1485). 
1 CONT David Stewart, Earl of Moray (c. 1456 - 1457. He was created Earl of Moray on 12 February 1456. 
1 CONT John Stewart, 1st Earl of Mar and Garioch (c. 1459 - 1479). 
1 CONT Princess Margaret Stewart of Scotland. Married William Crichton, 3rd Lord Crichton of Auchingoul. She was mother of Margaret Crichton and mother-in-law of George Leslie, 4th Earl of Rothes. 
1 CONT Princess Mary Stewart of Scotland (d. 1488). Married first Thomas Boyd, 1st Earl of Arran and secondly James Hamilton, 1st Lord Hamilton. She was mother of James Hamilton, 1st Earl of Arran. 
1 CONT He was also father to Sir John Stewart of Sticks (d. 1507), an illegitimate son.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT James I King of Scots Succeeded by:
1 CONT James III 
0 @N6000000002134213190@ NOTE JAMES II, KING OF SCOTS (1430-60)
1 CONT James II acceded to the throne at the age of six, and it seemed as if most of his father's restoration of a strong, central authority was lost during the struggles of various Scottish nobles to assert their own authority over the 
1 CONC new king.
1 CONT
1 CONT When James reached the age of 19, it was time for him to take command and try to restore royal authority. Part of his troubles with the Scottish nobles was erased when young Douglas (who had formed alliances with other Scottish lo
1 CONC rds) was killed by a dagger-wielding friend of the king himself (some sources give the king as the assassin). James then routed all those in Scotland in opposition to his reign. South of the border, the English nation was having i
1 CONC ts own troubles in the Wars of the Roses. Scotland enjoyed a period of peace, but the foolhardy decision of James to intervene in the English civil wars shattered everything.
1 CONT
1 CONT When a canon prematurely exploded at the siege of Roxburgh Castle, James lost his life and Scotland lost another chance to settle down and mature politically and economically (it had already begun its intellectual transformation w
1 CONC ith the founding of its three great universities). Intrigue and counter intrigue then became the order of the day.
0 @N6000000002176649182@ NOTE James II reigned from 1437-1460. His father, James I, fell victim to
1 CONT conspiracy and James II, age 6, became King, 1437-1460. James II was killed
1 CONT in an Artillery demonstration, hit by "a piece of mis-framed gun that brak in
1 CONT the shooting", leaving a young James III under regency of Mary Guildres, then
1 CONT Bishop James Kennedy, and finally unscrululous Lord Boyd of Kilmarnock, high
1 CONT justican, after whom James III assumed control in 1469 when Lord Boyd died.
