0 @N6000000003615781356@ NOTE Sir James de Innes, 12th of that Ilk; Esquire of the Body to James III, present at Battle of Blackness 1488; married 1st Lady Janet Gordon, daughter of 1st Earl of Huntly; married 2nd c1473 Margaret Culane, widow of William Roland
1 CONC , and died c1491.  [Burke's Peerage]
1 CONT
1 CONT ------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Thanks to James Pringle Weavers for the following information
1 CONT
1 CONT INNES: Derived from the Gaelic 'Innes' - an island, and such featured in the lands between the Spey and the Lossie granted by Malcolm IV to the Fleming 'Berowald' in 1160. His grandson was first to adopt the territorial name of wh
1 CONC ich lands he received confirmation from Alexander II in 1226. Sir Alexander, 9th Laird, married the heiress to the Thanedom of Aberchirder and by such consolidated his holdings and posterity. Their son, Sir Walter, was chief for 4
1 CONC 2 years, and dying in 1454, he was succeeded by 'Ill Sir Robert', 11th Laird and the Sheriff-depute of Moray who gained infamy by his wicked ways before expiated much of his sins by founding the Greyfriars of Elgin. His successor 
1 CONC was Esquire to James III and entertained James IV with much style at Innes in 1490.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir James, 12th of Innes was chief esquire to James III and entertained James IV at Innes Castle in 1490.
