0 @N6000000001563059457@ NOTE He was the son and successor of Robert Bruce, at whose death, in 1328, he was only five years old. On the invasion of Scotland by Baliol, David was sent to France; but his party prevailed, after a bloody contest, and he returned h
1 CONC ome in 1342. He made several inroads on England, but was taken prisoner at the battle of Nevil's Cross, 1346, conveyed to the Tower, and did not recover his liberty till 1357, on paying a heavy ransom. Died, 1371.  Succeeded by hi
1 CONC s Nephew Robert Stewart.
0 @N6000000002322328651@ NOTE Son of Robert Bruce, succeeded his father at 5 years of age, but in 1331,
1 CONT just  after coronation, Edward de Baliol, an English-backed claimant to the
1 CONT throne disposed him. David lived in exile in France for 8 years, & 5 years
1 CONT later was defeated in battle & incarcerated for 11 years 'til ransom released
1 CONT him in 1357. Thereafter, David regained his throne and enjoyed friendly
1 CONT relations with the English. His nephew, Robert II, succeeded him in 1371, the
1 CONT founder of the Stuart (Stewart) dynasty.
