0 @N6000000003043431717@ NOTE Sir John Montgomerie V was married, in 1361 according to one source or in 1372 according to another, to Elizabeth, the daughter and sole heiress of Sir Hugh Eglington of Eglington, one of the two great Justiciaries of Scotland dur
1 CONC ing the reign of David II.  Sir Hugh Eglington died in 1376 or 1378, leaving Sir John Montgomerie V the master of Eglington, Egglesham, Eastwood, Thorntoun  and Innerwick. Sir John and his son took part in the battle of Otterburn 
1 CONC in 1388, where he personally took prisoner the renownwed 'Hotspur', Henry Lord Percy; with the ransom which he received, he built Punone Castle at Egglesham, and had a carved spur over the entry to commemorate this event.  He died
1 CONC  in 1393, having held the properties for only about five years.  His wife Elizabeth of Egglington survived him.  They had four sons:
0 @N6000000003216110281@ NOTE Captured Harry Hotspur in the Battle of Otterburn in 1388.  The ransom he received for him started the Montgomery fortunes in Scotland, and he became the ancestor of the Earls of Eglington and Lairds of Braidstane.  Built the Cast
