0 @N6000000003216110273@ NOTE Hostage for the Earl of Douglas.
0 @N6000000003043431706@ NOTE Notes from Sally Walmsley <Geniedash@bigpond.com>:
1 CONT succeeded his father in 1393.   In 1402 he was one of the leaders of the Scottish army which was defeated at Hallidon Hill, where he was taken prisoner.    However, he was back in Scotland by 1404.  From 1408 to 1412 he returned t
1 CONC o England as a hostage for Archibald the fourth Earl of Douglas. He reputedly introduced the fugitive King Richard Ii of England to King Robert III of Scotland.   In 1423 he was one of the hostages sent to England to obtain the re
1 CONC lease of King James I: in 1425 he reduced a fortress on Loch Lomond held by Lord James Stewart against King James I.  In the same year, he was one of the peers who sat in judement on Murac, Duke of Albany.  Most authorities state 
1 CONC that he was married to Margaret the daughter of Sir Robert Maxwell of Caerlaverock, but one says that he was married to Agnes the daughter of Herbert Lord Maxwell.  he seems to have died in 1429 or 1430, leaving six children.  Sir
1 CONC  John held the honour of 'dominus Ejusdem', which showed that he was male heir and chief of the French house of Montgomerie, and was referred to as Lord Montgomerie, again a courtsey title implying that he was head of the family. 
1 CONC  His children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir Alexander Montgomerie, who succeeded
1 CONT Sir Robert Montgomerie, of Giffen
1 CONT Hugh Montgomerie
1 CONT Anne Montgomerie
1 CONT Janet Montgomerie - who married Thomas Boyd III
1 CONT Isabel Montgomerie
1 CONT
1 CONT Note: 18/1/01
1 CONT Other sources I have found on the Internet - all yet to be verified - state that his wife was 'Agnes of the Isles'.   On a posting in 'gen-medieval' from 16 December 1999, one John Ravilious says: A gap I noticed in the table of a
1 CONC ncestry for Dorothy Duninghame, was 'Agnes of the Isles', 1st wife of Sir John Montgomery, laird of Eaglesham and Ardrossan (d.ca. 1428).  Paul, in Scots Peerage, did not draw any connection directly to the MacDonalds of the Isles
1 CONC , but this has been done subsequently by others who have shown that she is Agnes, or Annes, daughter of Donald mac Donald, Lord of the Isles (d. 1423) by Margaret Leslie, heiress of Ross."  - yet more to investigate!
1 CONT
1 CONT Jared Olar in another post says: "'Agnes of the Isles' was Sir John Montgomerie's FIRST wife.  Scots Peerage, vol. III, p. 430, tells us that Agnes died before March 1413/3 adn that Sir John probably died a hostage in England abou
1 CONC t 1428."
