0 @N6000000005940228036@ NOTE of Gleneagles
0 @N6000000002188032379@ NOTE Name: John (Sir) Maxwell 
1 CONT Sex: M 
1 CONT Death: 1346 in In Tower of London after capture ar Neville's Cross. 
1 CONT Note: 
1 CONT "Caerlaverock's First Castle
1 CONT When Sir John Maxwell was granted the Caerlaverock estate in around 1220, he began to build a castle here. It seems that within fifty years this first castle had been abandoned, and the castle that you see today was built on an ou
1 CONC tcrop of rock. Before last year's dig, all that was visible of the old castle was a grassy earthen mound, surrounded by a moat. In 1998 the archaeologists uncovered a series of buildings on the castle mound, enclosed by a curtain 
1 CONC wall with corner towers. "
1 CONT From "The Caerlaverock Dig " at 
1 CONT http://www.suat.demon.co.uk/caerlaverock/
1 CONT
1 CONT "Caerlaverock Castle
1 CONT Surrounded by a double moat and hundreds of acres of flat marshy willow woods (known in Scotland as a "moss"), Caerlaverock was built to control the South-West entrance to Scotland which in early times was the waterway across the 
1 CONC Solway Firth. 
1 CONT Building began in about 1277, and by 1300 it was besieged by Edward I during his war against the Scottish king John (Balliol), a war still remembered for the brave resistance put up by the Scots under Sir William Wallace.
1 CONT
1 CONT Its triangular plan is unique in Scotland, and during the seige of 1300 it was noted as being 
1 CONT
1 CONT ". . . so strong a castle that it feared no siege... in shape it was like a shield for it has but three sides round it, with a tower at each corner... And I think that you will never see a more finely situated castle, for on one s
1 CONC ide can be seen the Irish Sea, towards the west and to the north the fair moorland, surrounded by an arm of the sea, so that no creature born can approach it on two sides without putting himself in danger of the sea. On the south 
1 CONC side it is not easy, for there are many places difficult to get through because of woods and marshes and ditches hollowed out by the sea where it meets the river." 
1 CONT From AboutScotland "Caerlaverock Castle" at http://www.aboutscotland.com/caer/caer.html
1 CONT
1 CONT UK Travel
1 CONT http://www.uktravel.com/castlecontent.asp?timeID=Caerlavero&offset=10
1 CONT Dumfries and Galloway Tourist Board
1 CONT http://www.visit-dumfries-and-galloway.co.uk/seeanddo/historicattractions/caerlaverock
1 CONT Historic Scotland 
1 CONT http://www.historic-scotland.gov.uk/op_ourproperties_searchdetail?id=90046
