0 @N6000000003615777401@ NOTE James Sandilands [2nd son], 1st Lord of Torphichen or perhaps Lord Saint John/Saint John of Torphichen, so created effectively 24 Jan 1563/4 by a regrant to himself and his heirs and assignees of the lands, (territorial) baronies,
1 CONC  estates and offices previously attaching to the office of Prior of the Knights of St John of Jerusalem in Scotland, he having held that post from 1546, just as Scotland was being transformed by the Reformation, of which he was a 
1 CONC supporter (the Priors of the Knights of St John had previously been entitled to a seat in Parliament among both the prelates and, through holding the feudal Barony of Torphichen, the greater Barons); Knight Order of St John 1530; 
1 CONC married a short while before 12 Sep 1560 Janet (married 2nd by 9 June 1584 John Grahame of Hallyards, married 3rd 6 May 1596 Sir Peter Young of Seaton and died 29 Nov 1596), daughter of William Murray of Polmaise and Touchadam, an
1 CONC d dsp 29 Sep 1579.  [Burke's Peerage]
1 CONT
1 CONT Note: Burke's Peerage states that James dsp., and that Alison, whom I have as his daughter was really his sister.
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT copied from www.geo.ed.ac.uk
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Torphichen
1 CONT West Lothian
1 CONT
1 CONT A village located 2 miles (3 km) north of Bathgate. Torphichen Preceptory, north of the village was the Scottish seat of the Knights of St John of Jerusalem (the Knights Hospitaller). They were established in the 12th century and 
1 CONC their church was finished in the mid-13th century, some of it surviving today. The estate surrounding the church provided sanctuary ground to criminals and debtors. Other buildings in Torphichen include the parish kirk (1756), and
1 CONC  three mansions in various states of repair: Wallhouse (1840), Couston (early 17th-century), and Bridge (15th-century). To the west is Gowanbank (1842-62), the seat of architect Sir James Gowans (1821-990), was built around an ear
1 CONC ly 19th-century farmhouse originally erected by his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT   ©1995-2002 Gazetteer for Scotland. Supported by:
1 CONT The Robertson Trust, The Carnegie Trust for the Universities of Scotland,
1 CONT The Royal Scottish Geographical Society, The Department of Geography, University of Edinburgh.
