0 @N6000000002187768953@ NOTE Colin Campbell, 1st Earl of Argyll
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Colin Campbell, 1st Earl of Argyll, Baron Campbell of Lorn (c. 1433 - May 10, 1493) was a Scottish nobleman. He was created Earl of Argyll in 1457; he had supported King James II against the "Black Douglases", led by the 8th Earl 
1 CONC of Douglas, and was given the peerage by King James III. James also gave him the position of Lord Chancellor of Scotland, but he eventually collaborated in the slaying of James III in 1484. His oldest son was Archibald Campbell, 2
1 CONC nd Earl of Argyll, his middle son was Thomas Campbell, and his youngest son was Sir Colin Campbell of Glen Orchy (who was the ancestor of the Earls of Breadalbane).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Duncan Campbell Lord Campbell
1 CONT 1453–1493 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Archibald Campbell 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT New Creation Earl of Argyll
1 CONT 1457–1493 
1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Colin Campbell, 1st Earl of Argyll1 (M)
1 CONT b. between 1435 and 1440, d. 10 May 1493, #109258
1 CONT Pedigree
1 CONT Last Edited=25 Oct 2005
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT      Colin Campbell, 1st Earl of Argyll was born between 1435 and 1440.1 He was the son of Archibald Campbell, Master of Campbell and Elizabeth Somerville.1 He married Isabel Stewart, daughter of John Stewart, 2nd Lord Lorn, befor
1 CONC e 9 April 1465.1 He died on 10 May 1493.1 
1 CONT      Colin Campbell, 1st Earl of Argyll succeeded to the title of 2nd Lord Campbell [S., 1445] in 1453.1 He was created 1st Earl of Argyll [Scotland] in 1457.1 He held the office of Bailie of Cowal in 1460.1 He held the office of 
1 CONC Master of the Household in 1464.1 He was created 1st Lord Lorne [Scotland] on 17 April 1470, together with a conveyance to him of the lands of and lordship of Lorne, after the resignation of his wife's uncle, Walter Stewart as Lor
1 CONC d Lorne (who was then created Lord Innermeath).1 He held the office of Hereditary Justiciary and Sheriff of the lordship of Lorne in 1471.1 He held the office of Lieutenant and Commissary of Argyll in 1479.1 He held the office of 
1 CONC Chancellor [Scotland] between 1483 and February 1488, being deprived of office after the rebellion and death of King James III.1 In 1487 he joined the conspiracy of the nobles against King James III.1 He held the office of Chancel
1 CONC lor [Scotland] between June 1488 and 1493, reinstated by King James IV.1 
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Colin Campbell, 1st Earl of Argyll and Isabel Stewart
1 CONT Lady Helen Campbell+ b. b 1468
1 CONT Lady Isabel Campbell+ b. b 1474
1 CONT Archibald Campbell, 2nd Earl of Argyll+ b. b 1478, d. 9 Sep 1513
1 CONT Lady Marion Campbell+ b. b 1481
1 CONT Lady Catherine Campbell b. b 1493
1 CONT Lady Mary Campbell+ b. b 1493
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT
1 CONT [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct 
1 CONC or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume I, page 198. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage. 
