0 @N6000000002187771502@ NOTE Sir John Stewart, Lord of Bonkyl (M)
1 CONT b. before 1269, d. 22 July 1298, #5135
1 CONT Pedigree
1 CONT Last Edited=13 Jan 2003
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT      Sir John Stewart, Lord of Bonkyl was born before 1269. He was the son of Alexander Stewart, 4th High Steward of Scotland and Jean. He died on 22 July 1298, slain fighting for Sir William Wallace. 
1 CONT      Sir John Stewart, Lord of Bonkyl gained the title of Lord of Bonkyl. He fought in the Battle of Falkirk on 22 July 1298 in Falkirk, Scotland. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Sir John Stewart, Lord of Bonkyl and Margaret de Bonkyl
1 CONT Sir Alexander Stewart+ b. b 1285, d. 1319
1 CONT Sir Alan Stewart+ b. b 1298, d. 19 Jul 1333
1 CONT Sir Walter Stewart+ b. b 1298
1 CONT Sir James Stewart+ b. b 1298, d. 19 Jul 1333
1 CONT Sir John Stewart b. b 1298, d. 19 Jul 1333
1 CONT Isabella Stewart b. b 1298, d. a 15 Jul 1351
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Battle of Falkirk (1298)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Battle of Falkirk 
1 CONT Part of the Wars of Scottish Independence 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Date: July 21, 1298 
1 CONT Location: Falkirk, Scotland 
1 CONT Result: English victory 
1 CONT Casus belli: {{{casus}}} 
1 CONT Territory changes: {{{territory}}} 
1 CONT
1 CONT Combatants 
1 CONT Scotland England 
1 CONT Commanders 
1 CONT William Wallace Edward I of England 
1 CONT Strength 
1 CONT 500 cavalry, 9,500 infantry 2,250 cavalry, 12,900 infantry(there was much desertion amongst the Welsh troops, who made up a substantial part of Edward's infantry so this number might have been lower). 
1 CONT Casualties 
1 CONT
1 CONT Wars of Scottish Independence 
1 CONT Dunbar – Stirling Bridge – Falkirk – Stirling Castle – Methven – Bannockburn – Dupplin Moor – Halidon Hill 
1 CONT The Battle of Falkirk was a battle of the Wars of Scottish Independence.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Prelude 
1 CONT 2 Battle 
1 CONT 3 Aftermath 
1 CONT 4 Braveheart 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Prelude
1 CONT William Wallace had secured control of Scotland with his victory against the English at the Battle of Stirling Bridge in 1297. Edward Longshanks had been distracted with his wars on the European continent, and did not return to re
1 CONC take Scotland until six months after the English defeat.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Battle
1 CONT Edward and his reluctant army forced Wallace's Scots into a pitched battle at Falkirk. Wallace placed his men on higher ground overlooking a small creek (Westquarter Burn) with the Callender Wood to his rear. The ground opposite h
1 CONC is centre was boggy. He deployed his men in four schiltrons, with archers and light troops between them, while his Noble cavalry were held in reserve.
1 CONT
1 CONT Longshank's army entered the field in column led by three bodies of knights. The centre unit became mired in the marshy ground but disengaged itself to circle left while the second unit circled right to hit the Scottish left and r
1 CONC ight flanks respectively. The Scottish schiltrons held, but many archers and light troops were cut down by the English Knights. At this point, Wallace's noble cavalry abandoned the battle.
1 CONT
1 CONT Unable to make headway against the leveled pikes of the Scots schiltrons with his Knights, Longshanks moved forward his archers and Irish mercenaries. Several historical sources record that Longshank's subject Welsh longbow units 
1 CONC refused to participate. The English and Irish mercenaries were able, however, to inflict casualties and create confusion in the schiltrons, which the English Knights were eventually able to exploit. Finally, the schiltrons broke a
1 CONC nd the remnants of Wallace's army fled.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Aftermath
1 CONT Wallace survived, and Edward was not able to follow up on his victory: lack of supplies forced him to return to England. Wallace's campaign suffered greatly by the defeat at Falkirk. The Scottish struggle for independence ultimate
1 CONC ly passed next to Robert the Bruce.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Scottish cavalry have been judged by some people as treacherous for leaving the field at Falkirk. However, Wallace's plan purportedly called for them to make a nearly suicidal charge on the English troops. The Scottish cavalry
1 CONC  were few and those at Falkirk were a significant portion, if not nearly all of those that were available at that time. By staying behind to fight, they would almost certainly have inflicted a large amount of additional casualties
1 CONC  to the English army, but whether that would have turned Falkirk into a Scottish victory is not able to be determined based on what information is available to us now. The Scottish cavalry did fight later at the Battle of Bannockb
1 CONC urn, and that battle resulted in a firm Scottish victory.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Braveheart
1 CONT Mel Gibson's film Braveheart made a number of historical mistakes in its depiction of the battle. It showed the Irish charging, then stopping, shaking hands with the Scottish troops, and switching sides. This may have been a confu
1 CONC sion with the Welsh longbow units. Also, it describes the Irish as conscripts, an anachronism in the Middle Ages. Wallace is shown losing a one-on-one fight with Robert the Bruce, who fights on the English side, defending the king
1 CONC .
