0 @N6000000003615400723@ NOTE
1 CONT Crest Badge:A Savage head affronte, erased, proper.
1 CONT Motto:Vill God I Zall (Will God I shall).
1 CONT Gaelic Name:Meinn, Meinnearach.
1 CONT Sept Names: Dewar, Macandeoir, MacIndeor, MacMenzies, MacMinn, MacMonnies, Means, Mein, Meine, Mengues, Mennie, Meyners, Minns, Minnus, Monzie
1 CONT The name Menzie has many variations: Mengues, Mingies and Meyners. It is of Norman descent and appears in records of the 12th and 13th centuries.
1 CONT In 1249 Robert de Menyers was Lord High Chamberlain, and his son possessed the lands of Durisdeer in Dumfrieshire, and Weem, Aberfedly and Glendochart in Perthshire, which all passed to one of his sons, Robert and the lands in For
1 CONC tingall to his other son, Thomas.
1 CONT The Menzies were staunch supporters of Robert the Bruce, and as a result of their loyalty, they received more lands. In 1423, the title of Governor of Orkney and Shetland was given to David Menzies, and in 1487 Sir Robert de Mengu
1 CONC es received some land under the barony of Menzies.
1 CONT It is noted in the 1587 Roll of Clans, that there are the 'Menyessis, in Athoill and Apnadull'. Other titles include a Baronet of Nova Scotia in 1665, given to Sir Alexander Menzies for his contribution to the colony.
1 CONT Another branch of the Clan Menzie were the Menzies of Pitfoddels, where at the Battle of Cabisdale in 1650, the young chief carried the Royal standard against the Covenanters. This branch of Menzies is now dead.
0 @N6000000003839142140@ NOTE
1 CONT Sir Alexander de Forbes, when King James I. was a prisoner in England, led a following of a hundred horse and forty spearmen to France, where he fought against the English under Henry V. at the battle of Beauge in 1421 and is immo
1 CONC rtalised by the poet Ariosto. Later in life—some time between 1436 and 1442 —he was created a Lord of Parliament by James II.  The precise date of creation is not known, but in a precept, directed by James II to the lords of the e
1 CONC xchequer, dated 12th July 1442, he is styled Lord Forbes. He died in 1448. By his wife, Lady Elizabeth (sometimes called Lady Mary) Douglas, only daughter of George, Earl of Angus, and grand-daughter of King Robert II, he had two 
1 CONC sons and three daughters.
