0 @N6000000003615291609@ NOTE http://awt.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=dalmanic&id=I17342
1 CONT
1 CONT Fortingall in Perthshire 
1 CONT
1 CONT Dunkeld Cathedral
1 CONT
1 CONT Dunkeld Cathedral
1 CONT Gazetteer for Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT This pre-reformation cathedral is located on the north bank of the River Tay, just behind the centre of Dunkeld. Built between 1260 and 1501, on a site which supported a wattle monastery from the 6th century, which was rebuilt in 
1 CONC stone by Kenneth MacAlpin, King of the Scots, in 848 AD.
1 CONT
1 CONT The current building, which is a mixture of Gothic and Norman styles, is dedicated to St. Columba, who is said to have preached on the site for six months, and whose relics are thought to be buried under the chancel steps, having 
1 CONC been transported from Iona by Kenneth MacAlpin to avoid their loss to marauding vikings.
1 CONT
1 CONT The cathedral has had a turbulent history, being partially destroyed in 1560 following the post-reformation removal of Roman Catholic 'idoltry'. Again in 1689, following the Jacobite victory at Killiecrankie, the cathedral and muc
1 CONC h of Dunkeld was devastated by fire.
1 CONT
1 CONT Restorations have been undertakn over the years, most noteably of the Choir in 1908 and 1975. The cathedral remains an active church, within the community of the Church of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Visitors can see the Black Watch Regimental Memorial and in the Chapter House Museum, the SHE bible (1611), stone remnants of the original monastery and the tombstone of the fiddler Niel Gow (1727 - 1807).
1 CONT
1 CONT (The following is by James Irvine Robertson <mailto:jamesir@zetnet.co.uk> of Aberfeldy Perthshire)
1 CONT
1 CONT "The nobles resented James' determined effort to enforce the law and make his government respected and obeyed everywhere, and a movement against the king was led by those nearest the throne - the old Earl of Atholl and his grandso
1 CONC n Sir Robert Stewart, and Sir Robert Graham, uncle to the absent Menteith.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the night of 20th February 1437, Graham, with Stewart and a large following, broke into the royal apartments in the Blackfriars at Perth, and murdered James, escaping during the uproar which followed. They fled to what was 
1 CONC then described as the country of the "Wild Scottes," but the Queen, profiting by the reaction in favour of her young son, had them pursued and captured within a month. Sir Robert Graham, found hiding near Blair Atholl, was taken b
1 CONC y John Gorme Stewart & Robert Duncanson - Robert Riabhach, 4th chief of Clan Donnachaidh. Stewart, ancester of the Stewards of Garth, is on record as receiving an exchequer grant of Ð66 13s. 4d., while Robert obtained more lasting
1 CONC  benefits. In 1451, when James II came of age he rewarded Robert by erecting his lands
1 CONT into the Barony of Struan, which gave him considerable added power and prestige - the charter stating that it was granted for "the love and favour which we bear to the said Robert Duncansone for the capture of that most vile trait
1 CONC or the late Robert the Graham." In addition to this charter, the heirs of Robert Duncanson have borne on their coat of arms two reminders of this famous capture - the crest showing a hand supporting a crown, and, an unusual featur
1 CONC e, on the compartment below the shield lies "a wild man chained."
1 CONT
1 CONT "Besides being heir to Garth & Fortingall, he had so much land on the garry that Gilchrist Taylor, the poet who celebrated the hunting down of the conspirators, called the Garry John Stewart's river.' John Gorm died honourably on 
1 CONC the North Inch of Perth fighting along with the Sheriff of the county in defence of law and justice, against an audacious band of thieves, who made a strong and almost successful attempt to kill the Sheriff and rescue a thief who 
1 CONC had been condemned to the gallows.' from Pitscottie ' John Cormack, an Athole man, one of the principal sworn tyrants to the Earl Douglas, with a company of bloody tyrants, set upon Sir William Ruthven of that Ilk, Sheriff Pricipa
1 CONC l of Perth, to have slain himself and rest a brigand theif of Athole from the King's justice, who the Sheriff would have justified. But this Sir William Ruthven, seeing the danger coming so suddenly upon him, was much abased, and 
1 CONC prayed his friends and gentlemen that were with him for the time to take good part with him, and not to thole themselves to be murdered with a number of universal theives. And it was foughten with doubtsome chance until at last th
1 CONC e Sheriff was victor and John Cormack with thirty of his men, were manfully slain upon Midsummer Day, 1443 years."
0 @N6000000002187788267@ NOTE John Stewart, 1st Earl of Mar and Garioch
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT For the son of James III of Scotland, see John Stewart, Earl of Mar. 
1 CONT John Stewart, Earl of Mar and Garioch (c. 1456–c. 1479) was the youngest son of James II of Scotland and Mary of Guelders.
1 CONT
1 CONT James II bestowed the titles of Earl of Mar and Earl of Garioch on his son sometime between 1458 and 1459. In 1479, John was accused of treason and imprisoned at Craigmillar Castle. Shortly thereafter he died in suspicious circums
1 CONC tances, possibly on the orders of his brother James III.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Lynch, Michael (1992). Scotland: A New History. Pimlico, 156. ISBN 0-7126-9893-0. 
1 CONT Tabraham, Chris (2003). Craigmillar Castle - The Official Souvenir Guide. Historic Scotland, 6. ISBN 1-900168-10-3. 
1 CONT thePeerage.com. Retrieved on 2006-11-26. 
