0 @N6000000002188144869@ NOTE Maol Íosa V, Earl of Strathearn
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Maol Íosa V of Strathearn (also Maol Íosa of Orkney) was the last of the native Gaelic family of Strathearn mormaers. He ruled Strathearn as Mormaer/Earl between 1330 and 1334 , and Earl of Orkney between 1331 and 1350.
1 CONT
1 CONT His career started off so promisingly. After the death of his father Maol Íosa IV in 1329, he inherited Strathearn. In 1330, he inherited the Earldom of Orkney (with the Mormaerdom of Caithness) through his great-grandmother, Earl
1 CONC  Gilbert (Gille Brighde)'s daughter Maud, who had married Maol Íosa II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Maol Íosa's downfall came as a result of renewed Balliol onslaught which followed the death of the great Robert I. Maol Íosa chose to side with Edward Balliol and the English, and fought for the Balliol side at the Battle of Halid
1 CONC on Hill. The Anglo-Balliol alliance, though, turned its back on Maol Íosa, awarding the Mormaerdom to John de Warenne, 8th Earl of Surrey.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon the return to power of King David II, Maol Íosa was forgiven, but his Mormaerdom was not restored, instead going to Sir Maurice de Moravia. The Mormaer spent the rest of his days vainly trying to regain it.
1 CONT
1 CONT He had two wives, the second of whom was Marjory, daughter of Aodh, Earl of Ross. He had four daughters, but no sons. He died in 1350. Strathearn never returned to his heirs, who divided or competed over his more northern inherita
1 CONC nce: a grandson named Alexander de l'Arde took seat in Caithness, whereas Erengisle Sunesson, the husband of one of his daughters (possibly named Agnes), received the earl's title of Orkney. Ultimately, lands in both of these and 
1 CONC the Norse earldom (that by decision of Haakon VI of Norway in 1379)[citation needed] passed to his grandson Henry Sinclair I, Earl of Orkney, son of William Sinclair, Lord of Rosslyn, and Maol Iosa's (youngest) daughter Isobel of 
1 CONC Strathearn.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Neville, Cynthia J., Native Lorship in Medieval Scotland: The Earldoms of Strathearn and Lennox, c. 1140-1365, (Portland & Dublin, 2005)
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Maol Íosa IV Mormaer of Strathearn
1 CONT 1330-1334 Succeeded by
1 CONT Forfeit;
1 CONT to Gilbert de Moravia
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Magnus V Earl of Orkney
1 CONT 1331-1350 Succeeded by
1 CONT Erengisle Sunesson
