0 @N6000000004533089971@ NOTE Lord of Annandale
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes for Robert "the Competitor" de Brus VI:
1 CONT
1 CONT The four surviving Guardians were ruling the country and one of them,William Fraser, Bishop of St Andrews, wrote to Edward I of their fearsthat civil war would ensue, since the rival claimants were alreadyassembling their armies. 
1 CONC Edward offered himself as arbitrator, oncondition that the various claimants acknowledged him as the feudalsuperior of Scotland. In the end, thirteen competitors put their namesforward, and nine of the thirteen made the acknowledg
1 CONC ement required byEdward. They also agreed that possession of the lands and castles ofScotland should be given to him so that he could pass them on to therightful king, when the choice had been made.
1 CONT
1 CONT The thirteen claimants were then reduced to three: John Balliol,Robert Bruce and John Hastings, all of whom were descendants of thethree daughters of David, Earl of Huntingdon. After a further periodfor deliberation, Edward I awar
1 CONC ded the crown to John Balliol, thedescendant of the Earl's eldest daughter. In law, however, RobertBruce's claim was equally good because, although he was descended fromthe second daughter, he was a generation nearer David I (as h
1 CONC isgrandson).
