0 @N6000000002188001027@ NOTE Aodh, Earl of Ross
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Aodh of Ross, commonly known as Earl Hugh of Ross, was the third successor of Ferchar mac in tSagairt as Mormaer of Ross (1323-1333).
1 CONT
1 CONT He was also Chief of Clan Ross.
1 CONT
1 CONT Aodh was a favorite of King Robert I of Scotland, who endowed him with many lands. Aodh even married Robert's sister, Maud. Aodh's young brother, Iain, was given marriage to the Margaret Comyn, heiress of Buchan (although he died 
1 CONC childless).
1 CONT
1 CONT With Maud, Aodh had six children. Four of them were daughters, including Euphemia de Ross. All received prestigious marriage partners (including to the Counts of Buchan and Moray, to Mormaer Maol Íosa IV, Earl of Strathearn and th
1 CONC e future king Robert II.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was killed along many other Scottish nobles at the Battle of Halidon Hill on July 19, 1333, and was succeeded by his son and successor, Uilleam.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Bibliography
1 CONT Barrow, G.W.S., Robert Bruce and the Community of the Realm of Scotland, (Edinburgh, 1988) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Genealogy Page 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Uilleam II Mormaer of Ross
1 CONT 1323-1333 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Uilleam III 
0 @N6000000004533931739@ NOTE EARLDOM OF ROSS [SCT] (VI, 4)
1 CONT
1 CONT HUGH (DE ROSS), 6th EARL OF ROSS [SCT], son and heir, was godson toAlexander III [SCT]. After having brought relief to the Englishgarrison of Urquhart Castle, he received a safe-conduct to visit hisfather in prison, August 1297. U
1 CONC nder the indenture of 1303, he enteredthe service or Edward I. In December 1307 he was among the Scottishmagnates whom Edward II [ENG] directed to keep the peace, and in 1308he received that King's thanks for his good service. Fai
1 CONC ling help fromEngland, for which his father and he had appealed, he made his peacewith Brus. He was knighted before 5 December 1315. As Hugh de Ros hewas one of the guarantors, required by England, of the truce made 1June 1323, ha
1 CONC ving been "made Erll" on 17 March preceding; and, byspecial command of King Robert, he swore to maintain the marriagecontract between Prince David [SCT] and the Princess Joan of England,1328.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married, [? 1stly], Maud, daughter of Robert DE BRUS, jure uxorisEARL OF CARRICK (by his 1st wife Margaret, suo jure COUNTESS OFCARRICK) and sister of ROBERT I [SCT] (e). She, who was living inSeptember 1323, was buried at Fear
1 CONC n. He married, [? 2ndly], some timebefore 1329 (g), Margaret GRAHAM, said to be daughter of Sir DavidGRAHAM (h). He was killed, in command of the reserve at the battle ofHalidon Hill, 19 July 1333; and was buried at Fearn. His wid
1 CONC owmarried, 2ndly (dispensation, 13 April 1340], John DE BARCLAY.[Complete Peerage XI:144-5, (transcribed by Dave Utzinger)]
1 CONT
1 CONT (e) At the unknown date of this charter, he was already a knight.'Breve Cron'., p. 319, says that their son William was b. before theirmarriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT (g) When dispensation and legitimation of past and future offspringwere granted, 29 Nov.
1 CONT
1 CONT (h) Sir David was of Old Montrose, which he acquired by exchange withRobert de Brus.]
