0 @N6000000002187742803@ NOTE Battle of Halidon Hill
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Battle of Halidon Hill 
1 CONT Part of the Wars of Scottish Independence 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Date: July 19, 1333 
1 CONT Location: Halidon Hill, near Berwick 
1 CONT Result: Decisive English victory 
1 CONT Casus belli: {{{casus}}} 
1 CONT Territory changes: {{{territory}}} 
1 CONT
1 CONT Combatants 
1 CONT Scotland England 
1 CONT Commanders 
1 CONT Sir Archibald Douglas Edward III of England 
1 CONT Strength 
1 CONT 13,000 9,000 
1 CONT Casualties 
1 CONT 4,500 killed 14 killed 
1 CONT Wars of Scottish Independence 
1 CONT Dunbar – Stirling Bridge – Falkirk – Stirling Castle – Methven – Bannockburn – Dupplin Moor – Halidon Hill 
1 CONT
1 CONT Battle of Halidon Hill (July 19, 1333) was the last of the Wars of Scottish Independence, ending that forty year struggle. Scottish forces under Sir Archibald Douglas were heavily defeated on unfavourable terrain while trying to r
1 CONC elieve Berwick-upon-Tweed.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Background
1 CONT Edward Balliol had crowned himself King of Scotland in 1332, but supporters of David II soon forced him out. After losing at Arran he is reported to have fled across the border almost naked.
1 CONT
1 CONT Balliol returned in the spring of 1333 with an English army. He had appealed to Edward III and the young King agreed to support him, deciding that the cross-border skirmishing had broken the Treaty of Northampton, and accompanied 
1 CONC the force northwards. The army reached Durham by April 1 and laid siege to Berwick on April 12. After a two month siege, as was not uncommon, the forces in Berwick eventually agreed to surrender on July 20 unless relieved. Sir Ale
1 CONC xander Sutton (or Seton) gave his two sons to Edward as part of the agreement, they were later hanged.
1 CONT
1 CONT After an abortive attempt to take Queen Philippa from Bamburgh Castle, Sir Archibald Douglas, the guardian of David II, approached from Northumberland and crossed the Tweed to relieve Berwick with around 13,000 men. An English for
1 CONC ce of around 10,000 was arrayed on Halidon Hill to meet them.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Battle
1 CONT The battle lasted throughout July 19 but was almost entirely a slaughter of the Scottish forces. In a reverse of the terrain at Bannockburn the Scots had to approach through boggy ground and then climb up the hill to the waiting E
1 CONC nglish, they were easy targets for archers. Attacking en masse, over 500 Scottish nobles were killed including Douglas and more than 4,000 soldiers, more were killed as the English cavalry finally charged and chased the routed Sco
1 CONC ttish troops from the battelfield. English casualties were around fifteen.
1 CONT
1 CONT The siege of Berwick was then renewed and the town surrendered soon afterwards.
0 @N6000000005597778001@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT Battle of Halidon Hill  19 Jul 1333  was fought during the Second War of Scottish Independence  forces under Sir Archibald Douglas  were heavily defeated on unfavourable terrain while trying to relieve Berwick-upon-Tweed .
0 @N6000000002188001069@ NOTE Uilleam III, Earl of Ross
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Uilleam III of Ross, or William III of Ross, was the fourth successor of Ferchar mac in tSagairt, as Mormaer of Ross (1333/6-1370).
1 CONT
1 CONT Uilleam came into his inheritance at a torrid time, his father Aodh dying at the Battle of Halidon Hill. Uilleam temporarily lost many of his lands. However, he returned from Norway in 1336 and regained them. Uilleam cultivated th
1 CONC e friendship of the Stewarts, and was rewarded by being appointed Justiciar of Scotia. Uilleam's reign was marked by continued strife with his nominal vassal, Ranald MacRuadridh (Lord of Skye). In 1348, Uilleam killed Ranald with 
1 CONC eight of his men at the nunnery of Elcho.
1 CONT
1 CONT Uilleam married Màiri, the daughter of Aonghus Óg MacDomhnaill. They had an heir, Uilleam, but he died in 1357. Uilleam's lands were forfeited by King David II in 1370. Uilleam died in 1372, and the Mormaerdom was given to his dau
1 CONC ghter Euphemia with her husband Sir Walter Leslie, thus ending the dynasty started a century and a half before by the great Ferchar. The dominions eventually passed to the MacDonald Lords of the Isles.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Bibliography
1 CONT Roberts, John L., Lost Kingdoms: Celtic Scotland in the Middle Ages, (Edinburgh, 1997) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Genealogy Page 
1 CONT Great Clan Ross (N/B Unfortunately, much info depends on this unreliable site) 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Aodh Mormaer of Ross
1 CONT 1333/6-1370/2 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Euphemia m. Walter Leslie 
